const n={id:"1.1.1",module:1,title:"Meanings of Six Sigma",subtitle:"A metric, a methodology and a management philosophy",minutes:14,objectives:["Explain the three meanings of Six Sigma — a metric, a methodology (DMAIC) and a management philosophy — and recognise which one an exam question is using.","Describe sigma (σ) as the standard deviation and a sigma level as “how many standard deviations fit between the process average and the nearest specification limit”.","State the conventional 3.4 DPMO claim and explain the assumptions behind it, including the 1.5σ long-term shift.","Distinguish defects from variation, and explain why reducing variation makes a process more consistent for the customer.","Contrast data-driven Six Sigma improvement with traditional, opinion-led problem solving.","Link better process quality to business outcomes such as cost, SLA compliance, customer retention and revenue."],overview:`Ask five people what “[[six-sigma|Six Sigma]]” means and you may hear five answers: a quality target, a set of statistical tools, a belt-training programme, a project method or a corporate culture. Surprisingly, most of them are partly right. The term carries **three related meanings**, and the Green Belt exam expects you to separate them cleanly.

1. **A metric** — a way to express how capable a process is, using the [[sigma|sigma level]]. “Six Sigma performance” is conventionally quoted as **3.4 defects per million opportunities ([[dpmo|DPMO]])**.
2. **A methodology** — a disciplined, data-driven project roadmap, [[dmaic|DMAIC]] (Define, Measure, Analyze, Improve, Control), used to find and remove the causes of defects.
3. **A management philosophy** — a way of running a business in which decisions are based on data, customer requirements set the definition of quality, and improvement is continuous rather than occasional.

This lesson builds each meaning from the ground up, starting with the simple statistical idea that gives the method its name.`,sections:[{id:"three-meanings",heading:"Three meanings, one idea",body:`Think of a mobile network operator promising business customers that priority-1 incidents will be restored within four hours. The three meanings of [[six-sigma|Six Sigma]] map neatly onto that promise:

- As a **metric**, Six Sigma asks: *how often do we break the promise?* If 1,000,000 restoration opportunities produced 3.4 breaches, the process would be performing at the conventional Six Sigma level.
- As a **methodology**, it asks: *how do we systematically find out why breaches happen and stop them?* The answer is a [[dmaic|DMAIC]] project with a charter, measured baseline, verified [[root-cause|root causes]], tested solutions and a control plan.
- As a **philosophy**, it asks: *how should leaders behave so that this keeps happening?* Decisions are made with data, the customer’s requirement defines “good”, and every function owns its processes.

A useful memory hook: **metric = how good, methodology = how to get better, philosophy = how we think and lead.** The training book’s glossary describes the Six Sigma *system* in a similar spirit — analytical tools combined with project management, reporting and management techniques into a problem-solving and business-improvement methodology.`},{id:"variation-defects",heading:"Defects, variation and process consistency",body:`A [[defect]] is any failure to meet a customer requirement — a ticket closed without a fix, an invoice with a wrong amount, a fibre installation completed after the promised date. A [[defective]] *unit* is one that contains at least one defect. Keep the two apart: one invoice can carry three defects but it is still only one defective invoice.

Why do defects happen? Very often not because the process is “broken” on average, but because it is **inconsistent**. Every process output varies: no two incident restorations take exactly the same time. That spread is [[variation]]. Customers do not experience your average; they experience *their* incident. If the average restoration time is 2.5 hours but some take 7 hours, the average looks healthy while individual customers are angry.

Six Sigma therefore attacks two things:

- **Centring** — moving the average to where the customer wants it.
- **Spread** — shrinking variation so nearly every result lands inside the [[specification-limits|specification limits]].

The book frames the main options the same way: shift the mean, reduce the variation, or do both — with doing both described as the primary objective of Six Sigma projects. Widening the specification is rarely acceptable, because the specification comes from the [[voc|voice of the customer]].`},{id:"sigma-standard-deviation",heading:"Sigma and the standard deviation — the picture behind the name",body:`The Greek letter σ (sigma) is the symbol for the [[standard-deviation|standard deviation]], a measure of how far results typically sit from their average. Small σ means tightly clustered results; large σ means scattered results.

Now picture a bell-shaped (normal) curve of restoration times, with the customer’s **upper specification limit (USL)** drawn as a vertical line. Ask one question: *how many standard deviations fit between the process average and the nearest specification limit?* That count is the process’s **sigma level** (also called the Z value):

$$Z = \\frac{\\text{nearest spec limit} - \\text{process mean}}{\\sigma}$$

If only one σ fits, a large share of results fall outside the limit. If six σ fit, results beyond the limit become extremely rare. Notice what changes as the sigma level rises: **the specification stays where it is — the curve becomes narrower.** That is the whole Six Sigma idea in one image, and it is exactly what the animated visual in this lesson shows.

| Sigma level | Long-term DPMO (conventional, with 1.5σ shift) | Long-term yield |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | 691,462 | ≈ 30.9% |
| 2 | 308,537 | 69.1% |
| 3 | 66,807 | 93.3% |
| 4 | 6,210 | 99.38% |
| 5 | 233 | 99.977% |
| 6 | 3.4 | 99.99966% |

Each step up the table is not a small improvement: moving from 3σ to 4σ cuts defects roughly tenfold, and 4σ to 6σ by nearly 2,000 times.`},{id:"assumptions",heading:"Where does 3.4 DPMO come from? The honest assumptions",body:`Strictly, a process with six standard deviations between a centred mean and each specification limit would produce only about 0.002 defects per million. So why is Six Sigma quoted as **3.4 DPMO**? Because of a convention known as the [[sigma-shift|1.5 sigma shift]].

The reasoning, popularised at Motorola, is that a process measured over a short period looks better than it will over months and years: materials, people, tools and demand drift, and the mean wanders. The convention assumes that, in the long term, the mean will drift by about **1.5σ**. Six short-term sigmas minus 1.5 leaves 4.5 long-term sigmas, and the tail beyond 4.5σ of a normal curve is about **3.4 per million**. The training book shows exactly this short-term versus long-term DPMO table and the rule “add 1.5 to long-term Z to get short-term Z”.

Be honest about what this rests on:

- **Normality** — the tail calculation assumes a normal distribution. Many service measures, such as resolution times, are skewed.
- **The 1.5σ figure is an empirical convention**, not a law of nature. Real processes may drift more or less.
- **One tail** — the 3.4 figure counts defects beyond only one limit.
- **Opportunity counting** — DPMO depends on how many [[opportunity|opportunities]] per unit you define, so two teams can quote different sigma levels for the same work.

For the exam: know that **Six Sigma = 3.4 DPMO** and that it **includes the 1.5σ shift**. In practice: always ask how a sigma figure was calculated before comparing it with someone else’s.`},{id:"vs-traditional",heading:"How Six Sigma differs from traditional problem solving",body:`Traditional problem solving in many operations teams follows a familiar pattern: a bad month triggers a meeting, the most senior or most confident voice proposes a cause, a fix is launched, and attention moves on — until the problem returns. Six Sigma replaces opinion with evidence at every step.

- It starts from the **customer requirement** ([[ctq|CTQ]]), not from internal convenience.
- It **measures before it acts**, establishing a [[baseline]] so improvement can be proven.
- It treats the output as a result of inputs, [[y-equals-f-x|Y = f(X)]], and **verifies** root causes with data instead of assuming them.
- It **controls** the gain with ownership, monitoring and standard work, so the improvement survives the next reorganisation.

An analogy: traditional fixing is like taking painkillers for a recurring headache; Six Sigma is the diagnostic work-up that finds out why the headache keeps coming back.`}],whyItMatters:"Quality is not an abstract virtue — it is money and reputation. Every defect consumes effort to detect, correct and apologise for. That is the [[copq|cost of poor quality]]: repeat truck rolls, service credits paid for [[sla|SLA]] breaches, contact-centre calls generated by billing errors, and customers who quietly leave. Reducing variation lowers these costs and makes performance *predictable*, which lets an operator promise tighter SLAs with confidence and win business on reliability. Six Sigma links process metrics to business outcomes explicitly: a project is only worth doing if a better process measure moves a measure the business cares about.",businessExample:{title:"Incident restoration at a telecom operator",body:`A Digital Service Operations team measures priority-1 incident restoration against a four-hour SLA. The monthly report shows an average of 2 hours 40 minutes, so leadership assumes all is well — yet 5 of 120 incidents last quarter breached the SLA, triggering service credits to enterprise customers.

Seen through the three meanings:

- **Metric:** 5 breaches in 120 incidents is about 4.2% defective, roughly 41,700 defects per million — between 3σ and 3.5σ on the conventional table. The average hid the problem; the tail revealed it.
- **Methodology:** a Green Belt opens a DMAIC project. Define frames the problem (“5 of 120 P1 incidents exceeded 4 hours in Q2, costing service credits”) and the goal. Measure shows restoration times are widely spread. Analyze finds that breaches cluster where escalation to the vendor’s second-line team happens outside business hours.
- **Philosophy:** instead of blaming the night shift, the team changes the escalation rule and monitors restoration time on a control chart owned by the process owner.

The average barely moves, but the spread shrinks — and breaches fall to zero for two consecutive quarters.`},visual:"sigmaMeaning",visualCaption:"The specification limits never move. As the sigma level rises from 1σ to 6σ, the process spread shrinks, more standard deviations fit inside the limits and long-term DPMO falls from about 691,462 to 3.4. The solid curve includes the conventional 1.5σ long-term shift; the dashed curve is the centred short-term process.",definitions:[{term:"Six Sigma (metric)",definition:"A capability level conventionally equal to 3.4 defects per million opportunities, which assumes a 1.5σ long-term shift of the process mean."},{term:"Six Sigma (methodology)",definition:"A structured, data-driven project roadmap — DMAIC — for finding and removing the root causes of defects and controlling the gains."},{term:"Six Sigma (philosophy)",definition:"A management approach in which customer requirements define quality, decisions are made with data and improvement is continuous."},{term:"Sigma (σ)",definition:"The standard deviation: a measure of how widely individual results spread around their average."},{term:"Sigma level (Z)",definition:"The number of standard deviations that fit between the process mean and the nearest specification limit; higher means fewer defects."},{term:"Defect",definition:"Any failure to meet a customer requirement (CTQ). A unit can contain several defects."},{term:"Defective",definition:"A unit that contains one or more defects. Counted once, however many defects it has."},{term:"Variation",definition:"The natural spread of process outputs. Reducing it makes a process consistent and predictable for the customer."},{term:"Specification limits (LSL/USL)",definition:"The boundaries of acceptable performance, set by the customer or business — not calculated from process data."},{term:"1.5σ shift",definition:"An empirical convention that a process mean drifts about 1.5 standard deviations over the long term; it converts 6σ short-term into 3.4 DPMO long-term."}],comparisons:[{title:"The three meanings of Six Sigma",headers:["","Metric","Methodology","Management philosophy"],rows:[["Core question","How good is the process?","How do we improve it?","How do we think and lead?"],["Expressed as","Sigma level, DPMO, yield","DMAIC phases and tollgates","Data-based decisions, customer focus, continuous improvement"],["Example","“Billing runs at 4.1σ”","“A Green Belt is leading a DMAIC project on billing errors”","“No change is approved without baseline data”"],["Typical exam cue","3.4 DPMO, sigma level, defects per million","Define, Measure, Analyze, Improve, Control","Culture, leadership, voice of the customer"]]},{title:"Traditional problem solving vs Six Sigma",headers:["","Traditional / firefighting","Six Sigma"],rows:[["Starting point","Symptom or complaint","Customer requirement (CTQ) and business impact"],["Evidence","Experience and opinion","Measured baseline and statistical analysis"],["Causes","Assumed","Verified with data before solutions are chosen"],["Focus","The output (Y)","The inputs that drive the output (X’s)"],["After the fix","Attention moves on","Control plan, owner and monitoring sustain the gain"]]}],misconceptions:[{myth:"Six Sigma means zero defects.",reality:"It is a very low but non-zero defect rate — conventionally 3.4 DPMO. Zero defects is a separate philosophy associated with Philip Crosby."},{myth:"A process at 6σ produces 3.4 defects per million units.",reality:"It is per million *opportunities*. A unit with many opportunities can still be defective far more often than 3.4 in a million."},{myth:"Raising the sigma level means widening the specification limits.",reality:"The limits come from the customer and stay fixed. Sigma level rises when the process spread shrinks or the mean is better centred."},{myth:"Six Sigma is only for manufacturing.",reality:"Any process with a measurable output and a customer requirement can use it — incident management, invoicing, provisioning, contact centres and healthcare included."},{myth:"If the average meets the target, the process is fine.",reality:"Customers feel individual results. A good average can hide a wide spread with many defects in the tail."}],examTraps:[{trap:"“Six Sigma corresponds to approximately how many defects per million opportunities?” with 0.002 as an option.",explanation:"0.002 ppm is the theoretical value for a perfectly centred 6σ process. The conventional answer the exam expects is 3.4 DPMO, which includes the 1.5σ shift."},{trap:"Treating “defects” and “defectives” as the same thing.",explanation:"Count defects when a question says defects or DPMO; count units with at least one defect when it says defective units.",tag:"defective-units"},{trap:"“Which is NOT a meaning of Six Sigma?” — options include metric, methodology, philosophy and “a guarantee of zero defects”.",explanation:"Read for the NOT. Six Sigma never promises zero defects.",tag:"not"},{trap:"Confusing sigma (σ, the standard deviation) with sigma level (how many σ fit inside the specs).",explanation:"A smaller σ gives a higher sigma level. A question saying “sigma increased” may mean the spread got worse, not better."},{trap:"“3.4 per million” described as per million units or per million customers.",explanation:"The conventional metric is per million opportunities. Watch the unit of the denominator.",tag:"per-opportunity"}],workedExamples:[{title:"How many sigmas fit? Reading a sigma level from a picture",problem:"Ticket resolution time for a broadband support desk is roughly normal with a mean of 20 hours and a standard deviation of 4 hours. The SLA (upper specification limit) is 32 hours. What is the short-term sigma level (Z) to the USL, and what happens if the standard deviation is halved?",steps:["Distance from mean to the nearest limit: $32 - 20 = 12$ hours.","Divide by the standard deviation: $Z = 12 / 4 = 3$. Three standard deviations fit, so the process is at about **3σ** to the USL.","Halve the spread: σ = 2 hours. Now $Z = 12 / 2 = 6$ — six standard deviations fit.","Nothing about the SLA changed. Reducing variation alone moved the process from 3σ to 6σ."],answer:"Z = 3 at σ = 4 h; Z = 6 at σ = 2 h. Halving the spread doubles the sigma level.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Z = 32 / 4 = 8σ",whyWrong:"The sigma level measures the distance from the *mean* to the limit, not from zero. Always subtract the mean first."}},{title:"Converting a breach rate into DPMO and a sigma level",problem:"An invoice-processing team checks 4 opportunities on each invoice (customer, amount, tax code, due date). In 2,500 invoices they find 62 defects. Estimate DPMO and place it on the conventional sigma table.",steps:["Total opportunities = units × opportunities per unit = $2{,}500 \\times 4 = 10{,}000$.","DPO = defects ÷ opportunities = $62 / 10{,}000 = 0.0062$.","DPMO = DPO × 1,000,000 = **6,200**.","On the conventional (long-term) table, 6,210 DPMO ≈ **4σ**."],answer:"≈ 6,200 DPMO, which is approximately a 4σ process using the conventional table (1.5σ shift included).",wrongApproach:{calculation:"62 / 2,500 × 1,000,000 = 24,800 DPMO",whyWrong:"That is defects per million *units* (DPU × 10⁶). DPMO must divide by total opportunities, units × opportunities per unit."}}],lab:null,check:[{id:"chk-1.1.1-1",topic:"1.1.1",category:"definition",difficulty:"beginner",numerical:!1,stem:"A process performing at the conventional Six Sigma level produces approximately how many defects?",options:["3.4 defects per million units","3.4 defects per million opportunities","0.002 defects per million opportunities","Zero defects"],answer:1,explanation:"Six Sigma is conventionally quoted as 3.4 DPMO — defects per million **opportunities** — which builds in the 1.5σ long-term shift.",whyWrong:["The denominator is opportunities, not units. A unit can have many opportunities for a defect.","","This is the theoretical value for a perfectly centred, unshifted 6σ process; the conventional figure includes the 1.5σ shift.","Six Sigma is a very low defect rate, not zero defects."],traps:["per-opportunity"]},{id:"chk-1.1.1-2",topic:"1.1.1",category:"concept",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"A contact centre’s specification limits stay the same, but its sigma level rises from 3 to 5. What most likely happened?",options:["The customer agreed to wider specification limits","The standard deviation of the process decreased","The standard deviation of the process increased","More units were inspected"],answer:1,explanation:"Sigma level counts how many standard deviations fit between the mean and the nearest limit. With fixed limits, more of them fit only if each one (σ) gets smaller — the process became more consistent.",whyWrong:["The stem says the limits stayed the same; widening them is also rarely acceptable to customers.","","A larger σ means fewer standard deviations fit inside the limits, so the sigma level would fall.","Inspecting more units changes the sample, not the capability of the process."]},{id:"chk-1.1.1-3",topic:"1.1.1",category:"incorrect-statement",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"Which statement about the meanings of Six Sigma is **NOT** correct?",options:["As a metric, it expresses process capability as a sigma level or DPMO","As a methodology, it follows the DMAIC roadmap","As a philosophy, it bases decisions on data and customer requirements","As a goal, it guarantees zero defects once DMAIC is complete"],answer:3,explanation:"Six Sigma never guarantees zero defects; even at the conventional 6σ level it allows 3.4 DPMO. The other three statements describe its three meanings.",whyWrong:["This is a correct description of Six Sigma as a metric.","This is a correct description of Six Sigma as a methodology.","This is a correct description of Six Sigma as a management philosophy.",""],traps:["not"]},{id:"chk-1.1.1-4",topic:"1.1.1",category:"concept",difficulty:"advanced",numerical:!1,stem:"Why is a 6σ process conventionally said to produce 3.4 DPMO rather than about 0.002 DPMO?",options:["Because attribute data cannot be used to calculate sigma levels","Because the convention assumes the process mean drifts about 1.5σ over the long term","Because customers always widen specifications over time","Because Six Sigma counts defectives rather than defects"],answer:1,explanation:"The 1.5σ shift convention assumes long-term drift of the mean. 6σ short-term minus 1.5σ leaves 4.5σ, and the normal tail beyond 4.5σ is about 3.4 per million.",whyWrong:["Attribute data are commonly converted to sigma levels; the book notes they are treated as long-term data.","","Specifications come from the customer and are not assumed to widen.","DPMO counts defects per opportunity, not defective units."]},{id:"chk-1.1.1-5",topic:"1.1.1",category:"scenario",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"A service manager says: “Our average fault-repair time is well inside the SLA, so we have no quality problem.” What is the best Six Sigma response?",options:["Agree — meeting the average target is the definition of quality","Ask for the spread of repair times and the number of individual repairs outside the SLA","Suggest renegotiating the SLA to a longer time","Recommend adding more engineers immediately"],answer:1,explanation:"Customers experience individual results, not the average. Six Sigma looks at variation and at how many results fall outside the specification, using data before choosing any solution.",whyWrong:["An average can sit inside the limit while many individual results breach it.","","Moving the specification ignores the voice of the customer and does not improve the process.","Jumping to a solution before measuring the problem is the traditional firefighting habit Six Sigma replaces."],traps:["best"]}],takeaways:["Six Sigma has three meanings: a metric (how good), a methodology — DMAIC (how to improve) and a management philosophy (how to think and lead).","σ is the standard deviation; the sigma level is how many σ fit between the mean and the nearest specification limit.","Specification limits come from the customer. Sigma level rises by reducing spread or centring the process, not by moving the limits.","Conventional Six Sigma = 3.4 DPMO, which includes an assumed 1.5σ long-term shift; a centred, unshifted 6σ process would give about 0.002 per million.","The 3.4 figure rests on assumptions — normality, a conventional 1.5σ drift, one tail and a chosen opportunity count — so always ask how a sigma figure was calculated.","A defect is a failure to meet a requirement; a defective unit has at least one defect.","Six Sigma differs from firefighting by measuring first, verifying root causes and controlling the gain.","Lower variation means lower cost of poor quality, more predictable SLAs and happier customers."],flashcards:[{front:"The three meanings of Six Sigma",back:"Metric (sigma level / DPMO), methodology (DMAIC) and management philosophy (data-driven, customer-focused, continuous improvement)."},{front:"Conventional DPMO at 6σ",back:"3.4 defects per million opportunities (includes the 1.5σ long-term shift)."},{front:"What does σ stand for?",back:"The standard deviation — the typical spread of results around the mean."},{front:"Sigma level (Z) in words",back:"How many standard deviations fit between the process mean and the nearest specification limit."},{front:"Why 3.4 and not 0.002?",back:"The 1.5σ shift convention: 6σ short-term − 1.5σ drift = 4.5σ long-term, whose tail ≈ 3.4 per million."},{front:"Defect vs defective",back:"Defect = one failure to meet a requirement. Defective = a unit with one or more defects."},{front:"Conventional DPMO at 3σ and 4σ",back:"3σ ≈ 66,807 DPMO; 4σ ≈ 6,210 DPMO."},{front:"Who sets specification limits?",back:"The customer (VOC) or the business — never the process data."},{front:"Two levers to raise sigma level",back:"Centre the mean and reduce variation (doing both is the primary objective of Six Sigma projects)."}],coverage:"official-only",supplementaryNotes:["The syllabus (p. 7) lists the topic title only. The explanation of the three meanings, process consistency and the comparison with traditional problem solving is written independently from general Lean Six Sigma knowledge, because the book’s Define chapter (printed pp. 1–97) was not supplied.","The sigma-level / DPMO table and the 1.5σ shift rule are supported by the Measure chapter of the book (p. 223); shift-the-mean / reduce-variation options by pp. 209–210.","The telecom incident-restoration example and worked examples are original illustrations.","The 1σ long-term value uses the book’s table figure (691,462.5 DPMO, book p. 223), rounded to the nearest whole defect."],sources:[{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 7",pdfPage:7,note:"Define topic 1.1.1 “Meanings of Six Sigma”"},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 223",pdfPage:26,note:"Short-term vs long-term DPMO by sigma level (6σ → 3.4 DPMO LT) and the ±1.5 conversion between Z short-term and Z long-term"},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"pp. 209–210",pdfPage:12,note:"Centring vs spread: shift the mean, reduce variation, or both as the primary objective of Six Sigma projects"},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 208",pdfPage:11,note:"Voice of the Process vs Voice of the Customer; specification limits and defects in the tails"},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 589",pdfPage:92,note:"Glossary: Six Sigma (System) as a problem-solving and business-improvement methodology"}]},C=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:n},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),r={id:"1.1.2",module:1,title:"General History of Six Sigma & Continuous Improvement",subtitle:"From Shewhart and Deming to Motorola, GE and Lean Six Sigma",minutes:12,objectives:["Place the main quality pioneers — Shewhart, Deming, Juran, Feigenbaum, Ishikawa and Crosby — on a timeline and state each one’s signature contribution.","Explain how the Toyota Production System gave rise to what the West later called Lean, and who named it.","Describe the birth of Six Sigma at Motorola in the mid-1980s and its spread through AlliedSignal and General Electric.","Explain why Lean and Six Sigma merged into Lean Six Sigma and what each tradition contributes.","Avoid the common exam confusions between pioneers, their tools and their dates."],overview:`Six Sigma did not appear from nowhere in a Motorola meeting room. It is the latest chapter in a hundred-year story of people asking the same stubborn question: *why does work that should be identical keep coming out differently — and what can we do about it?*

The story has three strands that eventually braid together. The first is **statistical quality control**, born at Bell Laboratories in the 1920s and carried to Japan after the Second World War. The second is the **Toyota Production System**, which attacked waste and delay on the factory floor and later became known in the West as [[lean|Lean]]. The third is **Six Sigma itself**, launched at [[motorola|Motorola]] in the mid-1980s and scaled up by AlliedSignal and [[general-electric|General Electric]] in the 1990s. Around the early 2000s, organisations stopped choosing between them and began practising **Lean Six Sigma**. The animated timeline below lets you walk through each era.`,sections:[{id:"statistical-roots",heading:"The statistical roots: Shewhart, Deming and Juran",body:`In 1924, **Walter [[shewhart|Shewhart]]**, working for the Bell telephone system, sketched the first **control chart** in an internal memo. His insight was profound and still underpins every Six Sigma project: all processes vary, but there are two kinds of [[variation]] — the ordinary, built-in “common cause” noise and the unusual “special cause” signal. Managers who react to every wiggle make things worse; managers who learn to tell the two apart can improve the system. Shewhart also described a cycle of specification, production and inspection that later evolved into **Plan–Do–Check–Act ([[pdca|PDCA]])**.

**W. Edwards [[deming|Deming]]** carried Shewhart’s ideas to Japan, lecturing to Japanese engineers and executives from 1950. Japan honoured him with the Deming Prize. Deming taught that most quality problems belong to the *system*, which only management can change. Later he summarised his philosophy as the **14 Points for Management** (in *Out of the Crisis*, 1980s) and preferred the name **Plan–Do–Study–Act (PDSA)**, stressing that the third step is learning, not just checking.

**Joseph [[juran|Juran]]**, author of the *Quality Control Handbook* (1951), also lectured in Japan in the 1950s. He framed quality management as the **Juran Trilogy** — quality planning, quality control and quality improvement — and popularised the idea of the “vital few and the trivial many”, naming it the **Pareto principle** after the economist Vilfredo Pareto. If you remember only one thing: Juran *named* the Pareto principle; Pareto himself studied the distribution of wealth.`},{id:"quality-movement",heading:"Widening the lens: Feigenbaum, Ishikawa and Crosby",body:`**Armand Feigenbaum** argued in the 1950s that quality cannot be inspected in by one department — it is everyone’s job, from design through sales and service. He called this **Total Quality Control**, an idea that fed into Total Quality Management (TQM) and into the modern view of quality costs.

**Kaoru Ishikawa** made quality tools accessible to front-line workers in Japan. He is known for the **cause-and-effect (fishbone) diagram** and for championing **quality circles** — small groups of employees solving problems in their own area — from the early 1960s.

**Philip Crosby** brought a sharper, executive-friendly message to the West. His book *Quality Is Free* (1979) argued that doing things right the first time costs less than fixing failures, and he promoted the standard of **zero defects** and the measurement of the *price of nonconformance* — an ancestor of today’s [[copq|cost of poor quality]]. Be careful: zero defects is Crosby’s slogan, **not** the definition of Six Sigma, which accepts 3.4 defects per million opportunities.`},{id:"toyota-lean",heading:"Toyota and the origins of Lean",body:`While the statisticians focused on variation, Toyota focused on **flow and waste**. Its roots go back to **Sakichi Toyoda**, whose automatic loom stopped itself when a thread broke — the origin of [[jidoka]], “automation with a human touch”. **Kiichiro Toyoda** pushed the idea of making only what is needed, when it is needed — [[jit|just-in-time]]. From the late 1940s through the 1970s, engineer **Taiichi Ohno** turned these ideas into the [[toyota-production-system|Toyota Production System (TPS)]], with tools such as kanban and a relentless war on [[muda]] (waste). **Shigeo Shingo** contributed quick changeover (SMED) and mistake-proofing (poka-yoke).

The West noticed when Japanese cars began winning on both quality and cost. In 1988, MIT researcher **John Krafcik** coined the term **“lean production”** to describe Toyota’s approach, and the 1990 book *The Machine That Changed the World* (Womack, Jones and Roos) made “Lean” famous. A later book, *Lean Thinking* (1996), generalised the principles beyond car plants.

Notice the difference in emphasis: Lean asks *“how fast and how smoothly does value flow?”*; the statistical tradition asks *“how consistent is the result?”*`},{id:"motorola-ge",heading:"Motorola invents Six Sigma; AlliedSignal and GE scale it",body:`By the early 1980s Motorola was losing ground to Japanese competitors on quality. Engineer **Bill Smith** observed that products with many defects found during manufacturing tended to fail in the field, and argued for designing and running processes with a large safety margin — enough that about six standard deviations fit inside the specification. Working with colleagues and with strong backing from chief executive **Bob Galvin**, Motorola launched [[six-sigma|Six Sigma]] around **1986–87** as a company-wide quality programme with a stretch target of 3.4 defects per million opportunities. In **1988**, Motorola was among the first winners of the newly created **Malcolm Baldrige National Quality Award** in the United States. The IASSC syllabus itself credits the DMAIC model to Motorola.

The method spread when **Larry Bossidy** made Six Sigma central at **AlliedSignal** in the early 1990s, and then when **Jack Welch** launched it across **[[general-electric|General Electric]]** from **1995**. GE turned Six Sigma into a leadership system: trained [[green-belt|Green]] and [[black-belt|Black Belts]], linked projects to financial results and tied executive bonuses to participation. GE’s public reports of large savings did more than anything else to make Six Sigma a global management movement — and to spread it into services, finance and, later, telecoms.`},{id:"lean-six-sigma",heading:"The merger: Lean Six Sigma",body:`For a while, Lean and Six Sigma competed for the same improvement budgets. Practitioners soon realised they solve different halves of the same problem. A process can be consistent but slow (low variation, lots of waiting) or fast but erratic. Lean removes waste and speeds up flow; Six Sigma removes variation and defects. By the **early 2000s**, books and corporate programmes were combining them as **Lean Six Sigma**, typically using [[dmaic|DMAIC]] as the backbone and Lean tools inside it.

Think of a fault-repair process at a telecom operator. Lean thinking spots the ticket sitting in a queue for six hours waiting for a dispatcher; Six Sigma thinking finds out why one in twenty repairs fails the first time. Customers need both problems solved.

| Era | Main idea | Key names |
|---|---|---|
| 1920s–1950s | Statistical control of variation | Shewhart, Deming, Juran |
| 1950s–1970s | Quality is everyone’s job; worker-led tools | Feigenbaum, Ishikawa |
| 1920s–1970s | Flow, waste elimination, just-in-time | Toyoda family, Ohno, Shingo |
| 1979–1990 | Cost of quality; “lean” named | Crosby, Krafcik, Womack & Jones |
| Mid-1980s–1990s | Six Sigma metric and DMAIC | Bill Smith, Motorola, Bossidy, Welch |
| Early 2000s → | Lean Six Sigma | Combined practice worldwide |`}],whyItMatters:"History is examinable, but it is also practical. Knowing *why* each idea appeared helps you choose the right tool. When a telecom NOC’s ticket volumes swing wildly, Shewhart’s question — is this common-cause noise or a special-cause signal? — stops the team from overreacting. When enterprise customers complain about provisioning lead times, Toyota’s lens on waiting and flow is the right one. When SLA breaches are rare but costly, the Six Sigma lens on defects and variation applies. And when you brief a vendor or a senior manager, being able to say “this is the same disciplined approach Motorola and GE used” gives the method credibility.",businessExample:{title:"A telecom operator retraces the history in one programme",body:`A regional mobile operator’s service-operations unit started its improvement journey with weekly incident counts plotted on a simple control chart — pure Shewhart. It quickly learned that most week-to-week swings were noise, and stopped launching investigations every time the count rose by two.

Next, a Lean review of the fibre-installation process (Toyota’s lens) found that a work order spent more time waiting for survey approval than being worked on; batching approvals daily removed several days of queue time.

Finally, a DMAIC project (Motorola’s and GE’s lens) tackled the remaining first-visit failure rate, verifying with data that most failures traced to one missing pre-check. Leadership now reports progress as a Lean Six Sigma programme — speed from Lean, consistency from Six Sigma — with each project linked to a business metric, as GE insisted.`},visual:"sixSigmaHistory",visualCaption:"Walk the eras of continuous improvement. Each step reveals an era; select any milestone to read what happened and why it matters for a Green Belt.",definitions:[{term:"Control chart",definition:"Shewhart’s 1920s chart that plots a process over time against statistically calculated limits to separate common-cause from special-cause variation."},{term:"PDCA / PDSA",definition:"Plan–Do–Check (or Study)–Act: an iterative improvement cycle derived from Shewhart’s work and popularised by Deming, who preferred “Study”."},{term:"Deming’s 14 Points",definition:"Deming’s management principles for transforming an organisation, such as constancy of purpose and ceasing dependence on mass inspection."},{term:"Juran Trilogy",definition:"Juran’s view of quality management as three processes: quality planning, quality control and quality improvement."},{term:"Total Quality Control",definition:"Feigenbaum’s idea that quality is the responsibility of every function across the organisation, not only inspection."},{term:"Zero defects",definition:"Crosby’s performance standard that work should be done right the first time; distinct from Six Sigma’s 3.4 DPMO target."},{term:"Toyota Production System (TPS)",definition:"Toyota’s system built on just-in-time flow and jidoka (built-in quality), developed largely by Taiichi Ohno; the basis of Lean."},{term:"Lean",definition:"Western name, coined by John Krafcik in 1988, for the TPS approach of maximising customer value by eliminating waste."},{term:"Lean Six Sigma",definition:"The combination, common since the early 2000s, of Lean’s waste and flow focus with Six Sigma’s variation and defect focus, usually structured by DMAIC."}],comparisons:[{title:"Who gave us what?",headers:["Pioneer","Signature contribution","Remember it as"],rows:[["Walter Shewhart","Control chart (1924); common vs special cause; cycle that became PDCA","The father of statistical quality control"],["W. Edwards Deming","Taught Japan from 1950; 14 Points; PDSA","Management owns the system"],["Joseph Juran","Quality Control Handbook; Juran Trilogy; named the Pareto principle","Vital few, trivial many"],["Armand Feigenbaum","Total Quality Control","Quality is everyone’s job"],["Kaoru Ishikawa","Cause-and-effect (fishbone) diagram; quality circles","Tools for the front line"],["Philip Crosby","Zero defects; Quality Is Free (1979); price of nonconformance","Do it right the first time"],["Taiichi Ohno","Toyota Production System; waste elimination","The architect of Lean"],["Bill Smith (Motorola)","Six Sigma concept, mid-1980s","The father of Six Sigma"],["Jack Welch (GE)","Company-wide Six Sigma from 1995","Six Sigma as a leadership system"]]},{title:"Lean vs Six Sigma traditions",headers:["","Lean (Toyota)","Six Sigma (Motorola)"],rows:[["Main enemy","Waste and delay","Variation and defects"],["Key question","How smoothly and quickly does value flow?","How consistent is the result against the requirement?"],["Typical tools","Value stream thinking, 5S, kanban, kaizen","DMAIC, statistics, capability, DPMO"],["Origin","Toyota, late 1940s–1970s; named 1988","Motorola, mid-1980s"]]}],misconceptions:[{myth:"Six Sigma was invented at General Electric.",reality:"It was developed at Motorola in the mid-1980s. GE, under Jack Welch from 1995, made it famous and scaled it as a leadership system."},{myth:"Vilfredo Pareto created the Pareto chart as a quality tool.",reality:"Pareto studied wealth distribution. Juran recognised the general “vital few” pattern and named it after him."},{myth:"Lean was invented in the United States in the 1990s.",reality:"The practices come from the Toyota Production System in Japan; only the word “lean” was coined by a US researcher, in 1988."},{myth:"Zero defects and Six Sigma mean the same thing.",reality:"Zero defects is Crosby’s philosophy; Six Sigma’s conventional target is 3.4 DPMO."},{myth:"Lean Six Sigma replaced statistical quality control.",reality:"It builds on it. Shewhart’s control chart is still a core Control-phase tool."}],examTraps:[{trap:"“Which company is credited with ORIGINATING Six Sigma?” with GE as a tempting option.",explanation:"GE popularised it; Motorola originated it. Watch for verbs like originated, developed, popularised, deployed.",tag:"first"},{trap:"Attributing PDCA solely to Deming.",explanation:"The cycle grew from Shewhart’s work; Deming popularised it (and later called it PDSA). An option naming Shewhart as the origin is usually the precise answer."},{trap:"Mixing up Juran and Pareto.",explanation:"A question about who applied the 80:20 idea to quality, or named the Pareto principle, expects Juran."},{trap:"“Which is NOT associated with the Toyota Production System?” — options include just-in-time, jidoka, kanban and DMAIC.",explanation:"DMAIC is Six Sigma (Motorola), not TPS.",tag:"not"}],workedExamples:[{title:"Matching a problem to the tradition that fits it",problem:"A contact-centre manager describes three problems: (a) weekly abandoned-call counts swing up and down and the team investigates every rise; (b) refund requests wait four days in a queue before anyone touches them; (c) 3% of refunds are paid at the wrong amount. Which historical tradition offers the most natural first lens for each?",steps:["(a) is about reacting to ordinary variation. Shewhart’s control chart separates common-cause noise from special-cause signals — use the statistical-quality lens.","(b) is about waiting, a form of waste in flow. The Toyota/Lean lens fits: map the flow and remove the queue.","(c) is about defects against a requirement. The Motorola Six Sigma lens fits: measure the defect rate, find verified root causes with DMAIC.","In a Lean Six Sigma programme all three would sit inside one improvement framework, often DMAIC."],answer:"(a) Shewhart / statistical process control; (b) Toyota / Lean; (c) Motorola / Six Sigma DMAIC.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Treat all three as “run a Six Sigma project on each”.",whyWrong:"Not wrong as a framework, but it misses that (a) may need no project at all — only stopping overreaction to noise — and that (b) is a flow problem best seen through Lean."}},{title:"Putting events in the right order",problem:"Order these events: GE launches Six Sigma company-wide; Shewhart’s control chart memo; Krafcik coins “lean production”; Deming begins lecturing in Japan; Motorola wins the Malcolm Baldrige award.",steps:["Shewhart’s control chart memo — 1924.","Deming begins lecturing in Japan — 1950.","Krafcik coins “lean production” — 1988.","Motorola among the first Baldrige winners — 1988 (Six Sigma had been launched at Motorola around 1986–87).","GE launches Six Sigma under Jack Welch — from 1995."],answer:"Shewhart (1924) → Deming in Japan (1950) → Krafcik and Motorola’s Baldrige award (both 1988) → GE (1995)."}],lab:null,check:[{id:"chk-1.1.2-1",topic:"1.1.2",category:"definition",difficulty:"beginner",numerical:!1,stem:"Which organisation originally developed Six Sigma and the DMAIC approach?",options:["General Electric","Toyota","Motorola","AlliedSignal"],answer:2,explanation:"Six Sigma was developed at Motorola in the mid-1980s; the IASSC syllabus also credits the DMAIC model to Motorola.",whyWrong:["GE, under Jack Welch from 1995, popularised and scaled Six Sigma but did not originate it.","Toyota developed the Toyota Production System, the basis of Lean.","","AlliedSignal adopted Six Sigma under Larry Bossidy in the early 1990s, after Motorola."],traps:["first"]},{id:"chk-1.1.2-2",topic:"1.1.2",category:"definition",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"Who is credited with naming the Pareto principle (“vital few and trivial many”) and applying it to quality problems?",options:["Vilfredo Pareto","Joseph Juran","Kaoru Ishikawa","Walter Shewhart"],answer:1,explanation:"Juran recognised the vital-few pattern as universal and named it after Pareto, who had observed it in the distribution of wealth.",whyWrong:["Pareto observed unequal wealth distribution; he did not apply it to quality.","","Ishikawa is known for the fishbone diagram and quality circles.","Shewhart is known for the control chart and the cycle that became PDCA."]},{id:"chk-1.1.2-3",topic:"1.1.2",category:"compare",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"Which pairing of person and contribution is correct?",options:["Philip Crosby — Total Quality Control","Taiichi Ohno — the Toyota Production System","Armand Feigenbaum — zero defects","Jack Welch — the first control chart"],answer:1,explanation:"Taiichi Ohno was the chief architect of the Toyota Production System, the foundation of Lean.",whyWrong:["Total Quality Control is Feigenbaum’s concept; Crosby is linked to zero defects.","","Zero defects is Crosby’s idea; Feigenbaum is linked to Total Quality Control.","The control chart is Shewhart’s (1924); Welch led GE’s Six Sigma deployment."]},{id:"chk-1.1.2-4",topic:"1.1.2",category:"concept",difficulty:"advanced",numerical:!1,stem:"Why did Lean and Six Sigma merge into Lean Six Sigma?",options:["Because Lean had been proven not to work in service industries","Because Six Sigma tools were too expensive for Toyota","Because Lean mainly targets waste and flow while Six Sigma mainly targets variation and defects, and processes usually suffer from both","Because the IASSC required it for certification"],answer:2,explanation:"The traditions are complementary: Lean speeds up flow by removing waste; Six Sigma makes results consistent by removing variation and defects. Combining them addresses both.",whyWrong:["Lean is widely used in services; the merger was about complementarity, not failure.","Cost was not the reason; Toyota’s system predates Six Sigma.","","The merger happened in industry practice; certification bodies followed it."]}],takeaways:["Shewhart (1924) created the control chart and the idea of common vs special cause variation; his cycle became PDCA.","Deming carried statistical thinking to Japan from 1950, wrote the 14 Points and preferred PDSA.","Juran gave us the Quality Trilogy and named the Pareto principle; Feigenbaum gave us Total Quality Control; Ishikawa the fishbone diagram and quality circles; Crosby zero defects and Quality Is Free.","The Toyota Production System (Toyoda family, Ohno, Shingo) is the root of Lean; Krafcik coined “lean” in 1988 and The Machine That Changed the World (1990) popularised it.","Six Sigma was born at Motorola around 1986–87 (Bill Smith, Bob Galvin); Motorola won an early Baldrige award in 1988.","AlliedSignal (Bossidy) and GE (Welch, from 1995) scaled Six Sigma into a leadership system linked to financial results.","Lean Six Sigma, common since the early 2000s, combines waste/flow (Lean) with variation/defects (Six Sigma), usually inside DMAIC."],flashcards:[{front:"Who created the control chart, and when?",back:"Walter Shewhart, 1924, at Western Electric (the Bell telephone system; Bell Labs was formed in 1925)."},{front:"PDCA vs PDSA",back:"Same cycle. It grew from Shewhart’s work; Deming popularised it and preferred “Study” to stress learning."},{front:"Juran Trilogy",back:"Quality planning, quality control, quality improvement."},{front:"Who named the Pareto principle?",back:"Joseph Juran (after economist Vilfredo Pareto)."},{front:"Crosby’s two big ideas",back:"Zero defects, and “quality is free” — the cost of nonconformance outweighs the cost of doing it right."},{front:"Who coined the term “lean”?",back:"John Krafcik, 1988; popularised by The Machine That Changed the World (1990)."},{front:"Architect of the Toyota Production System",back:"Taiichi Ohno (building on Sakichi and Kiichiro Toyoda; Shingo added SMED and poka-yoke)."},{front:"Where and when did Six Sigma start?",back:"Motorola, mid-1980s (around 1986–87), led by engineer Bill Smith with CEO Bob Galvin’s backing."},{front:"Who scaled Six Sigma at GE?",back:"Jack Welch, from 1995 (after Larry Bossidy at AlliedSignal)."},{front:"When did Lean Six Sigma emerge?",back:"Early 2000s, combining Lean’s waste/flow focus with Six Sigma’s variation/defect focus."}],coverage:"supplementary",supplementaryNotes:["None of the supplied book pages covers the history of Six Sigma or Lean (the Define chapter, printed pp. 1–97, was not supplied, and the other parts contain no history). This lesson is written from general, well-established quality-management history.","The only historical statement in the supplied sources is the syllabus Introduction (p. 2), which describes the DMAIC model as originally established by Motorola.","Dates are given only where they are widely documented (1924, 1950, 1951, 1979, 1988, 1990, 1995). Less precise events are phrased approximately (e.g. “around 1986–87”, “early 2000s”)."],sources:[{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 7",pdfPage:7,note:"Define topic 1.1.2 “General History of Six Sigma & Continuous Improvement”"},{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 2",pdfPage:2,note:"Introduction: Lean Six Sigma as the DMAIC model, originally established by Motorola"}]},A=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:r},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),l={id:"1.1.3",module:1,title:"Deliverables of a Lean Six Sigma Project",subtitle:"What each DMAIC phase must hand over — and what Define produces",minutes:13,objectives:["Explain what a phase deliverable is and why DMAIC projects are managed through deliverables and tollgate reviews.","List the main deliverables of each DMAIC phase and place a given output in the correct phase.","Describe the Define-phase outputs in detail: charter, business case, problem and goal statements, scope, VOC/CTQs, high-level process view, team, stakeholder analysis and preliminary COPQ.","Explain how projects are selected and aligned with business objectives before Define is closed.","Describe who is responsible for which deliverables, and what happens at a tollgate review."],overview:`A Lean Six Sigma project is not judged by how busy the team was; it is judged by what it **delivers**. Each phase of [[dmaic|DMAIC]] — Define, Measure, Analyze, Improve, Control — has a set of outputs that must exist, be evidenced and be accepted before the next phase begins. Those outputs are the project’s **deliverables**, and the formal checkpoint at the end of each phase is a [[tollgate|tollgate review]].

Think of DMAIC like the stages of building a mobile network site: you would never mount antennas before the structural survey is signed off, and you would never hand the site to operations without acceptance testing. Deliverables are the signed-off survey and the acceptance certificate of an improvement project. This lesson walks through all five phases, then zooms into Define, because Define deliverables are what the Green Belt exam examines most heavily in this module.`,sections:[{id:"why-deliverables",heading:"Why DMAIC is deliverable-driven",body:`Every phase answers a question, and its deliverables are the *evidence* that the question has been answered:

- **Define** — *What is the problem, why does it matter, and what will success look like?*
- **Measure** — *How big is the problem today, and can we trust our data?*
- **Analyze** — *What are the verified root causes?*
- **Improve** — *Which solutions remove those causes, and do they work?*
- **Control** — *How do we keep the gain and prove the benefit?*

Without explicit deliverables, teams drift. A classic failure in service operations is to jump from a bad SLA month straight to a solution (“let’s hire two more engineers”) without a measured [[baseline]] or a verified [[root-cause|root cause]]. Deliverables make that shortcut visible: at the Analyze tollgate, “we think it’s staffing” is not a deliverable; “staffing explains 8% of breaches; out-of-hours vendor escalation explains 61%” is.

The training book’s DMAIC roadmap shows the same discipline. It starts with the [[champion|Champion]] and [[process-owner|Process Owner]] identifying the problem area, then lists the Define steps as *determine appropriate project focus*, *estimate COPQ* and *establish the team*, and ends in Control with *verify financial impact*.`},{id:"phase-by-phase",heading:"Deliverables phase by phase",body:`The table below is a practical consolidation. The book’s phase-gate checklists for Measure, Analyze and Improve each repeat a common core — team members, [[primary-metric|primary metric]], [[secondary-metric|secondary metric(s)]], project plan, and issues and barriers — and then add phase-specific items.

| Phase | Purpose | Typical deliverables |
|---|---|---|
| **Define** | Frame the right problem | Project charter (business case, problem statement, goal statement, scope, team, timeline), VOC translated into CTQs, high-level process view, stakeholder analysis, preliminary COPQ estimate, Define tollgate sign-off |
| **Measure** | Quantify the current state | Detailed process map, data collection plan, validated measurement system (MSA), baseline performance and capability of Y, list of potential X’s |
| **Analyze** | Find verified root causes | Data and hypothesis-test results, prioritised and *verified* root causes (vital few X’s), updated benefit estimate |
| **Improve** | Implement proven solutions | Solution selection and justification, pilot or experiment results, implementation plan, risk assessment, measured improvement in Y |
| **Control** | Sustain and hand over | Control plan, monitoring (e.g. control charts), standard work and training, handover to the process owner, verified financial benefits, project closure |

Two boundaries are worth memorising. First, **root causes are verified in Analyze, not Define** — Define may list suspicions, but proving them is Analyze’s job. Second, **benefits are *estimated* in Define and *verified* in Control**; confusing the two is a favourite exam trap.`},{id:"define-outputs",heading:"Define outputs in detail",body:`Define produces the project’s foundation. Its central document is the [[project-charter|project charter]], a one-to-two-page agreement between the team and its sponsor.

- **[[business-case|Business case]]** — why this project, why now, and how it links to business goals (revenue, cost, customer experience, compliance).
- **[[problem-statement|Problem statement]]** — what is wrong, where, since when and how big, stated with data and *without* assuming a cause or a solution.
- **[[goal-statement|Goal statement]]** — the measurable improvement target and date, ideally a [[smart-goal|SMART]] goal.
- **[[project-scope|Scope]]** — what is [[in-scope|in scope]] and [[out-of-scope|out of scope]], to prevent [[scope-creep|scope creep]].
- **Team and roles** — Champion or sponsor, Belt, process owner, team members and, where benefits are financial, a [[finance-representative|finance representative]].
- **Milestones** — target dates for each tollgate.

Around the charter, Define also delivers: the [[voc|voice of the customer]] translated into measurable [[ctq|CTQs]]; a **high-level process view** that sets the process start and end (often drawn as a [[sipoc|SIPOC]], although the syllabus formally places SIPOC and process mapping under Measure, topic 2.1.2); a **[[stakeholder]] analysis** showing who is affected and how much support exists; and a **preliminary [[copq|COPQ]]** estimate showing what the problem costs today.

The book’s project status review summarises what Define should achieve: the team understands the problem and its impact on the business, has established firm improvement objectives, and has quantified the output characteristic. Notice that all three are about *understanding and framing*, not solving.`},{id:"selection-alignment",heading:"Project selection and business alignment",body:`The best charter in the world cannot rescue the wrong project. Before Define is closed, leadership should be confident the project is worth doing. Good Lean Six Sigma projects usually share these features:

- **Aligned** with a strategic objective or a key [[kpi|KPI]] (for example, enterprise SLA compliance or cost-to-serve).
- **Measurable** — there is, or can be, data on the output Y.
- **Meaningful** — the [[copq|COPQ]] or customer impact justifies the effort.
- **Unknown cause** — if the root cause and fix are already known, it is a “just do it” task, not a DMAIC project.
- **Manageable scope** — achievable in a few months by one team. “Fix customer experience” is a programme; “reduce repeat fault visits for FTTH customers in the northern region” is a project.

Alignment flows in both directions: the [[vob|voice of the business]] says what matters strategically, and the [[voc|voice of the customer]] says what “good” looks like. A project that improves an internal metric nobody downstream cares about is a poor choice, however elegant the statistics.`},{id:"roles-tollgates",heading:"Who owns what — and how tollgate reviews work",body:`Deliverables need owners. In a typical deployment:

- The **[[executive-sponsor|Sponsor]] or [[champion|Champion]]** selects and prioritises the project, approves the charter, removes barriers and signs each tollgate.
- The **[[green-belt|Green Belt]]** (or [[black-belt|Black Belt]]) leads the team and produces the deliverables.
- The **[[process-owner|Process Owner]]** provides access to the process, co-owns solutions and takes over the control plan at the end.
- **Team members** bring process knowledge and do much of the data collection.
- The **[[finance-representative|Finance representative]]** validates estimated benefits in Define and realised benefits in Control.

At a **tollgate review**, the Belt presents the phase deliverables and the Champion decides: **go** (proceed to the next phase), **recycle** (fix gaps and return), or **stop** (the project is no longer worthwhile — a legitimate and valuable outcome). A tollgate is not a status meeting; it is a decision point based on evidence. The book’s phase-gate checklists even remind the Belt that the review presentation is “your show” — the Belt owns the story the data tells.`}],whyItMatters:"Deliverables protect everyone’s time. They stop a team from solving the wrong problem, stop a manager from declaring victory without evidence, and give finance a defensible number. In vendor-managed service operations they are especially valuable: a charter with an agreed problem statement and scope creates shared facts between the client and the vendor, and a control plan with a named owner keeps a gain from evaporating at the next contract renewal. On the exam, many “what should the Green Belt do next?” questions are really deliverable questions in disguise — knowing what belongs to each phase lets you eliminate wrong options quickly.",businessExample:{title:"A repeat-fault project at a fixed-broadband operator",body:`A Champion in Digital Service Operations notices that 14% of FTTH fault tickets are reopened within 7 days — each repeat visit costs a truck roll and damages satisfaction.

**Define deliverables:** a charter with the business case (an estimated preliminary COPQ of roughly OMR 180,000 per year in repeat visits and credits — to be validated by Finance), a problem statement (“From January to June, 14% of FTTH fault tickets in the northern region were reopened within 7 days of closure”), a goal (“reduce to 6% by 31 December”), scope (northern region FTTH faults; copper and enterprise circuits out of scope), CTQs from customer feedback (“fixed on the first visit”), a high-level process view from ticket creation to closure, and a stakeholder map including the field-services vendor.

At the **Define tollgate**, the Champion pushes back on one line of the draft problem statement — “caused by poor technician training” — because it assumes a root cause. The team removes it. The Champion signs “go”, and Measure begins with a data-collection plan. Six months later, Control hands the process owner a control chart of reopen rate and Finance confirms the realised savings.`},visual:"dmaicRoadmap",visualCaption:"Click any phase to see its purpose, key questions, typical tools and deliverables. Define is highlighted because its outputs frame the whole project. Then test yourself: sort each deliverable into the phase it belongs to.",definitions:[{term:"Deliverable",definition:"A tangible, evidenced output a phase must produce before the project can pass its tollgate (for example, a signed charter or a validated measurement system)."},{term:"Tollgate review",definition:"A formal end-of-phase review where the Champion examines the deliverables and decides go, recycle or stop."},{term:"Project charter",definition:"The Define document agreeing the business case, problem statement, goal statement, scope, team, roles and milestones."},{term:"Business case",definition:"The justification for the project: why it matters to the business now and the expected benefit."},{term:"Problem statement",definition:"A factual, measurable description of the gap — what, where, when and how big — with no assumed cause or solution."},{term:"Goal statement",definition:"The measurable target for improvement and its deadline, ideally SMART."},{term:"Project scope",definition:"The boundaries of the project: what is in scope and what is explicitly out of scope."},{term:"Preliminary COPQ",definition:"An early, estimated cost of the problem used in Define to justify the project; verified later as realised benefits in Control."},{term:"Control plan",definition:"The Control deliverable defining how the improved process will be monitored, by whom, and what to do if it drifts."}],comparisons:[{title:"Commonly confused: which phase?",headers:["Item","Belongs in","Not in","Why"],rows:[["Listing suspected causes","Define / Measure (as potential X’s)","—","Suspicions may be recorded early but are not yet proven."],["Verifying root causes with data","Analyze","Define","Proof requires measured data and analysis."],["Estimating financial benefit","Define (business case)","Control","An estimate justifies the project."],["Confirming realised savings","Control","Define","Only real results after implementation can be verified."],["Validating the measurement system","Measure","Define","You need trusted data before establishing the baseline."],["Piloting a solution","Improve","Analyze","Solutions are tested only after causes are verified."]]},{title:"Problem statement vs goal statement vs business case",headers:["","Problem statement","Goal statement","Business case"],rows:[["Answers","What is wrong, and how big?","What will success look like, by when?","Why should the business invest in this now?"],["Contains","Metric, baseline, location, time period","Target value, metric, deadline","Strategic link, cost of the problem, expected benefit"],["Must avoid","Causes, blame and solutions","Vague aims (“improve”)","Unsupported or inflated claims"]]}],misconceptions:[{myth:"Define is just paperwork before the real work starts.",reality:"Define decides whether the project is worth doing and what success means. Weak Define deliverables are a leading reason projects fail or get stuck later."},{myth:"The problem statement should name the cause so everyone knows what to fix.",reality:"Naming a cause in Define biases the team and skips Analyze. The problem statement describes the gap only."},{myth:"Financial benefits are confirmed when the charter is signed.",reality:"Define contains estimated benefits. They are verified as realised benefits in Control, ideally by Finance."},{myth:"A tollgate review only checks whether the team is on schedule.",reality:"It is an evidence-based go / recycle / stop decision on the phase deliverables."},{myth:"Every problem should become a DMAIC project.",reality:"If the cause and solution are already known, it is a “just do it” action. DMAIC is for problems with unknown causes."}],examTraps:[{trap:"“Which is a deliverable of the Define phase?” with “verified root causes” as an option.",explanation:"Root causes are verified in Analyze. Define frames the problem; it does not prove causes.",tag:"verified-root-cause"},{trap:"“Benefits” in Define vs Control.",explanation:"Define holds estimated benefits (business case / preliminary COPQ); Control holds realised, verified benefits.",tag:"estimated-benefits"},{trap:"“What should the Green Belt do FIRST after the project is approved?” — options include collecting data, brainstorming solutions and drafting the charter.",explanation:"The charter frames the project; data collection and solutions come later.",tag:"first"},{trap:"“Which is NOT part of a project charter?” with “control plan” among the options.",explanation:"The control plan is a Control-phase deliverable, not a charter element.",tag:"not"},{trap:"Assuming SIPOC is a Define topic in the syllabus.",explanation:"SIPOC is often drawn during Define in practice, but the IASSC syllabus lists it under Measure (2.1.2 Process Mapping, SIPOC, Value Stream Map)."}],workedExamples:[{title:"Sorting deliverables into phases",problem:"A Green Belt’s project folder for an invoice-dispute project contains: (1) a gauge R&R study on how analysts classify disputes; (2) a signed charter; (3) a control chart handed to the billing manager; (4) a regression showing that disputes rise with manual tariff overrides; (5) a pilot of an automated tariff check; (6) a stakeholder map. Assign each to a phase.",steps:["(2) Signed charter and (6) stakeholder map frame the project and its people — **Define**.","(1) A gauge R&R (measurement system analysis) checks that the data can be trusted before the baseline — **Measure**.","(4) The regression verifies a root cause with data — **Analyze**.","(5) Piloting a solution that removes the verified cause — **Improve**.","(3) The control chart handed to the process owner sustains the gain — **Control**."],answer:"Define: 2, 6 · Measure: 1 · Analyze: 4 · Improve: 5 · Control: 3.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Placing the regression (4) in Define “because the team already suspected tariff overrides”.",whyWrong:"A suspicion can be noted in Define, but a statistical test proving it is Analyze evidence."}},{title:"Repairing a weak Define package",problem:"A draft charter for a contact centre reads: “Problem: agents are not trained well enough, so customers wait too long. Goal: improve waiting times. Scope: the contact centre.” What would a tollgate reviewer send back?",steps:["Problem statement assumes a cause (“not trained well enough”) and lacks data. Rewrite it: “From March to May, 38% of calls to the residential line waited longer than the 60-second service level (target ≤ 20%).”","Goal is not measurable or time-bound. Rewrite it: “Reduce calls waiting more than 60 seconds to ≤ 20% by 30 September.”","Scope is too broad. Clarify it: residential inbound voice queue in scope; business line, chat and outbound campaigns out of scope.","Add the missing elements: business case with preliminary COPQ (abandoned calls, repeat calls), team and roles, milestones, and CTQs from customer feedback."],answer:"Recycle the tollgate: rewrite the problem without a cause, make the goal SMART, tighten scope, and add the business case, team and CTQs."}],lab:null,check:[{id:"chk-1.1.3-1",topic:"1.1.3",category:"dmaic-boundary",difficulty:"beginner",numerical:!1,stem:"Which of the following is a deliverable of the **Define** phase?",options:["A validated measurement system","A project charter with problem and goal statements","A list of statistically verified root causes","A control plan"],answer:1,explanation:"The project charter — including the business case, problem statement, goal statement, scope and team — is the core Define deliverable.",whyWrong:["Measurement system analysis is a Measure deliverable.","","Root causes are verified with data in Analyze.","The control plan is a Control deliverable."],traps:["verified-root-cause"]},{id:"chk-1.1.3-2",topic:"1.1.3",category:"dmaic-boundary",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"In which phase are a project’s financial benefits **verified as realised**?",options:["Define","Measure","Improve","Control"],answer:3,explanation:"Define contains estimated benefits in the business case; realised benefits are verified in Control once the improvement has been sustained — the book’s roadmap ends with “Verify Financial Impact”.",whyWrong:["Define holds the estimated benefit that justifies the project, not realised results.","Measure establishes the baseline; there are no realised benefits yet.","Improve shows the solution works, but the sustained financial impact is confirmed in Control.",""],traps:["realized-benefits","estimated-benefits"]},{id:"chk-1.1.3-3",topic:"1.1.3",category:"wording",difficulty:"trap",numerical:!1,stem:"Which draft problem statement would a Champion most likely **accept** at the Define tollgate?",options:["“Ticket backlog is high because the vendor’s night shift is understaffed.”","“We need to automate ticket routing to cut backlog.”","“Between April and June, 23% of P2 incidents in the core network queue breached the 8-hour SLA, against a contractual target of 5%.”","“Customer satisfaction must improve significantly this year.”"],answer:2,explanation:"A good problem statement is factual and measurable — what, where, when and how big — and contains no assumed cause or solution.",whyWrong:["It assumes a root cause (night-shift staffing) before any analysis.","It is a solution, not a problem.","","It is vague and unmeasurable, and reads like a goal rather than a description of the gap."],traps:["most"]},{id:"chk-1.1.3-4",topic:"1.1.3",category:"scenario",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"At a Define tollgate, the Champion learns that the cause of the problem and its fix are already well known and agreed. What is the most appropriate decision?",options:["Proceed to Measure to establish a baseline anyway","Stop the DMAIC project and implement the known fix as a “just do it” action","Recycle Define and rewrite the goal statement","Skip directly to Control"],answer:1,explanation:"DMAIC is for problems whose causes are unknown. When the cause and solution are known, the efficient route is to implement the fix directly; stopping a project at a tollgate is a valid outcome.",whyWrong:["Running Measure and Analyze to rediscover a known cause wastes effort.","","The goal statement is not the issue; the project type is.","DMAIC phases are not skipped; if the fix is known, it is simply not a DMAIC project."],traps:["most"]}],takeaways:["Each DMAIC phase answers a question; its deliverables are the evidence, reviewed at a tollgate.","Define delivers the project charter (business case, problem, goal, scope, team, milestones), VOC/CTQs, a high-level process view, stakeholder analysis and a preliminary COPQ estimate.","Measure delivers trusted data and a baseline; Analyze delivers verified root causes; Improve delivers proven solutions; Control delivers a control plan, handover and verified benefits.","Root causes are verified in Analyze — never in Define.","Benefits are estimated in Define and verified as realised in Control.","Good projects are aligned with business goals, measurable, meaningful, have an unknown cause and a manageable scope.","The Champion owns tollgate decisions — go, recycle or stop; the Belt owns producing the deliverables; the process owner inherits the control plan.","SIPOC is often drawn in Define in practice, but the syllabus lists it under Measure (2.1.2)."],flashcards:[{front:"What is a tollgate review?",back:"An end-of-phase review where the Champion examines the deliverables and decides go, recycle or stop."},{front:"Core Define deliverable",back:"The project charter: business case, problem statement, goal statement, scope, team/roles and milestones."},{front:"Other Define outputs besides the charter",back:"VOC → CTQs, high-level process view, stakeholder analysis, preliminary COPQ, Define tollgate sign-off."},{front:"Key Measure deliverables",back:"Detailed process map, data collection plan, validated measurement system (MSA), baseline/capability of Y, potential X’s."},{front:"Key Analyze deliverable",back:"Statistically verified root causes — the vital few X’s."},{front:"Key Improve deliverables",back:"Selected and piloted solutions, implementation plan, measured improvement in Y."},{front:"Key Control deliverables",back:"Control plan, monitoring, standard work, handover to the process owner, verified financial benefits."},{front:"Estimated vs realised benefits",back:"Estimated in Define (business case); realised and verified in Control."},{front:"Book’s Define steps on the DMAIC roadmap",back:"Determine appropriate project focus, estimate COPQ, establish team (after the Champion/Process Owner identifies the problem area)."},{front:"When is a problem NOT a DMAIC project?",back:"When the root cause and solution are already known — implement it as a “just do it”."}],coverage:"official-only",supplementaryNotes:["The syllabus (p. 7) lists the topic title only, and the book’s Define chapter (printed pp. 1–97) was not supplied. The detailed list of Define outputs, project-selection criteria, role responsibilities and the go / recycle / stop tollgate model are written from general Lean Six Sigma practice.","The book supports the DMAIC roadmap and its Define steps (Estimate COPQ, Establish Team) on pp. 230, 237, 417, 424, 490 and 577; what Define achieves on p. 473; and the phase-gate deliverable lists for Measure (p. 229), Analyze (p. 416) and Improve (p. 488).","SIPOC is mentioned only as context; the syllabus classifies it under Measure 2.1.2.","The broadband and contact-centre examples and the COPQ figure are original illustrations."],sources:[{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 7",pdfPage:7,note:"Define topic 1.1.3 “Deliverables of a Lean Six Sigma Project”; SIPOC listed under Measure 2.1.2"},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 230",pdfPage:33,note:"DMAIC roadmap: Define steps Estimate COPQ and Establish Team; Control ends with Verify Financial Impact"},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 237",pdfPage:40,note:"DMAIC roadmap repeated at the start of Analyze"},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 229",pdfPage:32,note:"Measure phase deliverables list for the tollgate presentation"},{doc:"book-401-500",pages:"p. 416",pdfPage:19,note:"Analyze phase deliverables list"},{doc:"book-401-500",pages:"p. 417",pdfPage:20,note:"DMAIC roadmap with Champion/Process Owner Define steps"},{doc:"book-401-500",pages:"p. 424",pdfPage:27,note:"DMAIC roadmap (repeat)"},{doc:"book-401-500",pages:"p. 473",pdfPage:76,note:"Project status review: what Define achieves (problem and business impact understood, firm objectives, output quantified)"},{doc:"book-401-500",pages:"p. 488",pdfPage:91,note:"Improve phase deliverables list"},{doc:"book-401-500",pages:"p. 490",pdfPage:93,note:"DMAIC roadmap; problem and objective statements created at the start of the project"},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 577",pdfPage:80,note:"DMAIC roadmap in Control wrap-up; benefits tracking roadblocks"}]},P=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:l},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),c={id:"1.1.4",module:1,title:"The Problem Solving Strategy Y = f(x)",subtitle:"Outputs are the result of inputs — fix the X’s, not the Y",minutes:13,objectives:["Explain Y = f(X) in plain English and identify the dependent variable (Y) and the independent variables (X’s) in a service or telecom process.","Distinguish controllable, procedural and noise inputs, and explain why the error term ε exists.","Separate potential (candidate) causes from verified root causes, and state which DMAIC phase handles each.","Describe the “funnel” that narrows many candidate X’s down to the vital few.","Explain why monitoring Y alone cannot improve a process, and why lasting results come from controlling the critical X’s."],overview:`Every result you have ever reported — an SLA percentage, a monthly churn rate, a first-call resolution figure — was *produced* by something. It did not appear on the dashboard by itself. Lean Six Sigma captures this idea in one compact expression: **$Y = f(X)$**, read aloud as “Y is a function of X”. The output you care about (**Y**) is the result of a set of inputs and conditions (the **X’s**) acting through a process (the function **f**).

That sounds obvious, but it changes how a team behaves. When an outage-restoration target is missed, the instinctive reaction is to chase the number: send reminders, add a red cell to the report, ask people to “try harder”. [[y-equals-f-x|Y = f(X)]] thinking asks a different question: *which inputs produced this result, and which of them can we change?* In this lesson you will learn the vocabulary of Y and X, the types of inputs, why Define only lists *candidate* causes, and how later phases prove which few really matter.`,sections:[{id:"reading-the-equation",heading:"Reading the equation: Y, X and f",body:`Think of a recipe. The cake (Y) depends on the flour, the oven temperature, the baking time and the skill of the baker (the X’s). The recipe itself — how those things are combined — is the function **f**. If the cake is dry, you will not fix it by inspecting the cake more carefully. You fix it by changing an ingredient or a setting.

In Six Sigma language:

- **Y** is the [[dependent-variable]] — the **output**, the **effect**, the **symptom** you can see. It *depends* on other things. Examples: mean time to restore (MTTR), invoice cycle time, percentage of tickets resolved within SLA.
- **X** is an [[independent-variable]] — an **input**, a **cause**, a factor that can vary on its own and influences Y. Examples: shift staffing level, alert routing accuracy, knowledge-base quality.
- **f** is the [[process]] — the way the inputs are transformed into the output.

The fuller form is written

$$Y = f(X_1, X_2, X_3, \\ldots, X_n) + \\varepsilon$$

The Greek letter $\\varepsilon$ (epsilon) is the **error term**: the part of the [[variation]] in Y that our listed X’s do not explain — unmeasured factors, measurement noise and pure chance. It is a humble reminder that no model of a real process is ever complete.

| Y (output) | X’s (inputs) |
|---|---|
| Dependent | Independent |
| Output | Input |
| Effect | Cause |
| Symptom | Problem |
| Monitor | Control |

The last row is the heart of the strategy: **we monitor Y, but we control X.**`},{id:"types-of-inputs",heading:"Not all X’s are alike: controllable, procedural and noise",body:`When a team brainstorms the inputs to a process, it helps to tag each one by how much influence the team has over it. A common classification uses three types:

- **Controllable (C)** — inputs you can set or adjust deliberately: the number of engineers rostered on the night shift, the threshold at which a monitoring tool raises a P1 alert, the routing rule that sends fibre faults to the transmission team.
- **Procedural (P)** — inputs defined by a standard method or work instruction: the triage checklist, the escalation matrix, the major-incident bridge procedure. They are “controllable” through documentation and training, but only if people actually follow them.
- **Noise (N)** — inputs that vary and influence Y but which you cannot control (or choose not to control economically): weather causing fibre cuts, a regional power failure, a sudden traffic spike after a football final.

Noise inputs are not useless to know about. You cannot stop the storm, but you can make the process **robust** to it — pre-positioning spares before the rainy season, for example. And an input labelled “noise” today may become controllable tomorrow if the business decides it is worth the investment.

A useful rule of thumb from Six Sigma practice: if an X turns out to be **critical**, then by definition you must control it — which means it needs its own requirement and its own measurement.`},{id:"potential-vs-verified",heading:"Potential causes versus proven root causes",body:`This is where many learners — and many real projects — go wrong.

In **Define**, the team describes and scopes the **Y**: what output is unacceptable, for whom, and by how much. It is entirely appropriate to **list candidate X’s** at this stage — from a [[sipoc|SIPOC]] (formally a Measure-phase tool, 2.1.2, used here as context), from customer feedback, from the experience of the people doing the work. Those are **potential causes**: hypotheses.

What Define must **not** do is declare a [[root-cause]]. A root cause is a cause that has been **verified with data**: the team has shown that changing that X really changes Y, and by roughly how much. That verification is the work of **Measure** (establishing reliable data on Y and the X’s) and **Analyze** (testing which X’s truly matter).

| | Potential cause (candidate X) | Verified root cause (critical X) |
|---|---|---|
| Typical DMAIC phase | Define → listed; Measure → collected and prioritised | Analyze → proven with data |
| Evidence | Opinion, experience, brainstorming, team voting | Data, graphical and statistical analysis |
| Language | “We suspect…”, “a possible factor is…” | “Data show that…” |
| Safe action | Investigate, measure | Improve and control |

Jumping from a suspected cause straight to a solution is called “solution jumping”. It is fast, it feels decisive — and it often fixes the wrong thing while the real driver keeps hurting the customer.`},{id:"funnel",heading:"The funnel: from many X’s to the vital few",body:`A useful mental picture is a **funnel**. At the wide top, the Define phase gathers a long list of possible X’s using tools such as SIPOC, Voice of the Customer and the project scope. As the project moves through Measure and Analyze, tools like the [[process-map]], X–Y matrix, FMEA, graphs and statistical tests act like progressively finer filters. At the narrow bottom you are left with the [[vital-few]] — typically a handful of X’s that explain most of the behaviour of Y.

| Funnel stage | Typical question | Number of X’s |
|---|---|---|
| Define | “What could possibly be influencing Y?” | Many (often 20–50) |
| Measure | “Which X’s look most likely, and can we measure them reliably?” | Fewer (perhaps 8–15) |
| Analyze | “Which X’s are proven to drive Y?” | The vital few (often 2–5) |
| Improve & Control | “What settings of the vital few give the Y we need, and how do we hold them?” | Controlled |

(The counts above are typical illustrations, not rules.) The point is that the strategy is **progressive**. You do not need to know the answer in Define — you need an honest, wide list of candidates and a clearly scoped Y.`},{id:"why-not-the-y",heading:"Why chasing Y does not work",body:`Imagine a service desk that misses its 4-hour P2 resolution target. Management responds by adding the metric to a wall screen and holding a daily call about it. For a week or two, performance improves — people are watching. Then it drifts back.

Why? Because none of the X’s changed. The same ambiguous categorisation, the same skill gaps on the late shift, the same slow vendor hand-off are still there. Pressure on Y produces short-term effort, workarounds, or (worst of all) **gaming of the metric** — tickets being closed and reopened to stop the clock.

$Y = f(X)$ gives a disciplined alternative: treat Y as the *scoreboard*, not the *steering wheel*. Find which X’s move it, change those, and Y follows — sustainably.`}],whyItMatters:"For a Green Belt in service operations, Y = f(X) is the bridge between a KPI report and an improvement plan. It keeps conversations with vendors and managers factual (“which inputs changed?”) rather than emotional (“who is to blame?”). On the exam, it underpins questions about dependent versus independent variables, about what is appropriate in Define versus Analyze, and about why controlling inputs is more effective than inspecting outputs.",businessExample:{title:"Telecom NOC: what drives mean time to restore?",body:`A mobile operator’s network operations centre (NOC) is under pressure because **mean time to restore (MTTR)** for P1 incidents has risen from about 2.1 to 3.4 hours over two quarters, putting enterprise SLAs at risk.

**Define.** The Green Belt scopes the **Y**: *MTTR for P1 radio-access incidents, measured from alarm to service restoration, across all regions*. In a workshop with NOC engineers, field teams and the vendor, the team lists candidate X’s:

| Candidate X | Type |
|---|---|
| Shift staffing level (engineers on duty) | Controllable |
| Alert routing accuracy (alarms sent to the right resolver group first time) | Controllable |
| Knowledge-base quality (up-to-date fix procedures) | Procedural |
| Escalation delay (time to engage Tier-3 or vendor) | Procedural |
| Field travel time to remote sites | Controllable / Noise |
| Weather and power failures | Noise |

Written as a model: $\\text{MTTR} = f(\\text{staffing}, \\text{routing accuracy}, \\text{KB quality}, \\text{escalation delay}, \\ldots) + \\varepsilon$.

**What the team does *not* say in Define:** “The cause is understaffing.” Several managers believe it — but it is still a hypothesis.

**Measure and Analyze** (later) might reveal, for example, that misrouted alarms add a large share of the delay because tickets bounce between groups before reaching the right engineer. Only then is routing accuracy treated as a verified critical X. *Any relationships or numbers in this example are an educational illustration, not established causal facts about real NOCs.*`},visual:"yfx",visualCaption:"Candidate inputs flow into the process f( ) to produce Y. Define lists candidates; Measure and Analyze verify which few are critical. Illustrative model only.",definitions:[{term:"Y = f(X)",definition:"Shorthand for the idea that an output (Y) is a function of — is produced by — a set of inputs (X’s) acting through a process."},{term:"Y (dependent variable)",definition:"The output, effect or symptom being measured; its value depends on the inputs. Also called the response."},{term:"X (independent variable)",definition:"An input, cause or factor that can vary and may influence Y."},{term:"Error term (ε)",definition:"The portion of variation in Y not explained by the X’s in the model — unmeasured factors, measurement error and random variation."},{term:"Controllable input",definition:"An input the team can deliberately set or adjust, such as staffing level or an alert threshold."},{term:"Procedural input",definition:"An input defined by a standard method or work instruction, such as an escalation procedure."},{term:"Noise input",definition:"An input that affects Y but is impractical or impossible to control, such as weather or regional power failures."},{term:"Root cause (verified)",definition:"A cause whose effect on Y has been confirmed with data, normally in the Analyze phase."},{term:"Vital few",definition:"The small number of X’s that explain most of the variation or problem in Y."}],comparisons:[{title:"Define versus Analyze in the Y = f(X) strategy",headers:["","Define","Analyze"],rows:[["Main focus","Scope and describe the Y; quantify the gap","Test which X’s drive Y"],["X’s","Listed as candidates (potential causes)","Verified or rejected with data"],["Typical tools","Charter, VOC, high-level map, SIPOC (context)","Graphs, hypothesis tests, regression"],["Output","A clear problem and a wide list of suspects","The vital few critical X’s"],["Wrong move","Declaring a root cause","Accepting opinion without data"]]}],misconceptions:[{myth:"If we track the Y closely enough, it will improve.",reality:"Monitoring Y shows the result; only changing the X’s changes the result. Pressure on Y alone often produces temporary effort or metric gaming."},{myth:"The causes identified in Define are the root causes.",reality:"Define produces candidate X’s (hypotheses). Root causes are verified with data in Analyze."},{myth:"Noise inputs can be ignored because we cannot control them.",reality:"Noise still affects Y. The process can be made robust to noise, and some noise can later become controllable."},{myth:"A process has only one X.",reality:"Y = f(X₁, X₂, … Xₙ): most outputs depend on several inputs, and the inputs may interact."}],examTraps:[{trap:"An option says the Define phase “identifies the root cause”.",explanation:"Define lists potential causes and scopes Y. Root causes are proven in Analyze. Look for words like “verified”, “proven” or “confirmed” and match them to Analyze.",tag:"verified-root-cause"},{trap:"Mixing up dependent and independent.",explanation:"Y depends on X, so Y is the dependent variable. X is independent. Exam stems sometimes describe the variable in words (“the response”, “the factor”) instead of letters."},{trap:"“What should the team focus on FIRST to improve Y?”",explanation:"The best answer usually involves identifying and controlling the inputs (X’s), not inspecting or reporting the output more often.",tag:"first"},{trap:"Treating the error term as a mistake by the team.",explanation:"ε represents unexplained variation (unmeasured factors and randomness), not a calculation error."}],workedExamples:[{title:"Classify the variables: invoice processing",problem:"A shared-services team wants to reduce **invoice processing cycle time**. Candidate factors: number of approval levels, percentage of invoices received with a valid PO number, AP clerk experience, month-end volume spikes, and the OCR scanning settings. Identify Y and classify each X.",steps:["**Y** (dependent, output) = invoice processing cycle time — it is the result the business sees.","Number of approval levels → **procedural** X (set by the approval policy).","Percentage of invoices with a valid PO → an X largely driven by suppliers; partly **controllable** through supplier onboarding rules.","Clerk experience → **controllable** X (training, rostering).","Month-end volume spikes → **noise** X for this team (they cannot stop the calendar), though the process can be made robust with planned capacity.","OCR scanning settings → **controllable** X.","Model: $\\text{cycle time} = f(\\text{approvals}, \\text{PO validity}, \\text{experience}, \\text{volume}, \\text{OCR}) + \\varepsilon$ — all X’s are still **candidates** at this point."],answer:"Y = invoice cycle time; approval levels (P), PO validity (C, supplier-dependent), clerk experience (C), month-end spikes (N), OCR settings (C). None are verified causes yet.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Treating “cycle time is too long” as an X because it is the problem.",whyWrong:"The long cycle time is the symptom (Y). X’s are the inputs that produce it."}},{title:"Define or Analyze? The MTTR workshop",problem:"During the Define tollgate for an MTTR project, the Champion asks the Green Belt to confirm that “understaffed night shifts are the root cause” so that hiring can be approved. What should the Green Belt say?",steps:["Recognise the request: it asks for a **verified root cause** during **Define**.","Acknowledge that night-shift staffing is a **credible candidate X** and record it on the list.","Explain that Define has scoped the Y (P1 MTTR) and listed candidates; proof requires reliable data on staffing versus MTTR by shift (Measure) and analysis that rules out other X’s such as routing accuracy (Analyze).","Propose a short, focused data collection so the decision can be made on evidence soon."],answer:"Staffing is a potential cause, not yet a root cause. Recommend measuring and analysing before committing to hiring.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Approving hiring immediately because experienced managers agree.",whyWrong:"Consensus is not verification. If routing accuracy is the real driver, new hires would add cost without improving MTTR."}}],lab:"yfx",check:[{id:"chk-1.1.4-1",topic:"1.1.4",category:"definition",difficulty:"beginner",numerical:!1,stem:"In the expression $Y = f(X)$, which statement about **Y** is correct?",options:["Y is the dependent variable — the output or effect of the process.","Y is the independent variable that the team sets directly.","Y is the error term that captures unexplained variation.","Y is the process step that transforms inputs."],answer:0,explanation:"Y depends on the inputs, so it is the dependent variable: the output, effect or symptom.",whyWrong:["","That describes an X (an input the team may control).","The error term is ε, not Y.","The transformation is the function f, i.e. the process."]},{id:"chk-1.1.4-2",topic:"1.1.4",category:"dmaic-boundary",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"A team in the **Define** phase has brainstormed twelve factors that might increase ticket resolution time. How should these factors be described?",options:["As verified root causes ready for solutions.","As potential causes (candidate X’s) to be measured and analysed later.","As the project’s primary metrics.","As noise inputs that should be excluded from the project."],answer:1,explanation:"In Define, X’s are hypotheses. Measure and Analyze verify which ones truly drive Y.",whyWrong:["Verification with data has not happened yet; that is Analyze work.","","The primary metric is the Y (resolution time), not the list of X’s.","Nothing indicates they are uncontrollable; and noise is still recorded, not excluded."],traps:["verified-root-cause"]},{id:"chk-1.1.4-3",topic:"1.1.4",category:"concept",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"A fibre network suffers more faults during the monsoon season. For the restoration process, heavy rainfall is BEST classified as:",options:["A controllable input","A procedural input","A noise input","The dependent variable"],answer:2,explanation:"Rainfall influences Y but cannot be controlled by the team — a noise input. The process can still be made robust to it.",whyWrong:["The team cannot set the weather.","Procedural inputs are defined by methods or work instructions.","","The dependent variable is the output, e.g. restoration time."],traps:["best"]},{id:"chk-1.1.4-4",topic:"1.1.4",category:"scenario",difficulty:"advanced",numerical:!1,stem:"Management wants to improve a contact centre’s first-call resolution (FCR). Which approach is MOST consistent with the Y = f(X) strategy?",options:["Publish FCR on a wall screen and review it daily with agents.","Identify the inputs that drive FCR, verify which matter, and control them.","Increase inspection of closed calls by team leaders.","Set a stretch FCR target and link it to bonuses."],answer:1,explanation:"Y = f(X) says results come from inputs. Lasting improvement comes from finding and controlling the critical X’s.",whyWrong:["Monitoring Y more closely does not change the inputs.","","Inspection detects poor outputs after the fact; it does not change the causes.","Targets and incentives pressure Y without addressing X; they may encourage gaming."],traps:["most"]}],takeaways:["Y = f(X) means the output (Y) is produced by inputs (X’s) acting through a process (f).","Y is dependent: output, effect, symptom — we monitor it. X’s are independent: inputs, causes — we control them.","The full form Y = f(X₁…Xₙ) + ε admits that some variation is unexplained.","Inputs can be controllable, procedural or noise; critical X’s must be controlled and measured.","Define scopes the Y and lists candidate X’s; Analyze verifies root causes with data.","The funnel narrows many candidate X’s to the vital few.","Pressure on Y without changing X gives temporary gains or metric gaming."],flashcards:[{front:"What does Y = f(X) mean?",back:"The output Y is a function of (produced by) the inputs X through a process."},{front:"Is Y dependent or independent?",back:"Dependent — it depends on the X’s. It is the output, effect or symptom."},{front:"What does ε represent in Y = f(X) + ε?",back:"Unexplained variation: unmeasured factors, measurement error and randomness."},{front:"Three types of input?",back:"Controllable (C), Procedural (P) and Noise (N)."},{front:"Example of a noise input in a NOC?",back:"Weather, regional power failures, sudden traffic spikes — affect Y but cannot be controlled."},{front:"In which phase are root causes verified?",back:"Analyze (using reliable data from Measure). Define only lists candidates."},{front:"Monitor or control: Y or X?",back:"Monitor Y; control X."},{front:"What is the “vital few”?",back:"The small number of X’s that explain most of the problem in Y."},{front:"What is solution jumping?",back:"Implementing a fix for a suspected cause before verifying it with data."}],coverage:"official-only",supplementaryNotes:["The Define chapter of the training book was not supplied; the explanation is written independently to the syllabus topic, reinforced by Y = f(X) material appearing in later book chapters.","The term “solution jumping” and the typical X counts at each funnel stage are general practitioner knowledge used for teaching, not taken from the supplied pages.","All telecom and invoice examples, including MTTR relationships, are educational illustrations, not established causal findings."],sources:[{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 7",pdfPage:7,note:"Define topic 1.1.4 “The Problem Solving Strategy Y = f(x)”."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 208",pdfPage:11,note:"Inputs X1–X5 → Y = f(X) process function → outputs; critical X as a variable with undue influence on CTQs."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 240",pdfPage:43,note:"Funnel from the trivial many X’s to the vital few via Y = f(X) + e; Define-phase mapping identifies possible X’s."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"pp. 113–114",pdfPage:16,note:"Controllable, procedural and noise inputs; a critical X must be controlled and measured."},{doc:"book-401-500",pages:"p. 474",pdfPage:77,note:"Six Sigma strategy funnel: SIPOC → VOC → scope at the top, narrowing to the vital few X’s."},{doc:"book-401-500",pages:"p. 476",pdfPage:79,note:"Y = f(x) as the cause-and-effect relationship between X and Y variables."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 526",pdfPage:29,note:"Y vs X contrast: dependent/independent, output/input, effect/cause, symptom/problem, monitor/control."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 590",pdfPage:93,note:"Glossary: X and Y defined via Y = f(X)."}]},O=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:c},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),d={id:"1.1.5",module:1,title:"Voice of the Customer, Business and Employee",subtitle:"Listening to the three voices and turning words into requirements",minutes:14,objectives:["Define VOC, VOB and VOE, and recognise VOP as the related “voice” of the process itself.","Distinguish internal from external customers and give service-operations examples of each.","Choose suitable methods to capture each voice, including reactive versus proactive and qualitative versus quantitative sources.","Translate customer verbatims into needs and then into measurable requirements (CTQs), testing them with RUMBA.","Explain how to reconcile conflicting customer, business and employee priorities using data."],overview:`A Six Sigma project is only as good as the problem it chooses to solve — and the problem is defined by people with something to say about the process. Lean Six Sigma talks about these perspectives as **voices**. The [[voc|Voice of the Customer (VOC)]] tells us what the people receiving the output need. The [[vob|Voice of the Business (VOB)]] tells us what the organisation needs to stay healthy: profit, growth, compliance, risk control. The [[voe|Voice of the Employee (VOE)]] tells us what the people doing the work experience and need in order to deliver well.

A project that listens to only one voice tends to fail in a predictable way. Listen only to customers and you may design a service the company cannot afford. Listen only to the business and you may cut costs until customers leave. Ignore employees and the new process quietly collapses under workarounds. This lesson shows how to capture all three voices, how to turn vague words into measurable requirements, and how to handle the moments when the voices disagree.`,sections:[{id:"three-voices",heading:"The three voices (and a fourth that answers them)",body:`- **VOC — Voice of the Customer.** The expressed *and unexpressed* needs, wants and expectations of whoever receives the process output. Customers rarely speak in specifications: they say “the internet keeps dropping” or “nobody calls me back”. Part of the Green Belt’s job is hearing the need behind the words.
- **VOB — Voice of the Business.** The needs of the organisation and its key stakeholders: profitability, revenue, cost per contact, market share, regulatory compliance, contractual SLA penalties, strategic goals.
- **VOE — Voice of the Employee.** The perspective of the people who operate the process: workload, tools, training, clarity of procedures, safety, morale. Employees often know exactly where the process breaks because they patch it every day.

A fourth voice is useful context: the [[vop|Voice of the Process (VOP)]]. VOP is not an opinion — it is what the process *actually delivers*, shown by its performance data and natural variation. Six Sigma compares VOP with VOC: if customers need restoration within 4 hours and the process routinely varies between 2 and 9 hours, the gap between the two voices *is* the problem.

| Voice | Who speaks | Typical content | Telecom example |
|---|---|---|---|
| VOC | Recipients of the output | Needs, expectations, complaints | “My business line must be back within 4 hours.” |
| VOB | Leadership, finance, regulators | Cost, revenue, risk, compliance | “Reduce cost per contact by 10% this year.” |
| VOE | People doing the work | Workload, tools, procedures | “The ticketing tool forces six screens per update.” |
| VOP | The process (via data) | Actual performance and variation | “Median restore 3.1 h; 18% exceed 4 h.” |`},{id:"internal-external",heading:"Who is the customer? Internal and external",body:`A customer is anyone who receives the output of a process. An [[external-customer]] is outside the organisation and usually pays: a consumer with a home broadband plan, an enterprise buying a managed WAN, a government agency on a dedicated SLA. An [[internal-customer]] is inside the organisation and depends on your output to do their own job.

In service operations the internal chain is long. The NOC is the customer of the monitoring team’s alerts. The field-engineering team is the customer of the NOC’s dispatch information. Billing is the customer of the provisioning team’s order data. If provisioning sends incomplete data, billing errors follow — and the *external* customer eventually feels it as a wrong invoice.

Asking “who receives this output, and what do they need from it?” at every hand-off is one of the quickest ways to find hidden problems.`},{id:"capturing",heading:"Capturing the voices: methods and data types",body:`There is no single best listening method; good teams combine several.

| Method | Reactive or proactive | Mainly qualitative or quantitative |
|---|---|---|
| Complaints, escalations, regulator cases | Reactive | Both |
| CRM / ticket data (reopen rates, contact reasons) | Reactive | Quantitative |
| Transactional data (call logs, outage durations) | Reactive | Quantitative |
| Surveys, CSAT, NPS | Proactive | Quantitative (with free-text) |
| Interviews | Proactive | Qualitative |
| Focus groups | Proactive | Qualitative |
| Gemba — going to where the work happens | Proactive | Qualitative (observations) |
| Social listening (public posts, app reviews) | Reactive | Qualitative at scale |

**Reactive** sources arrive whether you ask or not; they are cheap but biased toward people who were upset enough to speak. **Proactive** sources are designed and requested; they reach quieter customers and can probe *why*.

**Qualitative** information (verbatims, stories, observations) tells you what matters and why. **Quantitative** information (scores, counts, durations) tells you how much and how often. You need both: a CSAT score of 6.8 says something is wrong; the free-text comments say what.

For VOE, the same toolkit applies: staff interviews, pulse surveys, shadowing a shift, and reviewing the workarounds people have invented. For VOB, sources include budgets, strategic plans, contracts, SLA penalty reports and regulatory requirements.`},{id:"verbatim-to-ctq",heading:"From verbatims to needs to CTQs",body:`Customers speak in **verbatims**; processes run on **requirements**. The translation usually takes three steps:

1. **Verbatim** — the customer’s own words: *“I waste my whole day waiting for the technician.”*
2. **Need** — the underlying requirement in plain language: *a short, reliable appointment window.*
3. **CTQ** — a measurable characteristic with a target or limit: *appointment window ≤ 2 hours; technician arrives within the window ≥ 95% of visits.*

This is the bridge to lesson 1.2.2, where the [[ctq-tree]] formalises the drill-down from need to drivers to [[ctq|CTQs]].

Before accepting a requirement, test it with **[[rumba|RUMBA]]**:

- **Reasonable** — is it realistic given the service being bought?
- **Understandable** — will everyone interpret it the same way (an [[operational-definition]])?
- **Measurable** — can we collect data on it?
- **Believable** — will stakeholders accept and commit to it?
- **Achievable** — can the process reach it with feasible changes?

A requirement such as “excellent service” fails *Measurable* and *Understandable*. “Restore P1 incidents within 4 hours, measured from alarm time to service-restored time in the ticketing system” passes.`},{id:"conflicts",heading:"When the voices disagree",body:`Conflicts are normal. Picture a contact-centre redesign:

- **VOC:** “I want to talk to a real person, 24/7.”
- **VOB:** “Cost per contact must fall by 10%.”
- **VOE:** “We cannot cover more night shifts without burnout.”

The temptation is to declare a winner. A better approach:

1. **Dig beneath the position.** Do customers want a *human*, or do they want *a fast fix for urgent problems*? Interviews often reveal the latter.
2. **Segment.** Perhaps 24/7 human access matters intensely for outage-related enterprise contacts, but much less for billing questions from consumers.
3. **Quantify with data.** Ticket data might show only a small share of night-time contacts are urgent.
4. **Design for the shared core.** A 24/7 human line for P1/outage contacts, plus high-quality self-service and call-back for the rest, can meet the customer need, lower cost per contact and protect staff workload.
5. **Make trade-offs explicit** in the charter and get the Champion to endorse them.

Tools such as the [[kano-model|Kano model]] (supplementary) help here: it separates *basic* expectations (must be present — e.g. the service works), *performance* needs (more is better — e.g. faster restoration) and *delighters* (unexpected extras — e.g. proactive outage SMS). Over time, delighters drift into basic expectations.`}],whyItMatters:"In SLA governance and vendor management, the three voices are everywhere: the business has contractual targets, the end customer experiences the outages, and vendor and internal staff carry the workload. A Green Belt who can show *which* voice a requirement comes from — and whether it passes RUMBA — writes stronger charters, sets fairer SLAs and avoids projects that improve a number nobody actually needs. On the exam, expect questions that ask you to classify a statement as VOC, VOB or VOE, or to pick the best method for capturing a voice.",businessExample:{title:"Broadband repair: three voices, one requirement set",body:`An operator’s home-broadband repair process has rising complaints. The Green Belt gathers:

- **VOC** from 400 recent repair-ticket surveys and 15 customer interviews. Top verbatims: *“Nobody told me when the technician would arrive”*, *“I had to call three times.”*
- **VOB** from the finance plan and regulator reports: repeat visits cost money, and the regulator publishes fault-repair times.
- **VOE** from a Gemba visit to the dispatch desk and field technicians: *“Half the jobs arrive without the right modem model noted.”*

| Verbatim / statement | Voice | Need | Candidate CTQ |
|---|---|---|---|
| “Nobody told me when the technician would arrive.” | VOC | Predictable visit | Customer receives a ≤ 2-hour window by SMS ≥ 98% of jobs |
| “I had to call three times.” | VOC | Fixed first time | Repeat repair within 7 days ≤ 5% |
| “Repeat visits are eating our margin.” | VOB | Lower cost to serve | Visits per fault ≤ 1.1 |
| “Jobs arrive without the modem model.” | VOE | Complete job data | Job tickets with complete equipment data ≥ 99% |

Notice how the VOE item explains part of the VOC pain: missing equipment data causes repeat visits, which the customer feels as “calling three times” and the business feels as cost. *Figures are illustrative.*`},visual:"voices",visualCaption:"Three voices flow into a set of measurable requirements. Try the quick sort: assign each statement to the voice it comes from.",definitions:[{term:"Voice of the Customer (VOC)",definition:"The expressed and unexpressed needs, wants and expectations of the recipients of a process output, ideally translated into requirements or specifications."},{term:"Voice of the Business (VOB)",definition:"The needs of the organisation and its key stakeholders, such as profitability, revenue, growth, cost, risk and compliance."},{term:"Voice of the Employee (VOE)",definition:"The needs and perspective of the people who operate the process, such as workload, tools, training and clarity of procedures."},{term:"Voice of the Process (VOP)",definition:"What the process actually delivers, shown by its performance data and variation. Compared against VOC to reveal gaps."},{term:"Internal customer",definition:"A person or team inside the organisation who receives and depends on a process output."},{term:"External customer",definition:"A person or organisation outside the company who receives the product or service, usually the paying customer."},{term:"Verbatim",definition:"A customer’s own words, captured as spoken or written, before interpretation."},{term:"RUMBA",definition:"A test for valid requirements: Reasonable, Understandable, Measurable, Believable, Achievable."},{term:"Kano model (supplementary)",definition:"A framework that classifies customer needs as basic, performance or delighter, based on how their presence or absence affects satisfaction."}],comparisons:[{title:"VOC, VOB and VOE side by side",headers:["","VOC","VOB","VOE"],rows:[["Focus","What customers need","What the organisation needs","What the workforce needs to deliver"],["Typical sources","Surveys, complaints, interviews, ticket data","Financial plans, contracts, regulations","Staff interviews, pulse surveys, Gemba"],["Typical measures","Restore time, first-time fix, CSAT","Cost per contact, revenue, penalties","Workload, tool time, attrition"],["Risk if ignored","Customers leave or complain","Project unaffordable or misaligned","Workarounds, burnout, solution not sustained"]]},{title:"Qualitative versus quantitative customer information",headers:["","Qualitative","Quantitative"],rows:[["Form","Words, stories, observations","Numbers, counts, durations, scores"],["Answers","What matters and why","How much and how often"],["Examples","Interviews, focus groups, survey comments","CSAT, NPS, ticket reopen rate, MTTR"],["Limitation","Hard to generalise from small samples","Shows a gap without explaining it"]]}],misconceptions:[{myth:"The customer is always the person who pays.",reality:"Customers include internal teams that receive your output. Most process hand-offs have an internal customer."},{myth:"A customer verbatim is a requirement.",reality:"Verbatims must be translated into a need and then into a measurable CTQ that passes RUMBA."},{myth:"Complaints tell you everything you need to know.",reality:"Complaints are reactive and biased toward the most upset customers. Proactive methods reach the silent majority."},{myth:"VOB always wins because the business funds the project.",reality:"A project that ignores VOC loses customers; one that ignores VOE is rarely sustained. Good projects reconcile all three with data."}],examTraps:[{trap:"A statement about profit, cost or compliance labelled as VOC.",explanation:"Profit, cost, revenue, risk and regulatory needs belong to the Voice of the Business, even when a customer-facing team says them."},{trap:"Confusing VOC with VOP.",explanation:"VOC is what the customer requires (the target or limits). VOP is what the process actually delivers (its data and variation)."},{trap:"“Which is the BEST first step to understand customer needs?”",explanation:"Prefer options that collect the customer’s voice directly (interviews, surveys, complaint/ticket analysis) over options that assume what the customer wants.",tag:"best"},{trap:"A requirement such as “fast service” offered as a finished CTQ.",explanation:"It is not measurable or understandable as written. A CTQ needs a metric and a target or limit."}],workedExamples:[{title:"Classify the statements",problem:"For an enterprise-support desk, classify each statement as VOC, VOB or VOE: (a) “Our account manager never knows the status of our tickets.” (b) “SLA penalty payments must fall below 1% of contract value.” (c) “We re-enter the same data in two tools for every ticket.” (d) “We need restore times we can plan around.”",steps:["(a) Comes from the client receiving the service → **VOC**.","(b) Concerns financial exposure and contract performance for the organisation → **VOB**.","(c) Describes the experience of the people doing the work → **VOE**.","(d) The client’s need for predictable restoration → **VOC**.","Note how (c) may *cause* (a): duplicate entry delays status updates. Voices often connect."],answer:"(a) VOC, (b) VOB, (c) VOE, (d) VOC."},{title:"From verbatim to a RUMBA-tested CTQ",problem:"Verbatim from an SME customer: *“When the line is down, I need you to fix it quickly.”* Turn this into a CTQ and test it with RUMBA.",steps:["**Need:** rapid restoration of a business-critical service.","**First draft:** “Fix faults quickly.” → fails *Measurable* and *Understandable*.","**Second draft:** “Restore 100% of faults within 1 hour.” → fails *Reasonable* and *Achievable* for field faults.","**Final draft:** “Restore SME line-down faults within 4 hours of the customer report, ≥ 95% of the time, measured from ticket creation to restored timestamp.”","**RUMBA check:** reasonable for the service tier, clearly defined start/stop, measurable from ticket data, believable to the customer, achievable with process changes."],answer:"CTQ: SME line-down restoration ≤ 4 hours from ticket creation, ≥ 95% of faults.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Adopting “fix it quickly” as the requirement.",whyWrong:"No metric, no target, no start/stop definition — two teams would measure it differently."}}],lab:"voice",check:[{id:"chk-1.1.5-1",topic:"1.1.5",category:"definition",difficulty:"beginner",numerical:!1,stem:"A finance director says: “This project must reduce cost per contact by 10%.” This statement is an example of:",options:["Voice of the Customer","Voice of the Business","Voice of the Employee","Voice of the Process"],answer:1,explanation:"Cost, profit and financial targets are needs of the organisation — the Voice of the Business.",whyWrong:["Customers rarely express needs in terms of the company’s cost per contact.","","VOE describes the workforce’s needs and experience.","VOP is the actual performance data of the process, not a target."]},{id:"chk-1.1.5-2",topic:"1.1.5",category:"concept",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"Which pair correctly matches a requirement test with its meaning in **RUMBA**?",options:["R — Reliable: the requirement is repeatable.","U — Urgent: the requirement must be met quickly.","M — Measurable: data can be collected on the requirement.","B — Budgeted: the requirement has funding."],answer:2,explanation:"RUMBA = Reasonable, Understandable, Measurable, Believable, Achievable.",whyWrong:["R stands for Reasonable.","U stands for Understandable.","","B stands for Believable."]},{id:"chk-1.1.5-3",topic:"1.1.5",category:"scenario",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"The billing team receives order data from the provisioning team. From provisioning’s point of view, the billing team is:",options:["An external customer","An internal customer","A supplier","A stakeholder with no customer role"],answer:1,explanation:"Billing is inside the organisation and depends on provisioning’s output, so it is an internal customer.",whyWrong:["External customers are outside the organisation.","","Billing receives the output; provisioning is the supplier here.","Billing directly receives and depends on the output, so it is a customer."]},{id:"chk-1.1.5-4",topic:"1.1.5",category:"best-tool",difficulty:"advanced",numerical:!1,stem:"A team knows CSAT dropped from 8.1 to 6.9 but does not understand **why**. Which method is BEST for the next step?",options:["Re-run the same CSAT survey with a larger sample.","Calculate the average handle time for the period.","Conduct customer interviews and analyse survey free-text comments.","Set a new CSAT target of 8.5."],answer:2,explanation:"The quantitative score shows that something is wrong; qualitative sources reveal what and why.",whyWrong:["More of the same score tells you “how much” again, not “why”.","Handle time is one possible X, not a way to hear the customer’s reasons.","","A target does not explain the cause of the drop."],traps:["best"]}],takeaways:["VOC = customer needs; VOB = organisation needs; VOE = workforce needs. VOP = what the process actually delivers.","Customers can be internal (next team in the chain) or external (outside the company).","Combine reactive and proactive, qualitative and quantitative listening methods.","Translate verbatim → need → measurable CTQ, then test it with RUMBA.","Conflicting voices are normal; segment, quantify and design for the shared core.","The voices often connect: an employee pain point can be the hidden cause of a customer complaint."],flashcards:[{front:"VOC",back:"Voice of the Customer — expressed and unexpressed needs of those who receive the output."},{front:"VOB",back:"Voice of the Business — profitability, revenue, cost, risk, compliance and strategic needs."},{front:"VOE",back:"Voice of the Employee — the workforce’s needs and experience: workload, tools, procedures."},{front:"VOP",back:"Voice of the Process — actual performance and variation, compared against VOC."},{front:"RUMBA stands for?",back:"Reasonable, Understandable, Measurable, Believable, Achievable."},{front:"Internal customer example?",back:"The field team receiving dispatch information from the NOC."},{front:"Reactive vs proactive VOC?",back:"Reactive arrives unasked (complaints, tickets); proactive is requested (surveys, interviews, focus groups)."},{front:"Verbatim → ? → ?",back:"Verbatim → need → CTQ (measurable requirement with a target or limit)."},{front:"Kano categories",back:"Basic (must-be), performance (more is better), delighter (unexpected). Supplementary."}],coverage:"official-only",supplementaryNotes:["The training book has no entry for Voice of the Employee (VOE); the VOE explanation is written independently from general Lean Six Sigma practice.","The Kano model, NPS/CSAT, social listening and the reactive/proactive classification are supplementary context, not taken from the supplied pages.","The Define chapter of the book was not supplied; book references below reinforce VOC, VOB, VOP and RUMBA from later chapters and the glossary."],sources:[{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 7",pdfPage:7,note:"Define topic 1.1.5 “Voice of the Customer, Business and Employee”."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 112",pdfPage:15,note:"Customer requirements called VOC; RUMBA test; outputs ranked to identify CTQs."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 208",pdfPage:11,note:"Capability analysis compares the Voice of the Process with the Voice of the Customer."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 212",pdfPage:15,note:"Sources of specifications: customer requirements (VOC), business requirements, compliance, design."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 588",pdfPage:91,note:"Glossary: R.U.M.B.A. used to validate customer requirements."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 590",pdfPage:93,note:"Glossary: VOB, VOC and VOP definitions (no VOE entry)."}]},M=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:d},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),h={id:"1.1.6",module:1,title:"Six Sigma Roles & Responsibilities",subtitle:"Sponsors, Champions, Belts, Process Owners and the team",minutes:12,objectives:["Describe the responsibilities of the Executive Sponsor, Champion, Master Black Belt, Black Belt, Green Belt and Yellow Belt.","Explain the role of the Process Owner, especially during and after handover in Control.","Identify who typically selects projects, removes barriers, coaches, leads, and validates financial benefits.","Use a RACI-style view to show who is responsible and accountable for key project activities.","Recognise the typical time commitment and training level of each belt role."],overview:`Six Sigma is often explained as a toolkit, but organisations that succeed with it treat it as a **team sport with clear positions**. Statistical tools are useless if nobody has the authority to free up people’s time, nobody owns the process after the project ends, or nobody checks that the promised savings are real. That is why the Body of Knowledge defines a set of roles — some named after martial-arts belts — each with a distinct job.

A helpful analogy is a football club. The **Executive Sponsor** is the club board: sets the ambition and funds it. The **Champion** is the manager: picks which matches (projects) to play and clears obstacles. The **Master Black Belt** is the technical coach. **Black Belts** and **Green Belts** are the players who lead on the pitch, supported by **Yellow Belts** and **team members**. The **Process Owner** is the groundskeeper who looks after the pitch long after the match is over. Let’s look at each position in turn.`,sections:[{id:"leadership",heading:"Leadership roles: Executive Sponsor and Champion",body:`The [[executive-sponsor]] is a senior leader (often a VP, Chief Operating Officer or business-unit head) who links the improvement programme to strategy. Typical responsibilities:

- Set the direction: which business priorities improvement should serve.
- Provide resources and visible support; make it clear that Six Sigma work is “real work”, not an extra.
- Review results at a portfolio level and hold Champions accountable.

The [[champion]] is usually a middle or senior manager closer to the processes. The Champion **recognises, defines, assigns and supports** projects and is accountable for their results. In practice the Champion:

- **Selects and scopes projects** — aligning them with the business case and the Sponsor’s priorities.
- **Approves the [[project-charter]]** and the team.
- **Removes barriers** — budget, access to data, cross-department politics, a vendor who will not share logs.
- **Chairs the [[tollgate]] reviews**, deciding whether the project moves to the next DMAIC phase.

A common exam distinction: the Sponsor *sets the strategic direction and funds*, the Champion *selects, sponsors and unblocks individual projects*. In smaller organisations one person may hold both roles.`},{id:"belts",heading:"The belts: Master Black Belt, Black Belt, Green Belt, Yellow Belt",body:`The belt roles differ in depth of training, scope of projects and time commitment.

- **[[master-black-belt|Master Black Belt (MBB)]]** — trained beyond Black Belt. Teaches and mentors Black and Green Belts, advises Champions on project selection, ensures statistical rigour, and often runs the deployment. Typically full-time.
- **[[black-belt|Black Belt (BB)]]** — a full-time improvement leader who works under a Champion on larger, often cross-functional projects. Leads teams, applies advanced tools and coaches Green Belts.
- **[[green-belt|Green Belt (GB)]]** — trained in the core DMAIC toolkit; works on improvement **part-time** alongside a regular job. Leads smaller-scope projects within their own area, or supports Black Belt projects with data collection and analysis.
- **[[yellow-belt|Yellow Belt (YB)]]** — has foundational awareness. Participates as a team member on Green and Black Belt projects, helps collect data and brings process knowledge.

| Belt | Typical training (indicative) | Typical time on improvement | Typical project role |
|---|---|---|---|
| Master Black Belt | Beyond Black Belt | Full-time | Coach, mentor, programme expert |
| Black Belt | About 4 weeks (≈120 h) | Full-time | Leads complex projects |
| Green Belt | About 2 weeks (≈80 h) | Part-time (often roughly 20–50%) | Leads local projects, supports BB |
| Yellow Belt | About 1 week or less (≈16 h) | Occasional | Team member |

Training durations vary by provider; the syllabus recommends about 80 hours for Green Belt, 16 for Yellow Belt and 120 for Black Belt. The time percentages are typical ranges, not rules.`},{id:"process-owner",heading:"The Process Owner: the role that outlives the project",body:`The [[process-owner]] is the manager responsible for the **end-to-end performance and resources** of the process being improved — for example, the head of incident management or the service-desk manager. The Process Owner:

- Helps define the problem and confirms the data reflect reality.
- Is **accountable for implementing the solutions** in daily operations.
- **Owns the process after handover** in Control: the control plan, monitoring, response plans and ongoing performance.

This last point is vital. A Belt leads a project for a few months; the process runs for years. If the Process Owner has not committed to the new way of working, the gains fade the moment the Belt moves on. In many deployments the Champion and Process Owner work side-by-side on the roadmap — they jointly identify the problem area and verify the financial impact at the end.`},{id:"team-stakeholders",heading:"Project team, stakeholders and the Finance representative",body:`The [[project-team]] is the group of people — often 4 to 8 — who bring process knowledge and do much of the work: mapping, collecting data, testing solutions. Members are usually chosen because they *work in or next to the process* (NOC engineers, dispatchers, a vendor service manager). Many are Yellow Belts or untrained colleagues who learn by doing.

[[stakeholder|Stakeholders]] are anyone affected by or able to affect the project: customers, IT, HR, the vendor’s account team, regulators, the union, other departments. They are not all on the team, but they must be identified, informed and — where needed — consulted.

A specific stakeholder deserves its own mention: the [[finance-representative]]. Their job is to provide an **independent evaluation of hard and soft savings**, supporting the Champion and the Belt. When a project claims “OMR 120,000 saved per year”, Finance confirms the baseline, the method and whether the savings are real. Without that, benefits are just opinions.`},{id:"raci",heading:"Who does what: a RACI-style view",body:`RACI stands for **R**esponsible (does the work), **A**ccountable (owns the outcome; one per activity), **C**onsulted (gives input) and **I**nformed (kept up to date). The table shows a *typical* pattern — real organisations adapt it.

| Activity | Sponsor | Champion | MBB | BB / GB (project lead) | Process Owner | Team | Finance |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Set strategic priorities | A | C | C | I | C | – | C |
| Select and scope the project | C | A | C | R | C | – | C |
| Approve charter and team | I | A | C | R | C | I | – |
| Remove organisational barriers | C | A/R | – | I | R | I | – |
| Lead DMAIC and apply tools | – | I | C | A/R | C | R | – |
| Coach on methods and statistics | – | – | A/R | R (BB coaches GB) | – | I | – |
| Validate financial benefits | I | A | – | R | C | – | R |
| Implement solution and sustain in Control | I | C | – | R | A | R | – |

Read the columns rather than memorising cells: **Champions select and unblock; Belts lead; MBBs coach; Process Owners sustain; Finance validates.**`}],whyItMatters:"When a project stalls, the cause is often a role gap rather than a tool gap — no Champion to resolve a vendor dispute, no Process Owner willing to adopt the new procedure, no Finance sign-off so the savings are disputed. As a Green Belt you will lead projects part-time, so you depend on others for authority, data and sustainment. Knowing exactly what to ask of each role makes you far more effective. The exam regularly tests the distinctions: who selects projects, who removes barriers, who coaches, and who owns the process after the project ends.",businessExample:{title:"Reducing P1 incident MTTR across a managed-services contract",body:`An operator outsources parts of its application operations to a managed-services vendor. P1 incident restoration times are breaching the SLA.

- **Executive Sponsor** — the VP of Digital Operations makes SLA performance a strategic priority for the year and funds a small improvement programme.
- **Champion** — the head of service operations selects the MTTR project, approves the charter, and secures the vendor’s agreement to share monitoring logs (a barrier the team could not remove itself).
- **Master Black Belt** — an internal OE specialist coaches the project lead on measurement-system checks for ticket timestamps.
- **Green Belt** — an operational-excellence engineer leads the project about two days a week alongside SLA reporting duties.
- **Yellow Belts and team** — two NOC shift leads, a vendor service-delivery manager and a problem manager map the escalation flow and collect data.
- **Process Owner** — the incident-management manager agrees to adopt the revised routing rules and owns the control chart after handover.
- **Finance representative** — validates the avoided SLA penalties as hard savings and classifies reduced overtime as soft savings.
- **Stakeholders** — enterprise account managers, the vendor’s delivery lead, and IT security (for log access) are kept informed.

Each role covered a gap the Green Belt could not fill alone.`},visual:"roles",visualCaption:"Explore the deployment structure: select a role to see its responsibilities, then test yourself in the matching challenge.",definitions:[{term:"Executive Sponsor",definition:"Senior leader who sets strategic direction for improvement, provides resources and reviews results across the portfolio."},{term:"Champion",definition:"Manager who recognises, defines, assigns and supports projects, removes barriers, chairs tollgates and is accountable for project results."},{term:"Master Black Belt",definition:"Expert trained beyond Black Belt who teaches and mentors Belts, advises Champions and safeguards methodological rigour."},{term:"Black Belt",definition:"Full-time improvement leader working under a Champion on larger, often cross-functional projects; coaches Green Belts."},{term:"Green Belt",definition:"Part-time practitioner who leads smaller-scope projects in their own area and supports Black Belt projects."},{term:"Yellow Belt",definition:"Team member with foundational awareness who participates in Green and Black Belt projects."},{term:"Process Owner",definition:"Manager responsible for a process’s performance and resources; accountable for implementing solutions and sustaining them after handover."},{term:"Project team",definition:"People with process knowledge who carry out project work such as mapping, data collection and solution testing."},{term:"Stakeholder",definition:"Anyone who is affected by, or can affect, the project or the process."},{term:"Finance representative",definition:"Provides independent evaluation of hard and soft savings, supporting Champions and project leaders."}],comparisons:[{title:"Commonly confused role pairs",headers:["Pair","First role","Second role","Key difference"],rows:[["Sponsor vs Champion","Sets strategy, funds programme","Selects, scopes and unblocks projects","Portfolio level vs project level"],["Champion vs Process Owner","Sponsors the project","Owns the process day to day","Project accountability vs ongoing process accountability"],["MBB vs Black Belt","Coaches and mentors Belts","Leads projects full-time","Coach vs lead"],["Black Belt vs Green Belt","Full-time, larger projects","Part-time, local projects","Time commitment and scope"],["Green Belt vs Yellow Belt","Leads smaller projects","Participates as team member","Leading vs participating"]]}],misconceptions:[{myth:"The Black Belt owns the process once the project is finished.",reality:"The Process Owner owns and sustains the process after handover. Belts move on to new projects."},{myth:"Green Belts are always full-time improvement specialists.",reality:"Green Belts typically work on projects part-time alongside their normal job."},{myth:"The Master Black Belt leads most projects.",reality:"The MBB mainly coaches, mentors and advises; Black and Green Belts lead projects."},{myth:"Finance only gets involved at the end.",reality:"Finance should help agree the baseline and benefit method early, then validate realised savings later."}],examTraps:[{trap:"“Who is PRIMARILY responsible for removing organisational barriers?”",explanation:"The Champion. Belts escalate barriers; they lack the authority to remove cross-functional obstacles.",tag:"primary"},{trap:"“Who owns the process AFTER the project is closed?”",explanation:"The Process Owner — not the Belt, the Champion or the MBB.",tag:"after"},{trap:"Options that make the Master Black Belt responsible for selecting projects.",explanation:"The MBB advises; the Champion (aligned with the Sponsor) selects and assigns projects."},{trap:"“Which role does NOT normally lead a DMAIC project?”",explanation:"Yellow Belts participate rather than lead. Read NOT carefully.",tag:"not"}],workedExamples:[{title:"Who should act?",problem:"A Green Belt’s project needs ticket data from a vendor system. The vendor’s account manager refuses, citing the contract. Two weeks are lost. Who should the Green Belt go to, and why?",steps:["Recognise the issue: an **organisational barrier** outside the Green Belt’s authority.","Removing barriers is a core **Champion** responsibility.","The Green Belt documents the impact (delay to Measure, risk to the charter timeline) and escalates to the Champion.","The Champion negotiates with the vendor or uses contract governance channels; the **Process Owner** may help if the data concerns their process."],answer:"Escalate to the Champion, who has the authority to remove the barrier.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Asking the Master Black Belt to obtain the data.",whyWrong:"The MBB coaches on methods; they typically lack line authority over vendor contracts."}},{title:"Map the activities to roles",problem:"Match each activity to the role that typically owns it: (1) independently validating that SLA-penalty savings are real; (2) mentoring a Green Belt on hypothesis testing; (3) maintaining the new control chart six months after closure; (4) choosing which of five proposed projects to launch this quarter.",steps:["(1) Independent validation of savings → **Finance representative**.","(2) Methodological mentoring → **Master Black Belt** (a Black Belt may also coach a Green Belt).","(3) Ongoing control after handover → **Process Owner**.","(4) Project selection → **Champion**, aligned with the Executive Sponsor’s priorities."],answer:"(1) Finance, (2) MBB, (3) Process Owner, (4) Champion."}],lab:null,check:[{id:"chk-1.1.6-1",topic:"1.1.6",category:"definition",difficulty:"beginner",numerical:!1,stem:"Which role is typically responsible for selecting projects and removing organisational barriers?",options:["Green Belt","Champion","Yellow Belt","Finance representative"],answer:1,explanation:"The Champion recognises, defines, assigns and supports projects and removes barriers.",whyWrong:["Green Belts lead projects but lack the authority to remove cross-functional barriers.","","Yellow Belts participate as team members.","Finance validates savings; it does not select projects."]},{id:"chk-1.1.6-2",topic:"1.1.6",category:"concept",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"After a project is closed, who is accountable for sustaining the improved process?",options:["The Black Belt who led the project","The Master Black Belt","The Process Owner","The Executive Sponsor"],answer:2,explanation:"The Process Owner is responsible for process performance and owns the control plan after handover.",whyWrong:["The Belt moves on to new projects after handover.","The MBB coaches and mentors; they do not own operational processes.","","The Sponsor sets direction at a strategic level, not day-to-day process control."],traps:["after"]},{id:"chk-1.1.6-3",topic:"1.1.6",category:"compare",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"Which statement BEST distinguishes a Green Belt from a Black Belt?",options:["Green Belts typically work on improvement part-time on smaller-scope projects; Black Belts typically work full-time on larger projects.","Green Belts coach Black Belts on advanced statistics.","Black Belts own the process after handover; Green Belts do not.","Green Belts select projects; Black Belts execute them."],answer:0,explanation:"The core differences are time commitment and project scope; Black Belts also coach Green Belts.",whyWrong:["","It is the other way round: Black Belts (and MBBs) coach Green Belts.","Neither belt owns the process; the Process Owner does.","Champions select projects."],traps:["best"]},{id:"chk-1.1.6-4",topic:"1.1.6",category:"scenario",difficulty:"advanced",numerical:!1,stem:"A project claims large savings, but operations managers dispute the figures. Which role should have been involved to provide an **independent** evaluation of the savings?",options:["Master Black Belt","Finance representative","Yellow Belt team member","Process Owner"],answer:1,explanation:"The Finance representative independently evaluates hard and soft savings, supporting the Champion and Belt.",whyWrong:["The MBB checks methodological rigour, not financial validation.","","A team member is not independent and lacks the financial mandate.","The Process Owner is a stakeholder in the outcome, so not independent."]}],takeaways:["Executive Sponsor: sets strategic direction and provides resources across the programme.","Champion: selects, scopes and supports projects; removes barriers; chairs tollgates; accountable for results.","Master Black Belt: coaches and mentors Belts and advises Champions.","Black Belt: full-time leader of larger projects; Green Belt: part-time leader of local projects.","Yellow Belts and team members contribute process knowledge and data.","Process Owner: implements and sustains the solution and owns the process after handover.","Finance representative: independently validates hard and soft savings."],flashcards:[{front:"Who removes barriers for a project?",back:"The Champion."},{front:"Who owns the process after handover?",back:"The Process Owner."},{front:"Main job of a Master Black Belt?",back:"Teach, coach and mentor Belts; advise Champions; ensure rigour."},{front:"Black Belt time commitment?",back:"Typically full-time on improvement."},{front:"Green Belt time commitment?",back:"Typically part-time, alongside a regular role."},{front:"Yellow Belt role?",back:"Participates as a team member on Green/Black Belt projects."},{front:"Who independently evaluates savings?",back:"The Finance representative."},{front:"Sponsor vs Champion?",back:"Sponsor: strategy and resources (portfolio). Champion: selects and unblocks individual projects."},{front:"What is a stakeholder?",back:"Anyone affected by, or able to affect, the project or process."},{front:"RACI letters?",back:"Responsible, Accountable, Consulted, Informed."}],coverage:"official-only",supplementaryNotes:["The Define chapter of the training book was not supplied. Role definitions are reinforced from the book glossary; the Executive Sponsor, project team and stakeholder explanations are written independently.","The RACI table, team sizes and time-commitment percentages are typical practitioner patterns, not taken from the supplied pages.","Training durations differ by source: the book glossary gives about 1/2/4 weeks for Yellow/Green/Black Belt; the syllabus recommends 16/80/120 hours."],sources:[{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 7",pdfPage:7,note:"Define topic 1.1.6 “Six Sigma Roles & Responsibilities”; ~80 recommended training hours for Green Belt."},{doc:"syllabus",pages:"pp. 3–4",pdfPage:3,note:"Training hours by certification: Yellow Belt 16 and Green Belt 80 (p. 3); Black Belt 120 (p. 4)."},{doc:"book-401-500",pages:"p. 417",pdfPage:20,note:"DMAIC roadmap with Champion/Process Owner owning problem identification; Define steps include Establish Team."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 584",pdfPage:87,note:"Glossary: Black Belt (full-time leader under a Champion); Champion (recognises, defines, assigns, supports; accountable)."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 585",pdfPage:88,note:"Glossary: Finance Representative — independent evaluation of hard and soft savings."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 586",pdfPage:89,note:"Glossary: Green Belt (part-time, smaller local projects); Master Black Belt (teaches, mentors)."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 588",pdfPage:91,note:"Glossary: Process Owner — responsible for process performance; accountable for implementing solutions."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 590",pdfPage:93,note:"Glossary: Yellow Belt — participates on Green and Black Belt projects."}]},D=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:h},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),p={id:"1.2.1",module:2,title:"Defining a Process",subtitle:"Inputs, activities, outputs, customers and boundaries",minutes:12,objectives:["Define a process as a repeatable set of activities that transforms inputs into outputs for a customer.","Identify the inputs, activities, outputs, customers and suppliers of a service process.","Set clear process boundaries using a start trigger and a stop trigger, and name a single process owner.","Build a high-level (Level 1) view of four to seven major steps without drowning in detail.","Distinguish outputs from outcomes, and a process from a function or department.","Explain where SIPOC and detailed process mapping sit in the syllabus (Measure 2.1.2) and why Define only needs the high-level view."],overview:`Every Lean Six Sigma project improves *something that happens repeatedly*. That something is a [[process]]: a sequence of activities that takes [[input|inputs]] and turns them into [[output|outputs]] that a customer needs. Before a team can measure a defect rate, estimate a [[copq|cost of poor quality]] or argue about root causes, it has to agree on exactly which process it is looking at, where that process starts, where it stops and who it serves.

That sounds obvious, yet it is where many projects quietly go wrong. Ask five people in a telecom operator to describe "the fibre installation process" and you will hear five different stories: Sales thinks it ends when the order is keyed, Field Operations thinks it starts when a job appears in the technician's app, and Billing has never heard of it. Defining the process gives everyone the same picture, at the same level of detail, with the same boundaries. In this lesson you will learn to do that quickly and precisely.`,sections:[{id:"what-is-a-process",heading:"What a process is (and is not)",body:`A useful working definition: **a process is a repeatable set of activities, with a flow of material and/or information, that transforms inputs into outputs for a customer.** Three ideas in that sentence matter for the exam.

- **Repeatable.** A one-off event (moving the data centre) is a project, not a process. Six Sigma needs repetition, because you can only measure variation in something that happens many times.
- **Transformation.** Something must change. Raw order details become an active broadband line; an incident ticket becomes a restored service; a supplier invoice becomes a payment.
- **For a customer.** If no one receives the output, the activity is a candidate for elimination, not improvement.

The training material describes a process simply as "a blending of inputs to produce some desired output" (book p. 99). This is the same logic as [[y-equals-f-x|Y = f(X)]]: the output Y is a function of the inputs and activities X. Defining the process is how you discover which X's even exist.

A process is **not** a department. Departments (functions) are how an organisation groups *people*; processes are how *work flows*. Real customer-facing processes almost always cross several functions, and the hand-offs between them are where delays, errors and finger-pointing breed.`},{id:"building-blocks",heading:"The building blocks: suppliers, inputs, activities, outputs, customers",body:`Think of a process like a kitchen in a busy restaurant. Suppliers deliver ingredients (inputs), cooks perform steps (activities), plated dishes leave the pass (outputs) and diners eat them (customers). Every service process has the same anatomy.

| Element | Question it answers | Fibre order example | Incident management example |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Supplier** | Who provides what we need? | Customer, network inventory, logistics | Monitoring tools, service desk, vendor |
| **Input (X)** | What do we consume or use? | Order details, coverage data, router | Alert, caller details, CI record |
| **Activities** | What do we do to transform it? | Capture, check, schedule, install, bill | Log, classify, diagnose, fix, close |
| **Output (Y)** | What do we hand over? | Active line, closed order, first bill | Restored service, resolution note |
| **Customer** | Who receives and judges the output? | Household or business subscriber | End user, business service owner |

Two points examiners like. First, customers can be [[internal-customer|internal]] (the Billing team receiving a completed order) or [[external-customer|external]] (the subscriber). Second, inputs are not only physical: information, people's skills, procedures, system settings and environmental conditions are all inputs. The book separates *controllable*, *procedural* and *noise* inputs (pp. 113–114); a noise input such as weather on installation day still affects the output even though you cannot set it.

You may recognise these five headings as the letters of a [[sipoc|SIPOC]]. That is deliberate. **In the syllabus, building a SIPOC and drawing detailed process maps are classified under Measure (2.1.2), not Define.** In Define you only need enough of this thinking to agree what the process is and to scope the project. Treat SIPOC here as context, not as the topic being examined.`},{id:"boundaries-ownership",heading:"Boundaries and ownership",body:`A [[process-boundary|process boundary]] is set by two events:

- **Start trigger:** the event that causes the process to begin. *"Customer submits a fibre order."* *"An incident is logged in the ITSM tool."* *"A supplier invoice arrives in the AP mailbox."*
- **Stop trigger:** the event that proves the process has finished and delivered its output. *"Service is live and confirmed by the customer."* *"The incident is resolved and the user confirms restoration."* *"The invoice is paid and posted."*

Write triggers as observable events, ideally ones with a system timestamp. "When the customer is happy" is not a stop trigger; "customer confirms service in the activation call" is. Good triggers make later measurement possible, because [[cycle-time|cycle time]] is literally the clock between them.

Boundaries also control scope. Draw them too wide ("everything from marketing campaign to contract renewal") and the project becomes unmanageable; too narrow ("the technician's 40 minutes on site") and you may miss the real cause, which could be an inaccurate coverage check three steps earlier. A practical rule from the training material is to include at least one step before and one after the problem area (book pp. 105–110).

Finally, every defined process needs a [[process-owner|process owner]]: one named person accountable for its end-to-end performance and resources. When a process crosses Sales, Network, Field Operations and Billing, no department head naturally owns the whole flow. Naming the owner during Define prevents the classic outcome where each function optimises its own step and the customer still waits three weeks.`},{id:"level-one",heading:"Seeing the process from 30,000 feet: the Level 1 view",body:`Process views come in levels, much like maps: a world map, a country map and a city street map (book pp. 102–103). **Level 1** is the macro or management view, typically four to seven big activities. Level 2 shows the work as performers experience it, and Level 3 is the detailed improvement view carrying yields, cycle times and value-added flags. Define works at Level 1.

To build a Level 1 view quickly:

1. **Name it** with a verb and a noun: "Fulfil new fibre order", not "Fibre".
2. **State its purpose** in one sentence: why does this process exist?
3. **Fix the start and stop triggers.**
4. **Walk the major steps mentally** and write each as a verb-noun box. Resist adding decisions, loops and exceptions yet.
5. **Attach the obvious inputs, outputs and customers**, and name the owner.

The discipline is to stay high-level. A Level 1 view that contains forty boxes is no longer a shared overview; it has become a detailed map, which belongs in Measure. Another useful caution from the book (p. 100) is that there are three versions of every process: what you *think* it is, what it *actually* is and what it *should* be. The actual process is almost always longer and more complicated, full of hidden checks and rework loops, which is exactly why Measure later maps it in detail.`},{id:"outputs-outcomes",heading:"Outputs versus outcomes",body:`This distinction separates average analysts from good ones.

- An **[[output]]** is the tangible thing the process produces and hands over: a closed ticket, an activated line, a paid invoice, a resolution note.
- An **[[outcome]]** is the effect the customer experiences as a result: service genuinely restored, the household online at the promised speed on the promised day, the supplier paid correctly without chasing.

Outputs can look perfect while outcomes are poor. A service desk can close 98% of tickets inside the [[sla|SLA]] and still see a flood of re-opened incidents, because closing a ticket (output) is not the same as fixing the user's problem (outcome). This is why closure-based [[kpi|KPIs]] are easy to game and why customers often disagree with green dashboards.

In Define, write down both. The output tells you what to count; the outcome tells you whether counting it matters. When you later develop [[ctq|CTQs]] (lesson 1.2.2), you will usually find that the most important ones describe the outcome, for example "service restored and not re-opened within 7 days", rather than the bare output.`}],whyItMatters:"Defining the process is the foundation for nearly everything else in DMAIC. Without agreed boundaries you cannot measure a [[baseline]], because nobody agrees when the clock starts. Without named outputs and customers you cannot write CTQs. Without inputs listed you cannot begin to think in Y = f(X). And without a process owner, improvements made by the team quietly decay once the project closes. Ten minutes spent agreeing the Level 1 view saves weeks of arguments later.",businessExample:{title:'Fibre installation: "our part is fine"',body:`A fixed-broadband operator was missing its 10-day installation promise on roughly a third of new fibre orders. Each department reported green: Sales keyed orders within a day, Field Operations completed most jobs within 48 hours of dispatch, and Billing started invoices on time.

The Green Belt's first act was to define the process end to end. Start trigger: *customer submits an order*. Stop trigger: *service live and confirmed in the activation call*. Five Level 1 steps: capture order, check feasibility, schedule installation, install and activate, confirm and bill. One owner: the Head of Fixed Fulfilment.

Laid out this way, the gap was obvious. Nobody measured the time between "feasibility checked" and "job dispatched", because it sat between two departments. Orders waited there for days whenever coverage data was out of date. The output (order closed) had always looked healthy; the outcome (customer online on the promised day) had not. The project charter was then scoped to that hand-off, with a clear owner and a measurable start and stop.`},visual:"processFlow",visualCaption:"Build the Level 1 view of a new fibre broadband order step by step: name, boundaries, activities, inputs from suppliers, outputs, the customer outcome and a single owner across functions.",definitions:[{term:"Process",definition:"A repeatable set of activities, with a flow of material and/or information, that transforms inputs into outputs for a customer."},{term:"Input (X)",definition:"Anything the process uses or consumes: material, information, people, methods, equipment, environment. Inputs can be controllable, procedural or noise."},{term:"Output (Y)",definition:"The tangible product, service or information the process delivers across its stop boundary."},{term:"Outcome",definition:"The effect the customer experiences as a result of the output, such as service restored and stable, rather than the ticket simply being closed."},{term:"Process boundary",definition:"The start trigger and stop trigger that define where the process begins and ends, and therefore what is in scope."},{term:"Supplier",definition:"A person, team, system or company that provides an input to the process. Suppliers can be internal or external."},{term:"Customer",definition:"Anyone who receives or uses the output of the process, internal or external."},{term:"Process owner",definition:"The single person accountable for the end-to-end performance and resources of a process, and for sustaining improvements."},{term:"Level 1 (macro) view",definition:"A high-level management view of a process showing about four to seven major activities, used in Define to agree what the process is."}],comparisons:[{title:"Process vs function (department)",headers:["","Process","Function / department"],rows:[["Organised around","The flow of work to a customer","A group of people with similar skills"],["Direction","Horizontal: crosses the organisation","Vertical: follows the reporting line"],["Example","Fulfil new fibre order","Field Operations department"],["Typical problem spot","Hand-offs between functions","Local efficiency targets"],["Accountable person","Process owner (end to end)","Department head (own team)"]]},{title:"Output vs outcome",headers:["","Output","Outcome"],rows:[["What it is","What the process delivers","What the customer experiences"],["Incident management","Ticket resolved and closed","Service restored and not re-opened"],["Fibre order","Line activated, order closed","Customer online at promised speed, on promised day"],["Invoice processing","Invoice posted for payment","Supplier paid correctly, on time, without chasing"],["Risk if used alone","Easy to count, easy to game","Harder to measure, but what really matters"]]}],misconceptions:[{myth:"A process is the same as a department.",reality:"Departments group people; processes describe how work flows to a customer. Most important processes cross several departments, and the hand-offs are where problems hide."},{myth:"The more detail in the first process view, the better.",reality:"Define needs a Level 1 view of four to seven major steps. Detailed Level 2/3 maps, SIPOC and value stream maps belong to Measure (2.1.2)."},{myth:"If outputs meet target, the customer must be satisfied.",reality:"Outputs (tickets closed) can meet target while outcomes (service actually restored) fail. Always define both."},{myth:"Inputs are only physical materials.",reality:"Information, skills, procedures, system settings and environmental conditions are inputs too, including noise inputs you cannot control."},{myth:"Process boundaries are a formality.",reality:"Boundaries decide scope and where the clock starts and stops. Vague boundaries make baselines and cycle times impossible to measure consistently."}],examTraps:[{trap:'A question asks which tool is used FIRST to define a process in Define, and offers "detailed process map" or "value stream map".',explanation:"In this syllabus, detailed mapping, SIPOC and VSM are Measure 2.1.2 items. In Define the first step is a high-level description: name, purpose, boundaries and major steps.",tag:"first"},{trap:'An option says "the process starts when the team is ready to work on it".',explanation:"Start and stop triggers are events in the process itself (an order arrives, an incident is logged), not the team’s working schedule.",tag:"not"},{trap:'A stem describes "number of tickets closed" and asks for the BEST measure of customer outcome.',explanation:"Tickets closed is an output count. An outcome measure reflects the customer’s result, such as incidents not re-opened within 7 days or service confirmed restored.",tag:"best"},{trap:"An answer names the department manager with the most staff in the process as the process owner.",explanation:"The process owner is the person accountable for end-to-end performance, regardless of which department has the most people in the process.",tag:"primary"}],workedExamples:[{title:"Setting boundaries for an incident management process",problem:'A service desk team says it wants to "improve incident handling". Define the process name, start and stop triggers, the main inputs, outputs and customers, and a Level 1 view.',steps:['**Name with a verb-noun:** "Resolve P2 incidents" (narrowing to one priority keeps the scope manageable).',"**Start trigger:** a P2 incident record is created in the ITSM tool (system timestamp available).","**Stop trigger:** the incident is resolved *and* the user confirms service restoration (the confirmation turns an output into a checked outcome).","**Level 1 steps (5):** log & classify → assign → diagnose → restore service → confirm & close.","**Inputs:** alert or user call, configuration item data, knowledge articles, engineer availability. **Suppliers:** monitoring tools, users, CMDB, resolver groups.","**Outputs:** restored service, resolution note, closed record. **Customers:** affected end users (external or internal) and the business service owner.","**Owner:** Incident Manager, accountable across the service desk and all resolver groups."],answer:"“Resolve P2 incidents”: from P2 record created to user-confirmed restoration, five Level 1 steps, owned by the Incident Manager.",wrongApproach:{calculation:'Start = "engineer picks up the ticket"; stop = "ticket status set to Resolved".',whyWrong:"Starting at pick-up hides the queue time before assignment, often the biggest delay, and stopping at a status change measures the output, not whether service was actually restored."}},{title:"Output looks fine, outcome does not",problem:"In one month a service desk closed **1,000** P2 incidents, **960** of them within the 8-hour resolution SLA. **150** of the 1,000 were re-opened within 7 days because the fault returned. Compare the output view with the outcome view.",steps:["Output view: on-time closure = $960 / 1{,}000 = 0.96$, so **96%**. The SLA dashboard is green.","Outcome view: incidents that stayed fixed = $1{,}000 - 150 = 850$; $850 / 1{,}000 = 0.85$, so **85%**.",'So 15% of "successful" closures did not deliver the outcome the customer wanted, and each re-open adds rework, a second handling cycle and frustration.','In Define, record both measures and make the outcome measure the candidate CTQ (for example "resolved and not re-opened within 7 days").'],answer:"Output: 96% closed on time. Outcome: only 85% stayed fixed. The process looks better than the customer experiences it.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"96% on-time closure, so the process is performing well.",whyWrong:"This judges the process only by its output. Re-opened incidents show the outcome is failing for 15% of customers."}}],lab:null,check:[{id:"chk-1.2.1-1",topic:"1.2.1",category:"definition",difficulty:"beginner",numerical:!1,stem:"Which statement BEST defines a process?",options:["A department that carries out a specialised business function","A repeatable set of activities that transforms inputs into outputs for a customer","A one-time series of tasks with a fixed budget and deadline","A list of KPIs reported to management each month"],answer:1,explanation:"A process is repeatable, transforms inputs into outputs, and serves a customer. Repetition is what lets Six Sigma measure variation.",whyWrong:["A department is an organisational unit (a function). Processes usually cross several departments.","","That describes a project, which is one-off rather than repeatable.","KPIs measure processes; they are not the process itself."],traps:["best"]},{id:"chk-1.2.1-2",topic:"1.2.1",category:"scenario",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"A Green Belt is defining an invoice processing process. Which is the MOST appropriate stop trigger?",options:["The AP clerk finishes their shift","The invoice is approved by the budget holder","The supplier payment is executed and posted in the ledger","The supplier is satisfied with the relationship"],answer:2,explanation:"A stop trigger is an observable event showing that the process has delivered its output. Payment executed and posted marks the end of invoice processing and can be timestamped.",whyWrong:["A shift ending is about staff schedules, not the process completing.","Approval is an intermediate step; payment and posting still follow.","","Satisfaction is an outcome to measure separately, not an observable stop event."],traps:["most"]},{id:"chk-1.2.1-3",topic:"1.2.1",category:"compare",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"For an incident management process, which of the following is an OUTCOME rather than an output?",options:["The incident record is closed","A resolution note is written","The user’s service is restored and the fault does not recur","The ticket is assigned to a resolver group"],answer:2,explanation:"An outcome is the effect the customer experiences. Restored service that stays fixed is the result the user actually cares about.",whyWrong:["Closing the record is an output: something the process produces.","A resolution note is a documented output.","","Assignment is an internal activity, neither the output nor the outcome."]},{id:"chk-1.2.1-4",topic:"1.2.1",category:"dmaic-boundary",difficulty:"trap",numerical:!1,stem:"According to the IASSC Green Belt syllabus, building a SIPOC and detailed process maps is classified under which phase?",options:["Define (1.2.1)","Measure (2.1.2)","Analyze (3.1)","Control (5.1)"],answer:1,explanation:'The syllabus lists "Process Mapping, SIPOC, Value Stream Map" as Measure item 2.1.2. Define 1.2.1 only requires defining the process at a high level.',whyWrong:["Many courses introduce SIPOC in Define, which makes this tempting, but the syllabus classifies it under Measure.","","Analyze focuses on identifying and verifying root causes.","Control focuses on sustaining the improvement."]}],takeaways:["A process is repeatable, transforms inputs into outputs, and serves a customer.","Name the suppliers, inputs, activities, outputs and customers; inputs include information, methods and noise.","Boundaries are observable start and stop triggers. They set scope and make cycle time measurable.","Define works at Level 1: about four to seven major steps. Detailed mapping and SIPOC are Measure 2.1.2.","Outputs are what the process delivers; outcomes are what the customer experiences. Define both.","A process is not a department. Problems hide in the hand-offs between functions.","Every process needs one named process owner accountable end to end."],flashcards:[{front:"Define “process”.",back:"A repeatable set of activities, with a flow of material and/or information, that transforms inputs into outputs for a customer."},{front:"What sets a process boundary?",back:"A start trigger (the event that begins it) and a stop trigger (the event that proves it is complete)."},{front:"Output vs outcome?",back:"Output = what the process delivers (ticket closed). Outcome = what the customer experiences (service restored and stable)."},{front:"How many steps in a Level 1 view?",back:"Roughly four to seven major activities: the management view."},{front:"Where is SIPOC classified in the syllabus?",back:"Measure 2.1.2 (Process Mapping, SIPOC, Value Stream Map). In Define it is context only."},{front:"Process vs department?",back:"A department groups people vertically; a process is the horizontal flow of work to a customer, usually crossing departments."},{front:"Who is the process owner?",back:"The one person accountable for the end-to-end performance and resources of the process, and for sustaining gains."},{front:"Three input types named in the training material?",back:"Controllable, procedural and noise inputs."},{front:"Three versions of every process?",back:"What you think it is, what it actually is, and what it should be."}],coverage:"official-only",supplementaryNotes:["The Define chapter of the training book was not supplied. This lesson is written from the syllabus topic title plus independent Green Belt knowledge, reinforced by Measure-phase pages (book pp. 99–117) that cover process definition and mapping.","The output-versus-outcome distinction and the process-versus-function comparison are standard improvement concepts, explained here independently rather than taken from a supplied page.","The fibre-order and incident examples, including all figures, are illustrative and invented for teaching."],sources:[{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 7",pdfPage:7,note:"Define topic 1.2.1 Defining a Process; SIPOC and process mapping listed under Measure 2.1.2"},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 99",pdfPage:2,note:"Process as a blending of inputs to produce a desired output; describing a process by name, map and operational description"},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 100",pdfPage:3,note:"What process mapping reveals; the three views (think / actually / should be)"},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"pp. 102–103",pdfPage:5,note:"Process map levels: Level 1 macro, Level 2, Level 3 micro"},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"pp. 105–110",pdfPage:8,note:"Level 1 steps: name, beginning and end, purpose, major steps; include a step before and after the problem area"},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 111",pdfPage:14,note:"SIPOC elements (supporting context; Measure 2.1.2)"},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"pp. 113–114",pdfPage:16,note:"Controllable, procedural and noise inputs"},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 117",pdfPage:20,note:"Value-added vs non-value-added tests"},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"pp. 587–588",pdfPage:90,note:"Glossary: Process; Process Owner"}]},I=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:p},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),u={id:"1.2.2",module:2,title:"Critical to Quality Characteristics (CTQ’s)",subtitle:"From “I want fast service” to a measurable requirement",minutes:14,objectives:["Define a CTQ as a measurable characteristic of a product or service that is critical to the customer’s perception of quality.","Translate a vague customer need into quality drivers and then into CTQs with targets and specification limits, using a CTQ tree.","Write a complete operational definition: what is measured, how, in which unit, when, and what counts as a defect.","Distinguish CTQs from KPIs and from SLAs, and explain how they relate.","Explain why vague customer statements are insufficient, and test requirements with RUMBA.","Relate CTQs to the Kano model and to Y = f(X)."],overview:`Customers rarely speak in specifications. They say "I want fast service", "the bill should be right" or "just make it work". These statements are genuine [[voc|Voice of the Customer]], and they matter, but you cannot measure, improve or verify "fast". A Six Sigma team needs something sharper: a characteristic you can observe, a number to aim for, a limit beyond which the customer is let down, and an agreed way of measuring it.

That sharper thing is a [[ctq|Critical to Quality characteristic (CTQ)]]. The training glossary describes a CTQ as any characteristic that is critical to the perceived quality of the product, process or system (book p. 585). In practice a CTQ turns a need into a requirement that every unit either meets or fails. This lesson shows you how to build CTQs with a [[ctq-tree|CTQ tree]], how to pin them down with an [[operational-definition|operational definition]], and how they differ from the [[kpi|KPIs]] and [[sla|SLAs]] you already work with.`,sections:[{id:"need-to-ctq",heading:"From need to driver to CTQ",body:`Think of a CTQ tree as a translator that works from left to right, from the customer’s language to the process’s language.

1. **Need.** The customer’s own words, broad and emotional: *"I want fast service when my line goes down."*
2. **[[quality-driver|Quality drivers]].** What "fast" actually means to this customer, found by asking, listening to calls and analysing complaints: *quick response*, *quick resolution*, *kept informed while I wait*. Drivers are more specific, but still words.
3. **CTQs.** A measurable characteristic under each driver, with a requirement: *first response time ≤ 15 minutes for 95% of P2 incidents*; *time to restore ≤ 4 hours for 95% of P2 incidents*; *a status update at least every 60 minutes until restored*.

A complete CTQ has four parts: the **characteristic** (first response time), the **measure and unit** (minutes from logging to first human response), the **target** (what we aim for, perhaps 10 minutes) and the **[[specification-limits|specification limit(s)]]** (the boundary of acceptability, here an upper limit of 15 minutes). The performance standard, "95% of the time", tells you how often the limit must be met.

Specification limits can be one-sided or two-sided. Time to respond only has an upper limit (USL); nobody complains that the engineer answered too early. Other CTQs need both: a promised callback "at 60 minutes ± 5 minutes" gives a target of 60, a lower limit (LSL) of 55 and an upper limit (USL) of 65, which mirrors the "1 hour ± 5 minutes" example in the training material (book p. 112). Anything outside the limits is a [[defect]].

The training material also summarises CTQ development in three steps: identify the customer, capture the Voice of the Customer, and validate the CTQs (book p. 509). Validation means going back to the customer and asking: if we hit these numbers, will you feel the service is fast?`},{id:"why-vague-fails",heading:"Why vague statements are not enough",body:`Imagine a team told to "make incident handling faster". The service desk shortens call scripts, the resolver group closes tickets sooner, and the vendor reports a better average. Six months later customers still complain. Why? Because nobody agreed what "fast" meant. The desk measured talk time, the resolvers measured closure time, and the customer cared about being able to work again.

Vague requirements cause four predictable failures:

- **No shared target.** Each team optimises its own interpretation.
- **No defect definition.** If you cannot say when a unit fails, you cannot count defects, so you cannot calculate DPU, DPMO or yield (lesson 1.2.5).
- **No baseline.** You cannot measure today’s performance against a word.
- **No proof of improvement.** At the end of the project, "faster" is an opinion, not a result.

The training material is blunt: outputs without requirements are unacceptable in a well-managed process, and requirements should be negotiated with the customer (book p. 112). A quick quality test is [[rumba|RUMBA]]: is the requirement **R**easonable, **U**nderstandable, **M**easurable, **B**elievable and **A**chievable? "Instant resolution of every fault" fails on Reasonable and Achievable. "Great service" fails on Understandable and Measurable.`},{id:"operational-definitions",heading:"Operational definitions: making a CTQ unarguable",body:`Two analysts given the same month of incident data should calculate exactly the same first response time. If they do not, the CTQ is not finished. An **operational definition** removes the ambiguity by stating:

| Element | First response time (P2) |
|---|---|
| **What** is measured | Time from incident logged as P2 to the first human response to the affected user |
| **How** it is measured | ITSM timestamps: "created" to first public work note or outbound call |
| **Unit** | Minutes, to one decimal place |
| **When / how often** | Every P2 incident, 24×7, reported weekly |
| **Defect definition** | Any P2 incident with first response greater than 15.0 minutes |

Notice the hidden decisions. Does an automated acknowledgement e-mail count as a "response"? (Here, no, only a human response.) Does the clock pause outside business hours? (No, P2 is 24×7.) What if the ticket is re-prioritised from P3 to P2? (You would add a rule, for example the clock starts at the time of re-prioritisation.) These are exactly the details that vendors and internal teams argue about at month end, and exactly what an operational definition settles in advance.

The book also notes that an operational definition of the process itself explains why it exists and what it delivers (book p. 112). The same mindset applies to every CTQ: say precisely what you mean before you measure anything.`},{id:"ctq-kpi-sla",heading:"CTQ vs KPI vs SLA",body:`In service operations these three terms are often used interchangeably. They are related but not the same.

| | CTQ | KPI | SLA |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Whose view?** | The customer’s: what matters to their perception of quality | The organisation’s: how well we are performing against goals | A contract: what the provider formally promises |
| **Purpose** | Define what "good" means for a unit of output | Monitor and manage performance over time | Set enforceable obligations, often with penalties or credits |
| **Typical form** | Characteristic + target + spec limit (≤ 15 min) | A rate or average (% P2 responded within 15 min this month) | Commitment + threshold + remedy (95% within 15 min, else service credit) |
| **Scope** | Derived from VOC | May reflect VOC, VOB or internal efficiency | Negotiated between provider and customer |
| **Example** | First response ≤ 15 min per P2 incident | P2 first response compliance = 93% | Vendor must achieve 95% monthly or pay a 2% credit |

The healthy pattern is: **VOC → CTQ → KPI → (sometimes) SLA**. The CTQ defines quality for each incident; the KPI aggregates CTQ performance across many incidents; the SLA turns that KPI into a contractual promise. Problems start when an SLA is written first and never checked against what customers actually need. A vendor can be 100% SLA-compliant on a metric customers do not care about, while failing a CTQ that drives complaints.`},{id:"kano-yfx",heading:"Where CTQs connect: Kano and Y = f(X)",body:`The [[kano-model|Kano model]] helps decide which needs deserve CTQs. **Basic (must-be)** needs, such as the line working and the bill being correct, cause great dissatisfaction when absent but little delight when present. **Performance** needs, such as restoration speed, increase satisfaction the better you perform. **Delighters**, such as a proactive text before the customer notices a fault, surprise and please. Most CTQs in a Green Belt project protect basic and performance needs, because that is where defects hurt.

CTQs also sit on the left-hand side of [[y-equals-f-x|Y = f(X)]]. The CTQ is a key output Y; the process inputs and steps are the X's. The training material describes a critical X as an input that exerts undue influence on the important outputs, the CTQs, of a process (book p. 208). So once a CTQ is defined in Define, Measure and Analyze go looking for the few X's that drive it, for example ticket mis-routing or missing configuration data driving slow first response.`,advanced:!0}],whyItMatters:"CTQs are the bridge between what the customer says and what the project measures. They define the defect for your DPU and DPMO calculations, set the baseline and goal in your project charter, and give you an objective way to prove the improvement at the end. For anyone governing vendor SLAs, they are also a powerful check: does each contractual metric actually trace back to something the customer cares about?",businessExample:{title:"Enterprise customers: green SLA, red complaints",body:`A telecom operator’s managed-services vendor reported 97% compliance with the contractual "P2 incident acknowledged within 15 minutes" SLA for six straight months. Yet enterprise account managers were escalating complaints about "slow support".

A Green Belt built a CTQ tree from twenty customer interviews. The need was "fast support when our sites go down". Drivers: *someone real responds quickly*, *the fault is fixed quickly*, *we know what is happening*. Two discoveries followed. First, "acknowledged" in the SLA included an automated e-mail, so the 97% said nothing about a human response. Second, customers cared most about time to restore and regular updates, neither of which was in the SLA.

The team wrote operational definitions for three CTQs: human first response ≤ 15 minutes, restoration ≤ 4 hours, and updates at least every 60 minutes, each for 95% of P2 incidents. Measured against these, the baseline was 81%, 74% and 52%. The project then had a meaningful goal, and the operator had evidence to request clarification and supporting data from the vendor on how acknowledgement was being counted.`},visual:"ctqTree",visualCaption:"Watch a CTQ tree grow from the vague need “I want fast service” into three quality drivers and six measurable CTQs with targets and limits, then see one CTQ given a full operational definition.",definitions:[{term:"CTQ (Critical to Quality characteristic)",definition:"A measurable characteristic of a product, service or process that is critical to the customer’s perception of quality, stated with a target and specification limit(s)."},{term:"Customer need",definition:"A broad statement of what the customer wants, in their own words, usually too vague to measure."},{term:"Quality driver",definition:"A more specific aspect of a need that drives the customer’s perception of quality, such as speed of response or accuracy of the bill."},{term:"CTQ tree",definition:"A tree diagram that breaks a customer need into quality drivers and then into measurable CTQs with requirements."},{term:"Operational definition",definition:"A precise statement of what is measured, how, in which unit, when, and what counts as a defect, so that anyone measuring gets the same result."},{term:"Specification limits (LSL / USL)",definition:"The lower and upper boundaries of acceptable performance for a CTQ, set from customer requirements. Outside the limits is a defect."},{term:"Target",definition:"The ideal value of a CTQ the process aims for, usually inside the specification limits."},{term:"RUMBA",definition:"A test for valid customer requirements: Reasonable, Understandable, Measurable, Believable, Achievable."},{term:"Defect",definition:"Any instance of a CTQ falling outside its specification, requirement or customer expectation."}],comparisons:[{title:"CTQ vs KPI vs SLA",headers:["","CTQ","KPI","SLA"],rows:[["Point of view","Customer: what defines quality","Organisation: how we perform","Contract: what is promised"],["Level","Each unit (each incident, order, invoice)","Aggregated over a period","Aggregated, with remedies"],["Example","First response ≤ 15 min per P2","93% of P2s met 15 min in May","95% monthly, else 2% service credit"],["Main use","Define defects, set project Y","Monitor and manage","Govern a provider relationship"],["Risk","Not validated with the customer","Measures what is easy, not what matters","Met on paper while customers suffer"]]},{title:"Vague statement vs CTQ specification",headers:["Customer says","Quality driver","CTQ with specification"],rows:[["“I want fast service.”","Quick first response","Human first response ≤ 15 min for 95% of P2 incidents"],["“My bill should be right.”","Billing accuracy","Invoices with zero billing errors ≥ 99.5% per cycle"],["“Install it when you said you would.”","Kept appointment","Technician arrives inside the booked 2-hour window for 98% of jobs"],["“Pay us on time.” (supplier)","Payment timeliness","Invoice paid ≤ 30 days from receipt for 97% of invoices"]]}],misconceptions:[{myth:"A CTQ is anything the business thinks is important.",reality:"A CTQ is critical to the customer’s perception of quality and is derived from VOC. Business priorities (VOB) matter, but they are not automatically CTQs."},{myth:"“Fast service” is a CTQ.",reality:"It is a need. A CTQ is measurable, with a unit, target and specification limit, such as first response ≤ 15 minutes."},{myth:"CTQ, KPI and SLA are the same thing.",reality:"A CTQ defines quality per unit; a KPI aggregates performance; an SLA is a contractual commitment. An SLA can be met while CTQs fail."},{myth:"Once the metric name is agreed, the CTQ is defined.",reality:"Without an operational definition (how, unit, when, defect rule), two people will measure the same CTQ differently."},{myth:"Every CTQ needs both an upper and a lower limit.",reality:"Many service CTQs are one-sided: response time only has an upper limit. Two-sided limits apply when both too low and too high are bad."}],examTraps:[{trap:"A question lists “I want reliable service” among options and asks which is a CTQ.",explanation:"A CTQ must be measurable with a requirement. Choose the option with a characteristic, unit and limit, not the customer’s words.",tag:"not"},{trap:"Asked what to do FIRST when developing CTQs, an option says “set the specification limits”.",explanation:"The sequence is identify the customer, capture VOC, then translate and validate CTQs. Limits come after you understand the need.",tag:"first"},{trap:"A stem describes an SLA the vendor meets every month and asks whether customer CTQs are therefore met.",explanation:"Not necessarily. SLAs may measure something different from what customers value (e.g. automated acknowledgement vs human response).",tag:"not"},{trap:"An option claims an operational definition is the same as the CTQ name.",explanation:"The operational definition adds how it is measured, unit, timing and the defect rule. Exam answers including the defect rule are usually the complete one.",tag:"best"}],workedExamples:[{title:"Translating “I want fast service” into a CTQ",problem:"A small-business broadband customer says: “When my line goes down I want fast service.” Build one branch of a CTQ tree down to a complete CTQ.",steps:["**Need:** fast service when the line is down (VOC, verbatim).","**Probe what “fast” means:** interviews show the customer is mostly anxious about whether anyone has picked the fault up. Quality driver: *quick first response*.","**Characteristic:** first response time for P2 incidents (small-business outage is P2 in this operator’s priority matrix).","**Measure & unit:** minutes from P2 record created to first human contact with the customer.","**Target & limit:** target 10 min, USL 15 min (one-sided; no lower limit).","**Performance standard:** 95% of P2 incidents must meet the USL.","**RUMBA check:** reasonable and achievable given staffing; understandable; measurable from ITSM timestamps; believable to customers. Validate with three customers before finalising."],answer:"CTQ: human first response ≤ 15 min (target 10 min) for 95% of P2 incidents, measured from ITSM “created” timestamp.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"CTQ: “improve response speed by 20%”.",whyWrong:"That is an improvement goal, not a CTQ. It has no unit, no limit for a single incident and no defect definition, and says nothing about what the customer requires."}},{title:"Is the CTQ being met? Counting defects",problem:"In one month there were **400** P2 incidents. **372** received a human first response within 15.0 minutes. The CTQ requires 95% compliance. Find the compliance rate, the number of defects and the DPU.",steps:["Compliance = $372 / 400 = 0.93$, so **93%**.","Defects = incidents outside the specification = $400 - 372 = 28$.","Each incident has one opportunity for this CTQ, so $\\text{DPU} = \\dfrac{28}{400} = 0.07$ (calculated with `dpu(28, 400)`).","Compare with the standard: 93% < 95%, so the CTQ is **not met**. To reach 95% the process may have at most $400 \\times 0.05 = 20$ defects, so the gap this month is 8 incidents."],answer:"93% compliance, 28 defects, DPU = 0.07. The CTQ is not met; the gap is 8 incidents.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Average first response was 9 minutes, below the 15-minute limit, so the CTQ is met.",whyWrong:"A CTQ is judged per unit against its limit. A good average can hide many individual incidents beyond 15 minutes; 28 customers were let down."}},{title:"Two-sided specification limits",problem:"Customers who request a callback are promised one “in about an hour”. Interviews show a callback earlier than 55 minutes is missed (customer not ready) and later than 65 minutes is too late. Express the CTQ with target and limits.",steps:["Target = 60 minutes (the promise).","LSL = $60 - 5 = 55$ min; USL = $60 + 5 = 65$ min. This mirrors the “1 hour ± 5 minutes” illustration in the training material.","Defect rule: any callback earlier than 55.0 or later than 65.0 minutes after the request."],answer:"Callback time: target 60 min, LSL 55 min, USL 65 min; outside either limit is a defect."}],lab:"ctq",check:[{id:"chk-1.2.2-1",topic:"1.2.2",category:"ctq",difficulty:"beginner",numerical:!1,stem:"Which of the following is the BEST example of a CTQ?",options:["“Customers want fast service.”","Human first response ≤ 15 minutes for 95% of P2 incidents","Improve customer satisfaction next year","The service desk team is friendly"],answer:1,explanation:"A CTQ is a measurable characteristic with a requirement. This option has a characteristic, unit, limit and performance standard.",whyWrong:["This is a customer need in the customer’s words: not measurable.","","This is a vague goal with no characteristic or limit.","This is a perception that has not been translated into a measurable characteristic."],traps:["best"]},{id:"chk-1.2.2-2",topic:"1.2.2",category:"concept",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"In a CTQ tree, what sits between the customer need and the CTQ?",options:["The specification limit","The quality driver","The root cause","The SLA penalty"],answer:1,explanation:"A CTQ tree flows need → quality drivers → CTQs. Drivers describe what the need means to the customer in more specific terms.",whyWrong:["The specification limit is part of the CTQ itself, at the end of the branch.","","Root causes are found in Analyze; they are X’s, not part of the CTQ tree.","Penalties belong to contracts, not to the CTQ tree."]},{id:"chk-1.2.2-3",topic:"1.2.2",category:"compare",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"A vendor meets its contractual “P2 acknowledged within 15 minutes” target every month, but customers still complain about slow support. What is the MOST likely explanation?",options:["The customers are measuring incorrectly","The SLA may not reflect the CTQs customers actually care about","SLAs and CTQs are identical, so the complaints are unfounded","The vendor should increase the SLA threshold to 20 minutes"],answer:1,explanation:"An SLA is a contractual commitment, while CTQs come from VOC. If the SLA measures something different (e.g. automated acknowledgement), it can be met while customer CTQs such as restoration time fail.",whyWrong:["Complaints are VOC; dismissing them ignores the customer.","","SLAs and CTQs are related but not identical.","Relaxing the threshold moves further from what customers want."],traps:["most"]},{id:"chk-1.2.2-4",topic:"1.2.2",category:"definition",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"Which element is NOT normally part of an operational definition for a CTQ?",options:["How the characteristic is measured","The unit of measure","What counts as a defect","The name of the team member who will fix defects"],answer:3,explanation:"An operational definition states what is measured, how, the unit, when, and the defect rule. Assigning people to fix defects is project or process management, not definition.",whyWrong:["The measurement method is essential so everyone gets the same result.","The unit removes ambiguity (minutes vs hours).","The defect rule is what allows defects to be counted.",""],traps:["not"]},{id:"chk-1.2.2-5",topic:"1.2.2",category:"calculation",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!0,stem:"A CTQ requires invoices to be paid within 30 days of receipt. Of **500** invoices, **465** were paid within 30 days. What percentage met the CTQ, and how many defects were there?",options:["93% met; 35 defects","7% met; 465 defects","93% met; 465 defects","97% met; 15 defects"],answer:0,explanation:"465 ÷ 500 = 0.93 = 93%. Defects are the invoices outside the requirement: 500 − 465 = 35.",whyWrong:["","7% is the defect rate, not the compliance rate, and 465 is the number of good invoices.","465 is the number that met the CTQ, not the number of defects.","These figures do not follow from the data."]}],takeaways:["A CTQ is a measurable characteristic critical to the customer’s perception of quality.","A CTQ tree translates need → quality drivers → CTQs with targets and specification limits.","Vague statements cannot be measured, so you cannot define defects, baselines or improvements.","An operational definition states what, how, unit, when and the defect rule.","Use RUMBA to test requirements: Reasonable, Understandable, Measurable, Believable, Achievable.","CTQ (per unit, customer view) → KPI (aggregated, organisation view) → SLA (contractual promise).","Spec limits may be one-sided (USL only for response time) or two-sided (target ± tolerance).","CTQs are the key Y’s in Y = f(X); later phases search for the critical X’s that drive them."],flashcards:[{front:"What is a CTQ?",back:"A measurable characteristic critical to the customer’s perception of quality, with a target and specification limit(s)."},{front:"Order of a CTQ tree?",back:"Need → quality drivers → CTQs (with measures, targets and specification limits)."},{front:"Five elements of an operational definition?",back:"What is measured, how it is measured, unit, when/how often, and the defect definition."},{front:"RUMBA stands for?",back:"Reasonable, Understandable, Measurable, Believable, Achievable."},{front:"CTQ vs KPI?",back:"CTQ defines quality per unit from the customer’s view; a KPI aggregates performance over time from the organisation’s view."},{front:"CTQ vs SLA?",back:"An SLA is a contractual commitment with remedies. It should trace to CTQs, but can be met while CTQs fail."},{front:"Target 60 min ± 5 min gives which limits?",back:"LSL = 55 min, USL = 65 min."},{front:"Three steps to develop CTQs (training material)?",back:"Identify the customer, capture the VOC, validate the CTQs."},{front:"Why is “I want fast service” not a CTQ?",back:"It has no characteristic, unit, limit or defect rule, so it cannot be measured or verified."}],coverage:"official-only",supplementaryNotes:["The Define chapter of the training book was not supplied. This lesson is built on the syllabus topic, independent Green Belt knowledge, and supporting pages: VOC/RUMBA/targets (book p. 112), CTQ steps (p. 509), and the CTQ and RUMBA glossary entries (pp. 585, 588).","The CTQ vs KPI vs SLA comparison and the Kano links are standard practice explained independently; they are not taken from a supplied page.","All incident, invoice and vendor figures are illustrative."],sources:[{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 7",pdfPage:7,note:"Define topic 1.2.2 Critical to Quality Characteristics (CTQ’s)"},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 112",pdfPage:15,note:"Customer requirements (VOC), RUMBA, target 1 hour ± 5 min with LSL/USL, ranking outputs to identify CTQs"},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 208",pdfPage:11,note:"Critical X influences the important outputs (CTQs); VOC vs VOP"},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 509",pdfPage:12,note:"Steps in developing CTQs: identify customer, capture VOC, validate CTQs"},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 585",pdfPage:88,note:"Glossary: CTQ; Defect"},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 588",pdfPage:91,note:"Glossary: R.U.M.B.A."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 590",pdfPage:93,note:"Glossary: VOC"}]},W=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:u},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),m={id:"1.2.3",module:2,title:"Cost of Poor Quality (COPQ)",subtitle:"Prevention, appraisal, internal and external failure — and the hidden factory",minutes:14,objectives:["Define the Cost of Quality (COQ) and the Cost of Poor Quality (COPQ), and explain the difference.","Classify costs into prevention, appraisal, internal failure and external failure, using service and manufacturing examples.","Explain hidden costs and the hidden factory, and why the visible costs are only the tip of the iceberg.","Calculate COPQ and total COQ from a cost breakdown, stating which convention is being used.","Explain why spending on prevention usually reduces total cost of quality.","Describe how estimating COPQ supports project selection and the business case in Define."],overview:`Every time something is not done right the first time, someone pays. A mis-provisioned fibre order has to be fixed; a technician has to go back to the same house; an enterprise customer receives an SLA penalty credit; an agent spends twenty minutes handling a complaint. These are not "the cost of doing business". They are the [[copq|Cost of Poor Quality (COPQ)]]: money spent because the process failed.

The training glossary captures it neatly: COPQ is the cost of any activity not "doing the right thing right the first time", the financial quantification of waste that is not integral to the product or service (book p. 584). The DMAIC roadmap in the training material lists **"Estimate COPQ"** as a Define activity (book pp. 230, 237), because the size of the COPQ is often what convinces a sponsor that a project is worth doing. In this lesson you will learn the four cost categories, the two common ways of adding them up, and why the biggest costs are usually the ones nobody sees.`,sections:[{id:"coq-vs-copq",heading:"Cost of Quality vs Cost of Poor Quality",body:`Two related totals cause a lot of exam confusion, so let us fix them first.

- **[[coq|Cost of Quality (COQ)]]** is the *total* cost associated with quality: what you spend to achieve good quality **plus** what you lose through poor quality. It has four categories: **prevention (P)**, **appraisal (A)**, **internal failure (IF)** and **external failure (EF)**.
- **Cost of Poor Quality (COPQ)** is the part caused by *failure*. The most common convention in Green Belt material is:

$$\\text{COPQ} = \\text{Internal failure} + \\text{External failure}$$

$$\\text{COQ} = \\text{Prevention} + \\text{Appraisal} + \\text{Internal failure} + \\text{External failure}$$

**Be aware that sources vary.** Some authors include appraisal in COPQ, reasoning that inspection exists only because the process cannot be trusted; in a perfect process you would not need it. A few use COPQ loosely to mean the whole COQ. Prevention and appraisal are often called the **cost of good quality** (or cost of conformance), and the failure costs the **cost of non-conformance**. In an exam, read the options carefully: if a question asks for the cost of poor quality and offers only failure categories versus "all four categories", the failure-only answer reflects the common convention. In your own business case, always state which convention you used.`},{id:"four-categories",heading:"The four categories, with service examples",body:`A handy way to remember the categories is the timeline of a defect: **prevent it**, **detect it**, **fix it before the customer sees it**, or **suffer it after the customer sees it**.

| Category | What it is | Telecom / service examples | Manufacturing examples |
|---|---|---|---|
| **[[prevention-cost|Prevention]] (P)** | Spending to stop defects happening at all | Training provisioning staff, error-proofing order forms, better coverage data, design reviews of a new product launch | Process design, supplier qualification, preventive maintenance |
| **[[appraisal-cost|Appraisal]] (A)** | Spending to find defects by checking | QA audits of closed tickets, test calls after activation, invoice checks before payment, monitoring tools | Incoming inspection, final testing, calibration |
| **[[internal-failure-cost|Internal failure]] (IF)** | Failures found *before* reaching the customer | [[rework|Rework]] of mis-provisioned orders caught before activation, re-keying invoices, overtime to clear backlogs, [[scrap|scrapped]] faulty routers | Scrap, rework, re-inspection, downtime |
| **[[external-failure-cost|External failure]] (EF)** | Failures found by, or affecting, the customer | SLA penalty credits, repeat truck rolls, complaint handling, re-opened incidents, warranty replacement of CPE, refunds, churn | Warranty claims, returns, recalls, field repairs, lost sales |

The key test for internal versus external is **who found it, or whom it affected**. The same mistake, a wrong speed profile on a fibre line, is an internal failure if a pre-activation check catches it, and an external failure if the customer discovers it and calls in. External failures are almost always more expensive, because they add customer-facing handling, penalties and the risk of losing the customer.`},{id:"hidden-factory",heading:"Hidden costs and the hidden factory",body:`Picture an iceberg. Above the waterline are the costs with their own line in the accounts: scrap write-offs, warranty claims, SLA credits. Finance can show you these. Below the waterline is a much larger mass that is rarely costed at all.

The training glossary defines the **[[hidden-factory|hidden factory]]** as corrective, non-value-added work that is not recognised as waste (book p. 586). In a telecom operator the hidden factory includes:

- engineers revisiting jobs that should have been right the first time;
- back-office staff correcting order data that Sales captured incorrectly;
- service desk agents chasing other teams for updates ("expediting");
- duplicate tickets, re-opened incidents and manual workarounds;
- managers in daily escalation calls instead of improving the process.

None of this has an invoice. It is paid for through normal salaries and overtime, so it looks like ordinary work. The book also warns that the actual process is almost always bigger and more costly than people think, full of "hidden operations" (book p. 100).

Deeper still are costs that are real but hard to measure: customers who quietly leave (churn), lost future sales and referrals, damage to reputation, and staff burnout. You will usually have to *estimate* these, for example churn attributable to repeated faults multiplied by average customer lifetime value. That is acceptable in Define, provided you state the assumptions and agree the method with your finance representative.`},{id:"prevention-pays",heading:"Why prevention reduces total cost",body:`There is a well-known rule of thumb, sometimes called the 1–10–100 rule: a defect that costs 1 to prevent might cost 10 to fix internally and 100 once it reaches the customer. The exact ratios are illustrative rather than universal, but the direction is reliable. Moving effort *upstream* is cheaper.

Consider repeat truck rolls. Each one costs a technician’s time, a vehicle, a second appointment slot and a frustrated customer who may call twice and post a review. Improving the feasibility check before installation (prevention) or adding a five-minute remote line test before the technician leaves (appraisal) costs far less than the failures they avoid.

This creates a typical pattern when a quality programme matures: **prevention spending rises a little, appraisal can then fall, and failure costs fall a lot**, so total COQ goes down. The opposite strategy, adding more inspectors at the end, often raises appraisal cost without removing the cause; defects are found more often, but they are still made. Six Sigma’s whole philosophy, fixing the X’s in [[y-equals-f-x|Y = f(X)]], is essentially a prevention strategy.`},{id:"copq-in-define",heading:"Using COPQ in Define",body:`In Define you do not need an audited figure; you need a credible *estimate* that answers "is this problem worth solving?". A practical approach:

1. **List the failure symptoms** in the process you defined (lesson 1.2.1): rework, repeat visits, credits, complaints.
2. **Count the frequency** from existing data: how many repeat truck rolls per month?
3. **Cost each occurrence** with your finance partner: labour, materials, penalties, handling time.
4. **Multiply and annualise**, then add carefully estimated hidden costs, clearly labelled as estimates.
5. **Feed the result into the [[business-case|business case]]** and [[project-charter|project charter]] (Module 3), where it helps prioritise projects and later becomes the basis for [[hard-savings|hard savings]] or [[cost-avoidance|cost avoidance]].

The Measure-phase checklist in the training material even asks "Have you identified more COPQ?" (book p. 231), a reminder that your Define estimate is a starting point that you refine as you learn more about the process.`}],whyItMatters:'COPQ translates quality problems into the language of management: money. A sponsor may shrug at "a 9% repeat-visit rate", but will pay attention to "about OMR 45,000 a year in avoidable truck rolls, before counting churn". Estimating COPQ helps choose the right projects, justifies resources, and sets up the financial benefits you will later need to verify. It also changes behaviour: once people see the hidden factory, they stop treating rework as normal work.',businessExample:{title:"Mis-provisioned orders in a fibre roll-out",body:`During a fibre roll-out, about one order in twelve was provisioned with the wrong speed profile or VLAN. Finance saw only one cost: service credits paid to business customers whose lines under-performed, about OMR 4,000 a month. That figure alone did not justify a project.

A Green Belt traced the full failure path. Some errors were caught by a pre-activation check and re-worked by the back office (internal failure: staff hours and delayed activation). Others reached customers, generating fault calls, a remote diagnosis, often a repeat truck roll and sometimes a credit (external failure). By costing the handling time, repeat visits and credits with the finance representative, the team estimated a COPQ of roughly OMR 21,000 a month, more than five times the visible figure. That excluded churn, which they listed separately as an estimate.

Most errors began with a free-text field on the order form. Replacing it with a validated drop-down (a prevention cost of a few days’ development) removed most mis-provisioning at source. The visible tip had suggested a small problem; the iceberg showed a large one.`},visual:"copqIceberg",visualCaption:"Reveal the COPQ iceberg layer by layer: visible failure costs, the hidden factory, the deep costs of churn and reputation, then classify every cost and compare COPQ with the full Cost of Quality.",definitions:[{term:"Cost of Quality (COQ)",definition:"The total of prevention, appraisal, internal failure and external failure costs: the cost of achieving quality plus the cost of failing to achieve it."},{term:"Cost of Poor Quality (COPQ)",definition:"The cost of not doing the right thing right the first time. Commonly calculated as internal plus external failure costs; some sources also include appraisal."},{term:"Prevention cost",definition:"Spending to stop defects from occurring, such as training, error-proofing, process design and supplier qualification."},{term:"Appraisal cost",definition:"Spending to detect defects by measuring, inspecting, testing or auditing."},{term:"Internal failure cost",definition:"Cost of defects found before the output reaches the customer: rework, scrap, re-inspection, delays and overtime."},{term:"External failure cost",definition:"Cost of defects found by or affecting the customer: warranty, returns, penalty credits, complaint handling, repeat visits and lost customers."},{term:"Hidden factory",definition:"Corrective, non-value-added work that is not recognised as waste because it is absorbed into normal activity."},{term:"Rework",definition:"Work done again to correct a defect so the output can meet requirements."},{term:"Scrap",definition:"Output that cannot be corrected and must be discarded, such as a damaged router that cannot be refurbished."}],comparisons:[{title:"Cost of good quality vs cost of poor quality",headers:["","Prevention + Appraisal","Internal + External failure"],rows:[["Other names","Cost of good quality, cost of conformance","Cost of poor quality (common convention), cost of non-conformance"],["Nature","Investment, chosen deliberately","Loss, caused by the process failing"],["When it happens","Before or during the work","After a defect is made"],["Typical trend after improvement","Prevention rises slightly; appraisal can fall","Falls substantially"],["Telecom example","Order-form validation; test call after activation","Re-provisioning; repeat truck roll; SLA credit"]]},{title:"Internal vs external failure",headers:["","Internal failure","External failure"],rows:[["Found by","The organisation, before delivery","The customer, or affects the customer"],["Incident management","QA review catches a wrong resolution before closure","Incident re-opened by the user; SLA credit paid"],["Invoice processing","Duplicate invoice caught and voided before payment","Duplicate payment made and recovered from supplier"],["Relative cost","Lower","Usually much higher, plus reputation risk"]]}],misconceptions:[{myth:"COPQ is just scrap and warranty costs.",reality:"Those are the visible tip. Most COPQ is hidden: rework, repeat visits, chasing, overtime, complaint handling and lost customers."},{myth:"Appraisal and prevention are always part of COPQ.",reality:"Under the common convention COPQ is internal + external failure. Some sources add appraisal; full COQ includes all four. State your convention."},{myth:"More inspection is the best way to reduce COPQ.",reality:"Inspection (appraisal) finds defects but does not stop them being made. Prevention that removes causes reduces total cost far more."},{myth:"Whether a failure is internal or external depends on which department caused it.",reality:"It depends on whether the defect was found before reaching the customer (internal) or by/affecting the customer (external)."},{myth:"Hidden costs cannot be included because they cannot be measured exactly.",reality:"They can be estimated with stated assumptions, agreed with finance, and labelled as estimates in the business case."}],examTraps:[{trap:"A question asks which category a cost belongs to, e.g. “customer complaint handling”, and offers internal failure.",explanation:"Complaints come from the customer, so this is external failure. Decide by who found the defect, not where it was fixed.",tag:"not"},{trap:"“Which of the following is NOT a cost of poor quality?” with training listed among options.",explanation:"Training is a prevention cost, part of the cost of good quality, so under the common convention it is not COPQ.",tag:"not"},{trap:"A calculation stem gives P, A, IF and EF values and asks for COPQ.",explanation:"Under the common convention add only IF + EF. Summing all four gives total COQ, a classic tempting wrong answer."},{trap:"Asked the BEST long-term way to reduce total cost of quality.",explanation:"Invest in prevention (removing causes). Adding final inspection raises appraisal and leaves the defects being made.",tag:"best"},{trap:"Asked when COPQ is FIRST estimated in DMAIC.",explanation:"In Define: the training roadmap lists “Estimate COPQ” as a Define step. It is refined in Measure and benefits are verified in Control.",tag:"first"}],workedExamples:[{title:"Calculating COPQ and total COQ for a fulfilment operation",problem:`A fixed-broadband fulfilment unit records these monthly costs (OMR):

| Item | OMR |
|---|---|
| Provisioning staff training | 12,000 |
| Order-form error-proofing | 8,000 |
| QA audits of activated lines | 18,000 |
| Post-activation test calls | 12,000 |
| Re-provisioning orders caught before activation | 50,000 |
| Scrapped faulty routers | 15,000 |
| Overtime to clear order backlog | 20,000 |
| SLA penalty credits | 60,000 |
| Repeat truck rolls | 45,000 |
| Complaint handling | 25,000 |
| Warranty replacement of routers at customer sites | 35,000 |

Classify each cost, then calculate COPQ (IF + EF) and total COQ.`,steps:["**Prevention:** training 12,000 + error-proofing 8,000 = **20,000**.","**Appraisal:** QA audits 18,000 + test calls 12,000 = **30,000**.","**Internal failure:** re-provisioning 50,000 + scrap 15,000 + overtime 20,000 = **85,000**.","**External failure:** SLA credits 60,000 + repeat truck rolls 45,000 + complaints 25,000 + warranty 35,000 = **165,000**.","$\\text{COPQ} = 85{,}000 + 165{,}000 = 250{,}000$ (calculated with `copq(85000, 165000)`).","$\\text{COQ} = 20{,}000 + 30{,}000 + 85{,}000 + 165{,}000 = 300{,}000$ (calculated with `coq(20000, 30000, 85000, 165000)`).","Failure costs are $250{,}000 / 300{,}000 \\approx 83\\%$ of total COQ, a sign that the operation is spending too little upstream."],answer:"COPQ = OMR 250,000 per month; total COQ = OMR 300,000 per month.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"COPQ = 20,000 + 30,000 + 85,000 + 165,000 = 300,000",whyWrong:"That is total Cost of Quality. Under the common convention COPQ includes only failure costs (internal + external). If your organisation includes appraisal, COPQ would be 280,000; state the convention."}},{title:"Does extra prevention pay?",problem:"Using the figures above, the team proposes spending an extra **OMR 15,000/month** on prevention (automated coverage and profile validation). Expected effects: re-provisioning falls from 50,000 to 30,000, and repeat truck rolls fall from 45,000 to 18,000. All other costs stay the same. What happens to COPQ and total COQ?",steps:["New prevention = $20{,}000 + 15{,}000 = 35{,}000$.","New internal failure = $85{,}000 - (50{,}000 - 30{,}000) = 65{,}000$.","New external failure = $165{,}000 - (45{,}000 - 18{,}000) = 138{,}000$.","New COPQ = $65{,}000 + 138{,}000 = 203{,}000$ (down 47,000).","New COQ = $35{,}000 + 30{,}000 + 65{,}000 + 138{,}000 = 268{,}000$.","Net change in total COQ = $300{,}000 - 268{,}000 = 32{,}000$ per month saved, after paying for the extra prevention."],answer:"COPQ falls to OMR 203,000; total COQ falls to OMR 268,000, a net saving of OMR 32,000 per month.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Saving = 47,000 per month (the fall in COPQ).",whyWrong:"The fall in failure costs ignores the extra 15,000 spent on prevention. The net effect on total cost is 47,000 − 15,000 = 32,000."}},{title:"Estimating a single COPQ line for the business case",problem:"A regional field team completes **1,200** installations a month. **9%** need a repeat visit. Finance estimates each repeat truck roll costs **OMR 35** (technician time, vehicle, slot). Estimate the annual COPQ from repeat visits.",steps:["Repeat visits per month = $1{,}200 \\times 0.09 = 108$.","Monthly cost = $108 \\times 35 = 3{,}780$ OMR.","Annual cost = $3{,}780 \\times 12 = 45{,}360$ OMR.","Label this as the *visible* external failure cost of repeat visits. Extra handling calls and churn risk would be added separately as stated estimates."],answer:"About OMR 45,360 per year, before hidden costs such as extra calls and churn."}],lab:"copq",check:[{id:"chk-1.2.3-1",topic:"1.2.3",category:"concept",difficulty:"beginner",numerical:!1,stem:"An SLA penalty credit paid to an enterprise customer after a missed restoration target is which type of cost?",options:["Prevention cost","Appraisal cost","Internal failure cost","External failure cost"],answer:3,explanation:"The failure reached and affected the customer, and the credit is paid to them. That is an external failure cost.",whyWrong:["Prevention costs are spent to stop defects happening, e.g. training.","Appraisal costs are spent checking for defects, e.g. audits.","Internal failures are caught before the customer is affected.",""]},{id:"chk-1.2.3-2",topic:"1.2.3",category:"incorrect-statement",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"Under the most common convention, which of the following is NOT part of the Cost of Poor Quality?",options:["Rework of mis-provisioned orders before activation","Training provisioning staff on the new order system","Repeat truck rolls to fix failed installations","Handling customer complaints about billing errors"],answer:1,explanation:"Training is a prevention cost, part of the cost of good quality. COPQ is commonly internal + external failure costs.",whyWrong:["Rework caught before the customer is affected is internal failure, part of COPQ.","","Repeat visits are external failure costs, part of COPQ.","Complaint handling is an external failure cost, part of COPQ."],traps:["not"]},{id:"chk-1.2.3-3",topic:"1.2.3",category:"calculation",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!0,stem:"A service operation reports monthly: prevention OMR 10,000; appraisal OMR 25,000; internal failure OMR 40,000; external failure OMR 75,000. Using the convention COPQ = internal + external failure, what is the COPQ?",options:["OMR 115,000","OMR 150,000","OMR 140,000","OMR 35,000"],answer:0,explanation:"COPQ = 40,000 + 75,000 = 115,000. Total COQ would be 150,000.",whyWrong:["","150,000 is total COQ (all four categories), not COPQ.","140,000 adds appraisal to failure costs, a different convention from the one stated.","35,000 is prevention + appraisal, the cost of good quality."]},{id:"chk-1.2.3-4",topic:"1.2.3",category:"definition",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"What does the term “hidden factory” describe?",options:["A secret production site that is not shown in the accounts","Corrective, non-value-added work not recognised as waste","Outsourced work performed by a vendor","Preventive maintenance performed outside working hours"],answer:1,explanation:"The hidden factory is the rework, chasing, re-checking and workarounds that absorb capacity but are treated as normal work, so they are rarely costed.",whyWrong:["The term is a metaphor, not a physical site.","","Outsourcing is a sourcing decision; outsourced work may or may not be waste.","Preventive maintenance is a prevention activity, not hidden corrective work."]},{id:"chk-1.2.3-5",topic:"1.2.3",category:"best-tool",difficulty:"advanced",numerical:!1,stem:"A team wants to reduce total Cost of Quality over the long term. Which action is BEST?",options:["Add a final inspection step before every activation","Increase SLA credits so customers are compensated faster","Remove the root cause of order errors with a validated order form","Stop recording complaint handling time to lower reported costs"],answer:2,explanation:"Prevention that removes the cause stops defects being made, which reduces both internal and external failure costs and usually lowers total COQ.",whyWrong:["More inspection raises appraisal cost and finds defects, but they are still being made.","Larger credits increase external failure cost.","","Hiding a cost does not reduce it; it grows the hidden factory."],traps:["best"]}],takeaways:["COPQ is the cost of not doing things right the first time.","Four categories: prevention, appraisal, internal failure, external failure.","Common convention: COPQ = IF + EF; full COQ = P + A + IF + EF. Some sources add appraisal to COPQ, so state your convention.","Internal vs external depends on whether the defect was found before reaching the customer.","Visible costs are the tip of the iceberg; the hidden factory and lost customers are usually larger.","Prevention is the cheapest place to spend; more inspection alone rarely lowers total cost.","“Estimate COPQ” is a Define activity that feeds the business case; refine it in Measure."],flashcards:[{front:"Four categories of Cost of Quality?",back:"Prevention, Appraisal, Internal failure, External failure (P, A, IF, EF)."},{front:"Common formula for COPQ?",back:"COPQ = Internal failure + External failure. (Some sources add appraisal.)"},{front:"Formula for total COQ?",back:"COQ = P + A + IF + EF."},{front:"Repeat truck roll: which category?",back:"External failure: the defect reached the customer."},{front:"QA audit of closed tickets: which category?",back:"Appraisal: checking for defects."},{front:"Re-keying an invoice caught before payment?",back:"Internal failure: found before affecting the supplier (the customer of AP)."},{front:"What is the hidden factory?",back:"Corrective, non-value-added work not recognised as waste: rework, chasing, workarounds, repeat handling."},{front:"Why does prevention reduce total COQ?",back:"It removes causes, so far fewer defects need costly internal fixing or external recovery."},{front:"When is COPQ first estimated in DMAIC?",back:"In Define (“Estimate COPQ” on the roadmap), then refined in Measure; benefits verified in Control."}],coverage:"official-only",supplementaryNotes:["The Define chapter of the training book was not supplied. The supplied pages define COPQ and the hidden factory (glossary pp. 584, 586) and place “Estimate COPQ” in Define (pp. 230, 237), but they do not list the four cost categories. The prevention/appraisal/internal/external classification is standard Cost of Quality theory, explained here independently.","The 1–10–100 rule is an illustrative rule of thumb, not a precise law, and is not taken from a supplied page.","Conventions vary between sources on whether appraisal is part of COPQ; the lesson presents the common convention and flags the variation.","All OMR figures are illustrative."],sources:[{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 7",pdfPage:7,note:"Define topic 1.2.3 Cost of Poor Quality (COPQ)"},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 230",pdfPage:33,note:"DMAIC roadmap: Define includes “Estimate COPQ”"},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 231",pdfPage:34,note:"Measure checklist: “Have you identified more COPQ?”; financial benefits prompts"},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 237",pdfPage:40,note:"DMAIC roadmap repeated with “Estimate COPQ” in Define"},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 100",pdfPage:3,note:"Actual process holds hidden operations; mapping reveals cost of poor quality collection points"},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 584",pdfPage:87,note:"Glossary: COPQ, not doing the right thing right the first time"},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 586",pdfPage:89,note:"Glossary: Hidden Factory"}]},q=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:m},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),t=String.raw,f={id:"1.2.4",module:2,title:"Pareto Analysis (80:20 rule)",subtitle:"Separating the vital few from the trivial many",minutes:13,objectives:["Explain the Pareto principle and why “80/20” is a heuristic rather than a law.","Build a Pareto chart correctly: descending bars, “Other” last, cumulative percentage line, two axes.","Calculate cumulative frequency and cumulative percentage from a tally table.","Choose between a frequency Pareto and a weighted (impact or cost) Pareto.","Use stratification (a second-level Pareto) to drill from a symptom category towards actionable causes.","Recognise the common construction and interpretation mistakes that exam questions target."],overview:t`Every service operation has more problems than it has people to fix them. **Pareto analysis** is the Green Belt’s first triage tool: you count how often each category of problem occurs, sort the categories from largest to smallest, and look at how quickly the running total climbs. Almost always, a handful of categories — the [[vital-few|vital few]] — account for most of the pain, while a long tail of small categories (the “trivial many”, or more kindly the “useful many”) accounts for the rest.

In the Define phase, a Pareto chart helps you **focus and scope** a project: it shows *where* to point the team, so that the problem statement targets the categories that will move the [[primary-metric|primary metric]]. It does not tell you *why* those problems happen — that question belongs to Analyze.`,sections:[{id:"principle",heading:"The Pareto principle — and why it is rarely exactly 80/20",body:t`The [[pareto-principle|Pareto principle]] is named after the economist Vilfredo Pareto, who noticed that a small share of the population held most of the land in Italy. Quality pioneer Joseph [[juran|Juran]] generalised the idea to quality problems: a minority of causes produces the majority of effects. The popular shorthand is **80:20** — roughly 80% of the effects come from roughly 20% of the causes.

Treat 80:20 as a *rule of thumb*, not a mathematical law. In real data you might find 70:30, 90:10, or 65:35. Several things change the split:

- **How you define the categories.** Merge two categories and the top bar grows; split one and it shrinks. The same tickets can look “very Pareto” or “flat” depending on the coding scheme.
- **The data period.** One bad week (a fibre cut, a billing-run failure) can dominate a monthly chart. A longer, representative window usually gives a more trustworthy ranking.
- **What you measure.** Counting *how often* something happens (frequency) can give a different ranking from measuring *how much it hurts* (minutes of outage, SLA penalties, cost of rework).

Notice also that the two numbers do not have to add up to 100. “80% of tickets come from 20% of categories” is about two different totals — tickets and categories — so 79:43 is a perfectly valid result, as you are about to see.

> **Memory hook:** Pareto is a *spotlight*, not a *verdict*. It shows you where to look first.`},{id:"build",heading:"Building the chart: sort, total, accumulate",body:t`A [[pareto-chart|Pareto chart]] is a bar chart of categories in **descending** order of frequency (or impact), combined with a line showing the **[[cumulative-percentage|cumulative percentage]]**. Here is a month of service-desk incident tickets at a telecom operator — 1,000 tickets in seven categories.

| Rank | Category | Count | % of total | Cumulative count | Cumulative % |
|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 1 | Access & password | 380 | 38% | 380 | 38% |
| 2 | Network connectivity | 260 | 26% | 640 | 64% |
| 3 | Billing system | 150 | 15% | 790 | 79% |
| 4 | Email & collaboration | 80 | 8% | 870 | 87% |
| 5 | End-user hardware | 60 | 6% | 930 | 93% |
| 6 | Printing | 40 | 4% | 970 | 97% |
| 7 | Other | 30 | 3% | 1,000 | 100% |

The recipe:

1. **Tally** each category for a defined period with a clear [[operational-definition|operational definition]] of each category.
2. **Sort** from largest to smallest. Place **“Other”** (a bucket of many tiny categories) **last**, even if it happens to be bigger than the smallest named bar — otherwise a meaningless mixture could masquerade as a top problem. If “Other” is one of your biggest bars, your categories need refining.
3. **Total** all counts (here 1,000).
4. **Percentage**: each count ÷ total × 100.
5. **Cumulative count**: a running sum down the sorted list.
6. **Cumulative %**: cumulative count ÷ total × 100. The last row must reach exactly 100% — a quick self-check.

The finished chart has **two vertical axes**. The **left axis** shows counts (scaled from 0 to the grand total so that the bars and the line share the same geometry); the **right axis** shows cumulative percentage from 0% to 100%. Each point on the line is plotted above its bar (or at the bar’s right edge, depending on convention), and the line always rises and flattens out, because each added bar is smaller than the one before.`},{id:"read",heading:"Reading the cut-off: where do the vital few stop?",body:t`Draw a horizontal reference line at 80% on the right axis and see where the cumulative line crosses it. In the ticket data the line runs 38% → 64% → **79%** → 87%. The first **three** categories (Access, Network, Billing) — 3 of 7, or about 43% of the categories — produce **79%** of the tickets. That is “Pareto-shaped”, but it is not 80:20, and that is normal.

Good analysts also look at the **shape**:

- A steep line with a sharp bend (an “elbow”) means a clear vital few. Focus there.
- A flat, almost straight line means the problems are spread evenly. A Pareto chart is still useful — it tells you that no single category will deliver a big win, and that you may need a different way of grouping the data (by root cause, by location, by shift) before you can prioritise.

The cut-off is a **judgement**, not an automatic 80% rule. If category 4 is cheap to fix and category 3 needs a vendor change request, you may sensibly tackle 1, 2 and 4 first. The chart informs the decision; the team, with the Champion, makes it.`},{id:"weighted",heading:"Frequency or impact? The weighted Pareto",body:t`Counting tickets treats a 2-minute password reset the same as a 6-hour network investigation. When categories differ greatly in severity, build a **weighted Pareto**: multiply each count by an impact weight (average effort hours, outage minutes, penalty cost, customers affected) and sort by the weighted total.

Using average effort per ticket (Access 0.25 h, Network 3 h, Billing 2 h, Email 0.5 h, Hardware 1.5 h, Printing 0.5 h, Other 0.5 h) gives 1,340 effort hours:

| Rank | Category | Effort hours | Cumulative % |
|---|---|---:|---:|
| 1 | Network connectivity | 780 | 58.2% |
| 2 | Billing system | 300 | 80.6% |
| 3 | Access & password | 95 | 87.7% |
| 4 | End-user hardware | 90 | 94.4% |
| 5 | Email & collaboration | 40 | 97.4% |
| 6 | Printing | 20 | 98.9% |
| 7 | Other | 15 | 100% |

The number-one problem by **frequency** (Access) drops to third by **effort**, and Network plus Billing alone now carry 80.6% of the workload. Neither chart is “right”: they answer different questions. A frequency Pareto suits problems such as customer annoyance or self-service deflection; a weighted Pareto suits [[copq|cost of poor quality]], SLA penalty exposure or staffing. State in your charter which one you used and why.`},{id:"stratify",heading:"Causes, symptoms and the second-level Pareto",body:t`A top-level category such as “Network connectivity” is usually a **symptom** bucket, not a cause. You cannot write an improvement action for “network”. So you **stratify**: take the biggest bar and build a second Pareto of just its contents.

Breaking down the 260 network tickets by diagnosed reason:

| Sub-category | Count | Cumulative % |
|---|---:|---:|
| Home router / CPE configuration | 104 | 40% |
| DNS resolution | 65 | 65% |
| VPN client | 39 | 80% |
| Last-mile fibre | 31 | 91.9% |
| Other | 21 | 100% |

Now the team has something it can scope: “CPE configuration incidents for home-broadband customers” is a narrow, measurable focus for a [[problem-statement|problem statement]]. You can keep drilling (by router model, by provisioning channel) until the categories become specific enough to investigate.

Even at this level, remember the boundary: a Pareto shows **where problems cluster**, not **why** they occur. Proving a [[root-cause|root cause]] needs Analyze-phase tools and data — hypothesis tests, cause-and-effect analysis, verification. Pareto helps you *prioritise* candidate causes; it never *proves* one.`},{id:"mistakes",heading:"Common mistakes (and what exam questions look for)",body:t`- **Unsorted bars.** A bar chart in alphabetical or chronological order is *not* a Pareto chart. Descending order is the defining feature.
- **“Other” in the wrong place.** A large “Other” bar placed first hides the real priorities. It belongs at the far right.
- **Mixing category types.** Putting “Router fault”, “Night shift” and “Customer in Muscat” on one chart mixes a cause, a time and a location — one ticket could fall into several bars and the counts no longer add up. Each chart should use **one** mutually exclusive classification.
- **Mixing time periods or units.** January tickets in one bar and a quarter’s tickets in another; or minutes in one bar and counts in another.
- **Reading the wrong axis.** The cumulative percentage is read from the **right** axis; bar counts from the **left**. Exam questions often ask “what cumulative percentage do the top two categories represent?” — that is the line value above bar 2, not the height of bar 2.
- **Using Pareto as proof of root cause.** “Access tickets are the biggest bar, so weak password policy is the root cause” jumps from frequency to causation without evidence.
- **Forcing 80:20.** Declaring exactly 20% of categories as the vital few regardless of where the line bends.
- **Never re-running it.** After improvement, a new Pareto should show the old top bar shrinking — and often reveals a new leader. That is how continuous improvement keeps moving.`}],whyItMatters:t`Service-operations teams rarely suffer from a lack of problems; they suffer from a lack of focus. A Pareto chart turns a noisy ticket queue, complaint log or SLA-breach report into a ranked list that a sponsor can understand in ten seconds. It protects the project from “boiling the ocean”, gives the charter a data-backed scope, and creates a baseline picture you can re-draw after the fix to show the vital few have genuinely shrunk.

For the exam, expect questions on chart construction (descending order, cumulative line, right-hand axis), on reading a cumulative percentage, on the 80:20 idea being approximate, and on the limitation that Pareto prioritises but does not prove causes.`,businessExample:{title:"Reducing SLA breaches on a managed-services contract",body:t`A telecom operator’s Digital Service Operations team sees 142 resolution-time SLA breaches in a quarter on its outsourced application-support contract. Rather than asking the vendor to “improve everything”, the Green Belt tallies the breaches by application: the CRM platform accounts for 61, mediation for 38 and the self-care portal for 19 — 118 of 142, or about 83%, from three of eleven applications.

A second-level Pareto of the 61 CRM breaches by delay reason shows “waiting for an L3 vendor response” at the top. The charter is scoped to **CRM P2 incidents and the L3 hand-off**, with SLA-breach count as the primary metric. Whether the hand-off delay is truly the root cause is left, correctly, for Measure and Analyze to confirm with data and with clarification from the vendor team.`},visual:"paretoBuild",visualCaption:"Watch 1,000 service-desk tickets become a Pareto chart: the bars sort into descending order with “Other” last, the cumulative line climbs 38% → 64% → 79%, and the 80% reference line reveals the vital few.",definitions:[{term:"Pareto principle",definition:"The observation that a minority of causes or categories accounts for the majority of effects; often summarised as 80:20, but the actual split varies."},{term:"Pareto chart",definition:"A bar chart with categories sorted in descending order of frequency or impact, usually combined with a cumulative percentage line on a secondary (right-hand) axis."},{term:"Vital few",definition:"The small number of categories that together account for most of the total effect; the priority targets for improvement."},{term:"Trivial (useful) many",definition:"The larger number of categories that each contribute a small share of the total; not ignored, but lower priority."},{term:"Cumulative frequency",definition:"The running total of counts down the sorted list of categories."},{term:"Cumulative percentage",definition:"The cumulative frequency divided by the grand total, × 100; it always ends at 100%."},{term:"Weighted Pareto",definition:"A Pareto chart in which each category’s count is multiplied by an impact weight (cost, time, severity) before sorting."},{term:"Stratification",definition:"Splitting data into sub-groups (by cause, location, shift, product, etc.) to reveal patterns hidden in the total."},{term:"Second-level Pareto",definition:"A Pareto chart drawn for the contents of one bar of a higher-level Pareto, used to drill down from a symptom category towards actionable causes."},{term:"“Other” category",definition:"A bucket combining many small categories; plotted as the last bar regardless of its size."}],comparisons:[{title:"Frequency Pareto vs weighted Pareto",headers:["","Frequency Pareto","Weighted (impact) Pareto"],rows:[["Bar height","Number of occurrences","Occurrences × impact (cost, minutes, penalty)"],["Answers","What happens most often?","What hurts the business most?"],["Telecom example","Ticket count by category","Effort hours or SLA penalty by category"],["Risk","Over-prioritises frequent but trivial issues","Depends on the accuracy of the weights"]]},{title:"Pareto chart vs ordinary bar chart vs histogram",headers:["","Pareto chart","Ordinary bar chart","Histogram"],rows:[["X-axis","Categories","Categories","Continuous measurement intervals"],["Order","Always descending (Other last)","Any order","Fixed numeric order"],["Cumulative line","Yes (right axis)","No","No"],["Purpose","Prioritise","Compare","Show a distribution’s shape"]]}],misconceptions:[{myth:"Exactly 20% of causes always produce exactly 80% of problems.",reality:"80:20 is a heuristic. Real splits vary (70:30, 90:10, 79:43); the point is that effects are unevenly distributed."},{myth:"The tallest bar is the root cause.",reality:"The tallest bar is the largest category — often a symptom. Root causes must be verified with Analyze-phase data."},{myth:"The two numbers in 80:20 must add up to 100.",reality:"They refer to different totals (effects and causes), so any combination is possible."},{myth:"Small categories can be ignored forever.",reality:"They are lower priority now. Once the vital few are fixed, a new Pareto often promotes one of them."},{myth:"A flat Pareto chart is useless.",reality:"It tells you there is no dominant category, which suggests re-stratifying the data by a different dimension."}],examTraps:[{trap:"Asked for the cumulative percentage of the top two categories, candidates read the height of the second bar.",explanation:"Cumulative percentage is read from the line on the right-hand axis (here 64%), not from a bar.",tag:"cumulative-percentage"},{trap:"“Which is the FIRST step in constructing a Pareto chart?”",explanation:"Collect and tally data by category over a defined period. Sorting, percentages and the line come after.",tag:"first"},{trap:"An option claims the Pareto chart “identifies the verified root cause”.",explanation:"Pareto prioritises categories; it does not verify causes. Look for wording such as “prioritise” or “focus”.",tag:"verified-root-cause"},{trap:"A chart with bars in alphabetical order is described as a Pareto chart.",explanation:"Without descending order it is just a bar chart. Descending order is the defining feature.",tag:"not"},{trap:"“Which statement about the 80/20 rule is NOT true?” with the option “It always splits exactly 80/20.”",explanation:"That is the untrue statement — and therefore the correct answer to a NOT question.",tag:"not"}],workedExamples:[{title:"Cumulative percentage from a ticket tally",problem:t`A service desk logs 1,000 tickets: Access 380, Network 260, Billing 150, Email 80, Hardware 60, Printing 40, Other 30. What cumulative percentage do the top three categories represent, and how many categories are needed to pass 90%?`,steps:["Sort descending (already sorted; “Other” is last). Total = 1,000.","Running sum: 380 → 640 → 790 → 870 → 930 → 970 → 1,000.","Cumulative %: 38% → 64% → **79%** → 87% → **93%** → 97% → 100%.","Top three = 79%. The line first exceeds 90% at category 5 (Hardware), so five categories are needed."],answer:"Top three = 79% of tickets; five categories are needed to exceed 90% (93%).",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Averaging the top three percentages: (38 + 26 + 15) ÷ 3 = 26.3%",whyWrong:"Cumulative percentage is a running sum, not an average. 38 + 26 + 15 = 79%."}},{title:"Frequency vs effort: does the priority change?",problem:t`Using average effort per ticket — Access 0.25 h, Network 3 h, Billing 2 h, Email 0.5 h, Hardware 1.5 h, Printing 0.5 h, Other 0.5 h — rebuild the Pareto by effort hours. Which category is now first, and what share do the top two carry?`,steps:["Weight each count: Access 380 × 0.25 = 95; Network 260 × 3 = 780; Billing 150 × 2 = 300; Email 80 × 0.5 = 40; Hardware 60 × 1.5 = 90; Printing 40 × 0.5 = 20; Other 30 × 0.5 = 15.","Total effort = 1,340 hours. Sort: Network 780, Billing 300, Access 95, Hardware 90, Email 40, Printing 20, Other 15.","Cumulative %: 780 ÷ 1,340 = 58.2%; (780 + 300) ÷ 1,340 = 80.6%."],answer:"Network connectivity becomes the top category; Network + Billing carry 80.6% of effort hours.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Keeping Access first because it has the most tickets.",whyWrong:"If the project goal is effort or cost, the ranking must use the weighted values. Frequency and impact can rank categories differently."}},{title:"Second-level Pareto for scoping",problem:t`The 260 network tickets split into CPE configuration 104, DNS 65, VPN 39, last-mile fibre 31 and Other 21. Which sub-categories form the vital few at the 80% line?`,steps:["Total = 260. Running sum: 104 → 169 → 208 → 239 → 260.","Cumulative %: 40% → 65% → 80% → 91.9% → 100%.","The line reaches 80% at the third sub-category (VPN)."],answer:"CPE configuration, DNS and VPN (80% of network tickets); CPE alone is 40% and is a natural first scope."}],lab:"pareto",check:[{id:"chk-1.2.4-1",topic:"1.2.4",category:"concept",difficulty:"beginner",numerical:!1,stem:"What is the defining feature that makes a bar chart a Pareto chart?",options:["Bars sorted in descending order of frequency or impact","Bars coloured by severity","A normal curve drawn over the bars","Categories listed alphabetically"],answer:0,explanation:"A Pareto chart ranks categories from largest to smallest (with “Other” last), usually with a cumulative percentage line.",whyWrong:["","Colour is optional decoration and does not define the chart.","A fitted curve belongs on a histogram, not a Pareto chart.","Alphabetical order is exactly what a Pareto chart replaces."]},{id:"chk-1.2.4-2",topic:"1.2.4",category:"chart",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!0,stem:"Ticket counts: Access 380, Network 260, Billing 150, Email 80, Hardware 60, Printing 40, Other 30. What cumulative percentage do the **top two** categories represent?",options:["26%","38%","64%","79%"],answer:2,explanation:"(380 + 260) ÷ 1,000 = 64%. Cumulative percentage is read from the line on the right axis.",whyWrong:["26% is the share of the second category alone.","38% is the share of the first category alone.","","79% is the cumulative share of the top three."],traps:["cumulative-percentage"],chart:{type:"pareto",labels:["Access","Network","Billing","Email","Hardware","Printing","Other"],values:[380,260,150,80,60,40,30]}},{id:"chk-1.2.4-3",topic:"1.2.4",category:"incorrect-statement",difficulty:"trap",numerical:!1,stem:"Which statement about the Pareto principle is **NOT** correct?",options:["The 80:20 split is approximate and varies with the data","A Pareto chart helps prioritise where to focus improvement","The largest bar on a Pareto chart is the verified root cause of the problem","The “Other” category is plotted last"],answer:2,explanation:"Pareto analysis prioritises categories; verifying a root cause requires Analyze-phase evidence.",whyWrong:["This is correct — real data rarely splits exactly 80:20.","This is correct — prioritisation is its main purpose.","","This is correct — “Other” goes last regardless of size."],traps:["not","verified-root-cause"]},{id:"chk-1.2.4-4",topic:"1.2.4",category:"scenario",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"Password resets are the most frequent ticket type, but network incidents consume far more engineer hours. The project goal is to reduce support effort. Which chart should guide scoping?",options:["A frequency Pareto of ticket counts","A Pareto weighted by effort hours","A pie chart of ticket categories","A run chart of daily ticket volume"],answer:1,explanation:"When the goal is effort or cost, weight each category by its impact so the ranking reflects what the project is trying to reduce.",whyWrong:["Counts would rank password resets first even though they consume little effort.","","A pie chart does not rank or accumulate and hides small differences.","A run chart shows change over time, not which category to prioritise."]},{id:"chk-1.2.4-5",topic:"1.2.4",category:"best-tool",difficulty:"advanced",numerical:!1,stem:"The top bar of a Pareto chart is “Network connectivity”, which is too broad to act on. What is the best next step?",options:["Declare network the root cause and start a solution","Build a second-level Pareto of the network tickets by sub-category","Remove the network bar and re-draw the chart","Merge network with the second bar to make the chart look more 80:20"],answer:1,explanation:"Stratifying the biggest bar into a second-level Pareto narrows the focus to an actionable, scopeable category.",whyWrong:["A category is a symptom bucket; jumping to a solution skips the analysis.","","Removing the biggest problem hides it rather than understanding it.","Manipulating categories to force 80:20 distorts the evidence."]}],takeaways:["Pareto analysis ranks categories so the team focuses on the vital few first.","80:20 is a heuristic; real splits vary with categories, period and measure.","Sort bars descending, put “Other” last, add a cumulative % line on the right axis.","Cumulative % = running total ÷ grand total × 100; it must end at 100%.","Use a weighted Pareto when categories differ in cost, time or severity.","Stratify the top bar with a second-level Pareto to reach actionable causes.","Pareto prioritises — it never proves a root cause."],flashcards:[{front:"Pareto principle",back:"A minority of causes produces the majority of effects (often “80:20”, but the split varies)."},{front:"Order of bars on a Pareto chart",back:"Descending — largest first — with “Other” always last."},{front:"Which axis shows cumulative %?",back:"The right-hand (secondary) axis, 0–100%."},{front:"Cumulative percentage formula",back:"Running total of counts ÷ grand total × 100."},{front:"Weighted Pareto",back:"Count × impact weight (cost, minutes, severity) before sorting — ranks by what hurts most."},{front:"Second-level Pareto",back:"A Pareto of the contents of one bar, used to drill from a symptom towards causes."},{front:"Does Pareto prove root cause?",back:"No. It prioritises where to look; Analyze verifies causes."},{front:"A flat Pareto line means…",back:"No dominant category — try stratifying the data by a different dimension."}],coverage:"official-only",supplementaryNotes:["The supplied training-book pages do not include the Define chapter, so the construction steps, weighted Pareto and stratification guidance are independently written from general Lean Six Sigma practice.","The book glossary (p. 587) supports the definitions of Pareto chart and Pareto principle; p. 124 mentions Pareto ranking in FMEA only in passing.","All ticket figures are illustrative teaching data, not real operator data."],sources:[{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 7",pdfPage:7,note:"Define topic 1.2.4 “Pareto Analysis (80:20 rule)”."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 587",pdfPage:90,note:"Glossary: Pareto chart (bar chart in descending frequency) and Pareto principle (majority of influence from a minority of inputs)."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 124",pdfPage:27,note:"Passing mention: FMEA corrective actions taken based on a Pareto analysis ranking."}]},B=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:f},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),e=String.raw,a=(i,o,s)=>e`**Practise it:** open the [${o}](${i}) to change the numbers and watch the result move, then drill unlimited generated problems in the [Formula Master](#/formulas/${s}).`,g={id:"1.2.5",module:2,title:"Basic Six Sigma Metrics: DPU, DPMO, FTY, RTY, Cycle Time",subtitle:"The numbers every Green Belt must calculate fast and correctly",minutes:28,objectives:["Define unit, defect, defective unit and opportunity — and decide what legitimately counts as an opportunity.","Calculate DPU, DPO and DPMO and choose the correct numerator and denominator every time.","Distinguish first time yield (FTY), final yield and rolled throughput yield (RTY), and explain how rework changes each.","Calculate RTY for a multi-step process by multiplying step yields — never adding or averaging.","Calculate cycle time with consistent time units and convert between minutes, hours, seconds, decimals and percentages.","Interpret DPMO as an approximate sigma level using the conventional 1.5σ shift (advanced).","Contrast cycle time with lead time and takt time (supplementary)."],overview:e`Six Sigma is famous for its numbers, and this lesson is where you learn to produce them with confidence. Every metric here answers a slightly different question about the same process: *How many defects does a typical unit carry?* (DPU). *How often does each chance to go wrong actually go wrong?* (DPO, DPMO). *How many units sail through a step without being touched twice?* (FTY). *How many sail through the whole process clean?* (RTY). *How long does it take to complete one unit?* (cycle time).

The exam rarely tests whether you can divide; it tests whether you divide **the right things**. Total defects or defective units? Units or opportunities? First pass or after rework? Minutes or hours? We will build each metric from the ground up, using a telecom SIM-activation process as our running example, and for every formula you will see the definition, the formula, the variables, when to use it, a worked example with steps, the classic exam trap, a wrong calculation and why it is wrong.`,sections:[{id:"building-blocks",heading:"The building blocks: unit, defect, defective, opportunity",body:e`Before any formula, agree on four words. Most calculation errors in this topic are really *vocabulary* errors.

**[[unit|Unit]]** — the item, transaction or service event you are counting: one SIM activation, one invoice, one incident ticket, one installed broadband line. Define it so that anyone would count the same population.

**[[defect|Defect]]** — any failure to meet a customer requirement or specification (a [[ctq|CTQ]]). A wrong tariff plan on an activation is a defect; so is a missing welcome SMS.

**[[defective|Defective unit]]** — a unit with **at least one** defect. A single activation with a wrong plan *and* a missing SMS is **two defects** but **one defective unit**. That is why total defects can be larger than defective units — and why a single unit can carry many defects.

**[[opportunity|Opportunity]]** — a characteristic of the unit that can be checked and classified as acceptable or unacceptable; each one is a chance for a defect. On a SIM activation we might count six: ICCID–IMSI match, tariff plan code, KYC record complete, MSISDN assigned, services enabled, welcome SMS sent.

What legitimately counts as an opportunity? Apply three tests:

1. **Measurable** — it has an [[operational-definition|operational definition]] and a clear pass/fail rule.
2. **Customer-relevant** — a failure would matter to the customer or to the business (it links to a CTQ). Checks nobody cares about are padding.
3. **Consistent** — the same list is used for every unit and every period, so DPMO can be compared over time.

> **Warning about inflating opportunities:** adding trivial opportunities (“font on the welcome SMS is correct”) increases the denominator and makes DPMO look better without a single real improvement. Agree the opportunity list in Define, write it down, and do not change it mid-project.`},{id:"dpu",heading:"DPU — defects per unit",body:e`**1 · Definition.** DPU is the average number of defects carried by one unit: total defects found divided by total units examined.

**2 · Formula.**

$$\text{DPU}=\frac{D}{U}=\frac{\text{total defects}}{\text{total units}}$$

**3 · Variables.** $D$ = every defect found, counting multiple defects on the same unit separately. $U$ = number of units inspected or produced in the same period.

**4 · When to use it.** When you care about *how much* is wrong per unit and units are broadly similar in complexity — e.g. defects per installation, errors per invoice batch. DPU feeds the Poisson yield estimate and is a common level-3 process-map metric.

**5 · Worked example.** An accounts-payable team processes **2,000** supplier invoices in a month. Audit finds **260** defects spread across **190** invoices. Find DPU and the percentage of invoices that are defective.

**6 · Step-by-step.**
1. Numerator = total defects = 260 (not 190).
2. Denominator = total units = 2,000.
3. DPU = 260 ÷ 2,000 = **0.13** defects per invoice.
4. Separately, % defective = defective units ÷ units = 190 ÷ 2,000 = **9.5%**.

**7 · Exam trap.** The stem gives you *both* defects and defective units. DPU always uses **total defects**. Look for the words “defects found” versus “units with defects”.

**8 · Incorrect calculation.** DPU = 190 ÷ 2,000 = 0.095.

**9 · Why it’s wrong.** 190 is the number of **defective units**; that ratio is the proportion defective, not defects per unit. It ignores the 70 extra defects sitting on invoices that had more than one problem.

DPU can exceed 1. Eight home routers returned for diagnosis with **12** defects between them give DPU = 12 ÷ 8 = **1.5** — a perfectly valid result, while “percentage defective” can never exceed 100%.

${a("#/labs/dpmo","DPU/DPMO Calculator","dpu")}`},{id:"dpo-dpmo",heading:"DPO and DPMO — defects per opportunity, per million",body:e`DPU has a weakness: a 40-field customer order and a 3-field password reset are not equally likely to go wrong. Dividing by **opportunities** levels the playing field so that very different processes can be compared.

### DPO (supporting background)

**1 · Definition.** The proportion of all opportunities that turned out to be defects.

**2 · Formula.**

$$\text{DPO}=\frac{D}{U\times O}$$

**3 · Variables.** $D$ = total defects; $U$ = units; $O$ = opportunities per unit; $U\times O$ = **total opportunities**.

**4 · When to use it.** As the stepping stone to DPMO, or whenever you want a defect *rate* that accounts for complexity.

**5 · Worked example.** The 2,000 invoices each have **5** opportunities (supplier ID, PO match, amount, VAT code, due date). With 260 defects, DPO = ?

**6 · Step-by-step.** Total opportunities = 2,000 × 5 = 10,000. DPO = 260 ÷ 10,000 = **0.026**.

**7 · Exam trap.** Forgetting to multiply units by opportunities — or multiplying the *defects* by opportunities instead.

**8 · Incorrect calculation.** DPO = 260 ÷ (2,000 + 5) ≈ 0.1297.

**9 · Why it’s wrong.** Opportunities per unit must be **multiplied** by the number of units to give total opportunities; adding them has no meaning.

### DPMO

**1 · Definition.** The number of defects that would occur in one million opportunities — DPO rescaled to “per million” so that small rates become readable and comparable.

**2 · Formula.**

$$\text{DPMO}=\frac{D}{U\times O}\times 10^{6}=\text{DPO}\times 1{,}000{,}000$$

**3 · Variables.** As for DPO; $10^{6}$ is simply the scale factor.

**4 · When to use it.** To compare processes or products of different complexity, to benchmark against a sigma level, or to set a baseline for the charter.

**5 · Worked example.** Our running case: **500** SIM activations, **6** opportunities each, **45** defects found on **38** activations. Find DPU, DPO, DPMO and % defective.

**6 · Step-by-step.**
1. DPU = 45 ÷ 500 = **0.09**.
2. Total opportunities = 500 × 6 = 3,000.
3. DPO = 45 ÷ 3,000 = **0.015**.
4. DPMO = 0.015 × 1,000,000 = **15,000**.
5. % defective = 38 ÷ 500 = **7.6%** (uses defective units, a different question).

A useful shortcut: DPO = DPU ÷ O, so 0.09 ÷ 6 = 0.015 — the same answer.

**7 · Exam trap.** “Per opportunity” in the stem means divide by $U\times O$; “per unit” means divide by $U$. Also watch for stems that hand you *defective units* where you need *defects*.

**8 · Incorrect calculation.** DPMO = DPU × 10⁶ = 0.09 × 1,000,000 = 90,000 — or, using defectives, 38 ÷ 3,000 × 10⁶ ≈ 12,667.

**9 · Why it’s wrong.** The first skips the opportunities in the denominator, so it is really “defects per million **units**”. The second uses defective units in the numerator, undercounting the activations that carried several defects.

${a("#/labs/dpmo","DPU/DPMO Calculator","dpmo")}`},{id:"fty",heading:"FTY and final yield — first pass versus after rework",body:e`**1 · Definition.** [[fty|First time yield]] (also called first pass yield) is the proportion of units that complete a step **correctly the first time** — with **no rework, no repair and no scrap**. [[final-yield|Final yield]] is the proportion that eventually come out good, **including** units that were reworked.

**2 · Formulas.**

$$\text{FTY}=\frac{\text{units passing first time (no rework, no scrap)}}{\text{units entering the step}}$$

$$\text{Final yield}=\frac{\text{good units out (including reworked)}}{\text{units entering}}$$

**3 · Variables.** “Units entering” is the denominator for both. The difference lies in the numerator: FTY excludes anything that was touched twice.

**4 · When to use it.** FTY exposes the **[[hidden-factory|hidden factory]]** — the effort spent fixing things that should have been right. Final yield tells you what the customer eventually receives. A large gap between them is pure [[copq|cost of poor quality]].

**5 · Worked example.** A resolver group handles **1,200** incident tickets. **1,020** are resolved first time and never reopened. **150** are reopened (rework) and later resolved. **30** are cancelled and re-raised as new problems (scrap). Find FTY and final yield.

**6 · Step-by-step.**
1. FTY numerator = first-time successes = 1,020.
2. FTY = 1,020 ÷ 1,200 = **0.85 = 85%**.
3. Final-yield numerator = 1,020 + 150 = 1,170 good tickets in the end.
4. Final yield = 1,170 ÷ 1,200 = **0.975 = 97.5%**.
5. The 12.5-point gap (150 reworked tickets) is hidden-factory effort.

**7 · Exam trap.** Words such as “**without rework**”, “**first pass**”, “**right first time**” demand FTY. Words such as “eventually”, “after repair”, “shipped” point to final yield.

**8 · Incorrect calculation.** FTY = 1,170 ÷ 1,200 = 97.5%.

**9 · Why it’s wrong.** It counts reworked tickets as first-time successes. The reopened tickets consumed a second round of effort, so by definition they did not pass the first time.

> **Telecom note:** an SLA dashboard that reports only “tickets resolved within target” is usually a final-yield view. Asking the vendor for the *first-time-fix* rate (the FTY view) is a fair request for clarification and supporting data — it often reveals rework that the headline number hides.

${a("#/labs/rty","FTY/RTY Simulation","fty")}`},{id:"rty",heading:"RTY — rolled throughput yield for multi-step processes",body:e`**1 · Definition.** [[rty|Rolled throughput yield]] is the probability that a unit passes through **every** step of a process with **zero defects** — first time, at every step. It is the end-to-end, right-first-time view.

**2 · Formula.**

$$\text{RTY}=\text{FTY}_1\times\text{FTY}_2\times\cdots\times\text{FTY}_n=\prod_{i=1}^{n}\text{FTY}_i$$

**3 · Variables.** $\text{FTY}_i$ = first time yield of step $i$, written as a decimal; $n$ = number of steps.

**4 · When to use it.** Whenever a process has more than one step — order-to-activation, incident-to-closure, procure-to-pay. RTY shows how small losses at each step **compound**, and it usually reveals which step is the biggest drain.

**5 · Worked example.** A broadband provisioning process has four steps with FTYs of **97%** (order capture), **95%** (credit check), **88%** (line configuration) and **93%** (activation test). Find RTY.

**6 · Step-by-step.**
1. Convert to decimals: 0.97, 0.95, 0.88, 0.93.
2. 0.97 × 0.95 = 0.9215.
3. 0.9215 × 0.88 = 0.81092.
4. 0.81092 × 0.93 = **0.7542 ≈ 75.4%**.
5. Interpretation: only about **75 of every 100** orders go from capture to activation without anyone touching them twice — even though no single step looks worse than 88%.

**7 · Exam trap.** Distractors will offer the **average** (93.25%), the **lowest step** (88%), or the **last step** (93%). RTY is always a **product**, and it is always **less than or equal to the lowest step FTY**.

**8 · Incorrect calculation.** RTY = (0.97 + 0.95 + 0.88 + 0.93) ÷ 4 = 0.9325 = 93.25%.

**9 · Why it’s wrong.** Averaging treats the steps as alternatives; in reality a unit must survive step 1 **and** step 2 **and** step 3 **and** step 4. Probabilities of “and” events multiply. Adding them (3.73) is even worse — a yield above 100% is impossible.

The training book’s value-stream example shows the same logic on a five-step cheque-processing stream: $0.92\times0.94\times0.59\times0.96\times0.96\approx0.47$, with the cumulative yield dropping .92 → .86 → .51 → .49 → .47 — one weak step (0.59) drags the whole stream below 50%. The book also notes that detailed level-3 process maps typically carry DPU, yield and RTY estimates for each step (detailed mapping itself is a Measure-phase topic, 2.1.x).

${a("#/labs/rty","FTY/RTY Simulation","rty")}`},{id:"cycle-time",heading:"Cycle time — and keeping your time units consistent",body:e`**1 · Definition.** [[cycle-time|Cycle time]] is the time it takes to complete one unit of work — from when a task, product or service is started until it is completed. As a calculated average for a step or team it is the working time used divided by the units completed.

**2 · Formula.**

$$\text{Cycle time}=\frac{\text{net working time}}{\text{units completed}}$$

**3 · Variables.** Net working time = the productive time actually available in the period (excluding breaks and planned downtime), expressed in **one** unit — minutes, say. Units completed = finished outputs in that same period.

**4 · When to use it.** To baseline how long a task takes, to find bottlenecks (the step with the longest cycle time limits the whole flow), and to compare with demand (see takt time below).

**5 · Worked example.** A provisioning desk has **7 h 30 min** of net working time in a shift and completes **225** orders. Find the average cycle time in minutes and seconds.

**6 · Step-by-step.**
1. Convert the time to a single unit: 7 h 30 min = 7 × 60 + 30 = **450 min**.
2. Cycle time = 450 ÷ 225 = **2 minutes per order**.
3. In seconds: 2 × 60 = **120 s** (or 27,000 s ÷ 225 = 120 s).

**7 · Exam trap.** Mixed units — hours and minutes, or “7.5 h” written as “7:30”. Convert everything to one unit *before* dividing, and state the unit in your answer.

**8 · Incorrect calculation.** 7.30 ÷ 225 = 0.0324 “hours” (treating 7 h 30 min as 7.30).

**9 · Why it’s wrong.** 7 h 30 min is 7.5 hours, not 7.30. Even 7.5 ÷ 225 = 0.0333 h is correct but unhelpful; converting to minutes (2 min) is clearer and avoids decimal-point slips.

A second example: a field technician completes **18** fibre installations over **3** days of **7.5** net hours each. Total = 3 × 7.5 × 60 = 1,350 min; cycle time = 1,350 ÷ 18 = **75 min** per installation. (Dividing 22.5 by 18 gives 1.25 — correct only if you label it **hours**.)

${a("#/labs/cycle","Cycle Time Lab","cycle")}`},{id:"denominators",heading:"Choosing denominators and converting units and percentages",body:e`Most metric questions can be solved in ten seconds once you identify the **numerator** and the **denominator**. Use this table as your mental checklist.

| Metric | Numerator | Denominator | Typical form |
|---|---|---|---|
| DPU | Total **defects** | Total **units** | 0.09 (can exceed 1) |
| % defective | **Defective units** | Total **units** | 7.6% (max 100%) |
| DPO | Total **defects** | **Units × opportunities** | 0.015 |
| DPMO | Total **defects** | **Units × opportunities**, × 10⁶ | 15,000 |
| FTY | Units right **first time** | Units **entering** the step | 85% |
| Final yield | Good units **incl. reworked** | Units **entering** | 97.5% |
| RTY | — (product of step FTYs) | — | 75.4% |
| Cycle time | Net working **time** | Units **completed** | 2 min/unit |

**Conversions you must do without thinking:**

- Decimal → percentage: × 100 (0.7542 → 75.42%). Percentage → decimal: ÷ 100 — always convert yields to decimals **before** multiplying for RTY.
- DPO → DPMO: × 1,000,000 (0.0042 → 4,200). DPMO → percentage of opportunities: ÷ 10,000 (4,200 DPMO = 0.42%).
- DPU → DPO: ÷ opportunities per unit (0.09 ÷ 6 = 0.015).
- Time: hours × 60 = minutes; minutes × 60 = seconds; “h:mm” is not a decimal (1:45 = 1.75 h = 105 min).

**Multiple defects in one unit** are the reason DPU and % defective differ. If every defective unit had exactly one defect, the two would match; the further DPU rises above the proportion defective, the more defects are clustering on the same units — a clue worth exploring in Analyze.`},{id:"sigma-level",heading:"From DPMO to sigma level, and the Poisson link",advanced:!0,body:e`*Advanced / supplementary — useful context for reading the Reference Document tables.*

DPMO is often translated into a **[[sigma|sigma level]]**, the familiar “Six Sigma = 3.4 DPMO” idea. The conversion uses the normal distribution plus the conventional **1.5σ [[sigma-shift|shift]]**: long-term data (which includes drift between samples) is assumed to perform about 1.5σ worse than the short-term capability. The training book’s table pairs sigma levels with long-term DPMO, for example:

| Sigma level (short term) | Long-term DPMO |
|---|---:|
| 3 | 66,807 |
| 4 | 6,210 |
| 5 | 233 |
| 6 | 3.4 |

Our SIM-activation process at **15,000 DPMO** sits at roughly **3.67σ** on this convention; the 26,000-DPMO invoice process is about **3.44σ**. You will not need to compute the inverse normal by hand — the exam provides tables — but you should know that **lower DPMO means higher sigma**, and that the 3.4 figure **includes** the 1.5σ shift.

The book also shows DPU being used for attribute capability: $\text{dpu}=2{,}666 \div 20{,}000=0.1333$, which is then looked up in a Z-table (giving $Z_{LT}\approx1.11$, then $Z_{ST}=1.11+1.5=2.61$). Treat this as a Measure-phase illustration.

**Poisson link.** When defects occur randomly and independently, the chance that a unit has **zero** defects is approximately

$$Y\approx e^{-\text{DPU}}$$

With DPU = 0.09, $e^{-0.09}\approx0.914$ — close to the observed FTY of 0.924 (462 clean activations out of 500). The observed value is a little higher because the 45 defects were concentrated on only 38 units rather than spread at random. For DPU = 0.1333 the estimate is about 0.875. Use this approximation only when you have DPU but no direct first-pass count.`},{id:"lead-takt",heading:"Cycle time vs lead time vs takt time (supplementary)",advanced:!0,body:e`*Supplementary — lead time and takt time are not named in the Define syllabus text, but exam scenarios and labs often place them beside cycle time, so learn the difference.*

- **[[cycle-time|Cycle time]]** — how long it takes to *complete one unit* at a step (or the average time between completed units). It is about **the work**.
- **[[lead-time|Lead time]]** — the total elapsed time the *customer* waits, from request to delivery, **including queues and waiting**. It is about **the customer’s experience**. By Little’s Law, average lead time ≈ work in progress ÷ throughput.
- **[[takt-time|Takt time]]** — the *pace of customer demand*: available working time ÷ units demanded. It is about **the requirement**.

**Example.** The provisioning desk has 450 min of net time and customers demand **300** orders per shift. Takt time = 450 ÷ 300 = **1.5 min** per order. The desk’s cycle time is **2 min** — slower than takt — so a backlog builds. If **90** orders are waiting and the desk completes **0.5** orders per minute, average lead time ≈ 90 ÷ 0.5 = **180 min**, even though each order needs only 2 minutes of work. That gap between 2 minutes of work and 3 hours of waiting is exactly what Lean attacks.

> **Rule of thumb:** cycle time ≤ takt time keeps up with demand; lead time is usually far longer than cycle time because most of it is waiting.`}],whyItMatters:e`These metrics are the language your sponsor, finance partner and vendor will use to agree whether a project succeeded. A baseline DPMO, FTY or RTY goes straight into the [[project-charter|project charter]]; the same metric, measured the same way, proves the improvement at Control. Getting the denominator wrong can make a process look ten times better or worse than it is — and can lead to the wrong project being chosen.

For a service-operations engineer they also expose hidden cost. A managed-services report showing 97.5% of tickets “resolved” may hide an 85% first-time-fix rate; a provisioning chain of individually “good” steps may deliver only 75% right first time. On the exam, expect several calculation questions in which every distractor is the result of a specific, predictable mistake — the traps in this lesson.`,businessExample:{title:"Incident data quality in a Digital Service Operations unit",body:e`A DSO team relies on vendor-maintained incident records for monthly SLA reporting. Each ticket has **4** fields that must be right for the SLA calculation (category, priority, resolution code, affected service). A sample audit of **2,400** tickets finds **312** field defects on **250** tickets.

- DPU = 312 ÷ 2,400 = **0.13** defects per ticket.
- DPO = 312 ÷ (2,400 × 4) = 312 ÷ 9,600 = **0.0325**; DPMO = **32,500** (about 3.35σ on the conventional table).
- % defective = 250 ÷ 2,400 ≈ **10.4%**; so roughly 89.6% of tickets are right first time.

Written up for the vendor-facing report, the finding is framed as a request: *based on the sample analysis, approximately 10% of tickets carry at least one SLA-relevant field error; the team is asked to clarify the classification rules applied and provide supporting evidence for the affected records*. The DPMO becomes the baseline in the project charter, and the agreed four-field opportunity list is frozen so that the after-improvement figure is comparable.`},visual:"metricsPipeline",visualCaption:"Two animations. DPMO pipeline: 500 SIM activations × 6 opportunities = 3,000 chances; 45 defects on 38 units → DPO 0.015 → 15,000 DPMO. RTY flow: 100 orders cross four provisioning steps; amber dots need rework, the teal ribbon carries only first-pass units, and RTY = 0.97 × 0.95 × 0.88 × 0.93 ≈ 75.4% — not the 93.25% average.",definitions:[{term:"Unit",definition:"The item, transaction or service event being counted (an activation, an invoice, a ticket)."},{term:"Defect",definition:"Any failure to meet a customer requirement or specification; one unit can contain several."},{term:"Defective (unit)",definition:"A unit containing at least one defect; counted once however many defects it has."},{term:"Opportunity",definition:"A measurable, customer-relevant characteristic of a unit that can be classified acceptable or unacceptable — a chance for a defect."},{term:"DPU (defects per unit)",definition:"Total defects ÷ total units; the average number of defects per unit. Can exceed 1."},{term:"DPO (defects per opportunity)",definition:"Total defects ÷ (units × opportunities per unit)."},{term:"DPMO (defects per million opportunities)",definition:"DPO × 1,000,000; total defects per million opportunities, allowing comparison across processes of different complexity."},{term:"FTY (first time yield)",definition:"Units completing a step correctly the first time, with no rework or scrap, ÷ units entering the step. Also called first pass yield."},{term:"Final yield",definition:"Good units out, including reworked units, ÷ units entering."},{term:"RTY (rolled throughput yield)",definition:"The probability a unit passes through all process steps with zero defects; the product of the step FTYs."},{term:"Rework",definition:"Correcting a unit that failed a requirement so that it can continue; it lowers FTY but not necessarily final yield."},{term:"Scrap",definition:"A unit that cannot be recovered and is discarded or abandoned; it lowers both FTY and final yield."},{term:"Hidden factory",definition:"The unrecognised effort spent on rework and correction; visible as the gap between final yield and FTY or RTY."},{term:"Cycle time",definition:"The time to complete one unit of work from start to finish; as an average, net working time ÷ units completed."},{term:"Lead time (supplementary)",definition:"Total elapsed time from customer request to delivery, including waiting."},{term:"Takt time (supplementary)",definition:"Available working time ÷ customer demand; the pace at which units must be completed to meet demand."}],comparisons:[{title:"Defect vs defective",headers:["","Defect","Defective unit"],rows:[["What it is","A single failure to meet a requirement","A unit with one or more defects"],["Per unit","A unit can have 0, 1, 2 … many","A unit is either defective or not"],["SIM example","45 defects","38 defective activations"],["Used in","DPU, DPO, DPMO","% defective, and FTY (as its complement when there is no rework)"],["Maximum","Unlimited (DPU can exceed 1)","100% of units"]]},{title:"DPU vs DPO vs DPMO",headers:["","DPU","DPO","DPMO"],rows:[["Formula","D ÷ U","D ÷ (U × O)","D ÷ (U × O) × 10⁶"],["Accounts for complexity?","No","Yes","Yes"],["SIM example","0.09","0.015","15,000"],["Best for","Defects per item, Poisson yield","Stepping stone","Comparing processes; sigma level"]]},{title:"FTY vs RTY vs final yield",headers:["","FTY","RTY","Final yield"],rows:[["Scope","One step","Whole multi-step process","One step or whole process"],["Reworked units count as good?","No","No","Yes"],["Calculation","First-pass good ÷ entering","Product of step FTYs","Good out ÷ entering"],["Example","Tickets: 85%","Provisioning: 75.4%","Tickets: 97.5%"],["Reveals","Rework at a step","Compounded loss end-to-end","What the customer finally receives"]]},{title:"Cycle time vs lead time vs takt time",headers:["","Cycle time","Lead time (supplementary)","Takt time (supplementary)"],rows:[["Question","How long to complete one unit?","How long does the customer wait?","How often must we finish one to meet demand?"],["Formula","Net working time ÷ units completed","≈ WIP ÷ throughput (Little’s Law)","Available time ÷ customer demand"],["Includes waiting?","No — the work itself","Yes — queues included","Not applicable — a target pace"],["Provisioning example","2 min","≈ 180 min","1.5 min"]]}],misconceptions:[{myth:"DPU and percentage defective are the same thing.",reality:"DPU counts every defect; % defective counts units with at least one defect. They match only when no unit has more than one defect."},{myth:"DPU can never be more than 1.",reality:"If units average more than one defect each (12 defects on 8 routers), DPU is 1.5."},{myth:"More opportunities per unit always means a worse process.",reality:"More opportunities enlarge the denominator, so the same defect count gives a lower DPMO — which is why the opportunity list must be fixed and honest."},{myth:"RTY is the average of the step yields.",reality:"RTY is the product. The average (93.25%) overstates the true end-to-end first-pass performance (75.4%)."},{myth:"If 97.5% of tickets are eventually resolved, the process is 97.5% efficient.",reality:"That is final yield. FTY (85%) shows that 12.5% of tickets needed rework — the hidden factory."},{myth:"Cycle time and lead time are interchangeable.",reality:"Cycle time is the work on one unit; lead time is the customer’s total wait, including queues, and is usually much longer."},{myth:"A 6σ process has a 3.4% defect rate.",reality:"It is 3.4 defects per million opportunities (0.00034%), based on the conventional 1.5σ shift."}],examTraps:[{trap:"The stem gives “45 defects found on 38 units” and asks for DPU.",explanation:"Use total defects (45 ÷ 500 = 0.09). 38 ÷ 500 = 7.6% is the proportion defective, a distractor.",tag:"total-defects"},{trap:"“What percentage of units were defective?” after a stem full of defect counts.",explanation:"Now the numerator is defective units (38 ÷ 500 = 7.6%), not defects.",tag:"defective-units"},{trap:"“Defects per opportunity” or “per million opportunities”.",explanation:"The denominator must be units × opportunities per unit (3,000), not units (500).",tag:"per-opportunity"},{trap:"“What is the first pass yield?” with rework data in the stem.",explanation:"Reworked units are not first-pass successes: 1,020 ÷ 1,200 = 85%, not 97.5%.",tag:"first-pass"},{trap:"“Proportion completed without rework” — the answer options include final yield.",explanation:"“Without rework” is FTY language. Exclude anything repaired or reopened.",tag:"without-rework"},{trap:"RTY options include the average of the step yields and the lowest step yield.",explanation:"RTY = product of step FTYs (0.7542). It can never exceed the lowest step’s FTY.",tag:"most"},{trap:"Cycle time with time given as “7 h 30 min” and options computed from 7.30.",explanation:"Convert to one unit first: 450 min ÷ 225 = 2 min. 7 h 30 min = 7.5 h, not 7.30."},{trap:"DPMO given as a percentage, e.g. “15,000 DPMO = 15%”.",explanation:"DPMO ÷ 10,000 gives the percentage of opportunities: 15,000 DPMO = 1.5%.",tag:"per-opportunity"},{trap:"“Which yield metric is LEAST affected by rework?”",explanation:"Final yield — rework recovers units, so final yield stays high while FTY and RTY fall.",tag:"least"}],workedExamples:[{title:"Mixed challenge: 500 SIM activations",problem:e`500 SIM activations are audited. Each has **6** opportunities. **45** defects are found on **38** activations. Calculate DPU, DPO, DPMO, % defective and FTY (assume defective activations had to be reworked).`,steps:["DPU = 45 ÷ 500 = **0.09**.","Total opportunities = 500 × 6 = 3,000; DPO = 45 ÷ 3,000 = **0.015**.","DPMO = 0.015 × 1,000,000 = **15,000**.","% defective = 38 ÷ 500 = **7.6%**.","FTY = (500 − 38) ÷ 500 = 462 ÷ 500 = **92.4%**."],answer:"DPU 0.09 · DPO 0.015 · DPMO 15,000 · 7.6% defective · FTY 92.4%",wrongApproach:{calculation:"DPMO = 45 ÷ 500 × 1,000,000 = 90,000",whyWrong:"This divides by units, not opportunities — it is defects per million units. The per-opportunity denominator is 500 × 6 = 3,000."}},{title:"DPU vs proportion defective on invoices",problem:e`2,000 invoices; 260 defects found on 190 invoices; 5 opportunities per invoice. Find DPU, % defective and DPMO.`,steps:["DPU = 260 ÷ 2,000 = **0.13**.","% defective = 190 ÷ 2,000 = **9.5%**.","DPO = 260 ÷ (2,000 × 5) = 260 ÷ 10,000 = 0.026; DPMO = **26,000**."],answer:"DPU 0.13 · 9.5% defective · DPMO 26,000",wrongApproach:{calculation:"DPU = 190 ÷ 2,000 = 0.095",whyWrong:"190 is defective units. DPU must use all 260 defects, including second and third defects on the same invoice."}},{title:"Multiple defects in one unit: DPU above 1",problem:e`8 customer-returned routers are diagnosed. Between them they have **12** defects; **5** of the 8 have at least one defect. Each router has 10 inspection opportunities. Find DPU, % defective and DPMO.`,steps:["DPU = 12 ÷ 8 = **1.5** defects per router.","% defective = 5 ÷ 8 = **62.5%**.","DPMO = 12 ÷ (8 × 10) × 1,000,000 = **150,000**."],answer:"DPU 1.5 · 62.5% defective · DPMO 150,000",wrongApproach:{calculation:"“DPU cannot exceed 1, so DPU = 5 ÷ 8 = 0.625.”",whyWrong:"DPU is an average count of defects, not a proportion; when units carry several defects it can exceed 1."}},{title:"4-step provisioning: RTY vs the wrong average",problem:e`Step FTYs: order capture 97%, credit check 95%, line configuration 88%, activation test 93%. Find RTY and comment on the average.`,steps:["Decimals: 0.97, 0.95, 0.88, 0.93.","0.97 × 0.95 = 0.9215 → × 0.88 = 0.81092 → × 0.93 = **0.7542**.","RTY ≈ **75.4%**: about 75 of 100 orders are right first time end-to-end.","Line configuration (88%) is the weakest step and the first target."],answer:"RTY ≈ 75.4%",wrongApproach:{calculation:"(97 + 95 + 88 + 93) ÷ 4 = 93.25%",whyWrong:"A unit must pass every step; probabilities of successive “and” events multiply. The average overstates performance by about 18 points."}},{title:"Book value-stream example: one weak step",problem:e`A five-step cheque-processing stream has step yields 0.92, 0.94, 0.59, 0.96 and 0.96 (training book, value stream map). What is RTY?`,steps:["Running product: 0.92 → 0.8648 → 0.5102 → 0.4898 → **0.4702**.","RTY ≈ **0.47**: fewer than half of items pass every step first time.","The 0.59 step alone removes over 40% of first-pass units."],answer:"RTY ≈ 0.47 (47%)",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Lowest step = 0.59, so RTY = 59%",whyWrong:"The lowest step is an upper bound on RTY, not RTY itself; the other four losses still compound on top of it."}},{title:"FTY with rework vs final yield",problem:e`1,200 incident tickets: 1,020 resolved first time; 150 reopened then resolved; 30 cancelled and re-raised. Find FTY and final yield.`,steps:["FTY = 1,020 ÷ 1,200 = **85%** (reworked and scrapped excluded).","Final yield = (1,020 + 150) ÷ 1,200 = 1,170 ÷ 1,200 = **97.5%**.","Gap = 12.5 points = 150 tickets of hidden-factory rework; 30 tickets (2.5%) were scrapped."],answer:"FTY 85% · final yield 97.5%",wrongApproach:{calculation:"FTY = 1,170 ÷ 1,200 = 97.5%",whyWrong:"That is final yield. Reopened tickets were not right the first time."}},{title:"RTY from counts in a contact centre",problem:e`A complaint-handling process has three steps, each measured on 1,000 cases: logging (980 right first time), diagnosis (900), resolution (855). Find each FTY and the RTY.`,steps:["FTYs: 980 ÷ 1,000 = 0.98; 900 ÷ 1,000 = 0.90; 855 ÷ 1,000 = 0.855.","RTY = 0.98 × 0.90 × 0.855 = **0.7541** ≈ 75.4%."],answer:"RTY ≈ 75.4%",wrongApproach:{calculation:"(980 + 900 + 855) ÷ 3,000 = 91.2%",whyWrong:"Pooling the counts is another form of averaging. RTY needs the product of the individual step yields."}},{title:"Cycle time with consistent units",problem:e`A provisioning desk has 7 h 30 min of net working time and completes 225 orders. Find the cycle time in minutes and in seconds.`,steps:["Convert: 7 h 30 min = 7 × 60 + 30 = 450 min.","Cycle time = 450 ÷ 225 = **2 min** per order.","Seconds: 2 × 60 = **120 s**."],answer:"2 minutes (120 seconds) per order",wrongApproach:{calculation:"7.30 ÷ 225 = 0.0324 h",whyWrong:"7 h 30 min is 7.5 h, not 7.30. Convert mixed units to one unit before dividing."}},{title:"Cycle time across several days",problem:e`A technician completes 18 fibre installations over 3 days with 7.5 net working hours per day. What is the average cycle time per installation in minutes?`,steps:["Total time = 3 × 7.5 h = 22.5 h = 22.5 × 60 = 1,350 min.","Cycle time = 1,350 ÷ 18 = **75 min**."],answer:"75 minutes per installation",wrongApproach:{calculation:"22.5 ÷ 18 = 1.25 “minutes”",whyWrong:"The arithmetic is right but the unit is hours (1.25 h = 75 min). An unlabelled or mislabelled unit is a wrong answer."}},{title:"Advanced: DPMO to sigma level, and a Poisson check",problem:e`For the SIM process (DPU 0.09, DPMO 15,000, observed FTY 92.4%), estimate the sigma level and the Poisson first-pass yield.`,steps:["Sigma level with the conventional 1.5σ shift: DPMO 15,000 → ≈ **3.67σ** (between 3σ = 66,807 and 4σ = 6,210 DPMO).","Poisson: Y ≈ e^(−0.09) ≈ **0.914**.","Observed FTY is 0.924 — slightly higher because the 45 defects clustered on 38 units instead of being spread randomly."],answer:"≈ 3.67σ; Poisson estimate ≈ 91.4% vs observed 92.4%",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Sigma level = 6 − (15,000 ÷ 1,000,000) = 5.985σ",whyWrong:"Sigma level comes from the normal distribution (via tables), not from subtracting a defect rate. Lower DPMO means higher sigma, but the relationship is not linear."}},{title:"Supplementary: cycle time vs takt time vs lead time",problem:e`The provisioning desk has 450 min of net time and customer demand of 300 orders per shift. Cycle time is 2 min. 90 orders are waiting; throughput is 0.5 orders per minute. Find takt time and the approximate lead time. Can the desk keep up?`,steps:["Takt time = 450 ÷ 300 = **1.5 min** per order.","Cycle time 2 min > takt 1.5 min → the desk cannot keep up; a queue grows.","Lead time ≈ WIP ÷ throughput = 90 ÷ 0.5 = **180 min**."],answer:"Takt 1.5 min; lead time ≈ 180 min; no — cycle time exceeds takt.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"“Each order takes 2 min, so customers wait 2 min.”",whyWrong:"That confuses cycle time with lead time. Customers also wait in the queue, which dominates the 180-minute lead time."}}],lab:"dpmo",check:[{id:"chk-1.2.5-1",topic:"1.2.5",category:"calculation",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!0,stem:"A billing team checks **1,200** bills, each with **5** opportunities for error, and finds **18** defects. What is the DPMO?",options:["15,000","3,000","1,500","3.6"],answer:1,explanation:"DPMO = 18 ÷ (1,200 × 5) × 1,000,000 = 18 ÷ 6,000 × 10⁶ = 3,000.",whyWrong:["15,000 = 18 ÷ 1,200 × 10⁶ — divides by units, not opportunities.","","1,500 doubles the denominator by mistake (18 ÷ 12,000 × 10⁶).","3.6 = 18 ÷ 5 — divides defects by opportunities per unit only."],traps:["per-opportunity"]},{id:"chk-1.2.5-2",topic:"1.2.5",category:"calculation",difficulty:"trap",numerical:!0,stem:"A three-step order process has first time yields of 90%, 90% and 90%. What is the rolled throughput yield?",options:["90.0%","72.9%","81.0%","270%"],answer:1,explanation:"RTY = 0.90 × 0.90 × 0.90 = 0.729 = 72.9%.",whyWrong:["90% is the average (and each step) — RTY multiplies.","","81% multiplies only two of the three steps.","270% adds the yields; a yield cannot exceed 100%."],traps:["first-pass"]},{id:"chk-1.2.5-3",topic:"1.2.5",category:"calculation",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!0,stem:"Of **300** change requests, **276** are implemented correctly first time, **18** are rolled back and successfully re-implemented, and **6** are abandoned. What is the first time yield?",options:["98%","94%","92%","8%"],answer:2,explanation:"FTY counts only first-time successes without rework: 276 ÷ 300 = 92%.",whyWrong:["98% = (276 + 18) ÷ 300 is final yield — it counts reworked changes as good.","94% has no basis in the data (it subtracts only the 18 rework cases from 100%).","","8% is the first-time failure rate, the complement of FTY."],traps:["without-rework","first-pass"]},{id:"chk-1.2.5-4",topic:"1.2.5",category:"calculation",difficulty:"beginner",numerical:!0,stem:"An NOC engineer has **4 hours** of net working time and closes **96** alarms. What is the average cycle time per alarm?",options:["0.042 minutes","24 minutes","2.5 minutes","0.4 minutes"],answer:2,explanation:"4 h = 240 min; 240 ÷ 96 = 2.5 minutes per alarm.",whyWrong:["4 ÷ 96 = 0.042 is in hours, mislabelled as minutes.","24 = 96 ÷ 4 is alarms per hour (throughput), not time per alarm.","","0.4 alarms per minute is throughput (96 ÷ 240), the reciprocal of cycle time."]},{id:"chk-1.2.5-5",topic:"1.2.5",category:"concept",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"One activation order has a wrong tariff code **and** a missing welcome SMS. How is it counted?",options:["One defect and one defective unit","Two defects and one defective unit","Two defects and two defective units","One defect and two defective units"],answer:1,explanation:"Each failed requirement is a defect (2), but the unit is counted once as defective (1).",whyWrong:["Both failures are separate defects.","","A single unit cannot be two defective units.","This reverses the two concepts."],traps:["total-defects","defective-units"]},{id:"chk-1.2.5-6",topic:"1.2.5",category:"calculation",difficulty:"beginner",numerical:!0,stem:"**600** service tickets contain **84** data-entry defects. What is the DPU?",options:["7.14","0.14","14","0.014"],answer:1,explanation:"DPU = 84 ÷ 600 = 0.14 defects per ticket.",whyWrong:["7.14 = 600 ÷ 84 — the ratio is upside down.","","14 is the percentage form (14%) written as if it were DPU.","0.014 slips a decimal place."],traps:["total-defects"]}],takeaways:["Fix the vocabulary first: unit, defect, defective, opportunity.","DPU = defects ÷ units; % defective = defective units ÷ units — they differ when units carry several defects.","DPMO = defects ÷ (units × opportunities) × 10⁶; opportunities must be measurable, customer-relevant and consistent.","FTY excludes rework and scrap; final yield includes reworked units; the gap is the hidden factory.","RTY = product of step FTYs — never add or average — and it is never higher than the weakest step.","Cycle time = net working time ÷ units completed; convert to one time unit before dividing.","Lower DPMO means higher sigma level; 3.4 DPMO at 6σ includes the conventional 1.5σ shift.","Lead time includes waiting; takt time is the demand pace (both supplementary)."],flashcards:[{front:"DPU formula",back:"Total defects ÷ total units. Can exceed 1."},{front:"DPO formula",back:"Total defects ÷ (units × opportunities per unit)."},{front:"DPMO formula",back:"Defects ÷ (units × opportunities) × 1,000,000."},{front:"Defect vs defective",back:"Defect = one failure to meet a requirement; defective = a unit with ≥ 1 defect."},{front:"Three tests for a valid opportunity",back:"Measurable, customer-relevant, consistently counted."},{front:"FTY",back:"Units right the first time (no rework, no scrap) ÷ units entering."},{front:"Final yield",back:"Good units out including reworked ÷ units entering."},{front:"RTY",back:"Product of all step FTYs; probability of passing every step with zero defects."},{front:"RTY of 97%, 95%, 88%, 93%",back:"≈ 75.4% (not the 93.25% average)."},{front:"Cycle time",back:"Net working time ÷ units completed — time to complete one unit."},{front:"7 h 30 min in minutes",back:"450 minutes (7.5 h, not 7.30)."},{front:"DPO → DPMO",back:"× 1,000,000. DPMO → % of opportunities: ÷ 10,000."},{front:"3.4 DPMO",back:"Six Sigma performance on the conventional 1.5σ-shift table."},{front:"Poisson yield from DPU",back:"Y ≈ e^(−DPU); DPU 0.09 → ≈ 0.914."},{front:"Takt time (supplementary)",back:"Available time ÷ customer demand — the required pace."},{front:"Lead time (supplementary)",back:"Total customer wait from request to delivery, including queues (≈ WIP ÷ throughput)."}],coverage:"official-only",supplementaryNotes:["The Define chapter of the training book was not supplied. Formulas and explanations here are independently written from standard Lean Six Sigma practice; the supplied pages support the glossary wording (DPU, DPMO, opportunities, RTY, cycle time, defect, defective), the RTY product example (p. 105), the DPU example (p. 225) and the sigma/DPMO table with the 1.5σ shift (p. 223).","The glossary in the supplied pages has no FTY entry; the FTY and final-yield definitions follow the IASSC Body of Knowledge convention.","Lead time and takt time are supplementary: they are not named in the Define syllabus text.","The sigma-level conversion and the Poisson approximation Y ≈ e^(−DPU) are advanced/supplementary context; sigma values were computed with the app’s verified inverse-normal function.","All telecom, invoice and contact-centre figures are illustrative teaching data, not real operator or vendor data."],sources:[{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 7",pdfPage:7,note:"Define topic 1.2.5 “Basic Six Sigma Metrics: including DPU, DPMO, FTY, RTY Cycle Time; deriving these metrics”."},{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 6",pdfPage:6,note:"Exam is closed book with a formula Reference Document."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 585",pdfPage:88,note:"Glossary: cycle time, defect, defective, DPMO (defects per total opportunities, per million) and DPU (defects ÷ units)."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 587",pdfPage:90,note:"Glossary: opportunities — characteristics classifiable as acceptable or unacceptable."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 588",pdfPage:91,note:"Glossary: RTY — probability of a unit passing all process steps with zero defects."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 584",pdfPage:87,note:"Glossary: capability can be expressed as yield, defect rate (dpm, dpmo) or sigma score."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 105",pdfPage:8,note:"Value stream map: RTY = .92 × .94 × .59 × .96 × .96 = .47 (Measure-phase context)."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"pp. 115–116",pdfPage:18,note:"Level-3 process maps usually carry estimates of DPU, yield and RTY (Measure 2.1.x context)."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 223",pdfPage:26,note:"Sigma level vs short- and long-term DPMO table; the 1.5σ shift."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 225",pdfPage:28,note:"DPU example: 2,666 ÷ 20,000 = .1333, then Z-table lookup (attribute capability)."}]},j=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:g},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),y={id:"1.3.1",module:3,title:"Building a Business Case & Project Charter",subtitle:"Problem, goal, scope, team and the case for doing it now",minutes:16,objectives:["Explain what a business case must answer — why this problem, why now, what happens if we do nothing, and how it links to strategy.","List the elements of a project charter and describe the job each element does.","Write a problem statement that states what, where, when, how big and the impact — with no cause, no solution and no blame.","Recognise a predefined solution disguised as a problem statement and rewrite it correctly.","Turn a baseline into a SMART goal statement and set clear in-scope and out-of-scope boundaries that resist scope creep.","Treat the charter as a living agreement that is reviewed and re-signed at every tollgate."],overview:`Every [[dmaic|DMAIC]] project starts with two short documents that carry a surprising amount of weight. The [[business-case]] answers the leadership question: *why should we spend scarce people, money and attention on this problem, and why now?* The [[project-charter]] answers the team question: *what exactly are we fixing, how will we know we have succeeded, where are the boundaries, who is involved and by when?*

Think of the business case as the reason the journey is worth taking and the charter as the agreed travel plan. A Green Belt who skips either one usually ends up with a project that is too big, aimed at the wrong target, or quietly abandoned when a more urgent fire appears. In this lesson you will learn to build both, with special attention to the skill examiners love to test: spotting a solution that is pretending to be a problem.`,sections:[{id:"business-case",heading:"The business case: why this, why now",body:`A business case is a short, evidence-based argument that a problem deserves a project. It is not a sales pitch and it is not a technical design. A strong business case answers four questions:

1. **Why this problem?** What is the measurable pain — in customer experience, cost, risk or capacity? Use real data from the [[voc|Voice of the Customer]] and the [[vob|Voice of the Business]].
2. **Why now?** What makes this the right moment — a contract renewal, a rising trend, a regulatory deadline, a new product launch?
3. **What happens if we do nothing?** The consequences of not acting: penalties keep accruing, churn rises, a backlog grows, a regulator escalates.
4. **How does it link to strategy?** Which business objective or [[kpi]] does it move — customer retention, operating margin, network availability?

Picture a telecom operator whose enterprise customers have contractual [[sla|SLAs]] for restoring Priority 1 incidents within four hours. Breaches trigger service credits. A good business case would read: *Service credits for restoration SLA breaches have risen for three consecutive quarters and are now the largest single item of enterprise revenue leakage. Two of our five largest accounts renew next year and have cited restoration performance in quarterly reviews. If the trend continues, credits will exceed the annual budget for this line by mid-year.* Notice that it quantifies the pain, explains the urgency and connects to retention strategy — without proposing any fix.

The business case usually carries an early estimate of the [[copq|cost of poor quality]]. The book's DMAIC roadmap places "Estimate COPQ" and "Establish Team" among the Define steps, and its Control checklist later asks whether the results match "the requirements of the business case and improvement goals". So the business case is not filed and forgotten: it becomes the yardstick the project is judged against at the end.`},{id:"charter-elements",heading:"Anatomy of a project charter",body:`A project charter is normally a single page. Its brevity is a feature: if you cannot fit the project on one page, it is probably too big or not yet understood. The typical elements are:

| Element | The job it does |
|---|---|
| Business case | Why the project matters and why now |
| [[problem-statement|Problem statement]] | What is wrong, where, since when, how big, with what impact |
| [[goal-statement|Goal statement]] | The measurable result and the date it will be achieved |
| [[project-scope|Scope]] — in and out | The process boundaries: where the project starts and stops |
| Milestones / timeline | Target dates for each DMAIC [[tollgate]] |
| Team and roles | Belt, team members, [[process-owner]], subject experts |
| Sponsor / [[champion]] sign-off | The leader who owns the result and removes barriers |
| [[stakeholder|Stakeholders]] | Who is affected and must be informed or consulted |
| Risks, assumptions, constraints | What could derail the project and what is being taken for granted |
| Resources | Time, data access, tools, budget the team needs |
| Preliminary benefits | The estimated financial and customer benefit (to be validated later) |

A few elements deserve a closer look. **Risks** are uncertain events that could hurt the project (*the vendor may not release ticket data in time*). **Assumptions** are things you are treating as true without proof (*incident priority codes are applied consistently*). **Constraints** are fixed limits you must work within (*no change to the vendor contract before renewal; no new headcount*). Mixing these up is a common charter weakness, because each needs a different response: risks need mitigation, assumptions need checking, constraints need respecting.

**Preliminary benefits** are deliberately labelled preliminary. They are [[estimated-benefits]], and they will be refined in Measure and validated with Finance before anyone claims them — a theme you will meet again in lesson 1.3.3.`},{id:"problem-statement",heading:"Writing a problem statement — and catching disguised solutions",body:`The book's glossary describes a problem statement as one that succinctly bounds and describes the business problem. In practice, a good problem statement answers five questions — **what, where, when, how big, and what impact** — and avoids three things: **no cause, no solution, no blame**.

> In Q2 2026, 225 of 1,250 Priority 1 and 2 enterprise incidents (18%) in the Northern region were restored after the 4-hour SLA, generating OMR 45,000 in service credits and two formal customer escalations.

Every word earns its place: the defect (restored late), the location (Northern region, enterprise P1/P2), the time window (Q2 2026), the size (225 of 1,250; 18%) and the impact (credits, escalations).

The trickiest exam skill in this topic is **recognising a predefined solution disguised as a problem statement**. A disguised solution sounds like a problem but already contains the answer. Watch for these warning signs: words such as *lack of, need to, insufficient, no system for, should implement*; a named tool, technology or headcount; or a stated cause.

| Disguised solution (weak) | Why it fails | Problem statement (strong) |
|---|---|---|
| "We lack an automated ticket-routing tool." | Names the fix; assumes the cause | "Since January, 31% of P2 incidents waited more than 45 minutes for first assignment, against a 15-minute target." |
| "Field teams need more training on fibre splicing." | Training is a solution; also implies blame | "In the last 6 months, 14% of fibre repairs in the Eastern zone required a repeat visit within 7 days, costing about OMR 18,000 per month." |
| "The vendor's NOC engineers are careless with priority codes." | Blames people; asserts a cause | "In Q1, 22% of incidents audited were assigned a priority that did not match the impact criteria, delaying response on 96 P1 cases." |
| "We need to hire two more invoice clerks." | Headcount is a solution | "In FY2025, 9% of supplier invoices were paid after the contractual due date, incurring OMR 27,000 in late-payment charges." |

Why does this matter so much? Because a disguised solution **skips Measure and Analyze**. If the charter says "implement auto-routing", the team will implement auto-routing — even if the real [[root-cause]] of slow assignment is unclear priority rules. Remember [[y-equals-f-x|Y = f(X)]]: the problem statement describes the Y; finding the X's is the work of later phases.`},{id:"goal-statement",heading:"Goal statements and SMART",body:`The goal statement (the book calls it an *objective statement* — a succinct statement of goals, timing and expectations) mirrors the problem statement. It uses the same metric and the same units, then states the target and the date. A [[smart-goal|SMART]] goal is:

- **Specific** — one clearly named metric and population.
- **Measurable** — a number with a baseline and a target, using an agreed [[operational-definition]].
- **Achievable** — ambitious but realistic for the team, budget and time.
- **Relevant** — it moves the business case, not a side issue.
- **Time-bound** — a completion date.

Continuing the telecom example: *Reduce the share of P1/P2 enterprise incidents in the Northern region restored after the 4-hour SLA from 18% to 6% by 31 March 2027, without increasing the repeat-incident rate.* Notice that the goal does not say *how* — the method will be discovered during Analyze and Improve. The final clause protects a guard-rail metric; you will meet those in lesson 1.3.2.

How ambitious should the target be? The book's glossary describes *breakthrough improvement* as improvement at or near 70% over baseline. Here the reduction from 18% to 6% is about 67%, close to that level. Not every Green Belt project must reach breakthrough, but a target of "a 2% improvement" rarely justifies a DMAIC project.`},{id:"scope",heading:"Scope, boundaries and scope creep",body:`Scope draws the fence around the project. Define it as a process with a clear start and end point, then list what is [[in-scope]] and [[out-of-scope]]. For an invoice-dispute project, in scope might be *enterprise invoices issued from the billing platform, from bill run to dispute closure*; out of scope might be *consumer prepaid billing, tariff design and the replacement of the billing platform*.

[[scope-creep|Scope creep]] is the gradual, unapproved expansion of a project: *while you're looking at disputes, could you also fix the collections letters?* Each addition seems small, but together they dilute the team's effort and push the finish line away. The defence is the charter itself: any change to scope is a deliberate decision by the Champion, recorded in a new charter version — not a favour agreed in a corridor. A scope that is too wide ("improve customer experience") is as dangerous as one that is too narrow to matter.`},{id:"living-document",heading:"Common charter mistakes and the charter as a living document",body:`The most frequent charter mistakes are predictable:

- A **solution in the problem statement** (the disguised-solution trap).
- A goal with **no baseline** or **no date**.
- A **different metric** in the problem and goal statements, so success cannot be judged.
- **Boil-the-ocean scope** — several processes, regions and products at once.
- **No real sponsor**, or a Champion who has not actually signed.
- **Benefits stated as fact** before any data has been validated.
- **Risks, assumptions and constraints** lumped together or left blank.

Finally, a charter is a **living document**. It is drafted in Define, but Measure may reveal that the baseline was wrong or the problem is concentrated in one region. The book's Measure checklist explicitly asks "Has the Problem Statement changed?". At each tollgate the Belt and Champion review the charter, update it if facts have changed, and re-confirm commitment. What must *not* happen is silent drift — a charter that says one thing while the team quietly works on another.`}],whyItMatters:"For an Operational Excellence engineer working with managed-service vendors, the charter is often the only document both sides sign before improvement work begins. A precise problem statement keeps conversations factual: it describes the gap in the data rather than accusing anyone. A clear scope stops a vendor improvement project turning into a contract renegotiation. A dated, measurable goal gives the Champion something concrete to sponsor and Finance something concrete to validate. Most failed improvement projects can be traced back to a weak charter — vague problem, missing baseline, uncontrolled scope or absent sponsor.",businessExample:{title:"Restoration SLA breaches at a telecom operator",body:`A Green Belt in a Digital Service Operations unit was asked to "roll out a new monitoring tool to fix SLA breaches". Instead of accepting the solution, she drafted a charter. The data showed that in Q2, 225 of 1,250 enterprise P1/P2 incidents (18%) in the Northern region were restored after the 4-hour SLA, costing OMR 45,000 in service credits.

Her charter set the goal of reducing late restorations to 6% by 31 March 2027, without raising the repeat-incident rate. In scope: incident logging to restoration confirmation for Northern-region enterprise incidents. Out of scope: consumer incidents, vendor contract terms and network capacity investment. The Head of Service Operations signed as Champion; the vendor's service delivery manager and the NOC lead joined as team members; Finance was named to validate benefits.

In Measure, the team discovered that most late restorations were waiting for a field dispatch decision — not a monitoring gap at all. Because the charter described the problem rather than the tool, the team was free to follow the data. The monitoring tool was never bought.`},visual:"charterAnatomy",visualCaption:"A one-page charter assembles section by section — each block answers one question, and the Champion signs only when every block is complete.",definitions:[{term:"Business case",definition:"A short, evidence-based argument for why a problem deserves a project now: the pain, the urgency, the consequence of inaction and the link to strategy."},{term:"Project charter",definition:"A one-page agreement between the team and its sponsor that records the business case, problem, goal, scope, milestones, team, stakeholders, risks and preliminary benefits."},{term:"Problem statement",definition:"A concise description of what is wrong, where, since when, how big and with what impact — containing no cause, no solution and no blame."},{term:"Goal (objective) statement",definition:"A statement of the measurable result to be achieved and by when, using the same metric as the problem statement."},{term:"SMART",definition:"Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant, Time-bound — a test of whether a goal statement is usable."},{term:"Scope (in / out)",definition:"The boundaries of the project: the process start and end points and an explicit list of what is included and excluded."},{term:"Scope creep",definition:"The gradual, unapproved expansion of a project beyond its chartered boundaries."},{term:"Champion / Sponsor",definition:"The leader who owns the business result, signs the charter, provides resources and removes barriers; accountable for results."},{term:"Assumption vs constraint",definition:"An assumption is something treated as true without proof and should be checked; a constraint is a fixed limit the project must work within."}],comparisons:[{title:"Business case vs problem statement vs goal statement",headers:["","Business case","Problem statement","Goal statement"],rows:[["Main question","Why this, why now?","What is wrong, how big?","What result, by when?"],["Audience","Leadership and Finance","Team and sponsor","Team, sponsor, Finance"],["Contains","Strategic link, urgency, consequence of inaction, estimated value","What, where, when, magnitude, impact","Metric, baseline, target, date"],["Must NOT contain","Detailed solution design","Cause, solution or blame","The method of achieving it"],["Telecom example","Credits are the largest leakage item; key accounts renew next year","18% of P1/P2 incidents restored after 4-h SLA in Q2","Reduce from 18% to 6% by 31 Mar 2027"]]},{title:"Risk vs assumption vs constraint",headers:["","Risk","Assumption","Constraint"],rows:[["Definition","An uncertain event that could harm the project","Something taken as true without proof","A fixed limit on the project"],["Response","Mitigate and monitor","Verify early","Respect and plan around"],["Example","Vendor ticket export may be delayed","Priority codes are applied consistently","No contract change before renewal"]]}],misconceptions:[{myth:"A good problem statement explains why the problem happens.",reality:"The cause is unknown in Define. Stating it skips Measure and Analyze and points the team at a guess."},{myth:'"We lack a dashboard" is a problem statement.',reality:"It is a solution in disguise. The problem is the measurable gap the dashboard is supposed to close."},{myth:"Once signed, the charter is fixed for the life of the project.",reality:"The charter is a living document. It is reviewed at every tollgate and updated, with sponsor approval, when the data changes the picture."},{myth:"A wider scope delivers more benefit.",reality:"Overly wide scope is a top cause of stalled projects. A tight scope that is finished beats a broad one that never closes."},{myth:"The benefits on the charter are the project’s savings.",reality:"Charter benefits are preliminary estimates. Only benefits validated after Control, usually with Finance, count as realized."}],examTraps:[{trap:'Choosing the option that sounds most decisive ("Implement auto-routing to reduce delays").',explanation:"When asked for the BEST problem statement, pick the one with what/where/when/how big/impact and no solution. Action verbs such as implement, install, hire or train are red flags.",tag:"best"},{trap:'Picking an option that states a cause ("…due to untrained staff").',explanation:'Causes belong to Analyze. Any problem statement containing "due to", "because of" or "caused by" in Define is suspect.',tag:"best"},{trap:'"Which is NOT an element of a project charter?"',explanation:"Business case, problem, goal, scope, milestones, team and sponsor are all charter elements. A verified root cause or a selected solution is NOT — those come later.",tag:"not"},{trap:'"What should the Belt do FIRST when the sponsor asks to add a second process?"',explanation:"Do not just absorb it. Assess the impact on scope, timeline and resources and take a formal charter change to the Champion for decision.",tag:"first"},{trap:"Treating charter benefits as already realized.",explanation:"Benefits on the charter are estimated benefits. Realized benefits are confirmed only after the improvement is sustained in Control and validated.",tag:"estimated-benefits"}],workedExamples:[{title:"Unmasking a disguised solution",problem:'A draft charter for a contact centre reads: *"Problem: We need a new knowledge base because agents don’t know the answers, causing repeat calls."* Rewrite it as a proper problem statement. Data available: in March, 41,600 calls were handled; 7,488 were repeat calls within 72 hours about the same issue; each repeat call costs about OMR 1.20.',steps:['Spot the warning signs: "need a new knowledge base" is a **solution**; "agents don’t know the answers" is an **asserted cause** with a hint of **blame**.',"Keep only the observable defect: a customer calls back about the same issue within 72 hours.","Add size: $7{,}488 \\div 41{,}600 = 0.18$, so 18% of calls were repeats.","Add impact: $7{,}488 \\times 1.20 = 8{,}985.60$, about OMR 9,000 in handling cost for the month, plus customer effort.",'Assemble: *"In March 2026, 7,488 of 41,600 calls (18%) handled by the consumer contact centre were repeat contacts within 72 hours on the same issue, costing about OMR 9,000 in handling time."*'],answer:"In March 2026, 7,488 of 41,600 calls (18%) were repeat contacts within 72 hours on the same issue, costing about OMR 9,000 — no cause, no solution, no blame.",wrongApproach:{calculation:'"Repeat calls are high because the knowledge base is outdated; we will replace it by Q3."',whyWrong:"It still states a cause and a solution. If the true driver is, say, an unclear billing message, the new knowledge base will not move the metric."}},{title:"From baseline to a SMART goal",problem:"In Q2, 225 of 1,250 enterprise P1/P2 incidents in the Northern region were restored after the 4-hour SLA. Each breach triggered an average service credit of OMR 200. The Champion wants a goal for the end of Q1 2027. Write the goal and check how ambitious it is if the target breach rate is 6%.",steps:["Baseline breach rate: $225 \\div 1{,}250 = 0.18 = 18\\%$.","Baseline impact: $225 \\times 200 = 45{,}000$ — OMR 45,000 per quarter in credits.","Proposed target: 6% breach rate. Percentage improvement: $\\dfrac{18 - 6}{18} \\times 100 = 66.7\\%$.","Compare with the book’s glossary description of breakthrough improvement (at or near 70% over baseline): 66.7% is close to breakthrough level — ambitious but defensible.",'Write it SMART: *"Reduce the share of Northern-region enterprise P1/P2 incidents restored after the 4-hour SLA from 18% to 6% by 31 March 2027, without increasing the repeat-incident rate."*'],answer:"Goal: 18% → 6% by 31 March 2027 (a 66.7% improvement on baseline), with a guard-rail on repeat incidents.",wrongApproach:{calculation:'18% − 6% = 12%, so "a 12% improvement".',whyWrong:"That is a 12 percentage-point reduction, not a 12% improvement. The relative improvement is 12 ÷ 18 = 66.7%. Mixing points and percent understates the goal."}},{title:"Holding the scope line",problem:"An invoice-dispute project is chartered for enterprise postpaid invoices, from bill run to dispute closure. Mid-Measure, three requests arrive: (a) include consumer prepaid top-up complaints; (b) analyse disputes caused by incorrect roaming charges on enterprise invoices; (c) redesign the collections letter. Which are in scope?",steps:["Re-read the scope boundary: enterprise postpaid invoices, bill run → dispute closure.","(a) Consumer prepaid is a different product and population — **out of scope**.","(b) Roaming disputes on enterprise invoices fall inside the population and process boundary — **in scope**; it is part of the existing problem, not an addition.","(c) Collections letters occur after the dispute process and address a different problem — **out of scope**; log it as a candidate for a future project.","Any request to change the boundary goes to the Champion as a formal charter revision, with its effect on timeline and resources stated."],answer:"Only (b) is in scope. (a) and (c) are scope creep and go to the Champion for a decision or into the project pipeline.",wrongApproach:{calculation:'Accept all three because "they are all about billing".',whyWrong:"A shared theme is not a shared process boundary. Absorbing them dilutes effort and pushes out the completion date without the sponsor’s agreement."}}],lab:"charter",check:[{id:"chk-1.3.1-1",topic:"1.3.1",category:"wording",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"Which of the following is the BEST problem statement for a Define-phase charter?",options:["We need to implement an automated dispatch tool to reduce field repair delays.","Field repairs are slow because technicians are poorly scheduled.","From January to June 2026, 27% of fibre repair tickets in the Eastern zone exceeded the 8-hour repair target, triggering OMR 22,000 in customer credits.","Improve field repair performance to world-class levels as soon as possible."],answer:2,explanation:"It states what (repairs over target), where (Eastern zone), when (Jan–Jun 2026), how big (27%) and impact (OMR 22,000), with no cause, solution or blame.",whyWrong:["A predefined solution disguised as a problem — it names the tool.","It asserts a cause (poor scheduling) that has not been verified.","","This reads like a vague goal: no baseline, no metric, no date."],traps:["best"]},{id:"chk-1.3.1-2",topic:"1.3.1",category:"concept",difficulty:"beginner",numerical:!1,stem:"Which item would NOT normally appear in a project charter written during Define?",options:["The verified root cause of the problem","In-scope and out-of-scope boundaries","Milestone dates for each tollgate","The project Champion and team members"],answer:0,explanation:"Root causes are verified in Analyze. A Define charter records the problem, not its cause.",whyWrong:["","Scope boundaries are a core charter element.","Milestones / timeline are a core charter element.","Team and sponsorship are core charter elements."],traps:["not"]},{id:"chk-1.3.1-3",topic:"1.3.1",category:"business-case",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"A business case for reducing invoice disputes is being drafted. Which content is MOST appropriate for it?",options:["A detailed design for a new invoice template","The rising cost of disputes, the upcoming audit deadline and the link to the cash-collection objective","A list of billing clerks responsible for the most errors","The statistical test that will be used in Analyze"],answer:1,explanation:"A business case explains why this problem, why now, the consequences of inaction and the strategic link.",whyWrong:["Solution design does not belong in a business case.","","Blame has no place in the business case or problem statement.","Analysis methods are chosen later and are not the business argument."],traps:["most"]},{id:"chk-1.3.1-4",topic:"1.3.1",category:"scenario",difficulty:"advanced",numerical:!1,stem:'Halfway through Measure, a director asks the Green Belt to "also look at" a second region’s incident backlog. What should the Belt do FIRST?',options:["Add the region quietly so the director stays supportive","Refuse, because the charter can never change once signed","Assess the impact on scope, resources and timeline and bring a charter change request to the Champion","Restart the project in Define with a new charter covering both regions"],answer:2,explanation:"The charter is a living document, but changes are deliberate decisions owned by the Champion, informed by their impact.",whyWrong:["This is classic scope creep — unapproved expansion.","Charters can change; they just must change formally.","","Restarting is premature; the Champion decides once the impact is known."],traps:["first"]},{id:"chk-1.3.1-5",topic:"1.3.1",category:"calculation",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!0,stem:"A charter’s baseline shows 18% of P1 incidents breaching SLA. The goal is 6%. What percentage improvement on baseline does the goal represent?",options:["12%","33.3%","66.7%","300%"],answer:2,explanation:"(18 − 6) ÷ 18 × 100 = 66.7%. The absolute change is 12 percentage points; the relative improvement is 66.7%.",whyWrong:["This is the change in percentage points, not the percentage improvement.","This is the new value as a share of the baseline (6 ÷ 18).","","This divides the baseline by the target (18 ÷ 6 = 3), which is a ratio, not an improvement."]}],takeaways:["The business case answers why this, why now, what if we do nothing, and how it links to strategy.","The charter is a one-page agreement: business case, problem, goal, scope, milestones, team, sponsor, stakeholders, risks/assumptions/constraints, resources and preliminary benefits.","A problem statement gives what, where, when, how big and impact — with no cause, no solution, no blame.",'Words like "lack of", "need to", "implement" or a named tool reveal a solution disguised as a problem.',"The goal statement uses the same metric as the problem statement, with a baseline, target and date (SMART).","Scope changes go to the Champion as formal charter revisions; anything else is scope creep.","Charter benefits are estimates; the charter is reviewed at every tollgate as a living document."],flashcards:[{front:"Four questions a business case must answer",back:"Why this problem? Why now? What happens if we do nothing? How does it link to strategy?"},{front:"Five things a problem statement states",back:"What, where, when, how big (magnitude) and the impact."},{front:"Three things a problem statement must NOT contain",back:"A cause, a solution, or blame."},{front:'"We lack an automated routing tool." Problem or solution?',back:"A solution in disguise — it names the fix and assumes the cause."},{front:"What does SMART stand for?",back:"Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant, Time-bound."},{front:"Scope creep",back:"Gradual, unapproved expansion of a project beyond its chartered boundaries."},{front:"Who signs the charter and owns the result?",back:"The Champion / sponsor."},{front:"Assumption vs constraint",back:"Assumption: taken as true, must be verified. Constraint: a fixed limit you must work within."},{front:"Is the charter fixed once signed?",back:"No — it is a living document, reviewed at each tollgate and formally updated when facts change."},{front:"Glossary threshold for breakthrough improvement",back:"At or near 70% over baseline performance."}],coverage:"official-only",supplementaryNotes:["The syllabus (p.7) lists this topic by title only. The book’s Define chapter (printed pp. 1–97) was not supplied, so the charter element list, the problem-statement rules (what/where/when/how big/impact; no cause, no solution, no blame), SMART and the risk/assumption/constraint distinction are independently written from general Lean Six Sigma practice.",'Book support used here is limited to: the DMAIC roadmap Define steps (Estimate COPQ, Establish Team), the Measure checklist question "Has the Problem Statement changed?", the Control checklist link back to the business case, and the glossary entries for problem statement, objective statement, Champion and breakthrough improvement.',"All telecom, contact-centre and invoice figures are illustrative."],sources:[{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 7",pdfPage:7,note:"Define topic 1.3.1 Building a Business Case & Project Charter (title only)."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 230",pdfPage:33,note:"DMAIC roadmap: Define steps include Estimate COPQ and Establish Team."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 231",pdfPage:34,note:'Measure checklist asks "Has the Problem Statement changed?" and for primary/secondary metrics and financial goal.'},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 285",pdfPage:88,note:"The required detectable difference is not necessarily the full shift in the project’s Business Case."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 578",pdfPage:81,note:"Control checklist: do results match the business case and goals; do Champion, Belt and Finance agree the project is complete."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 584",pdfPage:87,note:"Glossary: Champion; Breakthrough Improvement (at or near 70% over baseline)."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 587",pdfPage:90,note:"Glossary: Problem Statement (bounds and describes the business problem); Objective Statement (goals, timing, expectations)."}]},L=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:y},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),b={id:"1.3.2",module:3,title:"Developing Project Metrics",subtitle:"Baselines, primary and secondary metrics, and measurable goals",minutes:12,objectives:["Establish a baseline: choose the period, apply an operational definition and quantify current performance.","Distinguish the primary metric (the Y the project moves) from secondary or consequential metrics (guard-rails).","Classify metrics as outcome, process or financial, and as leading or lagging indicators.","Express goals in appropriate units — rates, percentages and normalised counts rather than raw totals.","Trace each project metric back to a customer CTQ and set targets using baseline, entitlement and benchmarks."],overview:`A project without metrics is an opinion with a budget. Once the charter has named the problem, the team must decide *exactly* how it will be measured, what the current level is, and what level counts as success. That is the work of project metrics.

Every Lean Six Sigma project has one **[[primary-metric]]** — the Y that the project exists to move — and a small set of **[[secondary-metric|secondary metrics]]** that make sure the improvement does not cause harm elsewhere. Around these sit a [[baseline]], a target, and usually a [[financial-metric]] that translates the improvement into money. Get these right in Define and every later tollgate becomes a simple question: did the primary metric move, and did the guard-rails hold?`,sections:[{id:"baseline",heading:"Baselines: knowing where you start",body:`A baseline is the current performance of the process, measured before any change. Without it, nobody can prove that the project made a difference. Building a credible baseline involves three decisions:

1. **The operational definition.** What exactly counts? For "restoration time", does the clock start when the alarm fires, when the ticket is logged or when the customer calls? Does it stop at service restored or at ticket closure? An [[operational-definition]] removes the ambiguity so that two analysts measuring the same incident get the same number.
2. **The period.** Long enough to include normal variation — busy and quiet weeks, month-end peaks, different shifts and vendors. Six to twelve months of history is common for monthly KPIs; a single good or bad week is not a baseline. The book makes the same point in Measure: the process baseline should reflect long-term performance with all inputs free to vary.
3. **The data source.** Which system of record, which population, which exclusions — and are they documented?

A baseline is often refined in Measure once the measurement system has been checked, but a provisional figure belongs on the charter from day one.`},{id:"primary-secondary",heading:"Primary and secondary metrics",body:`The **primary metric** is the single measure that best represents the problem in the charter — the project Y in [[y-equals-f-x|Y = f(X)]]. It should be the same metric used in the problem statement and the goal statement. One project, one primary metric: chasing three Y's at once usually means three half-finished projects.

**Secondary metrics** — sometimes called [[consequential-metric|consequential metrics]] — are guard-rails. They watch for side effects that would make the "improvement" a false victory. If a vendor NOC is asked to cut mean time to restore, a sensible guard-rail is the *repeat-incident rate*: closing tickets faster by applying temporary workarounds would improve the primary metric while making customers suffer again a week later. If an invoice team is asked to process invoices faster, the guard-rail is *invoice accuracy*.

The supplied book treats this pair as a constant companion. Its Measure, Analyze and Improve tollgate deliverables each list "Primary Metric" and "Secondary Metric(s)", and its Control checklist asks whether the project has succeeded on the primary metric *without compromising* the secondary metrics.`},{id:"metric-types",heading:"Outcome, process and financial metrics — leading and lagging",body:`Metrics can be grouped by what they describe:

- **Outcome metrics** describe results the customer or business feels: SLA compliance, first-contact resolution, invoice disputes per 1,000 invoices, churn.
- **Process metrics** describe how the work is performed: time to first assignment, percentage of tickets with a complete fault description, dispatch decision time.
- **Financial metrics** translate performance into money: service credits paid, cost per repair, cost of rework.

A second, supporting lens is timing. A [[lagging-indicator]] tells you what has already happened — monthly SLA compliance arrives after the month is over. A [[leading-indicator]] moves *before* the outcome and gives early warning — the share of P1 tickets still unassigned after 15 minutes predicts restoration breaches later that day. Outcome metrics tend to lag; process metrics tend to lead. Good projects pair them: the primary metric is usually an outcome, while process metrics help the team steer between monthly reports.`},{id:"units",heading:"Measurable goals and appropriate units",body:`A goal is only measurable if its unit is fair. Raw counts are dangerous when volume changes. Suppose complaints rise from 1,800 to 1,900 while the subscriber base grows from 120,000 to 152,000. The count went *up*, yet complaints per 1,000 subscribers fell from 15 to 12.5 — performance improved. Normalise by the right denominator: per 1,000 subscribers, per 100 incidents, per invoice, per site visit.

Other unit rules worth remembering:

- State **percentages and percentage points** precisely: 18% → 6% is a 12-point reduction and a 66.7% improvement.
- Use **time units consistently** — minutes or hours, monthly or annual — in baseline, target and benefit.
- Be explicit about **direction** (lower is better for breach rate; higher is better for first-time-fix).
- For time-based CTQs, consider the **percentage meeting the target** as well as the average: an average restoration of 3.5 hours can hide many incidents over 4 hours.`},{id:"ctq-targets",heading:"Linking metrics to CTQs and setting targets",body:`Every project metric should trace back to a [[ctq]]. The chain runs: customer need → CTQ → primary metric → target. An enterprise customer's need "my service must come back quickly" becomes the CTQ *restore within 4 hours*, which becomes the primary metric *% of P1/P2 incidents restored after 4 hours*. If you cannot draw that line, the metric may be convenient rather than meaningful.

Where should the target come from? Three reference points help:

- **Baseline** — where you are now.
- **[[entitlement|Entitlement]]** — the book's glossary defines it as the best demonstrated performance. If one region or one shift already restores 95% within SLA, that level is demonstrably possible in your own process.
- **Benchmark** — performance achieved by comparable organisations or contracts.

The book's glossary describes *breakthrough improvement* as at or near 70% over baseline — a useful reminder that a Six Sigma target should be ambitious, not cosmetic.`}],whyItMatters:'In vendor managed services, metrics are where arguments are won or avoided. If the operational definition of "restoration time" is not agreed in Define, the vendor and the operator will each produce a different baseline, and every tollgate becomes a debate about data instead of a review of progress. Clear primary and secondary metrics also protect the customer: they stop a team from hitting the SLA number by shifting the pain somewhere else.',businessExample:{title:"Repeat truck rolls in fixed broadband",body:`A broadband operator was sending technicians back to the same customer within 7 days on 15% of fault visits. The team set its metrics before touching the process:

- **CTQ:** "Fix it right the first time" — the customer's fault must stay fixed.
- **Primary metric:** repeat visits within 7 days ÷ total completed fault visits. Baseline 15% over the previous nine months, with an operational definition that excluded customer-requested appointments.
- **Target:** 7% by end of Q2 — between the baseline and the 5% entitlement already achieved by the best-performing depot.
- **Secondary (guard-rail) metrics:** average on-site time (must not rise by more than 10 minutes) and first-visit appointment lead time.
- **Process (leading) metric:** percentage of visits closed with a completed line-test result.
- **Financial metric:** repeat-visit cost per month, at the contractor's per-visit rate.

At the Improve tollgate the primary metric had fallen to 8%, on-site time had risen by only 4 minutes and the line-test completion rate had climbed from 61% to 92% — telling the Champion the gain was real and safe.`},visual:"metricHierarchy",visualCaption:"From customer need to money: a CTQ defines the primary metric, the baseline and target frame it, guard-rail metrics protect against side effects, and a financial metric converts the gain into value.",definitions:[{term:"Baseline",definition:"The measured performance of the process before any change, over a period long enough to capture normal variation, using an agreed operational definition."},{term:"Primary metric",definition:"The single measure (the project Y) that the project exists to improve; it appears in both the problem and goal statements."},{term:"Secondary / consequential metric",definition:"A guard-rail measure that must not get worse while the primary metric improves."},{term:"Outcome metric",definition:"Measures a result felt by the customer or business, such as SLA compliance or disputes per 1,000 invoices."},{term:"Process metric",definition:"Measures how the work is performed inside the process, such as time to first assignment."},{term:"Financial metric",definition:"Expresses performance in money, such as service credits paid or cost per repair."},{term:"Leading vs lagging indicator",definition:"A leading indicator moves before the outcome and gives early warning; a lagging indicator reports results after they have happened."},{term:"Operational definition",definition:"A precise, testable description of what is measured and how, so different people obtain the same result."},{term:"Entitlement",definition:"The best demonstrated performance of the process (book glossary) — evidence of what is achievable."}],comparisons:[{title:"Primary vs secondary vs financial metric",headers:["","Primary metric","Secondary metric","Financial metric"],rows:[["Purpose","The Y the project moves","Guard-rail against side effects","Translates the gain into money"],["How many","One","A few (typically 1–3)","One or two"],["Success means","Reaches the target","Does not get worse","Validated by Finance"],["SLA example","% P1/P2 restored after 4 h","Repeat-incident rate within 7 days","Service credits paid per quarter"],["Invoice example","Days from receipt to approval","Invoice accuracy %","Late-payment charges per month"]]},{title:"Leading vs lagging indicators",headers:["","Leading indicator","Lagging indicator"],rows:[["Timing","Moves before the outcome","Reports after the outcome"],["Usual type","Process metric","Outcome metric"],["Use","Early warning and daily steering","Proof of results"],["Example","% P1 tickets unassigned after 15 min","Monthly SLA compliance"]]}],misconceptions:[{myth:"More primary metrics give a fuller picture.",reality:"A project should have one primary metric. Several Y’s split the team’s focus; use secondary metrics for the rest."},{myth:"Secondary metrics are targets to improve too.",reality:"Their job is to stay stable or within limits. Improving them is a bonus, not the goal."},{myth:"Raw counts are the clearest measure.",reality:"Counts move with volume. Normalise to a rate (per 1,000 subscribers, per 100 incidents) when volume varies."},{myth:"One recent month is a good enough baseline.",reality:"A baseline needs a period that captures normal variation, often 6–12 months, under an agreed operational definition."}],examTraps:[{trap:'"Which is the PRIMARY metric for a project to reduce late SLA restorations?"',explanation:"Choose the metric that directly measures the charter problem (% restored after the SLA), not a guard-rail (repeat incidents) or a financial translation (credits paid).",tag:"primary"},{trap:"An option that improves the primary metric by sacrificing a secondary metric.",explanation:"Success means improving the primary metric WITHOUT compromising the secondary metrics. Faster closures with more repeats is not success.",tag:"best"},{trap:'"Which should be established FIRST?" — target, baseline or solution.',explanation:"The baseline (with an operational definition) comes before the target; the solution comes much later.",tag:"first"},{trap:"Confusing percentage points with percent improvement.",explanation:"From 18% to 6% is 12 points but a 66.7% improvement. Read whether the question asks for absolute or relative change.",tag:"most"},{trap:"Calling a leading indicator an outcome.",explanation:"Leading indicators usually measure the process and move first; the outcome (lagging) confirms the result later.",tag:"not"}],workedExamples:[{title:"Establishing an invoice-processing baseline",problem:"Over the last six months the accounts-payable team received 24,000 supplier invoices. 20,400 were approved without any query, correction or rework. Establish the baseline first-time-right rate and suggest an operational definition.",steps:["Agree the operational definition: an invoice is *first time right* if it passes matching and approval with no query to the supplier, no manual correction and no re-routing.","Use a period with normal variation: six months, including two quarter-ends.","Calculate: $\\text{FTY} = \\dfrac{20{,}400}{24{,}000} = 0.85$.","Report the baseline as **85% first time right** (equivalently, 15% of invoices needed rework)."],answer:"Baseline = 85% first time right over six months (3,600 of 24,000 invoices needed rework).",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Using last week only: 980 of 1,000 = 98%.",whyWrong:"A single quiet week ignores quarter-end peaks and normal variation, so it overstates performance and makes any future gain look like a loss."}},{title:"Setting a target against baseline and breakthrough",problem:"Mean time to restore (MTTR) for P2 incidents has averaged 6.4 hours over nine months. The team proposes a target of 4.0 hours. What is the percentage improvement, and what MTTR would a 70% (breakthrough-level) improvement require?",steps:["Percentage improvement (lower is better): $\\dfrac{6.4 - 4.0}{6.4} \\times 100 = 37.5\\%$.","A 70% improvement means the new value is 30% of baseline: $6.4 \\times (1 - 0.70) = 1.92$ hours.","Interpret: 37.5% is a solid improvement but below the glossary’s breakthrough level; whether to stretch depends on entitlement (best region already at, say, 3.1 h) and the business case."],answer:"37.5% improvement for a 4.0-hour target; breakthrough level would require about 1.92 hours.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"$(6.4 - 4.0) \\div 4.0 \\times 100 = 60\\%$",whyWrong:"Percentage improvement is always relative to the baseline, not to the new value. Dividing by 4.0 inflates the result."}},{title:"Choosing a fair unit when volume changes",problem:"Billing complaints were 1,800 in H1 with 120,000 subscribers and 1,900 in H2 with 152,000 subscribers. A manager says complaints got worse. Is that right?",steps:["Normalise to complaints per 1,000 subscribers. H1: $\\dfrac{1{,}800}{120{,}000} \\times 1{,}000 = 15.0$.","H2: $\\dfrac{1{,}900}{152{,}000} \\times 1{,}000 = 12.5$.","Improvement: $\\dfrac{15.0 - 12.5}{15.0} \\times 100 = 16.7\\%$.","Conclusion: the complaint *rate* improved by 16.7% even though the count rose by 100."],answer:"No — complaints per 1,000 subscribers fell from 15.0 to 12.5, a 16.7% improvement.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"1,900 − 1,800 = +100 complaints, so performance got worse.",whyWrong:"Raw counts ignore the 27% growth in the subscriber base. The right unit is a rate normalised by volume."}}],lab:"smart",check:[{id:"chk-1.3.2-1",topic:"1.3.2",category:"concept",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"A project aims to reduce late restoration of enterprise P1 incidents. Which is the MOST appropriate primary metric?",options:["Number of NOC engineers on shift","Percentage of P1 incidents restored after the 4-hour SLA","Repeat-incident rate within 7 days","Total service credits paid per year"],answer:1,explanation:"The primary metric directly measures the charter problem — late restoration against the SLA.",whyWrong:["This is a resource input (an X), not the project Y.","","This is a sensible secondary (guard-rail) metric, not the primary.","This is the financial translation of the problem, not the primary operational metric."],traps:["primary","most"]},{id:"chk-1.3.2-2",topic:"1.3.2",category:"scenario",difficulty:"advanced",numerical:!1,stem:"After Improve, average invoice processing time fell from 9 to 5 days, but invoice accuracy dropped from 97% to 89%. How should the result be judged?",options:["A success, because the primary metric beat its target","Not yet a success, because the improvement compromised a secondary metric","A success, because secondary metrics are optional","A failure of the baseline, which should be recalculated"],answer:1,explanation:"Success means improving the primary metric without compromising the secondary (guard-rail) metrics.",whyWrong:["Hitting the primary target while damaging a guard-rail is not true success.","","Secondary metrics exist precisely to catch this kind of side effect.","The baseline is not the issue; the side effect is."],traps:["primary"]},{id:"chk-1.3.2-3",topic:"1.3.2",category:"compare",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"Which of these is a LEADING indicator for monthly restoration-SLA compliance?",options:["Monthly service credits paid","Quarterly customer satisfaction score","Percentage of P1 tickets still unassigned 15 minutes after logging","Annual contract renewal rate"],answer:2,explanation:"Slow assignment is a process measure that moves before — and predicts — restoration breaches.",whyWrong:["Credits are paid after breaches occur — a lagging financial measure.","Satisfaction is measured after the experience — lagging.","","Renewal is a long-lagging business outcome."]},{id:"chk-1.3.2-4",topic:"1.3.2",category:"calculation",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!0,stem:"Complaints were 1,800 with 120,000 subscribers, then 1,900 with 152,000 subscribers. What is the percentage improvement in complaints per 1,000 subscribers?",options:["−5.6% (worse)","12.5%","16.7%","26.7%"],answer:2,explanation:"Rate fell from 15.0 to 12.5 per 1,000: (15.0 − 12.5) ÷ 15.0 × 100 = 16.7%.",whyWrong:["This uses raw counts and ignores the change in subscriber base.","12.5 is the new rate per 1,000, not the improvement.","","This is roughly the growth in subscribers, not the change in the complaint rate."]},{id:"chk-1.3.2-5",topic:"1.3.2",category:"definition",difficulty:"beginner",numerical:!1,stem:'In the supplied glossary, "entitlement" refers to:',options:["The contractual SLA target agreed with the customer","The best demonstrated performance of the process","The financial benefit the Belt is entitled to claim","The average performance during the baseline period"],answer:1,explanation:"Entitlement is the best demonstrated performance — evidence of what the process can achieve.",whyWrong:["That is a specification or requirement, not entitlement.","","Entitlement is not a financial term.","That is the baseline."]}],takeaways:["Establish a baseline before setting a target, using an operational definition and a period that captures normal variation.","One primary metric — the project Y — appears in the problem statement, goal statement and every tollgate.","Secondary (consequential) metrics are guard-rails: success means the primary improves without compromising them.","Outcome metrics show results, process metrics show how work is done, financial metrics show the money.","Leading indicators give early warning; lagging indicators confirm results.","Normalise counts into rates when volume changes, and keep percentage points distinct from percent improvement.","Every metric should trace back to a CTQ; targets draw on baseline, entitlement and benchmarks."],flashcards:[{front:"Primary metric",back:"The single measure (project Y) the project exists to improve."},{front:"Secondary (consequential) metric",back:"A guard-rail measure that must not get worse while the primary improves."},{front:"What must come before a target?",back:"A baseline, measured with an operational definition over a representative period."},{front:"Leading vs lagging indicator",back:"Leading moves first and warns early (often a process metric); lagging reports results afterwards (often an outcome)."},{front:"Entitlement (book glossary)",back:"Best demonstrated performance."},{front:"18% → 6%: points vs percent",back:"12 percentage points; a 66.7% improvement on baseline."},{front:"Why normalise counts?",back:"Counts move with volume; a rate per 1,000 or per 100 shows real performance."},{front:'Primary metric for "reduce repeat truck rolls"',back:"Repeat visits within N days ÷ total completed visits."},{front:"Success criterion at Control (book p.574)",back:"The primary metric improved without compromising the secondary metrics."}],coverage:"official-only",supplementaryNotes:['The syllabus (p.7) lists "Developing Project Metrics" by title only, and the book’s Define chapter was not supplied. Outcome/process/financial classification, leading/lagging indicators, unit normalisation and benchmarks are independently written from general practice.',"Book support used: primary and secondary metrics appear in the Measure, Analyze and Improve tollgate deliverables; the Control checklist asks whether the primary metric improved without compromising secondary metrics; the glossary defines entitlement and breakthrough improvement; the Measure chapter describes the process baseline as long-term performance.","All numerical examples are illustrative."],sources:[{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 7",pdfPage:7,note:"Define topic 1.3.2 Developing Project Metrics (title only)."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 212",pdfPage:15,note:"Process baseline: average long-term performance with inputs unconstrained; report with long-term data."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 231",pdfPage:34,note:'Planning for Action: "What is your Primary metric? Secondary metrics?"; project financial goal (target & time).'},{doc:"book-401-500",pages:"p. 416",pdfPage:19,note:"Analyze tollgate deliverables list Primary Metric and Secondary Metric(s)."},{doc:"book-401-500",pages:"p. 488",pdfPage:91,note:"Improve tollgate deliverables list Primary Metric and Secondary Metric(s)."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 574",pdfPage:77,note:"Has the project succeeded on the primary metric without compromising secondary metrics?"},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 584",pdfPage:87,note:"Glossary: Breakthrough Improvement (at or near 70% over baseline)."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 585",pdfPage:88,note:"Glossary: Entitlement (best demonstrated performance)."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 587",pdfPage:90,note:"Glossary: Metric (a KPI linked to goals)."}]},R=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:b},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),v={id:"1.3.3",module:3,title:"Financial Evaluation & Benefits Capture",subtitle:"Hard savings, cost avoidance, ROI, payback and verified benefits",minutes:14,objectives:["Classify project benefits as hard savings, soft savings, cost avoidance or revenue growth.","Distinguish estimated benefits (at the charter) from validated and realized benefits (after Control).","Calculate percentage improvement against a baseline and keep gross vs net amounts and time units consistent.","Calculate simple ROI and payback as supplementary financial-evaluation tools and interpret the results.","Describe how benefits are verified with Finance and tracked, typically over 12 months."],overview:`Lean Six Sigma projects are chosen because they create value, so sooner or later someone will ask: *how much, exactly, and how do we know?* Financial evaluation answers the first half of that question at the start of a project; [[benefits-capture]] answers the second half at the end.

The journey runs right across DMAIC. The book's roadmap places "Estimate COPQ" in Define and "Verify Financial Impact" at the very end of Control. In between, a number written on the charter as a hopeful estimate must turn into a figure that Finance recognises in the accounts. This lesson shows you how to classify benefits honestly, calculate them consistently and capture them so that they survive scrutiny.`,sections:[{id:"benefit-types",heading:"Kinds of project value",body:`Not all benefits are equal in the eyes of Finance. Learn to sort them into four groups:

- **[[hard-savings|Hard savings]]** — a measurable reduction in actual spending that shows up in the profit and loss statement against a baseline. Fewer [[sla]] service credits paid to enterprise customers, lower contractor invoices for repeat truck rolls, reduced late-payment charges.
- **[[soft-savings|Soft savings]]** — real but indirect benefits that do not reduce spending by themselves: engineer hours freed up, better customer satisfaction, faster decisions. They become hard only if the organisation acts on them (for example, by cancelling planned overtime or absorbing growth without hiring).
- **[[cost-avoidance|Cost avoidance]]** — preventing a future cost that has not yet been incurred: avoiding a regulatory fine, avoiding a planned capacity purchase, avoiding the contract penalty that would apply from next year. Valuable, but it does not reduce today's baseline spend.
- **Revenue growth** — extra income from retained customers, new sales or faster billing.

The book's glossary describes the Finance Representative as the person who provides an independent evaluation of hard and soft savings. That independence matters: the Belt is naturally optimistic about their own project.`},{id:"estimated-realized",heading:"Estimated, validated and realized benefits",body:`Benefits change status as the project matures:

1. **[[estimated-benefits|Estimated]]** — at the charter, based on the baseline [[copq|cost of poor quality]] and the goal. Rough by design.
2. **Validated** — after Improve, when pilot data shows the real effect and Finance agrees the calculation method: the baseline, the attribution and the classification.
3. **[[realized-benefits|Realized]]** — after Control, when the improvement has been sustained and the savings have actually appeared, normally tracked month by month for about 12 months.

The book's Control checklist asks whether the Champion, the Belt and Finance all agree the project is complete, and lists "financial benefits not tracked and integrated into the business" as a Control roadblock. Exam questions often hide the distinction in one word: a benefit is *estimated* in Define and *realized* only after Control.`},{id:"improvement",heading:"Percentage improvement",body:`The simplest financial evaluation compares the new performance with the baseline:

$$\\text{Improvement \\%} = \\frac{\\text{Baseline} - \\text{New}}{\\text{Baseline}} \\times 100$$

This form is for "lower is better" metrics such as credits paid or repeat visits. If annual SLA credits fall from OMR 540,000 to OMR 216,000, the improvement is $(540{,}000 - 216{,}000) \\div 540{,}000 \\times 100 = 60\\%$. Always divide by the **baseline**, never by the new value.

Two consistency rules prevent most errors:

- **Gross vs net.** Gross benefit is the total reduction in cost; net benefit subtracts the ongoing cost of running the new process (a monitoring licence, extra checks). Say which one you are reporting.
- **Consistent time units.** Do not divide a one-off cost by an annual benefit and call the result months. Convert everything to the same period first.`},{id:"roi-payback",heading:"ROI and payback (supplementary)",advanced:!0,body:`> **Supplementary:** ROI, payback period and NPV are common business tools, but they are not formulas given in the supplied syllabus or book pages. They are included here because real business cases use them.

**Return on investment** compares the benefit to the cost over the same period:

$$\\text{ROI \\%} = \\frac{\\text{Benefit} - \\text{Cost}}{\\text{Cost}} \\times 100$$

**Simple payback period** tells you how long it takes for the investment to be recovered:

$$\\text{Payback} = \\frac{\\text{Investment}}{\\text{Net benefit per period}}$$

The answer is in whatever period the benefit is expressed in — months if you use monthly benefit, years if you use annual benefit. **[[npv|Net present value]]** goes one step further by discounting future cash flows to today's value; it matters for multi-year investments but is rarely needed for a short Green Belt project.

A high [[roi|ROI]] with a short [[payback-period]] is the classic signal of a strong project. But remember the classification: a payback built on soft savings or on cost avoidance is weaker than one built on hard savings, and Finance will treat it that way.`},{id:"tracking",heading:"Verifying and tracking benefits",body:`Benefits capture is a discipline, not a celebration slide. Good practice:

- **Agree the method early.** Before Improve, the Belt and Finance agree the baseline period, the formula, any normalisation (for volume, tariff or exchange-rate changes) and which category each benefit belongs to.
- **Attribute honestly.** If incident volume fell because a large customer left, that reduction in credits is not a project benefit.
- **Track monthly for about 12 months.** A tracking sheet records the baseline, the actual value each month, the benefit and the cumulative total. Ramp-up months are often lower than the estimate; that is normal and should be visible.
- **Hand over ownership.** The [[process-owner]] keeps the gain; the Champion signs off; Finance records the realized figure.

A project whose benefits were estimated but never tracked has, in financial terms, delivered nothing that anyone can prove.`}],whyItMatters:'In managed-services governance, money is often the language that gets a vendor improvement funded and sustained. A Belt who can say "Finance validated OMR 306,000 of realized hard savings over 12 months, against an estimate of 360,000" earns far more credibility than one quoting an optimistic charter figure. Classifying benefits correctly also protects the relationship: cost avoidance and soft savings are real value, but presenting them as hard savings invites challenge when budgets do not move.',businessExample:{title:"Reducing SLA penalty credits for enterprise customers",body:`A telecom operator paid OMR 540,000 a year in service credits to enterprise customers for restoration breaches. The charter estimated that halving breaches would save around OMR 270,000.

After Improve, a pilot in one region showed breaches falling by more than half. Finance agreed the method: compare credits in the 12 months after Control with the 12-month baseline, normalised for the size of the enterprise customer base, and exclude credits linked to a single major customer who had left.

Twelve months after Control, credits had fallen to OMR 216,000 — a 60% improvement. The gross hard saving was OMR 324,000; after OMR 12,000 per year for the new escalation dashboard, the net benefit was OMR 312,000. The one-off project cost of OMR 60,000 was recovered in a little over two months. Engineer time freed by fewer escalations was reported separately as a soft saving, and the avoided contractual penalty clause due at renewal was reported as cost avoidance — neither was added to the hard-savings total.`},visual:"benefitsWaterfall",visualCaption:"Benefits mature: the charter estimate is adjusted to a Finance-validated figure, then tracked to a realized total — and the monthly net benefit pays back the investment early in the year.",definitions:[{term:"Hard savings",definition:"A measurable reduction in actual spending against a baseline that appears in the profit and loss statement."},{term:"Soft savings",definition:"Real but indirect benefits (freed capacity, satisfaction, faster decisions) that do not by themselves reduce spending."},{term:"Cost avoidance",definition:"Preventing a future cost that has not yet been incurred, such as a fine or a planned purchase."},{term:"Estimated benefits",definition:"The preliminary value stated on the charter, based on baseline COPQ and the goal."},{term:"Realized benefits",definition:"Benefits confirmed after Control, once the improvement is sustained and the savings are validated, usually by Finance."},{term:"Gross vs net benefit",definition:"Gross is the total cost reduction; net subtracts the ongoing cost of running the improved process."},{term:"ROI (supplementary)",definition:"(Benefit − Cost) ÷ Cost × 100, over the same period."},{term:"Payback period (supplementary)",definition:"Investment ÷ net benefit per period; the time to recover the investment."},{term:"Finance Representative",definition:"Provides an independent evaluation of hard and soft savings and supports Champions and project leaders (book glossary)."}],comparisons:[{title:"Hard savings vs soft savings vs cost avoidance vs revenue growth",headers:["","Hard savings","Soft savings","Cost avoidance","Revenue growth"],rows:[["Changes spending today?","Yes — reduces it","Not by itself","No — prevents future spend","Increases income"],["Visible in P&L vs baseline?","Yes","No","No (vs budget/plan only)","Yes"],["Telecom example","Fewer SLA credits paid","Engineer hours freed","Avoided regulator fine","Retained enterprise contract"],["Field-service example","Fewer contractor repeat visits billed","In-house technician time released","Avoided hiring planned for growth","Faster installs → earlier billing"]]},{title:"Estimated vs validated vs realized",headers:["","Estimated","Validated","Realized"],rows:[["When","Define (charter)","After Improve / pilot","After Control, tracked ~12 months"],["Based on","Baseline COPQ and goal","Pilot data and agreed method","Actual sustained results"],["Who confirms","Belt and Champion","Belt with Finance","Finance, Champion and Belt sign-off"]]}],misconceptions:[{myth:"The charter benefit is what the project saved.",reality:"It is an estimate. Only benefits validated and sustained after Control are realized."},{myth:"Freed-up staff hours are hard savings.",reality:"They are soft savings unless spending actually falls — overtime cancelled, contractors released, hiring avoided and recorded."},{myth:"Cost avoidance is the same as cost reduction.",reality:"Cost avoidance prevents a future cost; it does not lower today’s baseline spend."},{myth:"Percentage improvement can be divided by the new value.",reality:"It is always relative to the baseline: (Baseline − New) ÷ Baseline."},{myth:"ROI and payback are formulas from the Define syllabus.",reality:"They are supplementary business tools; the supplied sources do not give them as formulas."}],examTraps:[{trap:'"The project’s benefits are confirmed at the Define tollgate." True?',explanation:'No. Define holds estimated benefits; realized benefits are verified at the end of Control ("Verify Financial Impact").',tag:"realized-benefits"},{trap:"An option treating the charter figure as the final saving.",explanation:'Charter benefits are estimated and will be refined. Look for words like "preliminary", "estimated" or "projected".',tag:"estimated-benefits"},{trap:'"Which is an example of HARD savings?" with freed engineer hours as a distractor.',explanation:"Hard savings reduce actual spending (fewer credits paid, lower contractor invoices). Freed capacity is soft unless it removes cost.",tag:"best"},{trap:'"Who should validate the financial benefits?"',explanation:"An independent Finance representative, not the Belt alone. Sign-off is by the Champion, the Belt and Finance.",tag:"most"},{trap:"Mixing annual benefits with a monthly payback question.",explanation:"Convert to a consistent period before dividing. Payback is in the unit of the benefit rate.",tag:"not"}],workedExamples:[{title:"SLA penalty credits: improvement, ROI and payback",problem:"Baseline service credits were OMR 540,000 per year. In the 12 months after Control, Finance validated credits of OMR 216,000. One-off project cost: OMR 60,000. Ongoing dashboard cost: OMR 12,000 per year. Find the percentage improvement, the first-year ROI (supplementary) and the payback period (supplementary).",steps:["Improvement: $\\dfrac{540{,}000 - 216{,}000}{540{,}000} \\times 100 = 60\\%$.","Gross annual hard saving: $540{,}000 - 216{,}000 = 324{,}000$.","Total first-year cost: $60{,}000 + 12{,}000 = 72{,}000$. ROI: $\\dfrac{324{,}000 - 72{,}000}{72{,}000} \\times 100 = 350\\%$.","Net monthly benefit: $(324{,}000 - 12{,}000) \\div 12 = 26{,}000$.","Payback: $60{,}000 \\div 26{,}000 \\approx 2.31$ months."],answer:"60% improvement; first-year ROI 350%; payback about 2.3 months.",wrongApproach:{calculation:'$60{,}000 \\div 312{,}000 = 0.19$ "months"',whyWrong:"Dividing by an annual benefit gives years (0.19 years ≈ 2.3 months). Labelling it months mixes time units."}},{title:"Repeat truck rolls: classifying the saving",problem:"Repeat truck rolls fell from 1,500 to 900 per month. Each visit costs OMR 35. 60% of repeat visits were made by a contractor paid per visit; 40% by in-house technicians whose headcount did not change. Find the improvement and classify the annual benefit.",steps:["Improvement: $\\dfrac{1{,}500 - 900}{1{,}500} \\times 100 = 40\\%$.","Visits avoided per month: $1{,}500 - 900 = 600$, worth $600 \\times 35 = 21{,}000$ per month.","Contractor share (hard — invoices actually fall): $600 \\times 0.6 \\times 35 = 12{,}600$ per month $= 151{,}200$ per year.","In-house share (soft — capacity released, no cost removed): $600 \\times 0.4 \\times 35 = 8{,}400$ per month $= 100{,}800$ per year."],answer:"40% fewer repeat visits; OMR 151,200 per year hard savings plus OMR 100,800 per year soft savings (capacity).",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Report $21{,}000 \\times 12 = 252{,}000$ as hard savings.",whyWrong:"Only the contractor portion reduces spending. The in-house hours become hard savings only if overtime, contractors or planned hiring are actually removed."}},{title:"Estimated vs realized over 12 months",problem:"The charter estimated OMR 360,000 per year (OMR 30,000 per month). After Control, the tracked net monthly benefits (OMR thousands) were: 18, 22, 25, 26, 26, 27, 27, 26, 27, 28, 27, 27. The one-off investment was OMR 60,000. What was realized, what share of the estimate is that, and when did cumulative benefit pass the investment?",steps:["Realized total: $18 + 22 + 25 + 26 + 26 + 27 + 27 + 26 + 27 + 28 + 27 + 27 = 306$, i.e. OMR 306,000.","Share of estimate: $306{,}000 \\div 360{,}000 = 0.85 = 85\\%$.","Cumulative: month 1 = 18, month 2 = 40, month 3 = 65 — the investment of 60 is passed during month 3.","Average-rate check (supplementary): $60{,}000 \\div (306{,}000 \\div 12) = 60{,}000 \\div 25{,}500 \\approx 2.35$ months."],answer:"Realized OMR 306,000 (85% of the estimate); payback reached in month 3.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Report the charter figure of OMR 360,000 as the project’s savings.",whyWrong:"That is the estimated benefit. Ramp-up months were below the estimate; only the tracked, validated OMR 306,000 is realized."}}],lab:null,check:[{id:"chk-1.3.3-1",topic:"1.3.3",category:"concept",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"Which of the following is an example of HARD savings?",options:["NOC engineers spend 600 fewer hours per year on escalations, with no change in staffing","Service credits paid to enterprise customers fall from OMR 540,000 to OMR 216,000 per year","A regulator fine that might have been imposed next year is avoided","Customer satisfaction scores rise by 8 points"],answer:1,explanation:"Hard savings are a measurable reduction in actual spending against a baseline — fewer credits paid.",whyWrong:["Freed hours with unchanged staffing are soft savings.","","Preventing a future cost is cost avoidance.","Satisfaction is a soft benefit."]},{id:"chk-1.3.3-2",topic:"1.3.3",category:"dmaic-boundary",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"When are a project’s benefits considered REALIZED?",options:["When the Champion signs the charter in Define","When the root cause is verified in Analyze","When a pilot shows improvement in Improve","After Control, when the gain is sustained and Finance has validated the savings"],answer:3,explanation:'Realized benefits are confirmed at the end of Control ("Verify Financial Impact"), typically tracked over about 12 months.',whyWrong:["At the charter, benefits are only estimated.","A verified root cause does not yet deliver any savings.","Pilot results support validation but are not yet sustained, realized savings.",""],traps:["realized-benefits","after"]},{id:"chk-1.3.3-3",topic:"1.3.3",category:"calculation",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!0,stem:"Repeat truck rolls fell from 1,500 to 900 per month. What is the percentage improvement?",options:["40%","60%","66.7%","600%"],answer:0,explanation:"(1,500 − 900) ÷ 1,500 × 100 = 40%.",whyWrong:["","This is the new value as a share of the baseline (900 ÷ 1,500).","This divides the reduction by the new value (600 ÷ 900) instead of the baseline.","This confuses the number of visits avoided (600) with a percentage."]},{id:"chk-1.3.3-4",topic:"1.3.3",category:"calculation",difficulty:"advanced",numerical:!0,stem:"Supplementary: a project costs OMR 60,000 up front and delivers a net benefit of OMR 312,000 per year. What is the simple payback period in months?",options:["0.19 months","About 2.3 months","5.2 months","12 months"],answer:1,explanation:"Net monthly benefit = 312,000 ÷ 12 = 26,000. Payback = 60,000 ÷ 26,000 ≈ 2.3 months.",whyWrong:["60,000 ÷ 312,000 = 0.19 is in years, not months — a time-unit error.","","This comes from 312,000 ÷ 60,000 = 5.2, which inverts the formula.","Payback is not automatically one year; it depends on cost and benefit rate."]},{id:"chk-1.3.3-5",topic:"1.3.3",category:"business-case",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"Who should provide an independent evaluation of a project’s hard and soft savings?",options:["The Green Belt leading the project","The vendor whose process was improved","A Finance representative","The project team members"],answer:2,explanation:"The book’s glossary assigns independent evaluation of hard and soft savings to the Finance Representative; final sign-off involves the Champion, the Belt and Finance.",whyWrong:["The Belt is not independent of their own project.","The vendor has an interest in the result and is not independent.","","Team members contribute data but are not an independent financial check."]}],takeaways:["Hard savings reduce actual spending; soft savings release capacity or improve experience; cost avoidance prevents a future cost; revenue growth adds income.","Benefits are estimated at the charter, validated with Finance after Improve and realized after Control.","Percentage improvement = (Baseline − New) ÷ Baseline × 100 — always divide by the baseline.","State whether figures are gross or net, and convert everything to the same time unit before dividing.","ROI, payback and NPV are supplementary tools here — useful, but not formulas given in the supplied sources.","An independent Finance representative evaluates savings; Champion, Belt and Finance sign off together.","Track realized benefits monthly for about 12 months; ramp-up below estimate is normal and should be visible."],flashcards:[{front:"Hard savings",back:"A measurable reduction in actual spending against baseline, visible in the P&L."},{front:"Soft savings",back:"Indirect benefits (freed hours, satisfaction) that do not by themselves reduce spending."},{front:"Cost avoidance",back:"Preventing a future cost that has not yet been incurred."},{front:"Estimated vs realized benefits",back:"Estimated: on the charter in Define. Realized: validated after Control, once sustained."},{front:"Percentage improvement formula",back:"(Baseline − New) ÷ Baseline × 100."},{front:"ROI (supplementary)",back:"(Benefit − Cost) ÷ Cost × 100, same period."},{front:"Payback (supplementary)",back:"Investment ÷ net benefit per period; result is in that period’s unit."},{front:"Last Control step on the book’s DMAIC roadmap",back:"Verify Financial Impact."},{front:"Who signs off project completion (book p.578)?",back:"The Champion, the Belt and Finance."},{front:"Gross vs net benefit",back:"Gross = total cost reduction; net = gross minus ongoing running costs."}],coverage:"official-only",supplementaryNotes:['The syllabus (p.7) lists "Financial Evaluation & Benefits Capture" by title only, and the book’s Define chapter was not supplied.',"SUPPLEMENTARY: ROI, payback period and NPV are not given as formulas in the supplied syllabus or book pages. They are included as general business tools and computed with the app’s calculation library. The percentage-improvement formula is likewise general practice.","The hard / soft / cost avoidance / revenue classification, the estimated → validated → realized progression and the 12-month tracking convention are independently written from general Lean Six Sigma practice. Book support is limited to the roadmap (Estimate COPQ → Verify Financial Impact), the Finance Representative glossary entry, the Control sign-off checklist and the Control roadblock about untracked benefits.","All financial figures are illustrative."],sources:[{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 7",pdfPage:7,note:"Define topic 1.3.3 Financial Evaluation & Benefits Capture (title only)."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 230",pdfPage:33,note:"DMAIC roadmap: Estimate COPQ in Define; Verify Financial Impact in Control."},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 231",pdfPage:34,note:'COPQ and financial-benefit checklist: "Validate Financial Benefits"; project financial goal (target & time).'},{doc:"book-201-300",pages:"p. 285",pdfPage:88,note:"Practical (economic) difference vs statistical difference; the business case shift."},{doc:"book-401-500",pages:"p. 490",pdfPage:93,note:"Roadmap repeated: Verify Financial Impact as the final Control step."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 577",pdfPage:80,note:"Control roadblock: financial benefits not tracked and integrated into the business."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 578",pdfPage:81,note:"Do results match the business case? Financial implications? Champion, Belt and Finance agree the project is complete."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 585",pdfPage:88,note:"Glossary: Finance Representative — independent evaluation of hard and/or soft savings."}]},E=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:v},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),w={id:"1.4.1",module:4,title:"Understanding Lean",subtitle:"Value through the customer’s eyes, flow and waste removal",minutes:12,objectives:["Explain Lean as a philosophy that delivers customer-defined value with the least time, effort and material.","Apply the two value tests — does the step change form, fit or function, and would the customer pay for it? — to classify activities as value-added, non-value-added or business-value-added.","Describe the five Lean principles (value, value stream, flow, pull, perfection) and how they build on each other.","Distinguish efficiency from effectiveness, and resource utilisation from flow of the work item.","Connect waste reduction to continuous improvement (Kaizen) and recognise muda, mura and muri.","Calculate an illustrative process efficiency (value-added time ÷ total lead time) and interpret it."],overview:`Imagine following a single broadband fault ticket from the moment a customer calls until service is restored. If you timed only the moments when someone was *actually fixing the fault*, you might find twenty minutes of real work hidden inside four or five hours of elapsed time. The rest is queues, hand-offs, approvals, re-routing and rework. **That gap is what [[lean]] is about.**

Lean is a way of thinking about work that starts with one question: *what does the customer actually value?* Everything that creates that value is protected and made to flow smoothly; everything that consumes time, effort or material without creating value is treated as [[muda|waste]] to be reduced or removed. In this lesson you will learn how to judge value, how to separate [[value-added]] from [[non-value-added]] work, why flow matters more than keeping everyone busy, and how Lean turns improvement into a daily habit rather than a one-off project.`,sections:[{id:"lean-philosophy",heading:"Lean is a way of seeing work",body:`Many people first meet Lean as a toolbox — 5S, kanban, value stream maps. The tools matter, but they are expressions of a deeper philosophy: **deliver what the customer values, as smoothly and quickly as possible, using the fewest resources, and keep getting better at it.**

The most important shift Lean asks of you is a change of viewpoint. Traditional management watches the *resource*: is the engineer busy, is the machine running, is the agent on a call? Lean watches the *work item*: the ticket, the order, the patient, the invoice. A service desk where every agent is 100% utilised can still deliver terrible service if tickets sit in queues between those busy people. A useful mental habit is to **"follow the ticket, not the engineer."**

Lean also rests on respect for people. Waste is almost always a feature of the *system* — poor hand-offs, unclear standards, batching — rather than lazy individuals. The people doing the work usually see the waste first, which is why Lean improvement relies on involving them.`},{id:"customer-value",heading:"Value is defined by the customer",body:`In Lean, [[value]] is never what the organisation *thinks* is valuable; it is what the **customer** recognises and would willingly pay for. The training book gives two practical tests for any process step:

1. **Does the step change the form, fit or function** of the product or service?
2. **Is the customer willing to pay** for it?

A step that passes both tests is value-added. If it fails, it is a candidate for elimination. The book adds a handy hint: activities that begin with **"re-"** — rework, re-enter, re-check, re-approve, re-route — are very likely waste.

Apply the tests to a mobile customer whose data service has failed. Diagnosing the fault and correcting the network configuration change the *function* of their service — that is value. Transferring the ticket between three resolver groups changes nothing the customer can experience; nobody would pay extra for a ticket that was "reassigned beautifully". Connect this to the [[voc]]: the customer's language ("just get me back online, fast") tells you which steps truly matter.`},{id:"va-nva-bva",heading:"Three kinds of activity: VA, NVA and business-value-added",body:`Every step in a process falls into one of three groups:

- **[[value-added|Value-added (VA)]]** — passes both tests. *Example:* restoring the customer's line.
- **[[non-value-added|Non-value-added (NVA)]]** — fails the tests and is not required. Pure waste: waiting in a queue, mis-routing, searching for information, correcting errors. *Eliminate it.*
- **[[business-value-added|Business-value-added (BVA)]]**, also called *required* or *necessary* non-value-added — the customer would not pay for it, but the business must do it for legal, regulatory, safety or contractual reasons. *Examples:* recording an outage for the telecom regulator, a security identity check before a SIM swap, an SLA audit trail. *Minimise it and simplify it, but do not delete it.*

The book makes the same point: some non-value-added steps cannot be removed, regulatory data collection being the classic case. A good habit is to be **sceptical of the BVA label** — teams often defend habits as "required" when no regulation or contract actually demands them. Ask, "Who requires this, and where is it written?"`},{id:"five-principles",heading:"The five Lean principles",body:`Womack and Jones summarised Lean thinking in five principles that form a logical sequence (this framework is widely taught, but it is *supplementary* — it does not appear in the supplied book pages):

| # | Principle | The question it asks | Incident-management example |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | **Value** | What does the customer value? | Fast, first-time restoration of service |
| 2 | **[[value-stream|Value stream]]** | Which steps deliver it, end to end? | Every step from the call to confirmed restoration |
| 3 | **[[flow|Flow]]** | How do we make the value-added steps move without stopping? | Route correctly first time; remove queues and approvals |
| 4 | **[[pull|Pull]]** | Do we produce only in response to real demand? | Spares and engineers released by actual faults, not forecasts |
| 5 | **Perfection** | How do we keep improving? | Daily review of breaches, standard work refined continuously |

Notice the order. You cannot design flow until you know the value stream, and you cannot map the value stream until you have defined value from the customer's point of view.`},{id:"flow-efficiency",heading:"Flow, efficiency and effectiveness",body:`Two words are often confused. **Effectiveness** means doing the *right things* — producing the outcome the customer needs. **Efficiency** means doing things *right* — with the least time, cost and effort. A support process that closes tickets quickly without fixing the fault is efficient but not effective; one that always fixes the fault after six days is effective but not efficient. Lean wants both, and it always starts with effectiveness: there is no point in speeding up a step the customer does not value.

Lean also distinguishes *resource efficiency* (keeping people and assets busy) from *flow efficiency* (how much of the work item's elapsed time is spent having value added). A simple, supplementary way to express flow efficiency is:

$$\\text{Process efficiency} = \\frac{\\text{Value-added time}}{\\text{Total lead time}}$$

[[lead-time|Lead time]] is the total elapsed time a unit spends in the process, from entry to exit — not the same as [[cycle-time]]. In service processes it is very common to find that value-added time is a small fraction of lead time. That is good news: the biggest gains usually come from attacking waiting and hand-offs, not from making the value-added work faster.`},{id:"kaizen-three-ms",heading:"Continuous improvement and the three M’s",advanced:!0,body:`Lean is never "finished". The training book describes [[kaizen]] as the philosophy of continual improvement: every process can and should be evaluated and improved for time, resources, quality and other aspects. Kaizen is usually small, frequent and owned by the people doing the work, in contrast with [[kaikaku]] — radical, breakthrough change. This links Lean directly to [[continuous-improvement]] and the [[pdca]] cycle.

Japanese practice names three related enemies of flow (a supplementary nuance):

- **[[muda|Muda]]** — waste: activity that consumes resources without creating value (the seven wastes of lesson 1.4.4).
- **[[mura|Mura]]** — unevenness: lumpy, uneven workload, such as a flood of tickets every Monday morning after weekend changes.
- **[[muri|Muri]]** — overburden: pushing people or equipment beyond sensible capacity, such as one on-call engineer covering three regions overnight.

They feed each other: uneven demand (mura) creates overload peaks (muri), and overloaded people make errors and leave work waiting (muda). Removing only muda while ignoring mura and muri often lets the waste return.`}],whyItMatters:`For a Green Belt in service operations, Lean thinking is the fastest way to find improvement that customers actually feel. SLA breaches in incident management are rarely caused by the minutes engineers spend fixing faults; they are caused by the hours tickets spend waiting, being re-routed or bouncing back after a failed fix. When you learn to see a process through the customer's eyes and classify each step as VA, NVA or BVA, you can build a credible business case, target the real time-thieves and avoid "optimising" steps that add no value at all. On the exam, expect questions that ask you to classify an activity, identify the defining test for value, or recognise that required non-value-added work is minimised rather than eliminated.`,businessExample:{title:"A fault-repair value stream at a telecom operator",body:`A fixed-broadband operator was missing its 4-hour restoration SLA on about a quarter of business faults. A Green Belt timed 30 tickets end to end. A typical ticket spent **270 minutes** in the process: 5 minutes logging and categorising, **90 minutes waiting** in the first-line queue, **40 minutes** lost to mis-routing and reassignment, 25 minutes of diagnosis, **60 minutes waiting** for change approval, 20 minutes applying the fix, **15 minutes** reworking a fix that failed its first test, 10 minutes restoring and confirming with the customer, and 5 minutes writing closure notes for the regulator's outage record.

Only diagnosis, the fix and the confirmation — **55 minutes** — passed both value tests. Logging and closure notes were business-value-added. The remaining 205 minutes were pure waste.

The team introduced skills-based routing (mis-routing virtually disappeared), a pre-approved standard change for the most common fault types (approval wait fell to about 10 minutes), a test checklist before closure (rework removed) and a shared queue view (first-line wait fell to about 20 minutes). The value-added work did not change at all, yet lead time fell to about **95 minutes** and process efficiency rose from roughly 20% to almost 58%. *(Illustrative figures.)*`},visual:"leanValueStream",visualCaption:"Follow one incident ticket: classify each segment as value-added, business-value-added or waste, then watch the lead-time bar shrink when the waste is removed — while the value-added minutes stay exactly the same.",definitions:[{term:"Lean",definition:"A management philosophy and set of methods that maximise customer value by making value-adding work flow and by systematically removing waste."},{term:"Value",definition:"What the customer recognises and is willing to pay for; in practice, a change to the form, fit or function of the product or service."},{term:"Value-added (VA) activity",definition:"A step that changes form, fit or function and that the customer would willingly pay for."},{term:"Non-value-added (NVA) activity",definition:"A step that does not change form, fit or function and that the customer would not pay for — waste to be eliminated."},{term:"Business-value-added (BVA) activity",definition:"Also called required or necessary non-value-added: the customer would not pay for it, but legal, regulatory, safety or contractual needs require it. It is minimised, not eliminated."},{term:"Waste (muda)",definition:"Material, effort and time that does not add value in the eyes of key stakeholders."},{term:"Flow",definition:"The smooth, uninterrupted movement of a work item through the value-adding steps, without queues, stops or backflows."},{term:"Pull",definition:"Starting or replenishing work only in response to actual downstream demand, rather than pushing work based on forecasts."},{term:"Kaizen",definition:"The philosophy of continual, incremental improvement in which every process is regularly evaluated and improved."},{term:"Process efficiency (supplementary)",definition:"Value-added time divided by total lead time; shows how much of a unit’s elapsed time is spent having value added."}],comparisons:[{title:"VA vs NVA vs BVA",headers:["","Value-added (VA)","Non-value-added (NVA)","Business-value-added (BVA)"],rows:[["Changes form, fit or function?","Yes","No","No"],["Customer willing to pay?","Yes","No","No"],["Required by law, regulation or contract?","Not the reason it exists","No","Yes"],["Lean response","Protect, make it flow","Eliminate","Minimise and simplify"],["Incident-management example","Diagnosing and fixing the fault","Ticket waiting in queue; reassignment","Regulatory outage record; identity check"]]},{title:"Efficiency vs effectiveness",headers:["","Effectiveness","Efficiency"],rows:[["Plain meaning","Doing the right things","Doing things right, with minimum resources"],["Customer view","Did I get the result I needed?","How fast and at what cost?"],["Contact-centre example","Customer’s problem solved","Low handling time and cost per contact"],["Risk if pursued alone","Slow, expensive excellence","Fast closure without a real fix"]]}],misconceptions:[{myth:"Lean means cutting headcount.",reality:"Lean removes wasted time and effort from the process. Freed capacity is typically redeployed to growth or improvement; using Lean as a disguise for layoffs destroys the trust that improvement depends on."},{myth:"If everyone is busy, the process is efficient.",reality:"High resource utilisation can coexist with very long queues. Lean judges the flow of the work item, not how busy people look."},{myth:"Every non-value-added step can be eliminated.",reality:"Business-value-added (required) steps such as regulatory records or security checks must stay; they are minimised and simplified instead."},{myth:"The organisation decides what is value-added.",reality:"Value is defined by the customer. A step the organisation is proud of is still waste if it does not change form, fit or function in a way the customer would pay for."},{myth:"Lean is only for factories.",reality:"Waiting, rework and hand-offs are often worse in service and IT processes, because the work is invisible. Lean applies anywhere work flows."}],examTraps:[{trap:'An option says required regulatory steps should be "eliminated".',explanation:"Business-value-added activities are minimised, not eliminated. Pick the answer that keeps the requirement but simplifies the step.",tag:"not"},{trap:"Inspection, checking or approval steps are offered as value-added.",explanation:"Inspection does not change form, fit or function. It may be required (BVA), but it is not value-added."},{trap:"The question asks who defines value, and an option says management or the process owner.",explanation:"Value is always defined by the customer.",tag:"primary"},{trap:"A calculation question asks for process efficiency, and an option divides VA time by NVA time.",explanation:"Process efficiency divides value-added time by the total lead time (VA + NVA + BVA), not by the waste alone."},{trap:"Lead time and cycle time are used as if they were the same thing.",explanation:"Lead time is the total elapsed time a unit spends in the process. Cycle time is the time to complete one unit at a step (time ÷ units completed). Read which one the question asks for."}],workedExamples:[{title:"Classify the steps of a ticket-handling process",problem:`A service desk handles "cannot access email" tickets. Classify each step as **VA**, **NVA** or **BVA**:

1. Agent verifies the caller's identity (required by information-security policy).
2. Ticket waits in the queue for an agent.
3. Agent asks the user for the same details the self-service portal already captured.
4. Engineer resets the mailbox permission that was causing the fault.
5. Ticket is reassigned because it reached the wrong team.
6. Engineer confirms with the user that email now works.`,steps:["Apply test 1 to each step: does it change the **form, fit or function** of the service the user receives?","Apply test 2: would the user **willingly pay** for this step?","For steps that fail both tests, ask whether a law, regulation, contract or security policy **requires** it. If so it is BVA; if not it is NVA.",'Step 1 fails both tests but is required by policy → **BVA**. Step 2 is waiting → **NVA**. Step 3 is re-entering data that already exists → **NVA** (note the "re-" hint).',"Step 4 changes the function of the service → **VA**. Step 5 is mis-routing → **NVA**. Step 6 confirms the restored function and is part of what the user needs to receive → **VA** in most service definitions."],answer:"VA: steps 4 and 6. BVA: step 1 (minimise it, for example with automated identity verification). NVA: steps 2, 3 and 5 (eliminate them).",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Classifying step 1 as NVA and recommending it be removed to save time.",whyWrong:"The identity check is required by security policy. Removing it would create risk. Business-value-added work is simplified or automated, not deleted."}},{title:"Calculate process efficiency before and after waste removal (supplementary)",problem:"Using the fault-repair example: total lead time is **270 min**, of which value-added time is **55 min** (diagnosis 25 + fix 20 + confirmation 10). After the improvements, lead time is **95 min** and value-added time is unchanged. Calculate process efficiency before and after, and the percentage reduction in lead time.",steps:["Process efficiency = value-added time ÷ total lead time.","Before: 55 ÷ 270 = 0.204 → about **20.4%**.","After: 55 ÷ 95 = 0.579 → about **57.9%**.","Lead-time reduction = (270 − 95) ÷ 270 = 175 ÷ 270 = 0.648 → about **64.8%**.","Interpretation: efficiency almost tripled without anyone working faster on the value-added steps. The whole gain came from removing waiting, mis-routing, approval delay and rework."],answer:"Efficiency rose from about 20.4% to about 57.9%; lead time fell by about 65%.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"55 ÷ 215 = 25.6% (VA divided by NVA time only).",whyWrong:"The denominator must be the total lead time, including the business-value-added minutes and the value-added minutes themselves."}}],lab:null,check:[{id:"chk-1.4.1-1",topic:"1.4.1",category:"definition",difficulty:"beginner",numerical:!1,stem:"Which pair of questions is used to decide whether a process step is **value-added**?",options:["Does it change form, fit or function, and is the customer willing to pay for it?","Does it keep resources busy, and does it reduce cost?","Is it documented in the procedure, and has management approved it?","Does it reduce variation, and is it measured on the dashboard?"],answer:0,explanation:"A value-added step changes the form, fit or function of the product or service in a way the customer would willingly pay for. Both tests are judged from the customer’s point of view.",whyWrong:["","Keeping resources busy is resource utilisation, not value. A busy step can still be pure waste.","Documentation and approval make a step official, not valuable. Many documented steps are waste.","Reducing variation is a Six Sigma goal, and being measured does not make a step valuable to the customer."]},{id:"chk-1.4.1-2",topic:"1.4.1",category:"waste",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"A mobile operator must record every SIM-swap identity check for the telecom regulator. Customers would not pay for this step. How should a Lean team treat it?",options:["Eliminate it, because it is non-value-added.","Reclassify it as value-added, because it is mandatory.","Keep it as business-value-added work, but minimise and simplify it.","Ignore it, because regulatory steps are outside Lean."],answer:2,explanation:"A regulatory requirement makes the step business-value-added (required non-value-added). Lean keeps it but reduces its time and effort, for example through automation or simpler forms.",whyWrong:["Removing a required regulatory step would create compliance risk. Only pure NVA is eliminated.","Being mandatory does not change form, fit or function for the customer, so it is still not value-added.","","Lean examines every step in the value stream, including required ones. They are often good candidates for simplification."],traps:["not"]},{id:"chk-1.4.1-3",topic:"1.4.1",category:"calculation",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!0,stem:"A ticket takes **8 hours** from logging to closure. Engineers spend **1.2 hours** on value-added work, and **0.4 hours** go on a required audit record. What is the process efficiency? *(Supplementary metric.)*",options:["5%","15%","20%","18.8%"],answer:1,explanation:"Process efficiency = value-added time ÷ total lead time = 1.2 ÷ 8 = 0.15 = 15%. The audit record is business-value-added, so it is part of lead time but not of value-added time.",whyWrong:["5% is 0.4 ÷ 8: it uses the business-value-added time as the numerator instead of the value-added time.","","20% is (1.2 + 0.4) ÷ 8: it wrongly treats the required audit record as value-added.","18.8% is 1.2 ÷ (8 − 1.6), about 1.2 ÷ 6.4: it divides by the non-value-added time instead of the total lead time."]},{id:"chk-1.4.1-4",topic:"1.4.1",category:"concept",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"A service desk reports that every agent is 98% utilised, yet customers wait two days for a fix. From a Lean viewpoint, what is the **best** interpretation?",options:["The process is efficient; the problem must be customer expectations.","High resource utilisation hides poor flow — tickets are queuing between busy people.","The agents need to work faster on the value-added steps.","The process is effective because every agent is productive."],answer:1,explanation:"Lean follows the work item, not the resource. Very high utilisation usually creates long queues; the waiting time is waste that the customer feels.",whyWrong:["Resource efficiency is not customer value. Blaming expectations ignores the two days of waste in the process.","","The value-added minutes are rarely the problem. The delay sits in queues, so speeding up the work itself changes little.","Effectiveness means achieving the outcome the customer needs. A two-day wait suggests the process is not meeting that need."],traps:["best"]}],takeaways:["Lean delivers customer-defined value with the least time, effort and material, and keeps improving.","Value tests: does the step change form, fit or function, and would the customer pay for it?","VA is protected; NVA is eliminated; business-value-added (required NVA) is minimised, not removed.","Five principles in order: value → value stream → flow → pull → perfection.","Follow the ticket, not the engineer: flow of the work item matters more than resource utilisation.","Process efficiency = VA time ÷ total lead time (supplementary). Most service gains come from removing waiting and rework.","Kaizen makes improvement continuous; muda, mura and muri are the three enemies of flow."],flashcards:[{front:"The two tests for a value-added step",back:"1) Does it change form, fit or function? 2) Is the customer willing to pay for it?"},{front:"Business-value-added (required NVA)",back:"A step the customer would not pay for but that law, regulation, safety or contract requires. Minimise it; do not eliminate it."},{front:"Who defines value in Lean?",back:"The customer — never the organisation or its managers."},{front:"The five Lean principles (Womack & Jones)",back:"Value, Value stream, Flow, Pull, Perfection."},{front:"Efficiency vs effectiveness",back:"Effectiveness = doing the right things (the outcome). Efficiency = doing things right with minimum resources."},{front:"Process efficiency (supplementary)",back:"Value-added time ÷ total lead time."},{front:'The "re-" hint',back:'Steps starting with "re" — rework, re-enter, re-route, re-approve — are very likely waste.'},{front:"Muda, mura, muri",back:"Muda = waste; Mura = unevenness; Muri = overburden."},{front:"Kaizen",back:"The philosophy of continual, incremental improvement of every process."},{front:"Pull",back:"Work is started or replenished only when real downstream demand signals it."}],coverage:"official-and-book",supplementaryNotes:['The syllabus (p. 7) lists the title "Understanding Lean" only. The supplied training book pages support the value / non-value-added tests (p. 117), the definitions of waste and NVA (glossary pp. 587, 590), and Kaizen (p. 510). The book’s own Define chapter, where Lean is introduced, was not supplied.','The five Lean principles of Womack & Jones, the term "business-value-added", muda / mura / muri, the efficiency vs effectiveness distinction and the process-efficiency ratio come from general Lean knowledge and are not in the supplied pages.',"Lead time and process efficiency are supplementary metrics; they are not named in the Define syllabus text.","All telecom figures are illustrative."],sources:[{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 7",pdfPage:7,note:"Define topic 1.4.1 Understanding Lean (title only)."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 117",pdfPage:20,note:"Identifying waste: value changes “form, fit or function” and the customer is willing to pay; “re-” words hint at waste; some NVA steps, such as regulatory data collection, cannot be removed."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 502",pdfPage:5,note:"What is waste (MUDA): identifying waste is the first step to eliminating it; the seven wastes and the Lean tools pyramid."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 510",pdfPage:13,note:"Kaizen as the philosophy of continual improvement; Kaikaku as breakthrough improvement."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 587",pdfPage:90,note:"Glossary: NVA — does not change form, fit or function, and the customer is not willing to pay for it."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 590",pdfPage:93,note:"Glossary: Waste — material, effort and time that does not add value in the eyes of key stakeholders."}]},F=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:w},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),k={id:"1.4.2",module:4,title:"The History of Lean",subtitle:"Ford, Toyota, Ohno, Shingo and the Lean movement",minutes:10,objectives:["Describe how Ford’s 1913 moving assembly line introduced flow, and why mass production’s large batches did not suit post-war Japan.","Identify the contributions of Sakichi, Kiichiro and Eiji Toyoda, Taiichi Ohno and Shigeo Shingo to the Toyota Production System.","Explain the two pillars of the Toyota Production System — Just-in-Time and Jidoka — and the role of kanban and SMED.",'Trace how "Lean" got its name (Krafcik, 1988) and spread through The Machine That Changed the World (1990) and Lean Thinking (1996).',"Explain how Lean moved into services, healthcare and IT, and how it joined Six Sigma."],overview:`Every Lean tool you will use as a Green Belt — waste walks, kanban boards, quick changeovers, mistake-proofing — was invented to solve a real business problem. Knowing the story helps you remember *why* each tool exists, and it makes the exam’s history questions much easier, because the names, ideas and dates connect into one narrative.

The short version: Henry Ford showed the world the power of **flow**; post-war Toyota could not afford Ford’s huge batches and inventories, so Taiichi Ohno, Eiji Toyoda and Shigeo Shingo built a different system that produced **only what was needed, when it was needed**, and stopped to fix problems immediately. Western researchers studied that system in the 1980s, named it **"lean"**, and turned it into principles any organisation could apply — from car plants to hospitals to network operations centres.`,sections:[{id:"ford-flow",heading:"Before Lean: Ford and the power of flow",body:`Before the twentieth century, cars were built by **craft production**: skilled workers fitted hand-made parts one vehicle at a time. Quality varied and costs were high.

In **1913**, at its Highland Park plant, Ford introduced the **moving assembly line** for the Model T. Instead of workers walking to the car, the car moved past the workers, each performing a short, standardised task with interchangeable parts. Assembly time per car fell dramatically and the car became affordable for ordinary families.

Ford deserves a place in Lean history because he proved that **continuous [[flow]]** is enormously powerful. But his system had a weakness: it relied on very high volumes of a single, standard product, long production runs and large stocks of parts. It worked for one model in huge numbers; it struggled with variety and change. Hold on to that tension — it is exactly what Toyota had to solve.`},{id:"toyota-context",heading:"Post-war Japan and the Toyota challenge",body:`The Toyoda family brought two important ideas into the company before Lean had a name:

- **Sakichi Toyoda** invented automatic looms, including a loom that **stopped itself when a thread broke**, so a defect was not repeated and one worker could watch many machines. This is the origin of **[[jidoka]]** — "automation with a human touch".
- **Kiichiro Toyoda**, who founded the car company in 1937, promoted the idea of making parts **just in time** for use, rather than storing large stocks.

After the Second World War, Japan's situation was very different from Detroit's. Capital was scarce, the domestic market was small and needed many different vehicle types in low volumes, and the company could not afford warehouses full of parts. In **1950, Eiji Toyoda** visited Ford's plants in the United States. He and production manager **Taiichi Ohno** concluded that copying American mass production would not work in Japan — Toyota needed a system that delivered flow *with* variety and *without* waste.`},{id:"tps-pillars",heading:"The Toyota Production System: Just-in-Time and Jidoka",body:`Over the following decades, Ohno and his colleagues developed the **[[toyota-production-system|Toyota Production System (TPS)]]**. It is usually pictured as a house resting on two pillars:

1. **[[jit|Just-in-Time (JIT)]]** — make only what is needed, when it is needed, in the amount needed. Ohno was famously inspired by American supermarkets, where shelves are refilled only as customers take goods. That idea became **[[pull]]** production, controlled by **kanban** cards: a card travelling back upstream is the signal to produce or move more parts.
2. **[[jidoka|Jidoka]]** — build quality in at each step. When an abnormality appears, the line or machine stops so the problem is fixed at its source instead of being passed downstream.

To make small batches economic, changeovers had to become fast. **Shigeo Shingo** developed **SMED (Single-Minute Exchange of Die)** — converting a die change that once took hours into one taking under ten minutes. Shingo is also associated with **poka-yoke** (mistake-proofing). Underneath both pillars sat standardised work and continuous improvement, or **[[kaizen]]**.

Ohno also classified the waste he saw on the shop floor — the origin of the classic seven wastes you study in lesson 1.4.4.`},{id:"naming-lean",heading:'From TPS to "Lean": the name and the books',body:`For years TPS was little known outside Japan. Interest grew after the **1973 oil crisis**, when Toyota recovered faster than many manufacturers. Ohno published his own account, *Toyota Production System: Beyond Large-Scale Production*, in Japanese in **1978** (an English edition followed in 1988).

In the 1980s, the **International Motor Vehicle Program** at MIT compared car plants around the world. In **1988**, researcher **John Krafcik** published an article contrasting Toyota-style plants with traditional mass producers. He used the word **"lean"** because the system used less of everything — less effort, space, inventory and time — to produce more.

Two books then carried the idea worldwide:

- ***The Machine That Changed the World*** (**1990**), by James Womack, Daniel Jones and Daniel Roos, popularised the term "lean production" and its performance advantage.
- ***Lean Thinking*** (**1996**), by Womack and Jones, generalised Lean into the **five principles** — value, value stream, flow, pull and perfection — that you met in lesson 1.4.1, so that any organisation, not just car makers, could apply them.`},{id:"beyond-factory",heading:"Lean beyond the factory: services, healthcare and IT",body:`Once Lean was expressed as principles rather than car-plant techniques, it spread quickly:

- **Healthcare:** hospitals applied value stream thinking to patient journeys — reducing waiting for test results, duplicated paperwork and unnecessary movement of patients and staff.
- **Services and offices:** banks, insurers and governments used Lean on loan approvals, claims and invoice processing, where the "inventory" is a pile of unprocessed cases.
- **IT and telecom:** Lean ideas shaped **Lean IT**, Lean software development and, as wider context, the **DevOps** movement, which focuses on the fast, reliable flow of changes into production. Kanban boards are now common in service desks and network operations centres, and incident-management teams map the flow of tickets just as Ohno mapped the flow of parts.

In parallel, organisations that had adopted Six Sigma — originally a [[motorola]] quality method — began combining it with Lean, giving us **Lean Six Sigma**: Lean for speed and flow, Six Sigma for [[variation]] and defects. That combination is the subject of the next lesson.`}],whyItMatters:'History questions on the Green Belt exam are usually straightforward recall — *who* developed TPS, *what* JIT or jidoka means, *where* the word "lean" came from — but they are easy marks to lose if names and ideas are muddled. More importantly, history explains the logic of the tools. Kanban exists because Toyota could not afford inventory; SMED exists because small batches need fast changeovers; jidoka exists because passing defects downstream multiplies their cost. When you introduce a kanban board in a network operations centre, you are solving the same problem Ohno solved: limiting work-in-progress so that work flows.',businessExample:{title:"A telecom provisioning team rediscovers Ohno",body:`A business-connectivity provisioning team pushed every new order into a shared work queue the moment sales signed it. At any time there were over 400 orders "in progress", engineers switched between dozens of half-finished jobs, and customers waited weeks.

A Green Belt introduced three ideas taken straight from the Toyota Production System. First, a **kanban board with work-in-progress limits**: an engineer could only pull a new order when a slot was free — **pull** instead of push. Second, a **jidoka-style stop rule**: any order with missing site data was stopped and returned immediately to sales with a clear reason, instead of being worked on and failing later. Third, a **SMED-style review** of the router-configuration set-up, which moved preparation steps (template selection, address allocation) to before the engineer's slot, cutting set-up time per order.

Work-in-progress fell sharply and orders began to move steadily from start to finish. The team had not invented anything new; they had applied a history lesson. *(Illustrative scenario.)*`},visual:"leanHistory",visualCaption:"Step through the milestones from Ford’s flow line to Lean in services and IT, or select any milestone to read what happened and why it matters for a Green Belt today.",definitions:[{term:"Toyota Production System (TPS)",definition:"Toyota’s production philosophy and method, developed mainly by Taiichi Ohno with Eiji Toyoda, built on the two pillars of Just-in-Time and Jidoka."},{term:"Just-in-Time (JIT)",definition:"Producing or delivering only what is needed, when it is needed, in the quantity needed."},{term:"Jidoka",definition:'"Automation with a human touch": building quality in by stopping a machine or process automatically when an abnormality occurs, so defects are not passed on.'},{term:"Kanban",definition:"A visual signal (originally a card) that authorises production or movement of material, used to operate a pull system and limit work-in-progress."},{term:"SMED (Single-Minute Exchange of Die)",definition:"Shigeo Shingo’s method for cutting changeover time to under ten minutes, which makes small batches economic."},{term:"Poka-yoke",definition:"Mistake-proofing: designing a step so an error cannot happen or is detected immediately."},{term:"Lean (as a term)",definition:"Word used by John Krafcik in 1988 to describe Toyota-style production that uses less of every resource; popularised by The Machine That Changed the World (1990)."},{term:"Lean Thinking",definition:"Womack and Jones’ 1996 book that generalised Lean into five principles: value, value stream, flow, pull and perfection."}],comparisons:[{title:"Mass production vs the Toyota Production System",headers:["","Mass production (Ford era)","Toyota Production System"],rows:[["Volume and variety","Very high volume, little variety","Lower volumes, high variety"],["Batch size","Large batches, long runs","Small batches, fast changeovers (SMED)"],["Inventory",'Large buffers "just in case"','Minimal stock, "just in time"'],["Scheduling","Push to forecast","Pull to actual demand (kanban)"],["Quality","Inspect at the end, rework later","Build in at each step, stop on abnormality (jidoka)"],["Role of workers","Perform a fixed task","Perform the task and improve it (kaizen)"]]},{title:"Who contributed what",headers:["Person","Key contribution"],rows:[["Henry Ford","Moving assembly line (1913): continuous flow of standardised work"],["Sakichi Toyoda","Self-stopping automatic loom: the origin of jidoka"],["Kiichiro Toyoda","Founded the car company (1937); early just-in-time idea"],["Eiji Toyoda","Studied US plants (1950); championed TPS as Toyota leader"],["Taiichi Ohno","Main architect of TPS: JIT, kanban, pull and the classic wastes"],["Shigeo Shingo","SMED quick changeover and poka-yoke"],["John Krafcik",'Used the term "lean" in 1988'],["Womack, Jones & Roos","The Machine That Changed the World (1990); Womack & Jones, Lean Thinking (1996)"]]}],misconceptions:[{myth:"Henry Ford invented Lean.",reality:"Ford pioneered continuous flow, which inspired Toyota, but his system relied on large batches and inventories. Lean grew out of the Toyota Production System."},{myth:'"Lean" is a Japanese word used by Toyota.',reality:'Toyota called its approach the Toyota Production System. "Lean" was used by MIT researcher John Krafcik in 1988 and popularised in 1990.'},{myth:"Lean and the Toyota Production System are only about cars.",reality:"Lean Thinking (1996) expressed the ideas as general principles. They now apply widely in healthcare, services, telecom and IT."},{myth:"Just-in-Time means having no inventory at all.",reality:"JIT means having only what is needed, when it is needed. Small, controlled buffers managed by kanban are part of a pull system."}],examTraps:[{trap:"An option credits Henry Ford with the Toyota Production System or with jidoka.",explanation:"Ford introduced the moving assembly line (flow). TPS was developed at Toyota, mainly by Taiichi Ohno; jidoka traces back to Sakichi Toyoda’s loom."},{trap:"JIT and jidoka are swapped.",explanation:"JIT = the right amount at the right time (flow and pull). Jidoka = stop and fix abnormalities, building quality in. They are the two different pillars of TPS."},{trap:"SMED is attributed to Ohno, or described as a defect-reduction tool.",explanation:"SMED is associated with Shigeo Shingo and reduces changeover (set-up) time, enabling smaller batches."},{trap:'An option says the term "lean" was coined by Womack and Jones in Lean Thinking (1996).',explanation:'Krafcik used "lean" in 1988; The Machine That Changed the World (1990) popularised it. Lean Thinking (1996) gave the five principles.',tag:"first"}],workedExamples:[{title:"Put the milestones in order",problem:`Arrange these events in chronological order:

A. *The Machine That Changed the World* is published.
B. Ford’s moving assembly line starts at Highland Park.
C. Eiji Toyoda visits US car plants.
D. *Lean Thinking* sets out five Lean principles.
E. Krafcik uses the word "lean" to describe Toyota-style production.`,steps:["Anchor the earliest event: Ford’s moving line, **1913** (B).","Post-war Toyota: Eiji Toyoda’s study visit, **1950** (C).","The naming of Lean comes from MIT research in the late 1980s: Krafcik, **1988** (E).","The book that popularised the term: **1990** (A).","The book that generalised Lean into principles: **1996** (D)."],answer:"B (1913) → C (1950) → E (1988) → A (1990) → D (1996).",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Placing Lean Thinking (D) before The Machine That Changed the World (A).",whyWrong:'The Machine That Changed the World (1990) came first and named "lean production"; Lean Thinking (1996) followed and generalised it into five principles.'}},{title:"Match the problem to the TPS idea",problem:`A network operations centre has three problems. Which Toyota Production System idea addresses each one?

1. Engineers have 30 half-finished changes open at once and nothing gets finished.
2. Faulty configuration templates keep being deployed and fail downstream.
3. Switching an engineer from one router model to another takes 45 minutes of preparation.`,steps:["Problem 1 is excess work-in-progress pushed into the system. The TPS answer is **JIT / pull with kanban**: limit WIP and start new work only when capacity is free.","Problem 2 is defects travelling downstream. The TPS answer is **jidoka**: stop at the first sign of an abnormality, fix the cause, and add mistake-proofing (**poka-yoke**).","Problem 3 is a long changeover. The TPS answer is **SMED**: separate preparation that can be done in advance from work that must be done during the switch, and simplify both."],answer:"1 → JIT / pull with kanban (WIP limits); 2 → jidoka and poka-yoke; 3 → SMED (Shingo)."}],lab:null,check:[{id:"chk-1.4.2-1",topic:"1.4.2",category:"definition",difficulty:"beginner",numerical:!1,stem:"Which two concepts are traditionally described as the **pillars** of the Toyota Production System?",options:["Just-in-Time and Jidoka","DMAIC and Kaizen","Mass production and inspection","5S and the Pareto principle"],answer:0,explanation:"TPS is pictured as a house on two pillars: Just-in-Time (the right amount at the right time) and Jidoka (build quality in by stopping on abnormalities).",whyWrong:["","DMAIC is the Six Sigma roadmap. Kaizen supports TPS but is not one of the two pillars.","TPS was developed as an alternative to mass production and end-of-line inspection.","5S is a foundation tool, and the Pareto principle comes from quality management, not the TPS pillars."]},{id:"chk-1.4.2-2",topic:"1.4.2",category:"concept",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"Shigeo Shingo’s SMED method is **primarily** aimed at which problem?",options:["Reducing variation in product dimensions","Reducing changeover (set-up) time so small batches become economic","Measuring customer satisfaction with surveys","Organising the workplace with labels and shadow boards"],answer:1,explanation:"SMED (Single-Minute Exchange of Die) cuts changeover time, ideally to under ten minutes, which makes small batches and frequent switching affordable — a requirement for Just-in-Time.",whyWrong:["Reducing dimensional variation is a Six Sigma or SPC goal, not the purpose of SMED.","","Customer surveys gather the voice of the customer, which is unrelated to SMED.","Labels and shadow boards describe 5S and the visual factory."],traps:["primary"]},{id:"chk-1.4.2-3",topic:"1.4.2",category:"wording",difficulty:"trap",numerical:!1,stem:'Which statement about the origin of the word "lean" is correct?',options:["Taiichi Ohno named it in his 1978 book.","Henry Ford used it to describe the moving assembly line.","John Krafcik used it in 1988, and The Machine That Changed the World (1990) popularised it.","Womack and Jones first used it in Lean Thinking (1996)."],answer:2,explanation:'MIT researcher John Krafcik used "lean" in 1988 to describe Toyota-style production; the 1990 book by Womack, Jones and Roos made the term widely known.',whyWrong:['Ohno described the Toyota Production System. He did not call it "lean".','Ford’s system is known as mass production, and the term "lean" came decades later.',"","Lean Thinking (1996) gave the five principles, but the term was already in use from 1988–1990."],traps:["first"]},{id:"chk-1.4.2-4",topic:"1.4.2",category:"scenario",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"An automated deployment pipeline halts as soon as a test fails, so the faulty change cannot reach production. Which TPS concept does this **most** closely reflect?",options:["Kanban","Jidoka","SMED","Overproduction"],answer:1,explanation:"Stopping automatically on an abnormality so a defect is not passed downstream is jidoka, which goes back to Sakichi Toyoda’s self-stopping loom.",whyWrong:["Kanban is a signal for pull and limiting WIP, not a stop when a defect is found.","","SMED reduces changeover time and has nothing to do with stopping on defects.","Overproduction is one of the seven wastes, not a TPS countermeasure."],traps:["most"]}],takeaways:["Ford’s 1913 moving assembly line proved the power of flow, but relied on large batches and inventory.","Post-war Toyota needed flow with variety and little capital; Eiji Toyoda and Taiichi Ohno built TPS in response.","TPS stands on two pillars: Just-in-Time (pull, kanban) and Jidoka (stop and fix, quality built in).","Shigeo Shingo: SMED (quick changeover) and poka-yoke (mistake-proofing).",'Krafcik used "lean" in 1988; The Machine That Changed the World (1990) popularised it; Lean Thinking (1996) gave the five principles.',"Lean spread to healthcare, services, telecom and IT, and merged with Six Sigma into Lean Six Sigma."],flashcards:[{front:"What did Ford introduce in 1913?",back:"The moving assembly line at Highland Park: continuous flow of standardised work."},{front:"Main architect of the Toyota Production System",back:"Taiichi Ohno, supported by Eiji Toyoda."},{front:"The two pillars of TPS",back:"Just-in-Time and Jidoka."},{front:"Jidoka",back:'"Automation with a human touch": stop when an abnormality occurs so defects are not passed on. Origin: Sakichi Toyoda’s self-stopping loom.'},{front:"Kanban",back:"A visual signal that authorises production or movement, used to run a pull system and limit WIP."},{front:"SMED — who and what?",back:"Shigeo Shingo; Single-Minute Exchange of Die: changeovers in under ten minutes."},{front:'Who used the word "lean", and when?',back:"John Krafcik, 1988 (MIT International Motor Vehicle Program)."},{front:"The Machine That Changed the World",back:'1990, Womack, Jones and Roos: popularised "lean production".'},{front:"Lean Thinking",back:"1996, Womack and Jones: the five principles (value, value stream, flow, pull, perfection)."}],coverage:"supplementary",supplementaryNotes:['The syllabus (p. 7) lists the topic title "The History of Lean" only. None of the supplied training book pages cover the history of Lean, so this entire lesson is written from well-established general Lean knowledge and is not attributed to the book.',"Only widely documented dates are used: 1913 (Ford moving line), 1937 (Toyota Motor Co. founded), 1950 (Eiji Toyoda’s US visit), 1973 (oil crisis), 1978 (Ohno’s book in Japanese), 1988 (Krafcik), 1990 and 1996 (Womack & Jones books). Other developments are given by period, not by exact year.","Lean IT and DevOps are mentioned only as context for how Lean spread; they are not part of the Green Belt syllabus.","The provisioning example is illustrative."],sources:[{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 7",pdfPage:7,note:"Define topic 1.4.2 The History of Lean (title only). No supplied book page covers this topic."}]},Q=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:k},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),S={id:"1.4.3",module:4,title:"Lean & Six Sigma",subtitle:"Speed meets precision: waste and variation together",minutes:11,objectives:["Contrast Lean’s focus (speed, flow, waste) with Six Sigma’s focus (variation, defects, capability).","Classify a process problem as mainly a waste problem, mainly a variation problem, or both.","Explain why the two approaches are complementary, using the training book’s Lean tools pyramid and its Kaizen / Kaikaku view.","Choose between a quick-win kaizen, Lean tools and a full DMAIC project for a given situation.","Describe how Lean Six Sigma places Lean tools inside the DMAIC roadmap."],overview:`Picture two consultants looking at the same telecom fault-repair process. The first asks, *"Why does it take so long? Where are tickets waiting, bouncing and being reworked?"* The second asks, *"Why is it so inconsistent? Why do some faults take one hour and others twenty, and what drives the SLA breaches?"* The first is wearing **[[lean]]** glasses; the second is wearing **[[six-sigma]]** glasses. Both are right, and both are looking at real problems.

Lean attacks **waste** to make work flow faster. Six Sigma attacks **[[variation]]** to make outcomes predictable and defect-free. A process can be fast but unreliable, or reliable but slow; customers want it fast *and* reliable. That is why the two were combined into Lean Six Sigma — and why the exam expects you to know what each brings, where they overlap and when to reach for which.`,sections:[{id:"lean-focus",heading:"Lean’s focus: speed, flow and waste",body:`Lean judges a process by how smoothly and quickly **value flows** to the customer. Its main enemy is **[[muda|waste]]** — the seven wastes such as [[waiting]], [[correction]] and [[overprocessing]]. Its key questions are:

- What does the customer value, and which steps are [[value-added]]?
- Where does the work item wait, travel or get reworked?
- How can we create [[flow]] and [[pull]], and remove the obstacles?

Lean results are often **visible quickly**: a reorganised queue, a removed approval, a kanban board with WIP limits. Many Lean improvements need **little statistical analysis**, because the waste is obvious once the process is seen clearly — a ticket that waits 90 minutes for nobody in particular does not need a hypothesis test. Typical measures are [[lead-time]], work-in-progress, process efficiency and throughput.`},{id:"six-sigma-focus",heading:"Six Sigma’s focus: variation, defects and capability",body:`Six Sigma judges a process by how **consistently** it meets customer requirements. Its main enemy is **[[variation]]**, because variation is what produces [[defect|defects]] — outcomes outside the [[specification-limits]] set by the customer's [[ctq|CTQs]]. Its key questions are:

- What is the output **Y**, and which inputs **X** drive it? ([[y-equals-f-x|Y = f(X)]])
- How capable is the process of meeting the specification? ([[process-capability]])
- What is the **verified [[root-cause]]** of the defects?

Six Sigma is **data-driven and structured**. It follows the [[dmaic]] roadmap — Define, Measure, Analyze, Improve, Control — and uses statistics to separate real causes from opinions. Typical measures are [[dpmo]], yield, [[standard-deviation]] and sigma level. Projects usually take longer than a Lean quick win, because finding a hidden cause of variation needs evidence.`},{id:"waste-vs-variation",heading:"Waste or variation? Learning to tell them apart",body:`A simple way to tell the two problems apart: **waste makes the average too long or too costly; variation makes the result unpredictable.**

- *Waste example (telecom):* every fault ticket waits about an hour for change approval. The delay is consistent and obvious — Lean removes it.
- *Variation example (telecom):* restoration times for the same fault type range from 30 minutes to 9 hours, depending on the shift, the engineer and the region. The cause is hidden — Six Sigma investigates it.
- *Manufacturing:* moving parts between distant buildings is waste (conveyance); a drilled hole whose diameter drifts outside tolerance is variation.
- *Healthcare:* patients waiting for a bed after discharge is approved is waste; wide differences in medication-preparation time between nurses is variation.

The two are linked. The training book notes that reducing waste can reduce outliers and help prevent defects, because outliers arise from **differing waste** among procedures, machines and people. An extra manual re-entry step is waste — and it is also a fresh opportunity for a typing error that creates variation.`},{id:"complementary",heading:"Why they need each other",body:`Speed without reliability is not good service: a desk that closes tickets fast but re-opens a third of them is simply moving waste around. Reliability without speed fails the customer too: a perfectly consistent five-day repair is still five days. Each method covers the other's blind spot.

The supplied training book makes the connection explicit in several ways:

- **Lean tools can be used at any point** — before, during or after a project — and Belts often use them to *clean up* a badly out-of-control process before applying Six Sigma analysis (p. 501).
- **Waste is often the root of a Six Sigma project** (p. 502). Identifying waste is the first step toward eliminating it, and the project charter's primary metric often measures it.
- **Lean tools add the discipline needed to sustain gains** (p. 502). The book's Lean tools pyramid climbs from **5S → Visual Factory → Standardized Work → Kaizen → Kanban**, each level depending on the one below it.
- **"A Six Sigma project is actually a Kaizen"** (p. 510). The book describes [[kaizen|kaizens]] as Six Sigma projects with business impact, and [[kaikaku]] — breakthrough successes — as the first focus of Six Sigma projects.

The message for the exam: Lean and Six Sigma are **partners, not rivals**.`},{id:"which-when",heading:"Choosing the approach: quick win, Lean tools or DMAIC",body:`Not every problem deserves a four-month DMAIC project. A practical decision guide:

| Situation | Best fit |
|---|---|
| Cause and solution are both obvious, low risk, cheap to fix | **Just do it** — implement and confirm |
| Visible waste in a process (queues, hand-offs, rework loops, clutter) | **Lean tools / kaizen event** — a focused team, days not months |
| Cause unknown, problem is inconsistency or defects, data available | **DMAIC (Six Sigma)** |
| Slow *and* inconsistent, high business impact | **Lean Six Sigma** — DMAIC using Lean tools inside it |

Be careful with the word *kaizen*. In general Lean usage it can mean a short, intensive **kaizen event** (a quick win). In the supplied book it also means the wider philosophy of continual improvement, which includes Six Sigma projects. Read how the question uses it.`},{id:"integration",heading:"Lean Six Sigma: DMAIC with Lean tools inside",advanced:!0,body:`In Lean Six Sigma, DMAIC remains the backbone and Lean tools are used wherever they help:

- **Define:** customer value and [[voc]], a [[project-charter]] whose primary metric captures the waste or defects.
- **Measure:** baseline lead time and defect rates; value stream and process maps (these mapping tools are **Measure-phase syllabus items, 2.1.2**, mentioned here as context).
- **Analyze:** separate waste causes from variation causes and verify root causes with data.
- **Improve:** flow, pull and quick-changeover ideas alongside statistically tested solutions; mistake-proofing.
- **Control:** [[five-s|5S]], [[visual-factory|visual management]], standardized work and statistical control charts to hold the gains.

In a fault-repair project, that could mean removing approval queues and mis-routing (Lean) **and** standardising the diagnostic method for the fault type with the widest time spread (Six Sigma). The first shortens the average; the second narrows the spread. Together they move the whole distribution inside the SLA.`}],whyItMatters:'In service operations you meet both problems every week. SLA reports show averages creeping up because of queues and hand-offs (waste), and they show breaches clustering around certain shifts, vendors or fault types (variation). If you treat every problem as a statistics project, you will be too slow on obvious waste; if you treat every problem as a quick Lean fix, you will miss hidden causes and the breaches will return. Knowing which lens to use — and how to combine them — makes your projects faster, more convincing and more durable. The exam tests this with "which approach is most appropriate" scenarios and with statements about how Lean and Six Sigma relate.',businessExample:{title:"Fixing the 24-hour repair SLA with both lenses",body:`A residential broadband operator promises repair within **24 hours**. Repair times for the main fault type averaged about **20 hours** with a standard deviation of about **6 hours**, so roughly a quarter of repairs breached the SLA.

**Lean lens.** A value stream walk showed most of the elapsed time was waiting: tickets queued overnight for dispatch, engineers drove back to the depot for spare modems, and failed repairs were re-booked as new jobs. Daily dispatch slots, modems carried in vans and a "fix-and-verify before leaving" rule brought the average down to about **14 hours**. With the spread unchanged, breaches fell to around **5%**.

**Six Sigma lens.** Data analysis showed the wide spread came mainly from inconsistent diagnosis — some engineers replaced hardware first, others tested the line first. A standard diagnostic sequence, verified with data, narrowed the standard deviation to about **2.5 hours**. On its own, with the old 20-hour average, that would also have brought breaches to roughly **5%**.

**Lean Six Sigma.** Together — a **14-hour** average with a **2.5-hour** spread — the 24-hour limit sat four standard deviations above the mean, and SLA breaches became extremely rare. *(Illustrative figures, assuming roughly normal repair times.)*`},visual:"leanSixSigmaLens",visualCaption:"Switch lenses on the same fault-repair process. Lean shortens the queue and shifts the repair-time curve to the left; Six Sigma narrows the curve; Lean Six Sigma does both and pulls the distribution inside the 24-hour SLA.",definitions:[{term:"Lean",definition:"An approach that improves speed and flow of value to the customer by eliminating waste."},{term:"Six Sigma",definition:"A data-driven approach that reduces variation and defects so a process consistently meets customer requirements, usually through DMAIC."},{term:"Lean Six Sigma",definition:"The combination of the two: DMAIC as the structure, with Lean tools used inside it, to make processes both fast and reliable."},{term:"Waste (muda)",definition:"Material, effort and time that does not add value in the eyes of key stakeholders."},{term:"Variation",definition:"Differences in process output from one unit to the next; the source of defects when outputs fall outside specification limits."},{term:"Kaizen",definition:"Continual improvement of every process; in the supplied book, a Six Sigma project is described as a kaizen."},{term:"Kaikaku",definition:"Radical or breakthrough improvement; the book calls kaikaku the first focus of Six Sigma projects."},{term:"Lean tools pyramid",definition:"The book’s ladder of Lean tools for sustaining results: 5S → Visual Factory → Standardized Work → Kaizen → Kanban, each depending on the level below."}],comparisons:[{title:"Lean vs Six Sigma vs Lean Six Sigma",headers:["","Lean","Six Sigma","Lean Six Sigma"],rows:[["Core question","Why is it slow and wasteful?","Why is it inconsistent or defective?","How do we make it fast and reliable?"],["Main enemy","Waste (muda)","Variation and defects","Both"],["Typical metrics","Lead time, WIP, process efficiency","DPMO, yield, standard deviation, capability","Both sets"],["Typical tools","Value stream thinking, 5S, kanban, pull, SMED, kaizen events","DMAIC, statistics, root-cause analysis, SPC","DMAIC with Lean tools inside"],["Use of data","Often observation-based; light statistics","Heavy, statistical","As much as the problem needs"],["Typical pace","Days to weeks for quick wins","Months for a full project","Varies with scope"],["Telecom example","Remove approval queue on fault tickets","Reduce spread in repair time across engineers","Both, in one charter"]]}],misconceptions:[{myth:"Lean and Six Sigma are competing methods; you must choose one.",reality:"They are complementary. Lean improves speed and flow, Six Sigma improves consistency. The book treats Lean tools as a way to stabilise and sustain Six Sigma gains."},{myth:"Lean never uses data.",reality:"Lean measures lead time, WIP and throughput carefully. It simply needs less statistical inference when the waste is obvious."},{myth:"Reducing the average always fixes SLA breaches.",reality:"If the spread is wide, a lower average can still leave many results beyond the limit. Breaches depend on both the average and the variation."},{myth:"Every problem needs a full DMAIC project.",reality:"If the cause and solution are obvious and low-risk, just do it or run a kaizen event. DMAIC is for problems with unknown causes and real business impact."}],examTraps:[{trap:"The question asks which approach is primarily focused on reducing variation, and an option says Lean.",explanation:"Variation and defects are the primary focus of Six Sigma. Lean’s primary focus is waste, speed and flow.",tag:"primary"},{trap:"An option says Lean and Six Sigma should be applied to separate processes so they do not conflict.",explanation:"They are complementary and are routinely applied to the same process. Lean Six Sigma integrates them in one project.",tag:"not"},{trap:'A scenario with an obvious cause and a simple, cheap fix offers "launch a full DMAIC project" as an option.',explanation:"When the cause and solution are known, the best action is to implement the fix (or run a short kaizen), not to start a long project.",tag:"best"},{trap:'A true/false-style statement says "a Six Sigma project is a kaizen" and it looks wrong.',explanation:"In the supplied book this is true: kaizens are described as Six Sigma projects with business impact, and kaikaku as breakthrough successes."}],workedExamples:[{title:"Waste problem or variation problem?",problem:`Classify each problem as mainly **waste (Lean)**, mainly **variation (Six Sigma)**, or **both**:

1. Every new enterprise order waits two days for a credit check that is approved 99% of the time.
2. Invoice processing time ranges from 2 to 15 days with no obvious pattern.
3. Contact-centre handling time is long *and* varies widely, because agents search several systems in different orders.`,steps:["Ask: is the problem a consistent delay or extra effort (waste), or unpredictable outcomes (variation)?","Problem 1 is a consistent, visible delay for a check that almost never changes the outcome → mainly **waste**. A Lean fix (risk-based or automated checking) is likely.","Problem 2 is a wide, unexplained spread → mainly **variation**. A DMAIC project is needed to measure the spread and find the verified root cause.","Problem 3 is both: searching several systems is waste (motion and overprocessing), and the different search orders create variation → **both**, a Lean Six Sigma project."],answer:"1 → waste (Lean); 2 → variation (Six Sigma / DMAIC); 3 → both (Lean Six Sigma)."},{title:"Average and spread: why both lenses matter",problem:"Repair times have an average of **20 h** and a standard deviation of **6 h**; the SLA limit is **24 h**. Compare how many standard deviations separate the average from the limit in each case: (a) current; (b) Lean only — average 14 h, SD 6 h; (c) Six Sigma only — average 20 h, SD 2.5 h; (d) both — average 14 h, SD 2.5 h.",steps:["Distance to the limit, in standard deviations, is (limit − average) ÷ SD. A larger number means fewer results beyond the limit.","(a) (24 − 20) ÷ 6 = **0.67 SD** — a large share of repairs breach (about 25% if times are roughly normal).","(b) (24 − 14) ÷ 6 = **1.67 SD** — Lean shifts the average; breaches fall to about 5%.","(c) (24 − 20) ÷ 2.5 = **1.6 SD** — Six Sigma narrows the spread; breaches also fall to about 5%.","(d) (24 − 14) ÷ 2.5 = **4.0 SD** — combining both makes breaches extremely rare (well under 0.1%)."],answer:"0.67 → 1.67 (Lean) or 1.6 (Six Sigma) → 4.0 SD with both: the combined approach gives far more protection than either alone.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Concluding that because the Lean-only result already improves the average, the spread does not matter.",whyWrong:"With a 6-hour standard deviation, about 1 repair in 20 still breaches. Reducing variation is what moves the tail of the distribution inside the limit."}}],lab:null,check:[{id:"chk-1.4.3-1",topic:"1.4.3",category:"compare",difficulty:"beginner",numerical:!1,stem:"Which statement **best** describes the primary focus of each approach?",options:["Lean reduces variation; Six Sigma removes waste.","Lean removes waste and improves flow; Six Sigma reduces variation and defects.","Both focus only on cost reduction.","Lean is used only in manufacturing; Six Sigma only in services."],answer:1,explanation:"Lean targets waste, speed and flow. Six Sigma targets variation, defects and capability. Together they make processes fast and reliable.",whyWrong:["This swaps the two focuses.","","Both can reduce cost, but their primary focuses are waste/flow and variation/defects respectively.","Both are applied across manufacturing, services, healthcare and IT."],traps:["best"]},{id:"chk-1.4.3-2",topic:"1.4.3",category:"best-tool",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"A service desk finds that every password-reset ticket waits for a manager’s approval that has never been refused. The fix is simple and low-risk. What is the **most appropriate** approach?",options:["Launch a full DMAIC project with a designed experiment.","Implement the change as a quick win (just do it or a short kaizen), then confirm the result.","Collect six months of data before making any change.","Add a second approval step to make the process more robust."],answer:1,explanation:"The cause and solution are obvious and low-risk, and the step is pure waste. Implement the fix quickly and confirm it worked; a full DMAIC project would add no value.",whyWrong:["DMAIC is for problems with unknown root causes. Using it here is itself a form of overprocessing.","","Delaying an obvious, low-risk fix only prolongs the waste.","Adding another approval increases the waste."],traps:["most"]},{id:"chk-1.4.3-3",topic:"1.4.3",category:"concept",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"According to the training book, which sequence correctly shows the Lean tools pyramid from the **base** upward?",options:["Kanban → Kaizen → Standardized Work → Visual Factory → 5S","5S → Standardized Work → Visual Factory → Kanban → Kaizen","5S → Visual Factory → Standardized Work → Kaizen → Kanban","Visual Factory → 5S → Kaizen → Standardized Work → Kanban"],answer:2,explanation:"The pyramid starts with 5S workplace organisation, then Visual Factory, Standardized Work, Kaizen and Kanban, with each level depending on the one below it.",whyWrong:["This is the right order but upside down — it starts at the top instead of the base.","Visual Factory comes before Standardized Work, and Kaizen comes before Kanban.","","5S is the foundation, so it must come first."]},{id:"chk-1.4.3-4",topic:"1.4.3",category:"scenario",difficulty:"advanced",numerical:!1,stem:"Average repair time has fallen well below the 24-hour SLA after Lean improvements, but breaches still occur on certain shifts and with certain fault types. What should the Green Belt do **next**?",options:["Declare success, because the average now meets the SLA.","Remove more waiting time to push the average even lower.","Investigate the variation with data (DMAIC) to find the verified root cause of the long-tail repairs.","Raise the SLA limit to 30 hours."],answer:2,explanation:"The remaining breaches come from variation linked to shifts and fault types. That calls for the Six Sigma lens: measure the spread, analyse the drivers and fix the verified root cause.",whyWrong:["A good average does not stop breaches when the spread is wide. Customers in the tail still miss the SLA.","More waste removal may help a little, but it does not address the factors causing the spread.","","Changing the customer requirement to hide the problem is not improvement."],traps:["after"]}],takeaways:["Lean = speed, flow and waste. Six Sigma = variation, defects and capability.","Waste makes the average too long or costly; variation makes results unpredictable.","SLA breaches depend on both the average and the spread, so the two lenses complement each other.","The book: Lean tools stabilise processes and sustain gains; waste is often the root of a Six Sigma project.","Lean tools pyramid: 5S → Visual Factory → Standardized Work → Kaizen → Kanban.","The book calls a Six Sigma project a kaizen, and kaikaku its breakthrough focus.","Obvious cause → just do it or a kaizen event; unknown cause with variation → DMAIC; both slow and inconsistent → Lean Six Sigma."],flashcards:[{front:"Primary focus of Lean",back:"Speed and flow, by eliminating waste."},{front:"Primary focus of Six Sigma",back:"Consistency, by reducing variation and defects (DMAIC)."},{front:"Waste vs variation in one line",back:"Waste makes the average too long or costly; variation makes the result unpredictable."},{front:"Lean tools pyramid (base to top)",back:"5S → Visual Factory → Standardized Work → Kaizen → Kanban."},{front:'"A Six Sigma project is actually a ___"',back:"Kaizen (training book p. 510)."},{front:"Kaikaku",back:"Breakthrough improvement; the book calls it the first focus of Six Sigma projects."},{front:"When is a full DMAIC project not needed?",back:"When the cause and solution are obvious and low-risk: just do it or run a short kaizen event."},{front:"Lean Six Sigma",back:"DMAIC as the structure, with Lean tools inside, so the process becomes fast and reliable."},{front:"Why can a lower average still breach the SLA?",back:"A wide spread leaves a tail of results beyond the limit. Variation must also be reduced."}],coverage:"official-and-book",supplementaryNotes:['The syllabus (p. 7) lists the title "Lean & Six Sigma" only. The book support used here comes from its Control-phase Lean Controls module (pp. 501–502, 510), because the book’s Define chapter was not supplied.','The waste vs variation decision guide, the "just do it / kaizen event / DMAIC" choice and the DMAIC-with-Lean-tools mapping are general Lean Six Sigma knowledge, not quotations from the supplied pages.',"Value stream and process mapping are Measure-phase syllabus items (2.1.2) and are mentioned only as context.","Lead time and process efficiency are supplementary metrics. The repair-time figures and breach percentages are illustrative and assume roughly normal repair times."],sources:[{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 7",pdfPage:7,note:"Define topic 1.4.3 Lean & Six Sigma (title only)."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 501",pdfPage:4,note:"Lean tools can be used at any point; Belts use them to clean up out-of-control processes before applying Six Sigma."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 502",pdfPage:5,note:"Lean tools pyramid (5S → Visual Factory → Standardized Work → Kaizen → Kanban); Lean tools add discipline to sustain gains; waste is often the root of a Six Sigma project; reducing MUDA reduces outliers."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 510",pdfPage:13,note:"“A Six Sigma project is actually a Kaizen”; Kaikaku as breakthrough successes, the first focus of Six Sigma projects."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 590",pdfPage:93,note:"Glossary: Waste — material, effort and time that does not add value in the eyes of key stakeholders."}]},V=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:S},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),T={id:"1.4.4",module:4,title:"The Seven Elements of Waste",subtitle:"Overproduction, Correction, Inventory, Motion, Overprocessing, Conveyance, Waiting",minutes:14,objectives:["Name the seven elements of waste using the exact syllabus terms: Overproduction, Correction, Inventory, Motion, Overprocessing, Conveyance and Waiting.","Recognise each waste from its symptoms in service and manufacturing processes, and suggest a typical countermeasure.","Distinguish the commonly confused pairs: Motion vs Conveyance, Overproduction vs Overprocessing, Correction vs Inventory, and Waiting vs Inventory.","Map the seven elements to the eight-waste (DOWNTIME) framework shown in the syllabus introduction without merging the two lists.","Explain why Overproduction is often called the worst waste and how waste links to the hidden factory and COPQ."],overview:`In Lean thinking, **waste** — the Japanese word is [[muda]] — is any activity that consumes time, effort, space or money without adding [[value]] the customer would willingly pay for. The Green Belt syllabus (topic 1.4.4) names **seven elements of waste**: **Overproduction, Correction, Inventory, Motion, Overprocessing, Conveyance and Waiting**. Learn those seven words exactly as written, because the exam uses them.

Here is the encouraging part: once you can name a waste, you can see it everywhere — in a network operations centre (NOC) ticket queue, in an invoice approval chain, in a technician's van. The seven elements are a pair of glasses. This lesson teaches each waste with its causes, symptoms, examples and countermeasures, then shows how the popular *eight*-waste list relates to the official seven.`,sections:[{id:"seeing-waste",heading:"Seeing waste: the value lens",body:`Every step of a process falls into one of three buckets. A [[value-added]] step changes the product or service in a way the customer cares about and would pay for — the fibre actually being spliced, the refund actually being credited. A [[non-value-added]] step consumes resources without that change. Some non-value-added steps cannot yet be removed (a regulatory record, an audit trail); these are often labelled [[business-value-added]] — keep them lean, but keep them.

The seven elements are a checklist for finding the non-value-added work. The training book makes the point that **identifying waste is the first step toward eliminating it**, and that waste is often the root of a Six Sigma project (book p. 502). It also notes that reducing waste can reduce *outliers*, because different people, machines and procedures carry different amounts of waste — so Lean and Six Sigma reinforce each other.

A handy tip from process mapping: steps whose names begin with **"re-"** — re-enter, re-check, re-test, re-dispatch — are usually waste in disguise.`},{id:"first-three",heading:"Overproduction, Correction and Inventory",body:`**[[overproduction|Overproduction]] — making more, or earlier, than the next step needs.**
*Causes:* batching "to be efficient", forecasts instead of real demand, utilisation targets that reward being busy. *Symptoms:* piles of finished work nobody has asked for yet, reports produced but never read. *Service example:* a contact centre prints and mails a monthly statement to customers who already receive e-bills. *Manufacturing example:* a line builds 500 units when 300 were ordered. *Countermeasure:* produce to actual demand — [[pull]] signals, smaller batches, [[jit|just-in-time]] release. Overproduction is often called **the worst waste** because it *creates* the others: the extra output must be stored (Inventory), moved (Conveyance), counted, and it hides defects until much later (Correction).

**[[correction|Correction]] — work spent fixing errors.**
This is defects plus the [[rework]], inspection and repeat effort they trigger. *Symptoms:* repeat visits, reopened tickets, credit notes, "please resubmit" emails. *Service example:* an invoice keyed with the wrong purchase-order number is rejected and re-processed. *Manufacturing example:* a casting is reground after failing inspection. *Detect it* by counting reopen rates, first-time-right rates and repeat contacts. *Countermeasure:* error-proofing, clear operational definitions, fixing root causes rather than adding checkers. Much Correction lives in the [[hidden-factory]] and shows up as [[copq]].

**[[inventory|Inventory]] — more material, information or work-in-progress than the process needs right now.**
*Symptoms:* backlogs, full shelves, a ticket queue with hundreds of items "awaiting assignment". *Service example:* 1,200 unprocessed claims in a shared mailbox. *Hospital example:* a store room holding six months of dressings, some expiring. *Countermeasure:* limit work-in-progress, balance capacity, replenish on use. Inventory is a *quantity of stuff*; it ties up cash and hides problems, like water covering rocks in a river.`},{id:"last-four",heading:"Motion, Overprocessing, Conveyance and Waiting",body:`**[[motion|Motion]] — unnecessary movement of people (or of equipment) *within* the workstation.**
Reaching, bending, searching, walking back to the van, clicking through seven screens to find one field. *Service example:* a NOC engineer toggling between four monitoring tools to correlate one alarm. *Manufacturing example:* an operator walking to a shared cabinet for a gauge used every few minutes. *Countermeasure:* ergonomic layout, point-of-use storage, single-pane dashboards — [[five-s|5S]] is the classic tool.

**[[overprocessing|Overprocessing]] — doing more work, or to a higher standard, than the customer requires.**
*Symptoms:* extra approvals, duplicate data entry, polishing beyond spec, a 20-page report where a one-line status would do. *Service example:* a manager must sign off every standard broadband order, although the system has already validated it. *Manufacturing example:* machining a surface to a tolerance far tighter than the drawing demands. *Countermeasure:* confirm requirements with the [[voc|Voice of the Customer]] and remove steps that change nothing the customer values. The training book labels this waste simply **"Processing"** (p. 502) — same idea, shorter name.

**[[conveyance|Conveyance]] — moving materials, products or information *between* locations** more than necessary.
*Symptoms:* multiple hand-offs, double handling, items routed through hubs. *Service example:* a ticket bounced from the service desk to three resolver groups before reaching the right team; a router shipped warehouse → hub → depot → van. *Hospital example:* blood samples walked to a distant lab in batches. *Countermeasure:* co-locate, route directly, reduce hand-offs.

**[[waiting|Waiting]] — people, equipment or work idle because something upstream is not ready.**
*Symptoms:* "pending approval" statuses, technicians on site waiting for remote activation, patients in a waiting room. *Software example:* a release ready on Monday that waits until Thursday's change-advisory board. *Countermeasure:* balance workloads, synchronise hand-offs, pre-approve standard changes. Waiting is measured in *time*, and it stretches [[cycle-time|cycle time]] directly.`},{id:"distinctions",heading:"The pairs that trip people up",body:`**Motion vs Conveyance.** Ask *what* is moving and *where*. A person (or a machine part) moving within the work area is **Motion**. Materials, products or information travelling from one location to another is **Conveyance**. A technician walking back and forth to the van at the customer's door: Motion. A pallet of modems trucked from a regional warehouse to a depot: Conveyance.

**Overproduction vs Overprocessing.** Overproduction is about **quantity and timing** — too many, too soon. Overprocessing is about **effort and specification** — too much work on each item. Printing 1,000 brochures when 400 are needed is Overproduction; laminating each brochure when nobody asked for lamination is Overprocessing.

**Correction vs Inventory.** Correction is *effort spent fixing an error*. Inventory is *a quantity sitting in the system*. A stack of rejected invoices could reflect both — the stack is Inventory; the re-keying is Correction. Name the one the question describes.

**Waiting vs Inventory.** They are two views of the same queue. From the *work item's* point of view, a ticket sitting in a queue is Inventory (WIP). From the *person's or customer's* point of view, idle time is Waiting. Exam questions usually signal which perspective they mean: "the agent is idle until…" points to Waiting; "500 orders are queued" points to Inventory.`},{id:"eight-wastes",heading:"The seven elements vs the eight-waste (DOWNTIME) list",body:`You will meet a second, very popular framework: the **eight wastes**, remembered as **DOWNTIME** — **D**efects, **O**verproduction, **W**aiting, **N**on-utilized talent, **T**ransportation, **I**nventory, **M**otion, **E**xtra-processing. The syllabus *introduction* (p. 2) uses this eight-item wording, while the **Define topic 1.4.4** (p. 7) lists the **seven** elements. Treat them as two frameworks and know how they line up:

| Syllabus 1.4.4 (seven elements) | Book p. 502 label | Eight-waste list (syllabus p. 2) |
|---|---|---|
| Correction | Correction | Defects |
| Overproduction | Overproduction | Overproduction |
| Waiting | Waiting | Waiting |
| *(not one of the seven)* | — | **Non-utilized talent** |
| Conveyance | Conveyance | Transportation |
| Inventory | Inventory | Inventory |
| Motion | Motion | Motion |
| Overprocessing | Processing | Extra-processing |

Three renames and one addition. [[non-utilized-talent|Non-utilized talent]] — failing to use people's skills, ideas and knowledge, such as experienced NOC engineers doing manual copy-paste while their automation ideas go unheard — is a genuine and useful waste, but it is **not** one of the seven elements in topic 1.4.4. When a question asks about "the seven elements of waste", answer with the seven syllabus terms.`}],whyItMatters:'Waste is where cost, delay and customer frustration hide. Each repeat visit, re-keyed invoice or idle hour inflates [[copq|COPQ]] and stretches the time customers wait. For someone governing [[sla|SLAs]] and vendor performance, the seven elements give you a precise vocabulary: instead of "the process is slow", you can say "40% of elapsed time is Waiting for approvals, and 12% of tickets need Correction". Precise naming leads to precise countermeasures — and to Six Sigma projects with clear, measurable scope.',businessExample:{title:"Fibre installation: a waste walk in field service",body:`A telecom operator's fibre-to-the-home installations were missing their 5-day target. A Green Belt walked one order end to end and labelled what she saw:

- **Correction** — 9% of orders were captured with the wrong package and had to be re-keyed after the customer called back.
- **Overprocessing** — every order needed a manual feasibility survey and manager sign-off, even for buildings already marked "fibre-ready".
- **Overproduction** — on Mondays, dispatch prepared work packs for the whole week; a quarter were rescheduled and prepared again.
- **Inventory** — each van carried about 40 routers while using roughly three a day, and 300 orders sat unscheduled.
- **Conveyance** — routers travelled warehouse → regional hub → depot → van, handled four times.
- **Motion** — technicians walked back to the van five or six times per job because tools were not kitted.
- **Waiting** — technicians waited an average of 35 minutes on site for the NOC to activate the port.

None of these needed new technology to see. Naming them turned a vague complaint into seven specific improvement opportunities, which the team then prioritised with data.`},visual:"sevenWastesMap",visualCaption:"A telecom field-service order flows through seven steps. Each waste lights up where it typically hides — use step mode to study one at a time.",definitions:[{term:"Waste (Muda)",definition:"Any activity that consumes resources — time, effort, space, money — without adding value the customer is willing to pay for."},{term:"Overproduction",definition:"Producing more, or earlier, than the next process or customer needs. Often called the worst waste because it generates others."},{term:"Correction",definition:'Effort spent finding and fixing errors: defects, rework, repeat work and the inspection they trigger. Called "Defects" in the eight-waste list.'},{term:"Inventory",definition:"Any material, information or work-in-progress beyond what the process needs right now — backlogs, queues, excess stock."},{term:"Motion",definition:"Unnecessary movement of people or equipment within a workstation: reaching, searching, walking, excess clicking."},{term:"Overprocessing",definition:'Doing more work, or to a higher specification, than the customer requires. Labelled "Processing" in the book (p. 502) and "Extra-processing" in the eight-waste list.'},{term:"Conveyance",definition:'Unnecessary movement of materials, products or information between locations, including extra hand-offs. Called "Transportation" in the eight-waste list.'},{term:"Waiting",definition:"Idle time when people, equipment or work cannot proceed because an input, approval or resource is not ready."},{term:"Non-utilized talent",definition:"Failing to use people's skills, knowledge and ideas. Added in the eight-waste (DOWNTIME) framework; not one of the seven elements in topic 1.4.4."}],comparisons:[{title:"Seven elements (syllabus 1.4.4) vs eight wastes (syllabus intro, p. 2)",headers:["Syllabus 1.4.4 term","Book p. 502 label","Eight-waste (DOWNTIME) term","Change"],rows:[["Correction","Correction","Defects","Renamed"],["Overproduction","Overproduction","Overproduction","Same"],["Waiting","Waiting","Waiting","Same"],["—","—","Non-utilized talent","Added in eight-waste list only"],["Conveyance","Conveyance","Transportation","Renamed"],["Inventory","Inventory","Inventory","Same"],["Motion","Motion","Motion","Same"],["Overprocessing","Processing","Extra-processing","Renamed"]]},{title:"Commonly confused pairs",headers:["Pair","First waste is about…","Second waste is about…","Quick test"],rows:[["Motion vs Conveyance","People/equipment moving within the workstation","Materials/information moving between locations","Is a person moving at the work area, or is the item travelling?"],["Overproduction vs Overprocessing","Too many items, or too early","Too much work or spec per item","Quantity/timing, or effort/spec?"],["Correction vs Inventory","Effort spent fixing errors","A quantity of work or stock sitting in the system","Is someone fixing, or is something piling up?"],["Waiting vs Inventory","Idle time of people, equipment or customers","Items queued as work-in-progress","Whose point of view — the person's or the item's?"]]},{title:"Seven-waste field guide",headers:["Waste","Typical cause","Symptom you can see","Typical countermeasure"],rows:[["Overproduction","Batching, forecast-driven work, utilisation targets","Output nobody has asked for yet","Produce to demand; pull; smaller batches"],["Correction","Unclear standards, no error-proofing","Reopened tickets, repeat visits, credit notes","Root-cause fixes; error-proofing"],["Inventory","Unbalanced capacity, large batches","Backlogs, full shelves, long queues","WIP limits; replenish on use"],["Motion","Poor layout, tools not at point of use","Searching, walking, screen-hopping","5S; point-of-use storage"],["Overprocessing","Unclear customer requirements, distrust","Extra approvals, duplicate entry","Confirm VOC; remove non-value steps"],["Conveyance","Distant locations, many hand-offs","Items routed via hubs, ticket ping-pong","Co-locate; direct routing"],["Waiting","Unsynchronised hand-offs, bottlenecks",'"Pending" statuses, idle staff',"Balance load; pre-approve standard work"]]}],misconceptions:[{myth:"Non-utilized talent is the eighth of the syllabus's seven elements of waste.",reality:"Topic 1.4.4 lists exactly seven elements. Non-utilized talent belongs to the eight-waste (DOWNTIME) framework, which the syllabus introduction mentions separately."},{myth:'Motion and Conveyance are the same thing — both are "movement".',reality:"Motion is people or equipment moving within the workstation; Conveyance is materials, products or information moving between locations."},{myth:"Overproduction and Overprocessing mean the same: doing too much.",reality:"Overproduction is too many or too early (quantity/timing). Overprocessing is too much work or too high a specification on each item (effort/spec)."},{myth:"Waste only exists in factories.",reality:"Every waste appears in services: queued tickets (Inventory), re-keyed invoices (Correction), approval delays (Waiting), duplicate data entry (Overprocessing), ticket hand-offs (Conveyance)."},{myth:"Adding more inspection removes Correction.",reality:"Inspection finds errors after they happen and is itself non-value-added. Correction falls when root causes are removed and errors are prevented."},{myth:"All non-value-added steps can be deleted immediately.",reality:"Some are required by regulation or the business (business-value-added). Minimise them rather than removing them blindly."}],examTraps:[{trap:'An option list mixes "Defects", "Transportation" or "Extra-processing" with the seven syllabus terms.',explanation:"Those are the eight-waste names. If the question asks for the seven elements as listed in the syllabus, look for Correction, Conveyance and Overprocessing."},{trap:'"Which of the following is NOT one of the seven elements of waste?" with Non-utilized talent as an option.',explanation:'Non-utilized talent is the addition in the eight-waste framework, so it is the answer for a "NOT one of the seven" question.',tag:"not"},{trap:"A scenario describes a technician repeatedly walking to the van for tools, and offers Conveyance.",explanation:"A person moving within the work area is Motion. Conveyance would be materials or information moving between locations."},{trap:"A scenario describes extra approvals on a validated order, and offers Overproduction.",explanation:"Extra effort beyond the customer requirement is Overprocessing. Overproduction is about producing too many or too early."},{trap:'"Which waste is often considered the worst because it causes the others?"',explanation:"Overproduction — it generates Inventory, Conveyance and hidden Correction. Do not pick Correction just because defects feel most serious.",tag:"most"},{trap:'A question uses the book label "Processing".',explanation:'In the book (p. 502) "Processing" is the same waste the syllabus calls Overprocessing.'}],workedExamples:[{title:"Classify the wastes in an invoice-processing flow",problem:`An accounts-payable team describes its week:
1. Invoices are scanned in one office and couriered to a second office for keying.
2. 8% of invoices are keyed with the wrong cost centre and must be re-keyed.
3. Every invoice, even under 50 OMR, needs two signatures.
4. 900 invoices sit in the "awaiting approval" folder.
5. Clerks wait each morning until the overnight batch upload completes.

Name the waste for each item using the syllabus terms.`,steps:["Item 1: paper invoices travel between two locations — **Conveyance**.","Item 2: effort spent fixing an error — **Correction**.","Item 3: more approval effort than the risk or customer requires — **Overprocessing**.","Item 4: a quantity of work-in-progress piled in the system — **Inventory**.","Item 5: people idle because an upstream step is not ready — **Waiting**."],answer:"Conveyance, Correction, Overprocessing, Inventory, Waiting.",wrongApproach:{calculation:'Item 2 = "Defects"; Item 1 = "Transportation".',whyWrong:"These are eight-waste labels. They describe the same ideas, but an exam item on the seven elements expects Correction and Conveyance."}},{title:"Motion or Conveyance? Overproduction or Overprocessing?",problem:"In a NOC: (a) an engineer moves between four consoles to check one alarm; (b) alarm logs are exported nightly and emailed to three regional teams who never open them; (c) every P4 ticket gets a full root-cause report written to P1 standard.",steps:["(a) The person is moving (physically or across screens) within the workstation — **Motion**.","(b) Output is produced that no downstream customer needs — **Overproduction** (the emailing is a small Conveyance side effect, but the primary waste is producing unneeded output).","(c) Each ticket receives more work than the customer requires — **Overprocessing**."],answer:"(a) Motion, (b) Overproduction, (c) Overprocessing."}],lab:"waste",check:[{id:"chk-1.4.4-1",topic:"1.4.4",category:"waste",difficulty:"beginner",numerical:!1,stem:"Which of the following is **NOT** one of the seven elements of waste listed in syllabus topic 1.4.4?",options:["Conveyance","Non-utilized talent","Overprocessing","Correction"],answer:1,explanation:"Non-utilized talent is the addition in the eight-waste (DOWNTIME) framework. The seven elements are Overproduction, Correction, Inventory, Motion, Overprocessing, Conveyance and Waiting.",whyWrong:["Conveyance is one of the seven (the eight-waste list calls it Transportation).","","Overprocessing is one of the seven (Extra-processing in the eight-waste list).","Correction is one of the seven (Defects in the eight-waste list)."],traps:["not"]},{id:"chk-1.4.4-2",topic:"1.4.4",category:"waste",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"A field technician walks back to the van five times during each installation to fetch tools. Which waste is this?",options:["Conveyance","Waiting","Motion","Inventory"],answer:2,explanation:"A person moving unnecessarily within the work area is Motion. Keeping a standard tool kit at the point of use is the typical countermeasure.",whyWrong:["Conveyance is materials or information moving between locations, not a person moving within the workstation.","The technician is not idle; they are moving.","","No excess stock or queue of work is described."]},{id:"chk-1.4.4-3",topic:"1.4.4",category:"waste",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"Every standard broadband order requires a manager signature, even though the ordering system has already validated it. Which waste best describes the signature step?",options:["Overproduction","Overprocessing","Correction","Conveyance"],answer:1,explanation:"Doing more work than the customer or the risk requires is Overprocessing.",whyWrong:["Overproduction is producing too many or too early, not extra effort on each item.","","Nothing is being fixed; no error has occurred.","No material or information is being moved between locations as the main issue."]},{id:"chk-1.4.4-4",topic:"1.4.4",category:"waste",difficulty:"trap",numerical:!1,stem:"Which waste is often described as the **worst** because it tends to create the other wastes?",options:["Correction","Waiting","Inventory","Overproduction"],answer:3,explanation:"Overproduction creates excess Inventory, extra Conveyance and storage, and hides defects until later, so it is often called the worst waste.",whyWrong:["Correction is costly, but it is usually a consequence of other problems rather than the generator of the other wastes.","Waiting is often a symptom of imbalance, not the source of the others.","Inventory is largely created by Overproduction.",""],traps:["most"]},{id:"chk-1.4.4-5",topic:"1.4.4",category:"compare",difficulty:"advanced",numerical:!1,stem:'In the eight-waste (DOWNTIME) framework, "Transportation" corresponds to which syllabus 1.4.4 element?',options:["Conveyance","Motion","Waiting","Overprocessing"],answer:0,explanation:"Transportation is the eight-waste name for Conveyance: moving materials, products or information between locations.",whyWrong:["","Motion keeps the same name in both lists and concerns movement within the workstation.","Waiting keeps the same name in both lists.","Overprocessing corresponds to Extra-processing."]}],takeaways:["Waste (muda) is any activity that uses resources without adding value the customer would pay for.","The syllabus 1.4.4 seven elements: Overproduction, Correction, Inventory, Motion, Overprocessing, Conveyance, Waiting.","Motion = people/equipment moving within a workstation; Conveyance = items or information moving between locations.","Overproduction = too many or too early; Overprocessing = too much work or spec per item.","Overproduction is often called the worst waste because it creates the others.","The eight-waste (DOWNTIME) list renames three wastes (Defects, Transportation, Extra-processing) and adds Non-utilized talent — keep it separate from the seven.",'The book (p. 502) calls Overprocessing simply "Processing".',"Naming wastes precisely turns vague complaints into scoped, measurable improvement opportunities."],flashcards:[{front:"List the seven elements of waste (syllabus 1.4.4).",back:"Overproduction, Correction, Inventory, Motion, Overprocessing, Conveyance, Waiting."},{front:"What does DOWNTIME stand for?",back:"Defects, Overproduction, Waiting, Non-utilized talent, Transportation, Inventory, Motion, Extra-processing."},{front:"Which waste appears in the eight-waste list but not in the seven elements?",back:"Non-utilized talent."},{front:"Motion vs Conveyance?",back:"Motion: people or equipment moving within a workstation. Conveyance: materials, products or information moving between locations."},{front:"Overproduction vs Overprocessing?",back:"Overproduction: too many or too early. Overprocessing: more work or a higher spec than the customer requires."},{front:"Why is Overproduction called the worst waste?",back:"It creates others — extra inventory, storage, conveyance — and hides defects until later."},{front:"Eight-waste name for Correction?",back:"Defects."},{front:"What does the book (p. 502) call Overprocessing?",back:'"Processing".'},{front:"A ticket queue of 600 unassigned tickets is mainly which waste?",back:"Inventory (work-in-progress sitting in the system)."},{front:"An engineer idle until a change-advisory board approves a release — which waste?",back:"Waiting."}],coverage:"official-and-book",supplementaryNotes:["Causes, symptoms, countermeasures and all service, telecom, hospital and software examples are original explanations from general Lean knowledge. They are not taken from the supplied book pages.",'The DOWNTIME mnemonic and the description of Overproduction as "the worst waste" are widely used Lean teaching conventions. They are not quoted from the supplied book pages.',"The book's main Define treatment of waste (MUDA) is in the Define chapter, which was not supplied. Page 502 says 5S and MUDA were first covered there."],sources:[{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 7",pdfPage:7,note:"Define topic 1.4.4: The Seven Elements of Waste — Overproduction, Correction, Inventory, Motion, Overprocessing, Conveyance, Waiting."},{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 2",pdfPage:2,note:"Introduction lists the eight kinds of waste (muda): Defects, Over-Production, Waiting, Non-Utilized Talent, Transportation, Inventory, Motion, Extra-Processing."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 502",pdfPage:5,note:"What is Waste (MUDA)? Lists the seven wastes as Correction, Overproduction, Processing, Conveyance, Inventory, Motion, Waiting. Says waste identification is the first step to elimination and that 5S and MUDA were covered in the Define chapter (not supplied)."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 503",pdfPage:6,note:"Sustaining gains by eliminating MUDA; wastes can return if Lean tools are not used."},{doc:"book-101-200",pages:"p. 117",pdfPage:20,note:'Identifying waste: value-added tests (form, fit, function; customer willing to pay) and the "re-" word hint.'}]},N=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:T},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),x={id:"1.4.5",module:4,title:"5S: Sort, Straighten, Shine, Standardize, Self-Discipline",subtitle:"Workplace organization that makes problems visible",minutes:12,objectives:["State the five S's in sequence using the syllabus terms, with their Japanese names (Seiri, Seiton, Seiso, Seiketsu, Shitsuke) and common alternative English labels.","Apply the Sort decision (useful / useless / unknown, with red tags) and A/B/C frequency-based storage to a real workspace.","Explain how Straighten and Shine create visual controls and reveal abnormal conditions.","Distinguish Standardize (rules and visual standards) from Self-Discipline (habit, ownership and audit).","Apply 5S to digital work: shared drives, ticket queues, knowledge bases and dashboards.","Explain why 5S is the foundation of the visual factory and the other Lean tools."],overview:`[[five-s|5S]] is a five-step method for organising a workplace so that **everything needed is at hand, everything has a home, and anything abnormal stands out at a glance**. The syllabus names the steps **Sort, Straighten, Shine, Standardize and Self-Discipline**. They come from five Japanese words — *Seiri, Seiton, Seiso, Seiketsu, Shitsuke* — and the training book sums up the result neatly: the workplace is clean, there is a place for everything, and everything is in its place (book p. 503).

5S can look like housekeeping. It is more than that. A disorganised workstation hides waste: searching is [[motion|Motion]], surplus spares are [[inventory|Inventory]], and a cluttered alarm console hides the one alarm that matters. 5S makes those problems *visible*, which is why the book calls it the starting point for improving a process.`,sections:[{id:"foundation",heading:"Why 5S comes first",body:`The book's Lean tools pyramid (p. 502) puts **5S workplace organisation at the base**, with the [[visual-factory|visual factory]], standardised work, [[kaizen]] and kanban stacked above it. Each level depends on the one below: you cannot run a visual factory in a cluttered space, and you cannot standardise work when tools wander.

The sequence matters. The **first three S's are workplace actions** — physically changing the space. The **last two are sustaining actions** — keeping the change from unravelling (book p. 503). The book recommends starting with a pilot area, proving the benefit, then spreading step by step. A Green Belt might 5S one NOC shift bench, or one invoice team's shared folder, before rolling out across the department.

| Step | Syllabus term | Japanese | Common alternative label |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Sort | Seiri | Sorting, Clearing up |
| 2 | Straighten | Seiton | Set in order, Organising |
| 3 | Shine | Seiso | Sweep, Cleaning |
| 4 | Standardize | Seiketsu | Standardising |
| 5 | Self-Discipline | Shitsuke | Sustain, Training & discipline |

The book's table (p. 504) uses "Sustaining" for step 5 and spells step 4 *Seketsu*; the syllabus uses **Self-Discipline**. They mean the same step.`},{id:"sort",heading:"1. Sort (Seiri): keep only what is needed",body:`[[sort|Sort]] separates the necessary from the unnecessary. The book's decision is simple (p. 505). Every item is **useful, useless or unknown**:

- **Useless** — dispose of it now.
- **Unknown** — put a [[red-tag|red tag]] on it and move it to a holding area. If nobody uses it within a set period (say 30 days), dispose of it. This is "keep and monitor".
- **Useful** — keep it, but store it according to how often it is used.

That last step is **A/B/C storage by frequency of use** (p. 506):

| Class | How often used | Where it lives |
|---|---|---|
| A | Daily or several times a day | Within arm's reach |
| B | Weekly | Locally, in the work area |
| C | Monthly or quarterly | Remote storage, a short walk away |

At a NOC bench, the console, headset and runbook binder are A items. A spare SFP module used weekly is B. Last year's vendor cables and three dead laptops are useless. A box of unlabelled patch cords is unknown — red-tag it. Sort frees space, makes the area safer, and immediately cuts searching.`},{id:"straighten",heading:"2. Straighten (Seiton): a place for everything",body:`[[straighten|Straighten]] — also called **Set in order** — gives each remaining item a designated, labelled home so that anyone can find it, use it and put it back without thinking. Typical techniques (book p. 506): labels, floor tape, **shadow boards** (outlines showing where each tool belongs), things used together stored together, and shared items kept in a central spot.

The payoff is visual: an empty outline on a shadow board tells you *instantly* that a fibre cleaver is missing. Straighten cuts search time and walking distance, so it attacks Motion and [[waiting|Waiting]] directly. In a warehouse, it means fixed bin locations by part number. In a hospital ward, it means a crash cart with every drawer labelled in the same order on every floor.`},{id:"shine",heading:"3. Shine (Seiso): clean to inspect",body:`[[shine|Shine]] — also called **Sweep** — means cleaning the area and equipment, then making cleaning part of everyday work. The book's key idea is **mess prevention**: find out *why* the mess appears and stop it at the source (p. 507). A layout map that assigns cleaning areas to named people removes any "no man's land" that nobody owns.

Shine is really **cleaning as inspection**. When a technician wipes down a rack, they notice the loose connector or the blocked fan filter. When a NOC team clears hundreds of stale, acknowledged-but-ignored alarms, the genuinely new alarm is finally visible. A clean workplace reveals abnormal conditions — and that is the point.`},{id:"standardize-sustain",heading:"4–5. Standardize vs Self-Discipline",body:`These two are often confused, so let us separate them carefully.

**[[standardize|Standardize]] (Seiketsu)** creates the **rules and visual standards** that keep the first three S's in place: photo standards of how the bench should look, colour coding, labelled zones, a daily 5-minute checklist, a folder-naming convention. The book stresses **visual controls** — signboards, painted lines, colour coding — so that any deviation is obvious (p. 507). Standardize answers: *"What does good look like, and how do we all do it the same way?"*

**[[self-discipline|Self-Discipline]] (Shitsuke)** — also called **Sustain** — is the **habit and commitment** to follow those standards every day without being chased. The book describes it as practising and repeating until it becomes a way of life, supported by schedules and checklists, with the team empowered to maintain its own area (p. 508). Self-Discipline answers: *"Do we keep doing it, and how do we know?"* Its main tools are **5S audits** — regular, scored walk-throughs, ideally rotated among team members — plus leadership attention and recognition.

A quick test: a written rule or a visual marker is Standardize; the routine of honouring it, checked by audit, is Self-Discipline.`},{id:"digital-visual",heading:"Digital 5S and the visual factory",body:`Much modern work has no physical bench, but 5S still applies:

- **Shared drives** — Sort out obsolete and duplicate files; Straighten with a clear folder structure and file-naming convention; Shine by archiving monthly; Standardize with a folder template; Sustain with a quarterly owner review.
- **Ticket queues** — close or merge stale tickets, set clear categories and assignment groups, and make ageing tickets turn amber or red.
- **Knowledge bases** — retire outdated articles, use one template, show an owner and review date on every article.
- **Dashboards** — keep only the KPIs someone acts on, in a fixed layout, with consistent colours for "on target" and "breach".

This is the **[[visual-factory|visual factory]]**, which the book says is built on 5S standards (p. 508): a workplace where even a newly hired supervisor can see inventory levels, extra tools, scrap, downtime and changeover problems at a glance. On a well-5S'd SLA dashboard, a breach risk is obvious without opening a single report.

Some organisations add a sixth S — **Safety** — to make 6S. This is a common extension, not part of the syllabus list.`}],whyItMatters:"5S is cheap, fast and visible, so it is often the first Lean tool a team adopts — and it shapes behaviour. It cuts Motion (searching), Inventory (surplus stock) and Waiting (missing tools), and it makes abnormal conditions jump out. The book notes that outliers in a project metric, or a loss of earlier gains, are signs that 5S is needed (p. 513). For service operations, a disciplined queue, knowledge base or dashboard is the digital equivalent of a shadow board: problems surface early, before they become [[sla|SLA]] breaches.",businessExample:{title:"A field-service depot and its NOC: 5S in six weeks",body:`A regional field-service depot had technicians spending about 20 minutes each morning hunting for meters, splicers and spare optical modules. The NOC that supported them had a shared drive with 14,000 files and an alarm view cluttered with stale alerts.

- **Sort:** the team red-tagged 230 depot items; 60% were disposed of after 30 days unused. On the shared drive, 9,000 duplicate or obsolete files were archived.
- **Straighten:** shadow boards for splicing kits, A/B/C shelving for spares, and a standard van layout; one folder template for every region.
- **Shine:** a cleaning map with named owners; the NOC cleared 1,100 stale alarms and fixed the noisy sources rather than just acknowledging them.
- **Standardize:** photo standards for benches and vans, colour-coded bins, a daily 5-minute end-of-shift checklist.
- **Self-Discipline:** fortnightly scored 5S audits rotated across teams, with results posted on the depot board.

Morning search time fell to under five minutes, and the NOC's mean time to acknowledge genuine alarms dropped noticeably because real alarms were no longer buried.`},visual:"fiveSWorkstation",visualCaption:"A cluttered NOC/field-service bench is transformed one S at a time — watch what each step removes, places, cleans, standardizes and sustains.",definitions:[{term:"5S",definition:"A five-step workplace organisation method — Sort, Straighten, Shine, Standardize, Self-Discipline — that makes the workplace clean, orderly and visually controlled."},{term:"Sort (Seiri)",definition:"Separate needed from unneeded items; remove the unneeded and red-tag the uncertain."},{term:"Straighten (Seiton)",definition:'Also "Set in order": give every needed item a designated, labelled home, easy to see and reach.'},{term:"Shine (Seiso)",definition:'Also "Sweep": clean and inspect the workplace, and prevent mess at its source.'},{term:"Standardize (Seiketsu)",definition:"Create rules, visual standards and checklists that keep the first three S's in place."},{term:"Self-Discipline (Shitsuke)",definition:'Also "Sustain": make following the standards a habit, reinforced through audits, schedules and leadership.'},{term:"Red tag",definition:"A tag placed on an item whose need is uncertain. The item moves to a holding area and is disposed of if not used within a set period."},{term:"A/B/C storage",definition:"Storage by frequency of use: A (daily) within arm's reach, B (weekly) locally, C (monthly or quarterly) in remote storage."},{term:"Visual control",definition:"A visual device — label, colour code, floor line, shadow board, status colour — that shows the standard so deviations are obvious."},{term:"Visual factory",definition:"A workplace, built on 5S, where status, problems and abnormalities can be seen at a glance by anyone."}],comparisons:[{title:"The five S's: terms, Japanese names and alternative labels",headers:["Step","Syllabus term","Japanese","Alternative label","Core question"],rows:[["1","Sort","Seiri","Sorting / Clearing up","Do we need it here?"],["2","Straighten","Seiton","Set in order","Where does it live?"],["3","Shine","Seiso","Sweep","Is it clean, and why did it get dirty?"],["4","Standardize","Seiketsu","Standardising","What does good look like for everyone?"],["5","Self-Discipline","Shitsuke","Sustain","Do we keep doing it — and how do we know?"]]},{title:"Standardize vs Self-Discipline",headers:["Aspect","Standardize (Seiketsu)","Self-Discipline (Shitsuke)"],rows:[["Nature","Rules, standards, visual controls","Habit, commitment, culture"],["Typical tools","Photo standards, colour coding, checklists, templates","5S audits, schedules, recognition, leadership walks"],["Question answered","What does good look like?","Are we still doing it?"],["Failure looks like","Everyone organises differently","Standards exist but nobody follows them"]]},{title:"A/B/C storage by frequency of use (book p. 506)",headers:["Class","Frequency of use","Within arm's reach","Local location","Remote location"],rows:[["A","Daily or several times a day","Yes","Maybe","No"],["B","Weekly","Maybe","Yes","No"],["C","Monthly or quarterly","No","No","Yes"]]}],misconceptions:[{myth:"5S is just a cleaning campaign.",reality:"Cleaning is only Shine. 5S is a system for removing the unneeded, organising what remains, making the standard visible and sustaining it — so that problems become obvious."},{myth:"The order of the S's does not matter.",reality:"Sequence matters: you sort before you straighten (no point labelling junk), and you standardize and sustain only once the area is organised."},{myth:"Standardize and Self-Discipline are the same thing.",reality:"Standardize sets the rules and visual standards; Self-Discipline is the habit of following them, checked through audits."},{myth:"Red-tagged items are thrown away immediately.",reality:"Red tags are for uncertain items. They are held and monitored for a set period, and disposed of only if nobody uses them."},{myth:"5S only applies to factories and physical spaces.",reality:"Digital 5S applies the same steps to shared drives, ticket queues, knowledge bases and dashboards."},{myth:"Safety is the official sixth S in the syllabus.",reality:`"6S" with Safety is a common extension; the syllabus lists five S's.`}],examTraps:[{trap:'An option uses "Sustain", "Set in order" or "Sweep" instead of the syllabus term.',explanation:"These are accepted alternative labels for Self-Discipline, Straighten and Shine. Recognise them, but expect the syllabus terms in a question about topic 1.4.5."},{trap:'"Which S comes FIRST?"',explanation:"Sort (Seiri). You remove what is not needed before organising what remains.",tag:"first"},{trap:"A scenario describes photo standards and colour-coded zones, and asks which S.",explanation:"Creating rules and visual standards is Standardize — not Self-Discipline, which is about habit and audit."},{trap:"A scenario describes regular 5S audits and leadership follow-up, and offers Standardize.",explanation:"Audits and habit-building to keep standards alive are Self-Discipline (Sustain)."},{trap:"A scenario describes shadow boards and labelled locations, and offers Sort.",explanation:"Giving each item a designated home is Straighten (Set in order). Sort decides what stays at all."},{trap:'"Which S is primarily about inspection through cleaning?"',explanation:"Shine (Seiso). Cleaning reveals abnormal conditions such as leaks, loose parts or stale alarms.",tag:"primary"}],workedExamples:[{title:"Sort and store a NOC bench",problem:"A NOC shift bench holds: the alarm console (used constantly), a fibre power meter (used about weekly), a quarterly DR test kit, two broken headsets, and a box of unlabelled patch cords nobody recognises. Apply the Sort decision and A/B/C storage.",steps:["Classify each item as useful, useless or unknown. Broken headsets: **useless**, so dispose of them now. Unlabelled patch cords: **unknown**, so red-tag them and hold them for a set period.","The console, power meter and DR kit are **useful**. Assign classes by frequency: console = **A** (several times a day), power meter = **B** (weekly), DR kit = **C** (quarterly).","Place them: A within arm's reach, B in a local labelled cabinet, C in remote storage. Straighten then gives each a labelled home."],answer:"Dispose of the headsets; red-tag the patch cords; console = A at the bench, power meter = B locally, DR kit = C in remote storage.",wrongApproach:{calculation:"Throw out the unlabelled patch cords immediately.",whyWrong:'Unknown items are red-tagged and monitored first ("keep and monitor"). Disposing of them immediately risks losing something genuinely needed.'}},{title:"Name the S: digital 5S of an invoice team's shared drive",problem:"An invoice-processing team: (a) deletes 4,000 duplicate scans; (b) creates folders by supplier/year/month; (c) runs a monthly archive of closed invoices and fixes the scanner setting that created duplicates; (d) publishes a one-page naming standard with examples; (e) has a team member score the drive against the standard every quarter.",steps:["(a) Removing what is not needed — **Sort**.","(b) Giving every file a defined home — **Straighten**.","(c) Cleaning, plus preventing mess at its source — **Shine**.","(d) A written, visual rule everyone follows — **Standardize**.","(e) A recurring audit that keeps the habit alive — **Self-Discipline**."],answer:"Sort, Straighten, Shine, Standardize, Self-Discipline."}],lab:"fiveS",check:[{id:"chk-1.4.5-1",topic:"1.4.5",category:"definition",difficulty:"beginner",numerical:!1,stem:"What is the correct sequence of the 5S steps as listed in the syllabus?",options:["Sort, Shine, Straighten, Standardize, Self-Discipline","Sort, Straighten, Shine, Standardize, Self-Discipline","Straighten, Sort, Shine, Self-Discipline, Standardize","Shine, Sort, Straighten, Standardize, Self-Discipline"],answer:1,explanation:"The syllabus order is Sort, Straighten, Shine, Standardize, Self-Discipline (Seiri, Seiton, Seiso, Seiketsu, Shitsuke).",whyWrong:["Shine comes after Straighten, not before it.","","Sort comes first, and Standardize comes before Self-Discipline.","Shine is the third step; you remove unneeded items first."]},{id:"chk-1.4.5-2",topic:"1.4.5",category:"scenario",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"A team installs shadow boards so that each splicing tool has an outlined, labelled position. Which S is this?",options:["Sort","Shine","Straighten","Self-Discipline"],answer:2,explanation:"Giving each item a designated, visible home is Straighten (Set in order).",whyWrong:["Sort decides which items stay at all; it does not assign locations.","Shine is cleaning and inspection.","","Self-Discipline is the habit of maintaining the standards, usually checked by audit."]},{id:"chk-1.4.5-3",topic:"1.4.5",category:"compare",difficulty:"trap",numerical:!1,stem:"Which activity **best** represents Self-Discipline rather than Standardize?",options:["Publishing photo standards of how each bench should look","Colour-coding storage bins by part family","Running fortnightly scored 5S audits with posted results","Writing a folder-naming convention for the shared drive"],answer:2,explanation:"Self-Discipline (Sustain) is about habit and ongoing commitment; recurring audits are its main tool. The other options create rules or visual standards, which is Standardize.",whyWrong:["Photo standards define what good looks like — Standardize.","Colour coding is a visual control — Standardize.","","A naming convention is a rule — Standardize."],traps:["best"]},{id:"chk-1.4.5-4",topic:"1.4.5",category:"scenario",difficulty:"intermediate",numerical:!1,stem:"During Sort, an item is found whose purpose nobody knows. What should normally happen to it?",options:["Red-tag it and hold it for a set period; dispose of it if unused","Dispose of it immediately","Store it within arm's reach in case it is needed","Leave it where it is and label it permanently"],answer:0,explanation:"Unknown items are red-tagged and kept under monitoring for a set period. If unused, they are disposed of.",whyWrong:["","Immediate disposal is for items known to be useless.","Arm's-reach storage is for A items used daily, not for unknown items.","Labelling an unknown item permanently skips the Sort decision."]},{id:"chk-1.4.5-5",topic:"1.4.5",category:"concept",difficulty:"advanced",numerical:!1,stem:"Using frequency-based (A/B/C) storage, where should a tool used about once a quarter be kept?",options:["Within arm's reach","In a local cabinet in the work area","It should be red-tagged and discarded","In remote storage away from the work area"],answer:3,explanation:"Monthly or quarterly items are class C and belong in remote storage. Daily items (A) stay within reach, and weekly items (B) are kept locally.",whyWrong:["Arm's reach is for class A items used daily.","Local storage is for class B items used weekly.","The tool is used and needed, so it is not discarded.",""]}],takeaways:["Syllabus 5S terms: Sort, Straighten, Shine, Standardize, Self-Discipline — in that order.","Japanese: Seiri, Seiton, Seiso, Seiketsu, Shitsuke. Alternative labels: Set in order (Straighten), Sweep (Shine), Sustain (Self-Discipline).","Sort: useful / useless / unknown — dispose of useless items, red-tag and monitor unknown ones, store useful ones by A/B/C frequency.","Straighten gives every item a visible, labelled home; Shine means cleaning as inspection and mess prevention.","Standardize = rules and visual standards; Self-Discipline = habit, checked by audits.","5S is the base of the Lean tools pyramid and the foundation of the visual factory.","Digital 5S applies to shared drives, ticket queues, knowledge bases and dashboards.","Safety as a sixth S (6S) is a common extension, not part of the syllabus list."],flashcards:[{front:"The five S's in syllabus order?",back:"Sort, Straighten, Shine, Standardize, Self-Discipline."},{front:"Japanese names of the five S's?",back:"Seiri, Seiton, Seiso, Seiketsu, Shitsuke."},{front:"Alternative label for Straighten?",back:"Set in order."},{front:"Alternative label for Self-Discipline?",back:"Sustain."},{front:"Sort decision categories?",back:"Useful (store by A/B/C), useless (dispose of now), unknown (red-tag, keep and monitor)."},{front:"Where are A, B and C items stored?",back:"A (daily): within arm's reach. B (weekly): locally. C (monthly or quarterly): remote storage."},{front:"Standardize vs Self-Discipline?",back:"Standardize creates rules and visual standards; Self-Discipline makes following them a habit, checked by audits."},{front:"What is the key idea of Shine?",back:"Cleaning as inspection, and mess prevention at the source."},{front:"What is a visual factory?",back:"A workplace built on 5S where status and abnormalities are visible at a glance."},{front:"Give a digital 5S example for a ticket queue.",back:"Close stale tickets (Sort), set clear categories (Straighten), make ageing visible by colour (Standardize), audit regularly (Self-Discipline)."}],coverage:"official-and-book",supplementaryNotes:["Digital 5S (shared drives, ticket queues, knowledge bases, dashboards) and every telecom, NOC, invoice and hospital example are original applications. They are not taken from the supplied book pages.",'"6S" (adding Safety) is a common industry extension. It is not in the syllabus or the supplied book pages.',"The description of 5S audits (scored and rotated) is general Lean practice. The book names schedules and checklists as Sustaining tools (p. 508).","The book's first teaching of 5S is in the Define chapter, which was not supplied. Page 502 says 5S and MUDA were covered there; the supplied pages 503–508 are a Control-phase review."],sources:[{doc:"syllabus",pages:"p. 7",pdfPage:7,note:"Define topic 1.4.5: 5S — Sort, Straighten, Shine, Standardize, Self-Discipline."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 502",pdfPage:5,note:"Lean tools pyramid with 5S at the base; notes that 5S and MUDA were first covered in the Define chapter (not supplied)."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 503",pdfPage:6,note:`5S origin and meaning; "a place for everything and everything in its place"; first three S's are workplace actions, the last two sustain them; pilot then spread.`},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 504",pdfPage:7,note:"Japanese/English 5S table (Seiri, Seiton, Seiso, Seketsu, Shitsuke) with one-line definitions."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 505",pdfPage:8,note:"Sorting: decision tree (useful / useless / unknown; keep and monitor); tag items that cannot be removed right away."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 506",pdfPage:9,note:"A/B/C storage-by-frequency table; Straightening techniques (labels, tape, shadow outlines, shared items central)."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 507",pdfPage:10,note:"Shining (mess prevention, cleaning responsibility map); Standardizing (visual controls such as signboards, painted lines, colour coding)."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 508",pdfPage:11,note:"Sustaining (schedules, checklists, empowerment) and the Visual Factory built on 5S."},{doc:"book-501-600",pages:"p. 513",pdfPage:16,note:"Signs 5S is needed: outliers in the project metric or loss of initial gains."}]},Y=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:x},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"}));export{C as _,A as a,P as b,O as c,M as d,D as e,I as f,W as g,q as h,B as i,j,L as k,R as l,E as m,F as n,Q as o,V as p,N as q,Y as r};
